ening to see someone who hates America serving in the United States of representatives. I love America. And at the end of the day, that is the fundamentally difference here. And I would be happy to accept that investigation to have a debate right here on your show with Congresswoman Ilhan Omar.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She held a press conference about this incident tonight. She's not going to let it go, of course. Here's just a little piece.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ILHAN OMAR, (D-MN): We cannot pretend that this hate speech from leading politicians doesn't have real consequences. The truth is that anti- Muslim hate is on the rise both here at home and around the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congresswoman Boebert, she was elected to Congress. In your response to this, are you anti-Muslim?\par \par \uc2 BOEBERT: Oh, absolutely not. And I apologize to the Muslim community. And even Omar brought up a man who was calling with death threats, and I fully believe that that man needs to be found by \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  and held fully accountable just like the men who have called with death threats against my family, against my staff, my restaurant, all in the past few days. They need to be found and held accountable.\par \par \uc2 And you know how that is going to happen? By our brave law enforcement officers who I fully support and who Ilhan has disparaged and worked to defund. I certainly do not criticize anyone's religion or faith, and that is exactly why I released my statement to the Muslim community.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congresswoman Boebert, one would thing given what has happened in Minneapolis, and the rising crime, murder rate, everything that is happening in Minneapolis post George Floyd, one would think that Congresswoman Ilhan Omar would focus on that, especially after the defund the police thing went down in flames in that referendum. But you're the focus. You're the focus of Ilhan Omar. That's convenient.\par \par \uc2 BOEBERT: Right, because her policies are failed. And even Minnesota is seeing that. And that's why we're seeing the defund the police movement go away, because we do need our law enforcement officers. We deeply support them.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Gone down in flames.\par \par \uc2 BOEBERT: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congresswoman Boebert, we have got to run, but we appreciate your clearing things up and coming on the show tonight. And that debate would be fun.\par \par \uc2 Democratic senators are now threatening the Supreme Court over Roe, and Biden's vaccine mandates hit another wall. Ken Starr weighing in on both next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I hope the Supreme Court is listening to the people of the United States. I think if you want to see a revolution, go ahead. Outlaw Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: A moderate Democrat threatening revolution. Forget those nasty comments. We hope the Supreme Court is listening to us tonight, not to her. Why? Because if a six to three conservative Supreme Court isn't enough to overturn Roe, then the entire conservative legal movement will have been in vain. Yes, that's what I'm saying tonight.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Ken Starr, FOX News contributor, former independent counsel and federal appellate court judge. Ken, so in your view, how many originalists are there really on the Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 KEN STARR, FORMER INDEPENDENT COUNSEL: I think, and we may disagree on this, I think there's six. I really do. I think John Roberts, the chief justice, is an originalist, but he is an incrementalist. So there are times when some of us get frustrated with the way he votes, but I think there are six. And so tomorrow's case, which brings up Roe versus Wade is going to be, I think, a huge test. And maybe I'm wrong.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, we have four decades plus after Roe. We've had lots of fancy dinners hosted by the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 . I know you and I have attended many of them over the years. Millions of dollars donated to the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 , a lot of great people involved.\par \par \uc2 But again, six Republican appointees on this court. And yet you and I are sitting here wondering whether they are going to vote to confirm the idea that our founders in the Constitution believed it was a constitutional right to end life as we know it in the womb. To me, that is just staggering if that's what any of those six turn out to believe.\par \par \uc2 STARR: Well, I doubt that any of the six believe that at all. The question then becomes, if you think Roe v. Wade as a judge, you took an oath to uphold the Constitution of the United States and to give your best judgment. And so if you sincerely think that Roe versus Wade was wrongly decided and was, as Justice Kavanaugh has put it in describing what kinds of wrong decisions deserve to be overruled, egregiously wrong, if you agree with that proposition, then you should, in fact, vote to overrule.\par \par \uc2 But what is, as you well know, is guarding against that is the idea of the court's precedents have to be taken very seriously. And that's where you said, it's a half-century of law, and are we going to disrupt that. That's an enormous judgment call at least for three of the justices. So this may be one of those three-three-three splits that we saw so frequently in Justice Barrett's first term.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: If that's where it goes, I don't even have any words.\par \par \uc2 Ken, I want to get to something I mentioned at the top earlier tonight. Another federal judge blocked Biden's COVID vaccine mandate for health care workers now across the country, this Louisiana decision. These losses over the mandates keep piling up. How did Biden's legal team -- can't all be this out of it. How did they think this was going to pass legal muster?\par \par \uc2 STARR: Hope springs eternal, I guess, because they're having to scramble. For example, in this case and the judge's 34-page opinion today was very strong, first on constitutional power, does the agency have the power under the statute, et cetera. But one of the things it shows that the government could have handled differently was, hey, it took you two months to issue this rule --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, it's not an emergency.\par \par \uc2 STARR: Yes. It's not an emergency, and you should have through a process of let the people speak --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Ken, I'm sorry to cut you off. We've got a hard break. We got the point.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: First it was the San Fran hair salon, now Nancy Pelosi caught mask-less at a major event in Washington tonight. As you can see in this photo obtained by Breitbart, children at the event are masked, but not old Nan. So it's masks for thee, but not for me. How pathetic and predictable.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. Thank you for watching. And remember, it's America now and forever. Greg Gutfeld takes it all from here. And remember, freedom matters.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020211201ehbu00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Pandemic And Inflation Trouble Overshadow Biden Agenda; Collins Says Omicron May Be Milder; 15-Year-Old Michigan Sophomore In Custody; New Survey Shows Trust In Military Falling Rapidly; China Frequently Attacking U.S. Satellites; Police Say Jussie Smollett Paid The Osundairo Brothers To Attack Him; "Laptop From Hell" Book Claims \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Profited From Son's Business Dealings; CNN Indefinitely Suspends Chris Cuomo After Documents Released Showing His Involvement In Defense Of His Brother Former New York Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  Against Sexual Harassment Allegations; New Video Released Of Islamophobic Comments By Republican Representative \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  About Representative Ilhan Omar; Supreme Court To Hear Case Regarding Mississippi Law Restricting Abortion; Federal Judge In Western District Of Louisiana Rules Vaccine Mandates For Healthcare Workers Cannot Be Upheld\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Jonathan Serrie, Garrett Tenney, Jennifer  Griffin, Matt Finn, Kevin Corke, Howard Kurtz, Aishah Hasnie, Mara Liasson,  Katie Pavlich, David Spunt \par \uc2 7,746 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 November 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 EMILY COMPAGNO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR (voice-over): And all proceeds benefit the \uc2 Oregon Humane Society\uc2 . It's from 11:00 to 1:00. The \uc2 Oregon Humane Society\uc2  rescues, heals, and adopts more than 11,000 pets each year. And what you're looking at are a few of our favorite photos. Ardiri Winery this Saturday. Merry Christmas.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): That's cute.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Very cute.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You and your pets.\par \par \uc2 GERALDO RIVERA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT-AT-LARGE (on camera): Oregon?\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Yes, in Oregon.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Howliday, I think I get it.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: Oregon.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Howliday.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's it for us. SPECIAL REPORT is up next with Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Jesse, what's amazing is that Dana's potty mouth appears this time of year.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It's a tradition.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks, Jess.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Check my schedule and figure out why.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: (INAUDIBLE) tradition this day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier. "BREAKING TONIGHT", we are just within the past few minutes receiving word that a federal judge in Wilmington Delaware is blocking a Biden administration COVID-19 vaccination rule for health care workers. That's coming from the wire services. The judge is applying that ruling nationwide where the mandate is still in effect.\par \par \uc2 We're going to follow up on this, but it is another blow to the administration's efforts on COVID-19 mandates for vaccines.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, President Biden's twin challenges as he seeks to rebound from sinking poll numbers, in part, brought on by a spike in inflation. And the newest problem, a gut-punch of another coronavirus variant.\par \par \uc2 Those issues and warnings from the Fed chair today sent the markets tumbling on Wall Street. The president was on the road today trying to sell the benefits of his biggest legislative victory so far.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Peter Doocy starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 And the markets aren't spooked by the prospect of a government shutdown, Friday because \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  says that is not going to happen. The markets are spooked instead by either the pandemic or the president's policies -- it depends on who you ask.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This can help rebuild the backbone of this nation.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): This is what the White House wants you focused on. Their one big win, infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This law builds back our bridges, our water systems, our power line, our electric grid, better and stronger, more resilient, and resistant to the negative impacts and effects of climate change.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But the economy is once again in a perilous position because of the pandemic. As the chair of the Federal Reserve, \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  warns of new trouble with jobs and growth to come.\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, FEDERAL RESERVE: The recent rise in COVID-19 cases and the emergence of the Omicron variant post downside risks to the employment and economic activity, and increased uncertainty for inflation.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Higher prices are something Republicans blame on Biden's big spending.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (R-KY): Democrats didn't directly legislate a big chunk of this inflation into existence.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And it may be here to stay.\par \par \uc2 SEN. PAT TOOMEY\uc2  (R-PA): How long does inflation have to run above your target before the Fed decides maybe it's not so transitory?\par \par \uc2 POWELL: I think it's probably a good time to retire that word.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That word, transitory, has been used at the White House since the spring.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Our economists have conveyed that they feel that the impact of our proposals will be transitory.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And even as recently as seven days ago.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: White House officials who previously said they think inflation is transitory? Is that still the view of the White House right now?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: That's the view of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and outside economic experts, most importantly.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So, what word will the White House use instead?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: The question right now isn't what you call it, it's what you're going to do about it.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: What do White House officials do when the president goes off-script, even if it's just to talk to an 11-year-old? Cue the music.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is Brady Wallace.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Hey, Brady Wallace, how old are you?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): The president is on his way home right now. And home is where Democrats are ignoring Republican warnings that big government spending is a big driver of government inflation because it is all systems go on the multi-trillion-dollar Build Back Better social spending plan, Chuck Schumer said today, he expects that to pass the Senate by Christmas. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter, real quick. The administration has run into some stumbling blocks here, as far as getting this mandate for vaccines. We're just hearing that this federal judge has ruled against the mandate for health care workers applying that nationwide. What do they say when they are challenged by these legal hiccups, if you will, for them?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: They've basically just been instructing people to move forward -- instructing employers to keep implementing these vaccine requirements for their workers, even as courts have been saying, wait a second, we don't know if this is legal right now.\par \par \uc2 So, they don't outwardly say ignore the courts, but they basically say, listen to us at the White House, don't listen to judges in whichever circuit are hearing these challenges.\par \par \uc2 And so, once the president lands back here in Washington, and they start having briefings again tomorrow when he has an event about the supply chain, I would expect if they continue talking like they have been to hear more of that. Just do what we have said, with these vaccine requirements. We think it's really important, whatever -- regardless of what a judge says. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Law factors in there. All right, Peter Doocy, live on the North Lawn. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks plummeted today. The Dow sank 652. The S&P 500 lost 88. The \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  fell 245.\par \par \uc2 Major drug makers are testing to see if their COVID-19 vaccines are going to be effective against the Omicron variant. Correspondent Jonathan Serrie tells us where things stand tonight from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): It could take two weeks for laboratory tests and real-world surveillance to determine whether the Omicron variant is more resistant to current antibody treatments and vaccines.\par \par \uc2 But researchers are concerned about changes to the spike protein, what \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2  calls the business end of the virus.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISOR TO PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : And these mutations have been associated with increased transmissibility and immune evasion.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Moderna CEO speculates that could pose a challenge to existing vaccines.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANE BANCEL\uc2 , CHIEF EXECUTIVE OFFICER, MODERNA: Given the large number of mutation, this highly possible, and the efficacy of the vaccine, all of them is going down.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: But vaccine developers and federal health officials believe full vaccination plus a COVID booster shot will still lower the chances of severe disease.\par \par \uc2 ALBERT BOURLA\uc2 , CHIEF EXECUTIVE OFFICER, \uc2 PFIZER\uc2 : I don't know if it will be equally effective at 95 plus percent against the Omicron. But I don't think -- I would be very surprised if they are very, very low.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: It remains unclear whether Omicron causes more severe disease than previous variants, based on early cases in South Africa.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANGELIQUE COETZEE\uc2 , CHAIRPERSON OF THE SOUTH AFRICAN MEDICAL ASSOCIATION: The majority of what we are presenting to primary healthcare practitioners are extremely mild case, so mild to moderate.\par \par \uc2 DR. FRANCIS COLLINS, DIRECTOR, NATIONAL INSTITUTES OF HEALTH: Early indications maybe it's milder, but that was mostly looking at young people. So, I'd be hesitant to call it right now. But that is a critical question. We should have more answers soon.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: As a precaution, drug companies are already developing specific vaccines for Omicron as well as new treatments. An official at Merck tells Fox News, its experimental pill, Molnupiravir, will likely be active against the Omicron variant, although specific studies have yet to be done to confirm this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (on camera): And late this afternoon, and \uc2 FDA\uc2  advisory committee voted 13 to 10 to recommend authorization of this drug for use in non- pregnant adults. Now, if it is approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , this will become the first oral medication to treat COVID-19 and the first that patients could take at home. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Heard Admiral Giroir speaking about that last night. Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Certainly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Three students are dead tonight, several others are injured, and one student is in custody following a school shooting in Michigan. It happened this afternoon at Oxford High School in Oxford Township, about 30 miles north of Detroit.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Garrett Tenney has the latest from Chicago. Good evening, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 The three students who lost their lives today were just 14, 16, and 17 years old; two girls and a boy.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (voice-over): This past hour, we learned that eight other people were also shot, including a teacher, and are now being treated at local hospitals. Police have identified the shooter as a 15-year-old sophomore who they say used a semi-automatic handgun with multiple magazines to fire 15 to 20 shots today at Oxford High School.\par \par \uc2 At first, a lot of students didn't realize just what was happening, but then rushed outside and away from the school when reality set in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My boyfriend text to me about like someone in the school are like -- gun. Told me to hide, and at first, I didn't think much of it, you know? Because, like I just didn't think it would actually happen. But then, apparently, it wasn't a drill. So, I -- so, we all hid in (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Other students barricaded themselves inside of classrooms. The parent of the student who recorded this video told Fox Two in Detroit that moments after the shooting had ended, some teens hid under the desk when someone tried opening the door to this classroom and said sheriff's deputy, which they apparently knew from active shooter drills was not what a real deputy would do.\par \par \uc2 The actual deputies confronted the shooter within five minutes of the first 911 calls. And he gave himself up without any kind of a fight. At the police station, he refused to answer any questions and asked for an attorney.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We will get to the bottom of this. We're executing a search warrant at the suspect's house. As I said, he's not cooperating with us at this point in time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, you can't make --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): Michigan Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  and President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  also reacting to the shooting.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think this is every parent's worst nightmare.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: As we learned the full details, my heart goes out to the families enduring the unimaginable grief of losing a loved one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: School at Oxford High is canceled for tomorrow. And officials plan on giving an update later tonight on both the investigation and on the status of the victims. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Garrett Tenney in Chicago. Garrett, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of Chicago, Cook County, Illinois has surpassed the 1,000 mark in homicides this year. This is the first time the county has a clipped 1,000 homicides in more than 30 years.\par \par \uc2 Almost 800 of those homicides were in the city of Chicago. Again, with one month to go in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Most foreign policy experts have long believed that China, not Russia is the most dangerous threat to the U.S. With each week in recent weeks based on China's actions. That prediction is coming true.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a Fox News exclusive. First look at the Reagan National Defense survey. It uncovers a huge public relations problem for the Pentagon. Here is national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice- over): A new national defense survey, conducted by the Ronald Reagan Institute found that, for the first time, a minority of Americans, only 45 percent have a great deal of trust in the U.S. military. Down 25 percent in the past three years.\par \par \uc2 Confidence in the military among Republicans fell by 34 points to 53 percent. The declining trust in the military is occurring more rapidly than other institutions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAC THORNBERRY, FORMER REPRESENTATIVE OF TEXAS: My opinion is a lot of what caused it has been politicians who have roped the military into their own particular agenda. And so, they become more of a political football recently than they have before.\par