accinated from drills and training, which could also preclude the guard from accruing benefits like the G.I. Bill, and accessing the Department of Veterans Affairs if injured on duty. 54 percent of the Army National Guard are currently unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's a story to follow and ask about. Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Jen and I will be at the Reagan National Defense Forum this weekend at the Ronald Reagan Presidential Library. "SPECIAL REPORT" will come to you live Friday from Simi Valley.\par \par \uc2 Up next, is actor Jussie Smollett, an attention-seeking hoaxer or a victim? We'll have a live update from his trial in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox Five in New York, where the first officially authorized safe havens for people to use heroin and other narcotics are cleared to open.\par \par \uc2 New York Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2  says he hopes these supervised injection sites will curb overdoses that's from drug overdoses in the U.S. surpassed 100,000 in the year-long period ending in April.\par \par \uc2 Fox6 in Milwaukee with the withdrawal of the attorney for the man charged with driving an SUV through the Waukesha Christmas parade, killing six people and injuring dozens. Joseph Domask was representing Darrell Brooks Jr. in two previous felony cases.\par \par \uc2 He says he has a conflict of interest because he knows people affected by the deadly attack.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Philadelphia from Fox 29. The big story there tonight, \uc2 Dr. Mehmet Oz\uc2 , throws his hat into the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  race in Pennsylvania. The celebrity surgeon and television host plans to run as a Republican to try to replace retiring Republican Senator \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 . Dr. Oz, now, candidate Oz will be on "SEAN HANNITY SHOW" tonight, 9:00 p.m. Eastern Time.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from Special Report. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: A woman testified today, she had repeated sexual contact with disgraced financier Jeffrey Epstein when she was 14 and that Ghislaine Maxwell was there when it happened.\par \par \uc2 The witness was the first of four alleged victims to testify against Maxwell at the start of her trial in New York. She's facing charges, she recruited and groomed girls for Epstein to sexually abuse. She denies those charges.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, the lead investigator in the Jussie Smollett case is trying to refute the defense notion that police did not adequately look into actor Jussie Smollett's report of a racial hate crime attack. That was the dominant theme during today's testimony in Smollett's trial.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Matt Finn has details tonight live from Chicago. Good evening, Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. In opening statements, Jussie Smollett's defense attorney told the jury here that the \uc2 Chicago Police Department\uc2  rushed to judgment.\par \par \uc2 Well, as you mentioned, the lead Chicago police detective in this case is still testifying right now. He's been testifying all day against that claim.\par \par \uc2 He's revealing the painstaking detail of CPD Smollett investigation testifying that Chicago police treated Smollett as a real hate crime victim, assigning more than 24 officers and detectives to the case. More than 3,000 police hours, scouring more than 1,500 hours of video concluding that Smollett staged a hoax.\par \par \uc2 The detective also testified that police followed hate crime protocol and asked Smollett for phone data, medical records and a DNA cheek swab, but Smollett refused to give any.\par \par \uc2 The prosecution says Smollett pay the Osundairo brothers to pretend to beat him by rope for a noose and red hats appearing as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supporters.\par \par \uc2 In court, the prosecution presenting a text message police discovered that Smollett sent to one of the Osundairo brothers before the alleged attack reading: Might need your help on the low. You around to meet up and talk face to face?\par \par \uc2 Smollett's defense team says the Osundairo brothers are sophisticated criminals who did not like Jussie Smollett. The jury in this case is seven men, four women, majority white and middle aged, one black man is seated and one black woman is an alternate.\par \par \uc2 Smollett's mother and family has been joining him here in court, here is one of Smollett's brothers today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOJO SMOLLETT, BROTHER OF JUSSIE SMOLLETT: It has been incredibly painful as his family to watch someone you love be accused of something they did not do, all right?\par \par \uc2 We're confident in his legal team. And we look forward to people hearing the actual facts in this case.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FINN (on camera): In court right now, Jussie Smollett's defense attorney is questioning that lead Chicago police investigator. They are pointing out that a police search found that there were guns and drugs in the Osundairo brothers' apartment and also, that one of the brothers previously sent a social media message that had a homophobic slur and also allegedly attacked a person on the Empire set for being gay, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Matt Finn live in Chicago, we'll follow this case. Matt, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Breaking news, we said at the top of the hour that the federal -- a federal judge has blocked -- issued a nationwide injunction blocking President Biden's vaccine mandate for health care workers.\par \par \uc2 That judge is actually from Louisiana, the United States District Court the Western District in Louisiana. Judge Terry Doughty says during a pandemic such as this one, it's even more important to safeguard the separation of powers set forth in our Constitution to avoid erosion of our liberties.\par \par \uc2 Because the plaintiff states have satisfied all four elements required for a preliminary injunction to issue, this Court has determined that a preliminary injunction should issue against the government defendants. This matter will ultimately be decided by a higher court than this one.\par \par \uc2 However, it is important to preserve the status quo in this case, the liberty interests of the unvaccinated requires nothing less.\par \par \uc2 Again, a ruling from a federal judge blocking the mandate for vaccines for healthcare workers. We'll keep you updated. We'll talk more about it with the panel.\par \par \uc2 Up next, media misplaced, the Chris Cuomo situation at CNN and the cover up of the \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  laptop scandal.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond Our Borders tonight, talks to revive the nuclear deal with Iran appear to take a step back after the country's top nuclear negotiator suggests everything discussed in previous meetings could be renegotiated.\par \par \uc2 That contradicts comments from the \uc2 European Union\uc2  saying Iran accepted the work that had been done already as a good basis to build on.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Great Britain announcing new coronavirus restrictions to combat the Omicron variant. Facemasks are mandatory now in shops and on public transportation in the U.K.\par \par \uc2 People traveling to the United Kingdom must also take a COVID test and self-isolate until they receive a negative result.\par \par \uc2 And China says the 2022 Winter Olympics will proceed as planned. The country's foreign ministry says the Omicron variant could pose some challenges but officials expect the games to run as scheduled.\par \par \uc2 Beijing is set to host the Winter Olympics in early February without foreign spectators and with other coronavirus prevention measures.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond Our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Judges on a federal appeals court expressed skepticism today, it claims of executive privilege by former President Trump in his effort to keep records about his constant -- conversations and actions before and during the capital riot of January 6th away from congressional investigators.\par \par \uc2 At issue is the extent of former President can claim that privilege and whether courts should intervene in the first place in such a political dispute between the two other branches of government.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the former president's White House Chief of Staff at the time is cooperating with the investigation. Mississippi Democrat Congressman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  had threatened to hold former Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  in contempt as he did for long-time Trump aide \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We have new details tonight from a book about the \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  laptop scandal.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Kevin Corke has the latest tonight. Good evening, Kevin.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN CORKE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Great to see you my friend.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is a new round of explosive allegations coming from the Post's columnist Miranda Devine. You may have heard of her. She's got a new book out it's called Laptop from Hell. And it alleges that the president sought a 10 percent cut from a deal with Chinese government linked energy conglomerate CFEC.\par \par \uc2 Now, the deal according to the book also involved \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , as well as the President's brother Jim Biden, along with business associate, Tony Bobulinski.\par \par \uc2 Now, excerpts from that book published today by the Post also detail what appears to be according to the author, strong evidence of international influence peddling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIRANDA DEVINE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: I think the top line is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  lied to the American people during the campaign when he said that he knew nothing about his son Hunter's overseas business dealings.\par \par \uc2 There is evidence galore on this laptop of multiple meetings that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had when he was vice president with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's business partners from overseas, from Mexico, from China, from Russia, from Kazakhstan.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CORKE: So, there are a number of allegations in the book, among them Joe and \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  part of an international influence peddling operation, as well as Hunter and the President's brother Jim making millions in effect by selling access to the former vice president and current president.\par \par \uc2 There also appears to be evidence from the laptop that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  himself benefited financially from these deals.\par \par \uc2 However, the White House has repeatedly shut down the allegations even referring to them as Russian disinformation at one point. But for some in Washington, as little doubt about what actually happened.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEWT GINGRICH\uc2 , FORMER HOUSE SPEAKER: Story of absolute corruption and it's a great problem in America because our elites have become corrupted to such a degree that having this conversation would be impossible on a number of networks because their owners wouldn't tolerate it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CORKE: Now, there has been no official word tonight from the White House on the book's claims, but it should be noted that previously there was no reported income on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's tax returns supporting any allegations of income related to business dealings to Hunter or anyone else. And corporate records reviewed by "The Wall Street Journal" at the time showed no known role for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One to follow. Kevin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 CORKE: Yes, sir. You bet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, CNN is suspending anchor Chris Cuomo. This comes amid revelations about his involvement in the defense of his brother, then New York Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 , against sexual misconduct allegations. FOX News media analyst and host of FOX's MEDIA BUZZ FOX News Howie Kurtz has the latest tonight breaking just this hour. Good evening, Howie.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST: Yes, Bret, good evening, that news coming just moments ago, CNN suspending its primetime host, its top rated host, the statement saying that newly released documents point to a greater level of involvement in his brother's efforts than we previously knew. As a result, we have suspended Chris indefinitely pending further evaluation. And this is a reference to documents that have come out about his efforts to orchestrate the defense of his brother, the then Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 . And the CNN host didn't address it at all last night on his show, and he spoke only of why his brother resigned on his Sirius XM program today.\par \par \uc2 But the criticism had been mounting after disclosures that Chris Cuomo used his media connections to help the then governor fight sexual harassment allegations. The liberal "Atlantic" magazine -- this, of course, is before the suspension -- says "Chris Cuomo's position is now untenable. He should resign. If he doesn't CNN should sack him." "Rolling Stone's" editor says the "shocking ethical breaches" would be "fire-able offenses" anywhere else.\par \par \uc2 In some texts released by investigators, Cuomo appears to be gathering information on his brother's accusers. "I have a lead on the wedding girl," Cuomo wrote, referring to Anna Ruch, who'd said three days earlier the governor harassed her at a wedding reception.\par \par \uc2 As for another of Governor Cuomo's accusers, Charlotte Bennett, an adviser to the governor testified that Chris Cuomo forwarded a purported set of documents from her time in college. Cuomo denied doing oppo research, saying his calls to reporters to gather information were business as usual. He told top aide \uc2 Melissa DeRosa\uc2  that brother's statement about Charlotte Bennett was poor, and he should directly apologize to her and all New Yorkers. Bennett tweeted today that Cuomo had tried to smear her through representable and inexcusable behavior and called for his firing.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  in his video testimony said it would be insane for him to grope anyone, and he sparred with investigators.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you understand what a girlfriend is?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW CUOMO\uc2 , (D) FORMER NEW YORK GOVERNOR: Girlfriend means different things to different people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Even after the suspension, CNN chief \uc2 Jeff Zucker\uc2  faces a tough decision because Chris Cuomo is his top rated host. The problem for the network is appearing to condone conducted that would generally be deemed unacceptable now that new evidence has contradicted some of Cuomo's past denials. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Howie, there's multiple reporting now that CNN is saying that it indefinitely suspends Chris Cuomo. That's a different set up.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Right. It's not saying a week or two. That's sending a pretty strong message. Remember there had been no penalty, even though some of this came outer months ago against Chris Cuomo. And now I think there is a signal here that it's possible -- I don't want to speculate, but it's possible his time at the network could be limited based on this indefinite suspension.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Howie with the breaking news, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we set the stage for what could be the most monumental \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  case in decades.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, we have update in the feud between Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  and Democratic Congresswoman Ilhan Omar. There is now another incident of Boebert insulting Omar. Correspondent Aishah Hasnie has the latest tonight. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening to you, Bret. Representative Omar, in fact, is speaking right now about these comments, and she is flanked by the only other two Muslim members of Congress, and they say, look, this isn't about hurt feelings. This is about taking real action to protect the greater Muslim community.\par \par \uc2 So Representative Omar just said this, and I will read a quote for you. "When a sitting member of Congress calls a colleague a member of the jihad squad and falsifies a story to suggest that I will blow up the capitol, it's not just an attack on me but on millions of American Muslims across the country."\par \par \uc2 Now, this all comes hours after a second video emerged of Representative \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  making Islamophobic comments about Representative Ilhan Omar as early as September. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LAUREN BOEBERT\uc2 , (R-CO): I looked over and I said, well, look there, it's the jihad squad.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BOEBERT: She doesn't have a backpack, she wasn't dropping it and running, so we're good.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Now, Boebert has previously apologized to the Muslim community for similar comments she made earlier this month. This has now sparked another feud, Bret. Representative \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  now attacking her Republican colleague Nancy Mace for condemning Boebert's comments, tweeting this, "Nancy Mace is the trash of the GOP Conference. You can back up off of \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  or just go hang with your real gal pals, the Jihad Squad."\par \par \uc2 Omar has her own history of making controversial comments, some of which were condemned by her own party leadership, being called anti-Semitic. House Democratic leadership is calling on Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy to take action over Boebert's comments. Keep in mind, if Democrats choose to censure Boebert, as they did with Representative \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2 , this could put them in a really tricky tit-for-tat type of situation, especially if Republicans take back the House as early as next year, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Something to watch. Aishah Hasnie up on Capitol Hill. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: You bet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tomorrow is a huge day at the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . Justices will hear arguments about a Mississippi abortion law. It's possible their eventual ruling could overturn one of the most controversial and talked about decisions in the court's history. Correspondent David Spunt reports tonight from the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Nearly half a century after women were granted a constitutional right to an abortion, that right may soon look drastically different.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA PHILLIPS, CIVIL RIGHTS ATTORNEY: I was using birth control responsibly. It failed. So I'm devastated.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Barbara Phillips underwent the controversial procedure in Mississippi in 1973, the same year Roe became the law nationwide. Over the years, pro-life groups aggressively tried and almost came close to overturning Roe, but it never happened. This time, thanks to a six-three conservative majority, it may. The nine justices will hear arguments over a Mississippi state law banning abortions after 15 weeks. According to Roe, abortions are legal up to 24 weeks when a baby is determined to be able to survive outside the womb. The Mississippi case highlights states' rights versus respect for court precedent, in this case, 48 years of precedent.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WYDRA, CONSTITUTIONAL ACCOUNTABILITY CENTER: The court is going to decide whether or not people are going to be able to enjoy their right to choose for themselves whether, when, and how to have a child.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Pro-life supporters say Roe should have never become law, and now is the time to make a change in the name of children. Mississippi Attorney General Lynn Fitch is leading the charge to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 LYNN FITCH, (R) MISSISSIPPI ATTORNEY GENERAL: It's time. It's been nearly 50 years. The reality is this is a rule of law question. It's time to return it to the states.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Meaning, for examples, the state legislature in Nevada may outlaw the procedure, but lawmakers in California may allow it, which may cause women to cross state lines or seek even more dangerous methods.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: I do believe that many of us will go back to coat hangers and back alleys. Women will find ways to terminate pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): The case will be decided by early July in the middle of the 2022 midterm election year. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will follow it all. David Spunt at the court. David, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on President Biden's economic push, the latest challenge of the Omicron variant as well as the mandate ruling we have been talking about, and the breaking news just minutes ago, CNN indefinitely suspending Chris Cuomo. A lot to talk about after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, just in the past few minutes, CNN announces that it is indefinitely suspending anchor Chris Cuomo over the revelations of what he did to support his brother, the former governor of New York, and going after media sources to find information about the governor's accusers in the sexual harassment allegations, just happening in the past few minutes.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, \uc2 Mara Liasson\uc2 , national political correspondent of National Public Radio, Bill McGurn, columnist for "The Wall Street Journal," and Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com. Mara, it seemed like this was building throughout the day. Obviously, there are a lot of people saying something has to happen. CNN has done something as they investigate further.\par \par \uc2 MARA LIASSON\uc2 , NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, NATIONAL PUBLIC RADIO: Yes, as they investigate further. And, look, he always was in a difficult position. His brother was the governor of New York. That makes it even more important to have really strong firewalls between a journalist Cuomo and elected official Cuomo, and CNN is going to have to finish this investigation and decide what to do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Katie, what he said publicly, Chris Cuomo, is not what was seen privately in those texts and emails.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, Bret, this is something that we knew months and months ago. Over the summer it was obvious that Chris Cuomo was writing and approving statements for the governor's office that were then officially posted on the New York state's official government website. It wasn't a matter of just family dealings. It was a matter Chris Cuomo being an adviser to his brother while also claiming to be a journalist. And now we are seeing this onion being pealed back even further, and that Chris Cuomo was going after his brother's accusers, which, according to the report that Letitia James put out, a lot of those accusations by those women were true.\par \par \uc2 And so Chris Cuomo was afforded to a lot of privilege because his brother was the governor of New York, and now we are seeing as a result of that the opposite is now true, and it's boomeranging back on him, and there has now been some kind of accountability for behavior that has been going on for months and months.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill, CNN in part, in their statement says, "When Chris admitted to us that he had offered at vice to his brother's staff, he broke our rules and we acknowledged that publicly. But we also appreciated the unique position he was in and understood his need to put family first and job second. However, these documents point to a greater level of involvement in his brother's efforts than we previously knew. As a result we have suspended Chris indefinitely pending further evaluation."\par \par \uc2 BILL MCGURN, COLUMNIST, "THE WALL STREET JOURNAL": Yes. Look, I think CNN is right there. But look, let's be clear. CNN brought this on themselves. This is just karma coming back. CNN allowed Chris Cuomo, as a working journalist, to cover his brother despite the manifest conflict of interest.\par \par \uc2 Now we found out, as you pointed out, that some of Chris Cuomo's statements to the CNN audience about the extent of his involvement were misleading at best and lies at worse. So, they never should have been in this position in the first place.\par \par \uc2 And what a come down from two years ago when Governor Cuomo was being extolled and Chris Cuomo looked like the unbeatable host. There is some basics in journalism about conflicts of interest, and they just ran roughshod over them, and now they are paying the price. And it's not all Chris Cuomo. CNN gave him the blessing to go on with his brother.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to turn topics, and we will obviously follow this story and where it goes and what happens with CNN and Chris Cuomo. But the Omicron variant is obviously dominating people's thinking, and so are these mandates and vaccine mandates. And just at the top of this show, a federal judge in the district -- western district of Louisiana, ruled that the healthcare mandates for healthcare workers to get vaccines cannot be upheld. There needs to be a stop of that nationwide, and says a higher court is going to weigh in on this. Mara, this is significant in that these courts are making a stand, saying probably the Supreme Court is going to have to weigh in.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes, it sounds like it's going to go all the way to the Supreme Court. And the question is in some cases are there are vaccine mandates, in other cases there is a choice. You can get a vaccine, or you can be tested weekly. That's a choice. So it depends on whether it relates to healthcare workers or employers who have more than 100 employees, but, yes, it sounds like this is going to go all the way to the top.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Katie, Sarah tweeted in "As a school bus driver, a district -- our district is going ahead with requiring registration for the mandates. Deadline is January 3rd. I know that at least four drivers who will not comply." Four is a small number compared to what we have heard from different law enforcement, fire, healthcare workers. It's a big issue.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Yes. We have seen the consequences of this mandate play out in shortages of law enforcement, firefighters, as you said, teachers in some cases, and bus drivers and healthcare workers. And the shoot down of this vaccine mandate for healthcare workers across the country today comes on the heels of the ruling yesterday from a judge out of Missouri who argued that the mandate for Medicare and Medicaid workers, or federal contractors working for Medicare and Medicaid, had a number of problems with it, the first being that Congress didn't pass it. The second being that it's arbitrary and capricious. There is a number of reasons why they can't enforce it. And the third being that the risks and the consequences for a shortage of healthcare workers, especially in rural areas, was far worse than unvaccinated workers continuing to work inside of these government healthcare facilities or contracted jobs.\par \par \uc2 And so, when you look at the way the administration has handled this, they said six months ago that they didn't have a mandate to do this and they weren't going to implement any kind of vaccine mandate. Well, that has changed. You also had \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , the Speaker of the House, saying that they did not have the power, the ability to institute mandates. The administration knew this was not constitutional. They knew that they couldn't do it. They tried to get OSHA to do it, which is not implementing it at the federal level now. And so we saw this coming. They used it for months to try and push their agenda. But court after court continues to say there are a number of problems with this vaccine mandate for the private sector and for the government.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Bill, the administration surely is going to appeal this. It is going to go forward. What is the argument from the mandate side? Is it the public good? And is it legally tenable? Can you make that case in the court of law?\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: Well, certainly the public good, and it depends on which mandate you are talking about, what the legal basis for it is. The OSHA basis is very, very small.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think Katie alluded to this. The problem, again, this is karma coming back. In 2020, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  politicized COVID. He promised that he would be the guy that shut it down. He wouldn't use mandates. President Trump was -- shouldn't be president because there had been 220,000 deaths. The travel ban was basically racist. It was aimed at black and brown people.\par \par \uc2 Now, he has reversed himself on all these things. There's 350,000 deaths. So he is paying a price for this. And the worst thing of all is that none of it seems to be working. I think the Biden administration is giving off vibes that it is just artless and inept, and that the president is in way over his head and doesn't know whether a to do.\par \par \uc2 It's one thing -- in some senses I think it almost doesn't matter what the courts say about the mandates. I believe that the White House just did this to look like they are doing something. If it's overruled later, it is kind of a footnote by that time. The problem is none of what they are doing works.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Bill, the double-barreled karma analysis. That's pretty good.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you very much, panel. Thanks. A condensed show today.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, Tuesday Tweets.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally tonight, our Tuesday Tweets, #SRTwitterTuesday. From Dan first, "Bret, how do you think redistricting will change the balance of power in the House after the next election?" Could be significant. Republicans control 30 state houses, Democrats 17. Plus, Republicans red states gained seats from the census, so it's a big deal.\par \par \uc2 We have Matt who asked, "Will you ever have the full panel back in the studio?" Yes, working on it. Mike asks, "What was your favorite Christmas gift as a child?" Chi-Chi Rodriguez golf clubs when I was seven. Finally, Brie asks "Will you come to Arlington National Cemetery and lay wreaths with my friends and I at Section 60 on December 18th?" Yes. I will bring my family, too. Nice job.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. FOX NEWS PRIMETIME hosted by Pete Hegseth starts in just a few seconds. But, Pete, have you ever done that? That is something else, to lay the wreaths at Arlington National Cemetery.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, FOX NEWS HOST: Man, you hit a note. I want to bring my kids. I want to go there. I want them to experience it. It's on my calendar.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's do it, you and me.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020211201ehbu00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Officials Give Update On Michigan School Shooting; GOP Senators Speak On Challenge To Roe V. Wade; Mississippi A.G. On State's Challenge To Roe V Wade; Biden Administration May Raise Cost Of Drilling On Federal Land; 3 Killed, 6 Wounded In Michigan High School Shooting; RPT: \uc2 Taliban\uc2  Killing, "Disappearing" Former Officers; Parade Suspect Charged With 6th Homicide Count\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Jessica Dupnack, Alex Hogan, Keith Kellogg,  Griff Jenkins, Pete Hegseth \par \uc2 8,984 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 November 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCCABE, OAKLAND COUNTY UNDERSHERIFF: So, I'll just give you a quick brief and I'll answer whatever questions I can answer. And we'll ask for comments or anything, the chief can fill us in about EMS and all that. Around 12:51 today, we received a 9-1-1 call of an active shooter at the high school. The deputies immediately responded, and we received over a hundred 9-1-1 calls into our dispatch center, over 100.\par \par \uc2 The deputies took a suspect into custody within five minutes of the original 9-1-1 call. They recovered a handgun from the suspect. The suspect fired multiple shots, there are multiple victims. It's unfortunate, I have to report that we have three deceased victims right now, who are all believed to be students. We have six others that were shot. One was a school teacher. They're all at local hospitals being treated for various injuries.\par \par \uc2 Again, multiple shots were fired. He did not give us any resistance when he was taken into custody. He's currently being transported back to Pontiac, for potential now like -- he's already, already invoked his right to not speak so he wants an attorney. He's not telling us anything at this point in time. It's a very tragic situation, obviously. We will brief you again later. Maybe around 5:00. We can give you ages of the victims, conditions of the victims, where they live, things of that nature.\par \par \uc2 Behind us in the mire, they close the store down for us and helped us with reunification with the parents. We got a lot of upset parents want to know the, what's going on with their kids. There was an orderly evacuation. The school did everything right. Everybody remained in place, they barricaded themselves. And Tim here has done a great job in terms of preparing. You never want to prepare for something like this, but you have to, and the school district's done a wonderful job preparing all the doors over there and marked at the high school if you've seen them, deputies responded, they knew where to go.\par \par \uc2 We've had assistance from other police agencies nearby. Lapeer County Sheriff, Oxford Police Department, other local police departments, the response has been overwhelming. We're still doing a secondary and ordered a third search of the high school just to make sure there's no other victims out there. If there's anything else I missed, I can answer, I will answer and I'll defer to students.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (INAUDIBLE) was a student?\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: Yes, it's a 15-year-old student who is a sophomore at the high school.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: As he said -- before he invoked attorney, did he say anything about what precipitated this?\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: I'm not going to get into that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Was there any warning, Mike, ahead of time? Or was there any messaging? Did he put out anything?\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: that's all under investigation at this time? We have literally probably a hundred different detectives and support out here right now. We're going to talk to everybody, we're going to go through everything. But at this point in time, I can't tell you that we have that or we don't have that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Any reason to believe the initial victims were targeted directly, or was it?\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: I don't know at this point in time, still under investigation\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Have notification then made to parents yet or do parents still not know?\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: I believe all the parents know but I can't say that for certain, but I believe they do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, you're saying three deceased, and how many injuries?\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: We have three deceased, six others that were shot one of the six is a teacher.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: One of the witnesses I talked to said she heard shots and pause she heard shots again, do you have any idea how long he was in the school?\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: He fired multiple shots; I can tell you that. We've recovered multiple shell casings in the school. Somewhere in the area, obviously with that many people shot, we're thinking, you know, 15 to 20 shots that were fired.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, what kind of weapon was used?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How long was he --\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: It's a handgun.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How long is he in this school?\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: It's a semi-automatic handgun. That's all I can tell you at this point.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Did he have more than one magazine?\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: I'm not going to get it -- obviously, if he fired up multiple shots more than 15, he'd have more than one magazine. Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How did he got apprehended?\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: Deputies took him into custody, he responded. I can't tell you the exact circumstances of how that occurred, but deputies confronted him. He had the weapon on him, they took him into custody.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In school.\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Was it the child, the child that had the issue last week and weeks to go back, with your head?\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: I'm not aware of that, so I can't answer that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Over the course of like, 15-20 minutes, do we know how long --\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: Five minutes. The whole thing lasted five minutes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Was it a (INAUDIBLE) or in the common area?\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: I'm not sure on that. I'll have to get back to you. And we'll do another briefing later on when we get more details.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Any indication that he was targeting a certain room for these students and the specific teacher?\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: I can't tell you that because I don't know.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You told us a lot and we appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Can we talk to the superintendent for a moment, please?\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: That's up the Tim.\par \par \uc2 TIM THRONE, SUPERINTENDENT OF OXFORD COMMUNITY SCHOOLS: I really don't have anything, I really don't have anything more to say other than that you certainly can pray for our families here in Oxford, and our students. I'm learning information real time just as you all are so I really don't have any more info to add --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you have any light on who the student is, a 15-year- old?\par \par \uc2 THRONE: I can't, no.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What's your reaction to this?\par \par \uc2 THRONE: Of course, I'm shocked. It's devastating/\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Superintendent, can you say and spell your name, please Sir?\par \par \uc2 THRONE: Tim Throne, T-H-R-O-N-E.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, you have a lot of things in place, one point of entry when school is open. These students get ALICE training as well. I know that students and teachers, or other teachers are able to communicate with one another. You also have this point of safety that you're able to get students too, as well. Can you talk to me about why you didn't have metal detectors?\par \par \uc2 THRONE: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK.\par \par \uc2 THRONE: That's my answers to your question, no.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is that something that's ever come up?\par \par \uc2 THRONE: Not that I can recall, no.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you have any background information on the student? Have there been any issues with this student prior to today?\par \par \uc2 THRONE: Not yet.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You know who the student is?\par \par \uc2 THRONE: I -- we don't know the name. I don't know the names of the victims yet.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 THRONE: I don't know that families have been contacted yet. So, we got our homework to do. Let us go do it and we'll come back.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And we know, of course you know and we know that this is difficult, so we appreciate you coming. One more other thing, Superintendent --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What are you saying?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Can you tell us if there are any class for tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 THRONE: I'll let you know as soon as I get back, and have a chance to talk to my team. But what time is it right now?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: 2:30.\par \par \uc2 THRONE: So, if they're going to do another interview at 5:00, we'll let our families know by five with plans for tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you, Sir. We appreciate --\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: We got John Lyman here, speaking for all the Fire Departments. They'll tell you what resources were brought here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Chief, can you say your name and spell everything for us please?\par \par \uc2 JOHN LYMAN, PUBLIC INFORMATION OFFICER, ROCHESTER HILLS FIRE DEPARTMENT: John Lyman, L-Y-M-A-N. I'm the Public Information Officer. I worked for Rochester Hills Fire Department.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Got it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LYMAN: So, we had -- we were dispatched along with the P.D. and the Sheriff's Office, we had about 25 agencies that responded close to 60 ambulances. We had a couple of helicopters land. Also, we did transport those six patients. The number -- I'm not totally for sure on, but we did transport multiple patients to area hospitals.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And how many how, how many units?\par \par \uc2 LYMAN: We had a close to 60 units that were dispatched here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, you call for mutual aid, obviously.\par \par \uc2 LYMAN: Obviously, yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And so, you brought in both many units, why?\par \par \uc2 LYMAN: Well, we didn't know how many how many victims we had originally. So, you just you just start bringing everybody in.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Could you describe --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right. We lost that feed momentarily. But you can have heard the gist of this. This is an absolutely horrific school shooting that has taken place in Oxford, Michigan. Three children are dead, gunned down by a 15-year-old sophomore, according to the sheriff who was filling us in on this horrible, horrible story.\par \par \uc2 Multiple shots were fired, multiple shell casings found at the scene of this as they tried to figure out what happened and three families learn the horrific news that they have lost their child at school today in yet another school shooting, this one in Oxford, Michigan. We're going to continue to stay on top of this story. They said they airlifted at least six injured students.\par \par \uc2 I think they also referred to potentially a teacher in that group as well who were wounded in the shooting. We'll stay on top of that. We'll take you back there as we get more information out of Oxford, Michigan, obviously a terrible tragic happening there today at the Oxford High School.\par \par \uc2 Also, we are following this breaking story for you as Republican senators hold a press conference right now on the eve of the Supreme Court hearing perhaps the most consequential case of this term. On a challenge to Roe v. Wade. Let's listen in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. CINDY HYDE-SMITH (R-MS): By the Supreme Court and Roe vs. Wade. Completely unworkable. With these medical advancements we know now, that affecting weak, unborn baby has fully developed heart and facial structures -- eyes, nose, mouth, even eyebrows -- they can yawn, they can hiccup, and they even start sucking their thumbs in indicate if they're left-handed or right-handed. These significant advances make clear what those of us in the pro-life movement already knew that unborn children are human beings.\par \par \uc2 I'm proud that my state of Mississippi is in the heart of this landmark case. The humanity of the unborn is undeniable. Dobbs versus Jackson Women's Health Organization is the crucial opportunity for our Supreme Court to overturn Roe vs. Wade and return the abortion issue to the political process and away from unelected activist judges. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN THUNE (R-SD): Well, in addition to being able to yawn and suck you his or her thumb, babies at 15 weeks also heartbeat, the heart has beat now 15 million times is hard to argue when you look at that that that is not a human being that deserves to be protected. The Supreme Court reached far outside the Constitution when it enacted a new abortion regime for the entire country.\par \par \uc2 Prior to that, the states, as has already been pointed out, have the prerogative through elected officials to make determinations and we believe that, that's where this needs to go back to. We need to have a court, a hold for the Supreme Court will be the one with the Dobbs case that once and for all rights a very serious wrong. And the 1973 decision was wrongly decided, and as has been pointed out the decision if it is overturned, does not eliminate abortion, it simply returns that to the States.\par \par \uc2 Now, the United States has as already been pointed out as well is really an outlier when it comes to how we treat unborn babies. They're only seven countries in the world where elective abortions are allowed. You know, after -- I should say, seven countries in the world, including China, North Korea, where and United States is among those, where abortions are allowed beyond 20 weeks.\par \par \uc2 And there are 47 out of 50 European nations where today, you either need a rationale or reason to have an abortion, or elective abortions are banned at 15 weeks. 47 out of 50 European nations. And when it comes to the 20- week threshold, and United States, as I said is one of only seven countries in the world that allows abortions beyond that point. It's not the company you want to be in when you're talking about China and North Korea.\par \par \uc2 We can do better; we must do better. And I hope this court, through you the arguments that they're going to hear tomorrow, the deliberations that they'll enter into and the decision they'll ultimately come to will once and for all correct this wrong and allow these decisions to be made by the elected representatives of the people in the states across this country.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROGER WICKER (R-MS): Thank you, John. Before Senator Hyde-Smith and I came to the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 , each of us served in the State Senate in Mississippi. And during the time I was there, and during the time she served after I did the, the legislature and with the help of, of pro-life governors and pro-life officials made some strides. We, we began in, in the 80s with a clinic regulation bill.\par \par \uc2 The next year too, we pass a 24-hour waiting period for those moms that came in and needed more time to make a decision about making this very, very momentous decision. I want to say how proud I am and join Senator Hyde-Smith and saying how proud we are of the state of Mississippi for the Dobbs case, which will, I think, make history. And we begin making history tomorrow with a decision.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you, millions of Americans are praying today for our country. They're praying today for the Supreme Court. They'll, they'll be given wisdom, they'll be given courage, and that they make a decision for life. My wife, Gayle and I, had our babies in the late 70s and 80s, and when we had the first picture made of them, we put them on the refrigerator.\par \par \uc2 We stuck them in an envelope and mailed them to relatives. When our eight grandchildren came along, it was pictures like this well before the birth that went on the refrigerator with the magnets, and that were sent around to well-wishers, to friends, and prospective aunts and uncles of the child that was still in the womb. This is the science that has convinced our European friends to restrict abortions after 15 weeks, even after 12 weeks.\par \par \uc2 If the Supreme Court rules in our way in the Dobbs case, the United States will be placed in the broad mainstream of international understanding, joining Denmark, Norway, Belgium, France, Germany, at a 15-week period.\par \par \uc2 Some countries even go earlier than that with 12 weeks. So, I'm proud of our state, I'm proud to join my colleagues here. And I join so many of my fellow Americans in praying for the attorneys tomorrow, and praying for the judges, and praying for the future of our great country.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN (R-TN): Thank you so much. I think we all are very hopeful for the case that is going forward tomorrow. And very proud of Lynn Fitch, who is the attorney general for the State of Mississippi, she is truly a powerhouse. And is the perfect person to layout this case, as defined the issue of abortion should go back to the states where it rightfully belongs. So, we are really hopeful for tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And there is good reason for this, as Senator Wicker was just pointing out, this is an area of where science is on our side. And because of science, being on the side of life, that is why there are now only seven countries that allow abortion after 20 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Now, think about that. Only seven countries will allow abortion after 20 weeks. And it's because we now have that window into the womb, where you can see the baby as it forms and its features. And you know if it's a girl or if it's a boy, and you share in that excitement to welcome this new life.\par \par \uc2 And interestingly enough, 80 percent of the American people agree, agree that there should be restrictions on abortion after that 20-week mark. So, legislation, each of us here on this stage have worked together. And throughout our careers, whether it's here in the Senate or the House and the Senate, or whether it's a state legislature and in the Senate to protect and preserve life.\par \par \uc2 So, I am really pleased to join my colleagues today in support of life and in support of the State of Mississippi, and Attorney General Lynn Fitch as they carry forward tomorrow with the Dobbs case.\par \par \uc2 Thanks, everyone for being here today. It is so important that we're able to have this discussion and thanks to my colleagues for showing their support as well for life.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JONI ERNST (R-IA): So, 15 weeks, folks. 15 weeks. We all know and science has proven that after 15 weeks in the womb, these babies have a fully developed heart. They have fingers and toes. They have arms and legs. They can kick and they'll even jump if they are startled in their mother's wombs. And certainly, they feel pain.\par \par \uc2 So, science has developed through the years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): All right, this is Joni Ernst -- Senator Joni Ernst. As you have been listening to the senators -- Republican senators talking about the importance of this case that the Supreme Court is about to hear.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): The State of Mississippi's 15-week abortion ban is at the heart of this challenge to Roe versus Wade. And joining me now exclusively is a woman that you just heard mentioned by Senator Marsha Blackburn of Tennessee. This is the Attorney General of Mississippi Lynn Fitch, who is defending that ban before the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General, very good to have you with us today. Tell me what -- you know, we just heard sort of some of this case laid out. And I know that you have written and spoken obviously about this a lot.\par \par \uc2 This is the other side. This is from the brief by Jackson Women's Health Organization on September 13th, 2021. They say, "two generations spanning almost five decades have come to depend on the availability of legal abortion, and the right to make this decision has been further cemented as critical to gender equality."\par \par \uc2 What do you say about the case and about what they say on their side?\par \par \uc2 LYNN FITCH, ATTORNEY GENERAL OF MISSISSIPPI: Well, thank you, Martha. And It's great to be with you today.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 FITCH: This is a tremendous opportunity. You know, we are asking the court to overturn Roe v. Wade, and it's time, it's been nearly 50 years. The reality is this is a rule of law question. It's time to return it to the states because abortion policymaking should rest with the elected legislators and elected governors, to make those decisions on behalf of the people, because that's what they're elected to do. That's the will of the people.\par \par \uc2 And so, it's certainly important for us to make that request and know that the Constitution gives us the power for people. That's what they're supposed to do. And as you look at what you just read, you know, for 50 years, Roe v. Wade has pitted women against their children, woman against woman. And now it's time to really stop and empower women and support life.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you say that you believe that a win, in this case, would be a win, as you just mentioned, not only for the unborn but for women as well. And you've outlined why you think that the argument for Roe v. Wade, essentially that it -- that, you know, that to prevent women from abortion would put an undue burden on them has not really turned out to be the case as you look back over the history of that ruling.\par \par \uc2 Can you sort of, you know, as succinctly as possible wrestle that very large topic? What has changed, and why do you think that, that has been proven?\par \par \uc2 FITCH: Well, and in over 50 years, if you stop and reflect, women in the workplace have changed, that the roles of men and women have been altered. Women have professional success, it's been easier to balance your professional life and your family life. And so, the culture is changed in that regard.\par \par \uc2 But unfortunately, because of Roe v. Wade, those changes have not been able to be incorporated into our different state laws. So, it's been a very much a hindrance. And so, abortion policy has been frozen since 1973. And it's time that our laws caught up because we're definitely making a change in our society.\par \par \uc2 You know, women are very successful. And women have different careers. And there's so many opportunities now that weren't there 50 years ago. And if you look at all the different arenas that women are participating in different career paths, that they weren't 50 years ago, now we have that opportunity.\par \par \uc2 And so, abortion is not the answer. That we should stop selling that false lie that that's the only choice to level the playing field, because it's not, there are so many choices and options. And so, it's time to stop and reflect and have those decisions turned back to our states.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. You know, when we've watched the hearings for some of these Supreme Court justices who now sit on the court, and we'll be hearing this case tomorrow, it's going to start at about 10:30 in the morning. It's -- they have all basically committed to Roe v. Wade as settled law, when they've been questioned about it.\par \par \uc2 And then I look at this Fifth Circuit Court, which is generally known to be a conservative court of appeals. They blocked the ban in Mississippi post 15 weeks, and here is what they said in December of 2019. "In an unbroken line dating to Roe v. Wade, the Supreme Court's abortion cases have established a woman's right to choose an abortion before viability. States may regulate abortion procedures prior to viability so long as they do not impose an undue burden on the woman's right, but they may not ban abortions." What will you argue against that?\par \par \uc2 FITCH: Well, we'll certainly make the argument that, you know, for 50 years that Roe v. Wade set this special set of rules that we've all tried to obey and go forward with. And it's they're unworkable, they're unmanageable, and it's not fair to women.\par \par \uc2 And so definitely, it's time to overturn that, return it back to the states, and have the right applicable laws in each state, will there be different ones in every state, of course, there will be. But again, we need to have that option to have that in our states to have that healthy dialogue.\par \par \uc2 And this is the first time to have a real conversation about empowering women and truly supporting lives because we have to talk about women through their pregnancy, the children, these precious babies. And so, it's inherent that we had this discussion, and this has given us the opportunity with the Dobbs case in the first time in 50 years to really have a true conversation about this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I just want to ask you a question about you and what has made you so passionate about this topic. You're the first woman attorney general of Mississippi. You're the first Republican attorney general in quite some time in Mississippi and I -- it's my understanding that you raised your family as a single mom, which is a hard thing to do. And I believe you have three children.\par \par \uc2 What do you bring from your personal experience to this -- to this fight?\par \par \uc2 FITCH: Well, thank you. It truly has been certainly a challenge as any single mother that's raising children and I was very fortunate. I raised three beautiful children as a single mother and, and so for me to have the opportunity to step up and empower women and talk about the importance of life and children. It's very humbling and it's such an honor.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, what would you say to a woman who does not share your point of view? Who says that she believes that she has the right over her own body to make that decision, and she does not want that right to be taken away from her?\par \par \uc2 FITCH: You know, for 50 years, we didn't really help those women. We didn't talk about how we could empower them. We didn't talk about opportunities. Where if you look at the statistics now, I mean, women are successful, we have been able to balance our professional lives and our family lives. We didn't do that before. Now, you have maternity leave, paternity leave. So many things that were not available to women. And so, now is the right time to have that conversation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: How persuasive do you think the issue that was just brought up by the senators, which is how much science has changed? And how much we now understand about what a 15-week-old unborn baby can do? And can sense? How powerful is that part of this equation going to be for these justices?\par \par \uc2 FITCH: Oh, it's significant, Martha, because again, if you look how science has advanced, and what we know now, what we didn't know, 50 years ago, and the viability? Absolutely, it is scientific, the technology has made it so that we can truly see that these are true babies. And so, it's important to acknowledge that and realize that the science has played such a big part of what we're going to be arguing tomorrow as well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we're going to be watching this very closely, of course. The whole nation will. And no doubt, it's going to be a very, very hot topic as we watch this play out, and we head into the 2022 midterms.\par \par \uc2 I hope you'll come back and talk to us about it again. Attorney General, very good to have you here today. Lynn Fitch, Thank you.\par \par \uc2 FITCH: Great. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 All right. We got a lot going on here this afternoon. And I want to bring you another update on this school shooting that happened today at Oxford High School in Oxford, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Tragically, three students have lost their lives in this shooting today. And we will give you an update on what we are learning there about those who are wounded and taken from the scene as well.\par \par \uc2 Also ahead, we want to update this story as well. This serial criminal Darrell Brooks, accused of ramming his car swinging back and forth along that road, killing adults and children; six of them, and injuring more than 40 is back in court today on yet another set of charges.\par \par \uc2 So, why is the coverage of this story about what happened in Wisconsin, so sparse?\par \par \uc2 Pete Hegseth going to talk to us about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): You can run over a Christmas parade and kill twice as many people as the Boston Marathon bombers, and they'll call it an accident.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So gas prices could be headed to $5 a gallon perhaps over the coming months and perhaps over the next couple of years as \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 . Today, the national average is around $3.40 a gallon. But now President Biden is considering a measure that would make it more expensive for oil companies to drill on federal land.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Mike Sommers, President of the American Petroleum Institute. Mike, good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 MIKE SOMMERS, PRESIDENT, \uc2 AMERICAN PETROLEUM INSTITUTE\uc2 : Martha, good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: First of all, what do you make of those numbers? It's $3.40 average right now, do you think that we're headed to the $5 range?\par \par \uc2 SOMMERS: Well, Martha, we're really concerned about what this administration is doing to domestic energy production. And another example of that is what happened on the day after Thanksgiving, when the President announced that they're going to increase the rates that companies have to pay to produce oil and gas on federal lands and in federal waters. Those resources, both in federal lands and federal waters, account for more than 10% of American production, and increasing those rates is only going to limit production at a time we should be increasing production here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, it's clear that the MO of the of the Obama administration where President Biden was vice president then was heading in this direction that what they want is to eliminate oil from the bag of tricks in our energy basket. I just want to go back to this, this is then Senator Obama candidate back in a way making it pretty clear. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA, FORMER SENATOR: For the sake of our economy, our security, and the future of our planet, we have to end the age of oil.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, do you see the current situation of inflation having any impact on that long term goal from this administration now?\par \par \uc2 SOMMERS: Well, that is certainly the concern. When you increase prices on the American people, I actually think the reverse is going to happen. The American people right now are asking further to be more production in the United States. And they don't want their leaders going to OPEC to beg for more oil from a price fixing cartel.\par \par \uc2 I mean, think about the irony. A couple of weeks ago, this administration actually asked the FTC to look into price fixing within the American oil and gas industry, when in fact, they're begging OPEC to produce more, which is a price fixing cartel in the Middle East. Every president since Jimmy Carter has wanted this country to be more energy independent. And it's as if this administration wants to make us more dependent on foreign sources for our energy. We don't think that that's the right way to go.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. It's clear that in the Build Back Better Program, they want to give people $12,500 to purchase electric cars and Pete Buttigieg, the Transportation secretary says, we're not going to have to worry about oil when we all have electric cars. So we'll see where that is headed.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sommers, we have breaking news in Michigan so I got to leave it there, but I thank you very much for being with us today.\par \par \uc2 SOMMERS: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We talk again soon, all right. This is just an awful story coming out of Michigan. And we now have a reporter on the scene there in Oxford, Michigan, where three students lost their lives today. They were shot and killed, believed to be, by a student at that school as well. So we're going to give you that in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 Also coming up, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  promised that if we got out, the people that we left behind, would be safe. But now some of them are disappearing, and others are being publicly executed. General Keith Kellogg and Pete Hegseth on the Afghanistan story that continues with time to just get worse.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking out of Oxford, Michigan, a deadly high school shooting, three people have been killed, all are believed to be students. The suspect is in custody and a handgun was recovered. Reporter Jessica Dupnack from our Fox Station WJBK in Detroit reporting live from the scene in Oxford, Michigan. Jessica, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA DUPNACK, FOX 2 WJBK REPORTER: Well, right now, for a little bit context, so right behind me there is a grocery store where parents were reunited with their children within the last couple of hours since this happened. Now taking you back to just before 2:00 today, a male student, a 15-year-old sophomore at Oxford High School here in Oxford Township, just about an hour outside of Detroit, open fired. He fired 15 to 20 shots hitting nine students, one of those actually a teacher, three of them are deceased, six are still in the hospital right now.\par \par \uc2 Within about five minutes or so the school officer, the deputy that is assigned to that high school, along with some help with other law enforcement, able to get that student in custody and arrested along with a handgun believed to be used in this school shooting. And now, again, those people transported, the injured transferred to local hospitals and are being treated right now.\par \par \uc2 After the arrest, that 15-year-old student invoked his rights. He is not saying anything to law enforcement asking for a lawyer. There's no indication at this time that this was premeditated, as we've seen in other school shootings where a note or a social media post was made, but just a very tragic situation here in Metro Detroit. It does appear that school is cleared at this point. And most of those kids have been reunited with their parents. But sadly, for three of those families that have been notified that their student has died. Just a sad, sad, chaotic situation here in Metro Detroit.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Awful, awful and tragic. Jessica, thank you very much. What an awful day for the families of that town and we will keep them in our thoughts this afternoon. Jessica, thank you. Let us know if you hear any more from the scene there.\par \par \uc2 So now to this, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 's promise of amnesty for Afghans who fought against them was an empty one, as many predicted at the time. The militants have executed or have, "forcibly disappeared" dozens of former police and intelligence officers who were working to bring their country together and to stabilize things there, that according to \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2 . In some cases, they have intentionally left the bodies for their families to find a message one would imagine that they could be next or that they were also in potential danger.\par \par \uc2 In moments, retired three star General Keith Kellogg, former national security adviser to Vice President Mike Pence joins me with his thoughts on this which continues to get worse in Afghanistan. First, to correspondent Alex Hogan live for us in London, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. In just three months, this report found that in only four provinces 100 People have either gone missing or have been killed. Now, these are former police officers members of the military. These are also intelligence service members. The report also detailed the targeting of their family members.\par \par \uc2 During its investigation, the \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2  interviewed more than 60 people about life under the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . Afghans share their stories of unexpected raids, public beatings, disappearances and revenge killings. Now remember when the Taliban first took over and vowed to give amnesty to former authorities and really anyone who's surrendered. Since then, \uc2 Taliban\uc2  officials deny that their fighters are behind any killings.\par \par \uc2 This weekend, Taliban Prime Minister Mullah Mohammad Hassan Akhund said that there have been no forms of retaliation. Well, today's investigation paints a very dark picture of a very different reality in those provinces, and the report calls it a pattern of activity around the country. This comes as international aid organizations warn that the humanitarian disaster on the ground continues to deteriorate. About 700,000 people are displaced. Most of them are women and children. According to the UN, 10 million children in Afghanistan need humanitarian aid.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, I talked with a director of the Red Cross who just returned from Afghanistan, and he says that hospitals are overwhelmed with emaciated starving children. UN says that as many as 1 million children will be malnourished by the end of next month. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bleak, bleak. Alex, thank you very much. Alex Hogan reporting live from London.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in retired three star General Keith Kellogg, former national security adviser to Vice President Mike Pence, and the author of the book "War by Other Means: A General in the Trump White House,", also a Fox News contributor. General, thank you.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that report and what \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2  is telling us?\par \par \uc2 KEITH KELLOGG, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. Thanks, Martha, for having me. Look, you said it a minute ago. This was entirely predictable. Look, when you look at who runs security inside of Afghanistan, it's the Interior Ministry. And in the Interior minister is Siraj Haqqani.\par \par \uc2 Siraj Haqqani is a designated terrorist by the United States, he's got a $10 million bounty on his head. And this is the guy who's running security. Of course they were going to do this. It shouldn't have surprised anybody, it didn't surprise me. I think it probably surprised the Biden administration because clearly they didn't think about it.\par \par \uc2 But there's a bigger issue here. And the my bigger issue is what kind of messages is now sending to our allies and our adversaries as well, about how well national security is done within the Biden administration when you've got Ukraine and Russia, and China, and Taiwan, the Uighurs in China, and you're looking at the current talks, the Iranian nuclear talks going on in Vienna. This does not augur well for confidence in the United States decision making or the national security apparatus as the United States has.\par \par \uc2 So this was predictable. You called it, I called it. The only people didn't call it was the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know what they did call, though, general? They said we will pick a time and place of our choosing to retaliate against ISIS-K, against any forces that killed those 13 brave young men and women, Marines and Navy Corpsman who served at the abbey gate. They said, President Biden stood in front of everyone and said, you know, we will retaliate for this attack.\par \par \uc2 He also said that anybody who was left behind, including people who had served in the Afghan government, which essentially were, you know, our allies in that fight because they were trying to stabilize the government, that anybody who wanted to get out would get out. I haven't seen an update on this from the President or from \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  about how this is going. When was the last time we had a plane come with people on it, who wanted to leave this situation before their bodies were found at the front door of their family home, general?\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes. Well, their stories haven't aged very well. In fact, their track record is terrible. And what I'm talking about is a presidential pattern of Joe Biden when he was president and vice president as well. It's always been indecision or making bad decisions. In fact, let's go back to what Bob Gates, former director of the CIA and the Defense Department said, when he said that Joe Biden has been wrong on nearly every national security decision in the last 40 years. And that pattern continues. And that's what's concerning to me, because that's the pattern that has been seen by our allies and adversaries as well. It's a pattern of indecision or just making bad decisions.\par \par \uc2 And you're absolutely right. They talk about it, but there's no actions being taken at all.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, not to mention, you know, a hit that was supposed to be taking out ISIS-K members in an imminent attack, which turned out to be humanitarian workers and their family. This is -- it looks like they just want to turn the page on this and forget the whole thing. You hear about those reports of so many starving children in Afghanistan, you just wonder how that looks to the rest of the world and how it looks to the Afghan is for sure.\par \par \uc2 Quick thought from you on this, as the US commander calls for more aircraft carriers in the Pacific to deter China, and talk of sort of sprucing up the bases there as well. The US Seventh Fleet Commander called for an expanded presence by the United States and allied aircraft carriers in the Pacific to persuade China and Russia that "today is not the day" to start a conflict, general.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes. Well, look, Martha, that's exactly what I would do. I'd put an aircraft carrier, USS Ron Reagan is in Japan, and I put one into training in Vietnam. And actually, we've got them covered north and south, with two aircraft carriers, the carrier battle groups. I do that. And as something that's very smart. I think that a military commander has made the right call is pinch to Chinese and do it by putting overwhelming force in the region. And both aircraft carrier battle groups would do that.\par \par \uc2 I think it's a smart move. I bet but they won't do it. I can guarantee you they won't do it at all