 although we will see him again on December 20th in court, in a hearing set to appoint a public defender and today's charges. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Griff, thank you very much. Fox and Friends Weekend co-host Pete Hegseth joins me now. Pete, I can't help but think if they had slapped a $5 million bail on him after one of those prior offenses, molesting a child, all of those prior offenses. These people whose pictures that we keep putting on the screen, and this little boy, would be alive today.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, FOX NEWS HOST: Absolutely. He shouldn't even have a $5 million bill today. Why is their bill set at all? And when you look at those pictures where you were talking that picture on the left, Jackson Sparks, that is the picture, and all of all of those people their pure innocence, pure innocence taken away by pure evil. And the reason why this is treated so differently is because of politics, is because of narrative.\par \par \uc2 And in this particular case, you had district attorneys with very particular views about what's wrong with our society and it's usually siding on the side of criminals. It wasn't just him running over his girlfriend, there was a prior attack six months earlier as well in Georgia, where he was charged with misdemeanor abuse for beating that very same woman there, shot a firearm at his cousin outside of his house. I mean, it's a 20-year record. They risk, they deemed him high at risk. This is an attack that was entirely preventable based entirely on a philosophy that sides with criminals.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's just so insane and it lacks any common sense what kind of judge, what kind of attorney looks at this case and doesn't figure out this is not going to be reformed. This person is not going to be reformed. He has broken his social contract time and time, and time again. So where are these people's brains? Where are their heads?\par \par \uc2 You know, I understand people who want to do reform, who want to make sure that people get equal time, you know, depending on your economic status, I get that. This is horrible. People should pay for having passed this over and allow this man to be free.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Absolutely they should. Ideology, often clouds judgment and common sense in this particular case --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Or laziness I think in some cases --\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Or laziness.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Like what he did?\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Or rushing things through or using COVID as an excuse to say we're not going to detain people anymore who are at risk. Any number of reasons are unacceptable. That's why you saw John Chisholm, the DA, come out say we made a mistake here, except he's been saying for over a decade his philosophy is. And he knew and he's admitted that it would lead to people getting killed at some point. And here it did, right?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I want to play this quickly. This is Peter Doocy and Jen Psaki. Watch this. I want to get quick thought, Pete, on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Why hasn't the President visited the members of this Christmas parade attack?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Obviously, any President going to visit a community requires a lot of assets requires taking their resources. And it's not something that I have -- a trip previewed at this point in time, but we remain in touch with local officials and certainly our hearts are with the community as they've gone through such a difficult time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that's comforting. Why do you think that is?\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Wrong narrative, wrong narrative, wrong perpetrator and wrong motives. And in any particular case, they're looking at something that advances their narrative. In this particular case, Darrell Brooks, no one's talked about what -- they've talked a lot about his rap sheet, what about his ideological background? What about the fact that he's member of militant black organizations that believed in killing Jews blatantly anti- Semitic, blatantly anti-white people.\par \par \uc2 They don't want to talk about those things, they want to talk about an SUV as if a vehicle did it in this particular case. And it's pretty tough to look families in the eye when you're a part of a party that's been willing to enable defunding the police and softening sentences for criminals. So they're flying right to Minnesota, I'm from there, it's not far.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Yes, as Greg Gutfeld said you can run over a Christmas parade and kill twice as many people as the Boston Marathon bombers and they will call it an accident. Many in the media said that the car did this. This guy is the suspect not the car. Pete, thank you. It's always good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks for sticking around tonight, appreciate it. So that is THE STORY of Tuesday, November the 30th, last day of November. THE STORY goes on. We'll be back on December 1st at 3:00 tomorrow, but stick around "YOUR WORLD" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020211201ehbu00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with U.S. Surgeon General, Dr. Vivek Murthy; Interview with Texas Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Wallace, David Spunt \par \uc2 9,165 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Chris Wallace.\par \par \uc2 Public health officials warned of a winter surge as COVID cases rise across the country. And the Biden administration's vaccine mandate faces challenges in court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (voice-over): States and private businesses suing the government over rules requiring shots or testing for tens of millions of workers.\par \par \uc2 KEN PAXTON (R), TEXAS ATTORNEY GENERAL: The president does not have the authority to force people to get a vaccine and then threatened them with their jobs.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE, WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: We're confident in our authority to protect American workers.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: We'll ask surgeon general, Dr. Vivek Murthy, about the debate over public health versus personal freedom. Then, we'll get reaction from Texas Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , whose state is at the forefront of legal battles over vaccines, masks, abortion, and voting rights.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Trump advisor \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  hit with contempt of Congress charges for refusing to provide to the House committee investigating the January 6 attack on the \uc2 Capitol\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We'll ask our Sunday panel about the test of a president's executive powers.\par \par \uc2 And our Power Player of the Week, Virginia's incoming lieutenant governor, Winsome Sears, and how she made history.\par \par \uc2 WINSOME SEARS (R), VIRGINIA INCOMING LT. GOVERNOR: I think they wanted to take a chance on me. They're tired of politicians who won't let the wounds of the past heal.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: All right now on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (on camera): And hello again from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 There's a new spike in COVID cases across the country. Just as a federal appeals court has blocked one of the main ways President Biden wants to fight the virus. The court ruled for the second time against a new administration rule that businesses with more than 100 workers must mandate vaccines or weekly tests or face thousands of dollars in fines.\par \par \uc2 The court says that the rule grossly exceeds the government's authority and rejects the argument stopping the mandate could cost dozens or even hundreds of lives per day. In a moment, we'll speak with U.S. surgeon general, Dr. Vivek Murthy, and we'll get reaction from Texas Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  who is suing the administration.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's bring in David Spunt at the White House with the latest on the administration's fight against COVID and against its critics -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Chris, during his first few months in office, President Biden pushed back against vaccine mandates. But COVID infections rose and that all changed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice-over): The Biden White House receiving another blow from a federal court, halting the vaccine and testing requirements pending review. A judge on the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals in New Orleans in an opinion late Friday wrote that the public is maintaining our constitutional structure and maintaining the liberty of individuals to make intensely personal decisions according to their own convictions even or perhaps particularly when those decisions frustrate government officials.\par \par \uc2 White House officials insists the mandate is literally a lifesaver.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: We are confident in our authority to protect American workers as this virus is killing 1,100 -- approximately 1,100 Americans a day.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The Department of Justice prepared to fight all the way to the Supreme Court, writing in part: The department will continue to vigorously defend the standard and looks forward to obtaining a definitive resolution following consolidation of all the pending cases for further review.\par \par \uc2 The mandate lost in federal court to a growing list of supply chain issues and the highest inflation rate in 30 years and it spells trouble ahead for a president not even a year into his term.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Even though wages are going up, we still face challenges that we have to tackle on. We have to tackle them head-on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Tomorrow, President Biden will sign the bipartisan infrastructure package into law, Chris. Then he'll head out on the road in Michigan and New Hampshire to tout the plan -- Chris.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: David Spunt reporting from the White House -- David, thanks.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now, U.S. surgeon general, Dr. Vivek Murthy.\par \par \uc2 Doctor, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 DR. VIVEK MURTHY, U.S. SURGEON GENERAL: Thanks so much, Chris. It's good to see you again.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: COVID cases are up 11 percent over the last two weeks and you can see the spike across the country.\par \par \uc2 The virus is on the rise in 26 states from California to Maine. In some states, increases of more than 50 percent. And the CDC says 68 percent, two-thirds of U.S. counties, now have, quote, high community transmission.\par \par \uc2 As the weather gets colder and people spend more time indoors, what are the chances that we're going to see another winter surge?\par \par \uc2 MURTHY: Well, Chris, the good news is that we are certainly well down from the peaks that we hit during the delta wave earlier, we see in the late summer. But as winter does approach, people do go indoors, and virus is also better able to transmit in cold dry air. So, we know that increases up the possibility that there will be spread.\par \par \uc2 A couple of things that are critical, though, for people to keep in mind. And number one is if you are vaccinated, your chances of both getting sick and transmitting the virus to someone else are much, much lower. So, this makes it all the more important as winter approaches to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 And second, if you are eligible to get a booster shot, it's especially helpful if you can go ahead and do that now as winter approaches again, and as people get prepared for the holidays.\par \par \uc2 So, we should be prepared for the fact that there may be an uptick in cases that we see in various parts of the country with cold weather. But what has held true for the last year is so true, which is vaccines still give you a high degree of protection, especially against the worst outcomes of COVID like hospitalization and death.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: You talk about being eligible for the booster. Three states, California, Colorado, and New Mexico, have jumped ahead of federal authorities and said that anyone, everyone above the age of 18 can now get a booster. Do you have a problem with that?\par \par \uc2 MURTHY: I certainly understand, Chris, why these states are doing that. You know, they are looking at what's happening in their own states, that they are seeing cases go up and they want to have broad protection for the entire state. And I get that.\par \par \uc2 What people should know, though, is that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has already made millions of people eligible for booster shots -- people who are above 65 who have other illnesses that put them at higher risk and who are at higher risk of exposure based on where they live or where they work. Millions of people are eligible who have not yet gone their booster shot and we want to focus on that.\par \par \uc2 What people should also know, though, is that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is considering broadening that eligibility after a request from \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  to do so. But what they're going to do is take a close look at the data. They want to make, you know, especially sure that the booster shots are both safe and effective for the populations that are not currently eligible. And once they determined that, then they will make a recommendation there.\par \par \uc2 But the bottom line is that millions of people can get boosted right now and we want them to get boosted, because that will both extend and enhance the protection that they've already been getting from the vaccines.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: There is reportedly a split inside the Biden administration between folks like Dr. Fauci and you who, reportedly, favor making boosters available to all adults, and CDC director, Dr. Walensky, who is reportedly not as persuaded that young, healthy adults need the extra protection.\par \par \uc2 Does the split inside the administration add to the public confusion about vaccinations?\par \par \uc2 MURTHY: Well, Chris, I'm glad you ask that question because the reality is that there is much more agreement here than I think people appreciate. In August, in fact, all three of us, Dr. Fauci, Dr. Walensky, myself, as well as other medical leaders within the administration, including the FDA commissioner, the NIH director and others, all signed a letter saying that we believe that protection was starting to wane and that boosters would be needed at some point later in the year for people across the age spectrum, you know, 18 and above.\par \par \uc2 What we also said, though, in that letter was that we wanted the CDC and the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to weigh in on both the efficacy and the safety of booster shots. And all that actually remains true. And so, you know, while we haven't millions of people eligible for booster shots, we are going through an \uc2 FDA\uc2  process and we'll go through a CDC process to see if that eligibility should be widen.\par \par \uc2 But the bottom line is all of us are in the same page about the strong protection that the vaccines afford and we know that where boosters are indicated, that they will be hopeful to extending people's protection against COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: On Friday, the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals in New Orleans for the second time continued to block the Biden administration's mandate that all businesses with more than 100 employees vaccinate their workers or get a test. And here's what the judges wrote: Rather than a delicately handled scalpel, the mandate is a one-size-fits-all sledgehammer that makes hardly any attempt to account for differences in workplaces.\par \par \uc2 Now, you're not a lawyer, so I'm not going to ask about their legal reasoning. But as a public health expert, if the courts continue to block the vaccine mandate for big companies with over 100 employees, what's the impact on public health?\par \par \uc2 MURTHY: Well, Chris, I think it would be a setback for public health. What we know very clearly is that when people get vaccinated and the more people who get vaccinated, the quicker we're able to bring this pandemic to an end, the more lives we can an ultimately save. And that's my primary concern as a doctor and as a public health expert is how do we end this pandemic as quickly as possible and save lives in the process?\par \par \uc2 Look, Chris, what's really important that people understand about these requirements in workplace are a few things. Number one, they are not new. We've had requirements in various settings in our country since the founding of the United States of America, including in schools since the 1800s, workplaces like hospitals have had requirements for vaccines for years. The military has had such requirements.\par \par \uc2 But we also know, Chris, that they work remarkably well. Even with COVID- 19, we've seen a 20 percent bump on average in vaccination rates with organizations that put requirements in place, and many already have.\par \par \uc2 Keep in mind, many businesses are not waiting for the federal mandate. In fact, a third of Fortune 100 companies have already put requirements in place and millions of people gotten vaccinated as a result.\par \par \uc2 What's really at the heart of this for a vaccinate requirement strategy is to create safer workplaces for workers, for customers, and to increase vaccination rates overall, because that's ultimately how we're going to end this pandemic. And that's our collective, shared goal as a country.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But if public safety is the issue, how do you balance the drive for more vaccinations with the fact that police and health care workers in a number of cities are walking off of the jobs and now -- now, you have the Oklahoma National Guard refusing the mandate to get vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 MURTHY: Well, Chris, what I think is important to appreciate in the broader picture is that the vast, vast majority of people across this country, vast majority of workers are, in fact, in line with and will be in compliance with the general requirements. We've already seen that in the businesses that have put these requirements in place. Many of them have achieved greater than 95 percent, sort of, adherence, if you will, with the vaccine requirements. So, I think that's really important to keep in mind.\par \par \uc2 But ultimately this is about --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Well, I mean, I just want to say, but on the other hand, there is a large percentage, thousands of Border Patrol agents who haven't been vaccinated and are threatening to leave the job. You see in police departments in big cities, health care workers, and as I say now, the entire Oklahoma National Guard.\par \par \uc2 MURTHY: So, here's what's important there, Chris. I think, number one, look, I think everyone's point of view matters. And for those who are concerned about the vaccine requirements, I think it's important for them to have their questions answered, their concerns heard.\par \par \uc2 But if we're concerned about the safety of the workforce, Chris, the single biggest threat to our workforce in hospitals, our police force, and other workforces has been COVID itself. The number of workers who've gotten sick, who have been quarantined and off of the job as a result, who have lost their lives is extraordinary and really heartbreaking during this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 So, if we want to protect our workers, Chris, these vaccine requirements will ultimately help us to advance that caused to the greatest extent that we need to.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: For a lot of people, though, this comes down to personal freedom. And Green Bay Packers quarterback Aaron Rodgers came down -- and I can see you nodding, you know -- with COVID after deciding to take alternative treatments instead of the vaccination. He spoke out this week. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AARON RODGERS, GREEN BAY PACKERS QUARTERBACK: I believe strongly in bodily autonomy, and the ability to make choices for your body. And not have to acquiesce to some woke culture.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Doctor, what do you say to Aaron Rodgers?\par \par \uc2 MURTHY: Look, I think the principle of freedom, when it comes to health, but in general, is a very important one. Freedom is an important value that brought my family to this country, as immigrants years ago, and has brought so many immigrant families to this nation. It's extraordinarily important.\par \par \uc2 But keep in mind, we are a community of 300 million people. We are not sole individuals entirely on our own. In any community, sometimes our decisions do affect other people. It's why, Chris, we have speed limits on highways because we know our decision about how we fast we drive affects the safety of others.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to getting vaccinated, we know that people who are unvaccinated are high risk of getting sick and spreading it to others, which is why in many settings, Chris, we have made the decision as a community to require vaccines in schools, for example, all across our country --\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MURTHY: -- and in certain workplace settings.\par \par \uc2 That's why these are so important here, too, Chris. So, while freedom is absolutely important, we also have a collective responsibility to one another when our decisions impact the health and well-being of others.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Dr. Murthy, thank you. Thanks for your time this week and always good to talk with you, sir.\par \par \uc2 MURTHY: Always good to talk to you, too, Chris. Take care and stay well.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: You, too.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll get reaction from one of the state attorneys general fighting the vaccine mandate. \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  of Texas joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Texas has been taking on the Biden administration on a range of issues, from abortion, to voting reform, and now vaccine mandates. They all have one thing in common, they cross the desk of the state's top law enforcement officer.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now is the Texas attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Paxton, you're suing the Trump -- rather, the Trump -- the Biden administration over this vaccine mandate which you say is unconstitutional and a federal overreach. But coronavirus, the virus is still killing more than 1,000 people every day. And a top White House spokesperson had a question this week. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE, WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: The question is really that I have is, why are these legislatures, these Republicans, getting in the way of that, getting in the way of saving lives, getting in the way of us making sure that the economy is working as well and getting out of this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And so that's the question for them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: How to answer that question, sir?\par \par \uc2 KEN PAXTON\uc2 , TEXAS ATTORNEY GENERAL: Well, first of all, I'm not in the legislature, so I can't quite answer for our legislature.\par \par \uc2 But I think that they want people to have choices over their own decisions as to their health care with their doctor's advice. And so people have different opinions about what that should be for them and it shouldn't be made by the federal government from -- from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's desk.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I want to play a clip of you from this week going over, going against the vaccine mandate.\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: I would urge businesses, don't listen to the president. He's bullying Americans. He's bullying businesses. And what they should do is take care of their own businesses, take care of their own workers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: You say Texas companies should take care of their own workers. So, given that, how do you justify the governor of Texas, Greg Abbott, issuing an executive order that bans any business in Texas from issuing a vaccine mandate and -- and how do you justify the governor issuing a ban on all school districts on mask mandate, a ban that was overturned just this week by a federal judge?\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: So, I justified that the governor has the authority under state law, in an emergency, to respond to these types of issues. And so he's done just that.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, it's his view that these mask mandates are unnecessary and that vaccine requirements are also unnecessary. So it's my job as a state's attorney to go defend what he's done and what the legislature's done. And I'm perfectly comfortable doing that.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Well, I just want to drill down on this a little bit. You said that you think that Texas company, businesses, should take care of their own workers. Is that consistent with the governor's executive order and your enforcement of that order which bans companies from taking care of their own workers as they see fit?\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: Yes, so what I was trying to say in that clip was the governor -- the -- the president doesn't have the authority to force companies. Obviously, we've gotten a stay in the Fifth Circuit to stop him from forcing companies to require their workers to get the -- the vaccine or be fired. And what I'm saying to these companies is, you don't have to listen to him because he's out there saying now you should do it anywhere -- do it anyway, despite the fact that we have a stay from a higher court.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Yes, but -- but you said that the business should take care of their own workers and the governor is saying that they can't take care of their own workers as they see fit, they're prohibited from deciding if they so choose to issue a vaccine mandate. That's not consistent.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: Yes, so -- well, we're dealing with different types of requirements here. We've got state government requirements. So it's clear that the governor has a different executive order for them. And we've been in all types of litigation with school district and counties trying to stop them from forcing mandates, in San Antonio, vaccines, and we've been successful.\par \par \uc2 We've actually had no lawsuits against businesses. There's a lot more freedom with businesses to make their own decisions. But what I would say is that they -- they should definitely consider their employees because we're in a situation right now with our economy where we can't afford to lose transportation employees or health care workers or law enforcement officers. And that's -- that's happening all over the country. And I think it's going to have a negative impact on our economy and our ability to help people.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But -- but, I -- I -- I just want to go through this one more time. You're saying that they should have the authority and the ability to decide what their workers should do. The governor's executive order prohibits them from deciding what they want to do. He bans vaccine mandates.\par \par \uc2 Isn't a mandate by the federal government -- are you saying there's a different between a mandate to get a vaccine from the federal government is different in terms of the ability to take care of their own from a state mandate not to have vaccine mandates?\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: Well, I think your question is a little confusing, but, yes, the federal government has no authority to do this. Right now we have OSHA guidelines that have not been authorized by Congress. They absolutely have no authority to do this. The governor has a different authority under state law that the legislatures give him and he's operating under that state law. And so we're doing our best to defend --\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: So -- so he can -- so he can tell private businesses what to do? It's -- it's OK? And -- and they can't take care of their own?\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: Well, look, I've -- I'm more -- I definitely agree that states have more authority over these areas than the federal government. The federal government has limited authority. And if Congress has not granted that authority to OSHA -- and -- and I would even question whether Congress has authority. Yes, states have a lot of authority to deal with what's going on in their states. And -- and I think that's -- that's been clear from the founding of our country.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: You're also in court defending the new abortion law in Texas that deputizes anyone, however disconnected from it, from -- for charging, for suing anyone who assists in an abortion.\par \par \uc2 And I want to put up what you said. You said that this law, the Texas law, is not at odds with Roe v. Wade, but merely creating the potential for liability for some abortions is not a ban.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Attorney General, are you saying if someone wants to assist with an abortion and just pay the $10,000 fine, that's OK?\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: I'm not saying it's OK. It's a violation of state law. But you'll have to realize, I am not responsible for drafting these laws. I defend them. And we are doing that. And I think the -- the legislature has made a choice about how they're going to relate abortion and how they're going to protect the unborn. And they've done it here. And my job is go defend those lawsuits. And we've done it all the way to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And you should know that this case, so far, is really not about the substance of whether Roe v. Wade is going to be overturned. It's about whether these -- whether the federal government, this other plaintiff, have -- have standings to sue me, the attorney general, who has no authority right now to actually implement any of this.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Well, let me ask you a direct question. What do you think of the new abortion law in Texas?\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: Well, look, I -- I applaud the legislature for -- for doing anything they can to protect human life. They try to balance the current Supreme Court provisions and cases with -- with what they want to do in the state. They're elected by people in this state and they have the right to go pass whatever laws they want and we'll deal with them in court when -- when it comes up.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But you applaud the law.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you about one aspect of the law. It makes no exception for cases of rape and incest. You know, if a pregnancy results from a crime, like rape or incest, you have no problem with forcing the woman to carry that fetus to term?\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: So, again, I didn't pass this law. My job is to defend it. Whether -- if I was a legislature, I would have made some changes to this, I -- I don't know. I -- I would have -- I would have certainly had that opportunity. I defend what the legislature put in place here and I'm glad to do my job representing the people of Texas.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Well, again, I'm -- I'm asking you, as a public official, you must have an opinion. Do you think there should be an exception for rape and incest, as there are in most abortion laws in the country, or do you favor what Texas did, which was to say that there's no exception for rape or incest?\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: Here's what I'd say. I think it's very important to protect life. I think this bill is defensible and I think we're going to -- we are going to do a really good job defending it. We already have.\par \par \uc2 It's gone to the Supreme Court twice. And the first time we were successful. I have no idea what's going to happen on the second round. But, again, we're not even talking about the substance right now, we're talking about the procedure, which is whether they even have standing to sue us, which we don't believe they do.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: You face your own legal problems, as you well know. You're under indictment on an allegation of securities fraud, and you're also the subject of an \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation because some former top officials in your own office accused you of bribery.\par \par \uc2 George P. Bush\uc2 , the son of Jeb Bush, is running against you next year. You're seeing re-election as attorney general. Here's what he says. Our top lawyer needs to be above reproach. There shouldn't even be a question of one's character and competence for this important role. How big a problem do you think these allegations against you will be in your efforts to win reelection?\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: Look, I've been dealing with this kind of fight. When you go out and do the types of things that I've done, your going to be challenge. You're going to have issues like this pop up. And -- and I've -- this happened seven years ago, almost seven years ago. I was re-elected when it happened. And I think we've done a great job defending the state of Texas. That's what I'm going to talk about, what my job is to do for the state of Texas and make sure that I am defending the state, that I'm defending the governor, the legislature, and I'm going to continue to do that job.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Attorney General Paxton, thank you. Thanks for coming in today. Good to talk with you, sir.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: Hey, have a great day. Thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Up next, we'll bring in our Sunday group to discuss former Trump advisors \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 's indictment on contempt of Congress charges for refusing to appear before the January 6 committee. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Up next, President Biden doubling down to sell his build back better plan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This bill is going to ease inflationary pressures, lowering the cost to working families.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: We'll ask our Sunday panel about the president's effort to reassure members of his own party.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 STEVE BANNON\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF STRATEGIST (JANUARY 5, 2021): All hell is going to break lose tomorrow. Just understand this, all hell is going to break lose tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Former Trump advisors \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  on January 5th, the day before the insurrection at the U.S. Capitol. Bannon has a first court appearance tomorrow on contempt of Congress charges for refusing to provide information to the House committee investigating the riot.\par \par \uc2 And it's time now for our Sunday group. Former RNC communications director Doug Heye, Julie Pace, executive editor for the "Associated Press," and Mo Elleithee of Georgetown University's Institute of Politics and Public Service.\par \par \uc2 Julie, it is rare for the Justice Department to prosecute criminal contempt of Congress charges. But what does this prosecution, what does this case say about the ability down the line for the House committee investigating the January 6th riot to actually be able to hear from former Trump advisors despite the former president's claim of executive privilege?\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE, EXECUTIVE EDITOR, "ASSOCIATED PRESS": I think that this is potentially a boost for this committee, which has been struggling to get some key people to actually show up, to talk with them, to turn over documents. And this is a warning shot from the Justice Department that if individuals who were close to President Trump are refusing to participate in the process, they could face criminal prosecution.\par \par \uc2 I do think Bannon's case is a bit unique because he was trying to fall within the executive privilege umbrella, even though he had left the White House in 2017. I think there are big questions that we'll be looking to get answers from the Justice Department on in terms of \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , who was chief of staff, who was in the White House, does executive privilege extend to him? But certainly, for this committee, which wants to move rapidly, wants to get answers from key people around President Trump, I think this is a real potential boost for them.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Yes, Doug, I want to pick up on a couple of the points that Julie made, because the banning case is different. First of all, President Trump was no longer the president. He's the former president. As she points out, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  was not a current White House official at this time. He had been out of the White House since 2017. And, also, we're not talking about the official business of the president. What we're talking about is investigating a crime, which was the insurrection at the \uc2 Capitol\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So what does all that say about the strength of Bannon's case, and is there a big difference between Bannon's case and that, let's say, of former White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 DOUG HEYE, FORMER RNC COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: Sure, there's an enormous different. Obviously, the former chief of staff, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , is going to go through the process of seeing what is allowed in executive privilege and what isn't. And we don't know those answers yet. That's part of what we'll find out.\par \par \uc2 Bannon just does not have those protections. And, you know, I can tell you, Chris, when I worked in the House of Representatives for then Majority Leader \uc2 Eric Cantor\uc2 , Congress held \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2 , the then attorney general, in contempt for not appearing before Congress.\par \par \uc2 Congress takes these things very seriously. And whether you're talking about a Republican House or a Democratic House, the primacy of Congress and its oversight is paramount. And Republicans and Democrats should be able to unify on this. They obviously won't. But if we have a Republican House in the future, they will have no hesitation in holding Democrats to contempt, just as we're seeing right now. It may be why the best option should have been having an independent committee all along.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Of course that was blocked the Senate Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 , who's a member of the January 6th House Committee, had a very strong reaction this week to the Bannon indictment.\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN\uc2  (D-MD): It was a criminal offense. He violated the law when he stood us up, when he blew off the subpoena, and he violated the law when he refused to produce the documents and the papers we were looking for.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Mo, the problem for the committee is that undoubtedly \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  and his lawyers are going to try to delay this case as long as possible, do everything they can procedurally to play it out. And there's a good chance, I think probably better than 50/50 we'd say now, that a year from now, in the midterms, that Republicans may take control of the House, in which case no matter what happens in this case, they would probably kill the committee and there would be no place for Bannon to testify even if he's ordered to.\par \par \uc2 MO ELLEITHEE, GEORGETOWN INSTITUTE OF POLITICS AND PUBLIC SERVICE: Yes. And, look, I agree with what Doug just said. I think there are a couple of long terms implications here. One is getting to the truth, right, of what happened on January 6th, and that is first and foremost what this is all about.\par \par \uc2 But, number two, it is about establishing and defending the role of Congress in oversight. And that cannot be a partisan thing.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have held people in contempt. Democrats have held people in contempt. If you believe in the rule of law, if you believe in the Constitution, if you believe in the role that Congress plays, then -- then you've got to support this.\par \par \uc2 And I do think if Republicans take over a year from now and kill this whole endeavor, they are completely undermining their ability to hold oversight in the future.\par \par \uc2 So there's a lot of different balls at play here.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Yes, but, Mo --\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Chris, can I say quickly, this will make the lame duck Congress in -- in next December one of the wildest that we will ever have seen in Washington.\par \par \uc2 ELLEITHEE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But, Mo, let me just pick up on this idea that -- that this should be above partisanship, this should be about the law.\par \par \uc2 The House held \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2 , then the attorney general for \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , in contempt, and the Obama administration and Obama Justice Department refused to prosecute that case. I mean there's politics all over these kinds of cases.\par \par \uc2 ELLEITHEE: Sure. But, look, I do think -- and you've already alluded to this in -- in your -- in the early conversation, what happens with Bannon may be different than what happens with others. Bannon was not there. He was not in the White House. He talked about this on air the day before. There's no way to make the executive privilege claim.\par \par \uc2 Whether or not others are successful in that, that will play out. That is - - you know, whether or not you're in government, that will play out.\par \par \uc2 But this, I think, sends a very strong message that they're not playing around and that they want to get to the truth.\par \par \uc2 This is unprecedented. And -- and it's -- it's going to be treated as such.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Julie, you know, we're -- we're talking about all of the reasons that the Bannon cases is different, but there is talk here in Washington, concern that it could open up a dangerous precedent. And -- and, you know, seeing the willingness of the Biden administration to go after \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  and potentially to go after people with a -- maybe a stronger case, like the White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , that this opens the door for one administration to go after officials in the previous administration.\par \par \uc2 PACE: And that's why I think the decisions that are still to come for the Justice Department are potentially even more significant. You know, because, again, I think Bannon is a bit of a unique situation, having left the White House in 2017 and still trying to claim executive privilege because there is a precedent that will be set by the decisions that come forward. If a sitting chief of staff is going to be prosecuted for failing to appear before Congress and talk about his direct conversation with the sitting president of the United States. I do think that that is something that -- that both parties would have some measure of concern about, but I think that the Justice Department here is also weighing, as you mentioned earlier, the incident that is at the center of this discussion. You know, this -- this is -- and it was an attack on American democracy. It was an insurrection at the U.S. Capitol. And I do think that that raises the significance of these decisions that they're making right now.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Finally, Doug, you wrote an interesting article this week talking about the continued drag that you feel that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and especially his claims of a stolen election have on the Republican Party going into the 2022 midterms. Explain.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Yes. Well, it's not just 2022, it's 2024 as well. And my big concern is if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn't run again, and, obviously, nobody knows what he's going to do, perhaps except for Trump himself, is that we could have a scenario where we have 17 Republicans running for president, like we did in 2016, and that there is no incentive for any Republican to accept the results of the Iowa caucus or the New Hampshire primary. In fact, they'd be incentivize if they come in second or even third to say that it's a fraud, to say that it was fixed, which could cause an entire presidential primary system to be in chaos in a state-by-state almost rolling tour. And that's something that I don't think the Republican Party is prepared for or really the country.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: And what that means is that you have to make a break with the idea, if you're -- if you're going to establish this principle, we're going to abide by the results, you have to make a break with the -- President Trump, and that's a dangerous position for a Republican.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: It's a dangerous position for any Republican. It's also a challenge for the party and for state parties as to, will they accept their own results or not indifference to Trump or indifference to any of those candidates who might call into question again say the Iowa caucus, which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said was fixed when he lost it in 2016 to Ted Cruz.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Panel, we have to take a break here, but when we return, how a surge in inflation will affect a president's agenda and your plans to celebrate the holidays.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: People are feeling it. They're feeling it. Did you ever think you'd be paying this much for a gallon of gas? In some parts in California, they're paying $4.50 a gallon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: President Biden reacting to the latest inflation numbers that consumer prices rose 6.2 percent from a year ago.\par \par \uc2 And we're back now with the panel.\par \par \uc2 Well, I think it's fair to say this is the week that inflation really hit home and became a top political issue beyond that overall 6.2 percent increase, that figure.\par \par \uc2 I want you to look at some of the spikes on specific items. Gas prices, up 50 percent. Beef prices, up 20 percent. Used car prices up 26 percent.\par \par \uc2 Doug, how big a problem for President Biden and Democrats just a little bit less than a year out from the midterms?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: This is a big problem that's going to get worse for Biden and the Democrats. And I -- think of "Game of Thrones," Chris, winter is coming. And what does that mean? Home heating oil prices. And if you're a Democrat in the Northeast, where those prices are going to be felt the most, you now have a new issue where you need to separate yourself with the president, whose popularity continues to fall in every poll that we see.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Mo, I think what makes it even worse for this White House is that for months they've been talking down the threat of inflation.\par \par \uc2 Listen to them and also to former Clinton and \uc2 Obama\uc2  top economic advisor, \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , who's been sounding the alarm for a long time.\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Economists called all these things a transitory fix.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRANHOLM\uc2 , SECRETARY OF ENERGY: There is a transitory nature to the inflation problem.\par \par \uc2 LARRY SUMMERS\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF TREASURY: I think that the policymakers in Washington, unfortunately, have almost every month been behind the curve. They said it was transitory. It doesn't look so transitory.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Mo, when \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  says he told them so, he did tell them so. He was right all along and they were wrong.\par \par \uc2 ELLEITHEE: Rule number one of political communications is don't tell people what they're feeling isn't real. You can tell them that -- that the situation is temporary, that we're going to turn a corner, but acknowledging the pain and the concern people are feeling right out of the box is critical.\par \par \uc2 The president's popularity was -- was sky-high for a while because they were pointed to results. They were able to go out there and say, we put money in your wallets, we put shots in your arms. Then they got into this big, big fight. They were getting ready to say, we put jobs in your communities with infrastructure. That got overcome by a big, internal party fight over a big pile of money. Over a number that didn't really have anything to it that people could wrap their heads around.\par \par \uc2 Inflation layered on top of that, it's a big mess. They've got to get back out there and say we get it, as they started to this week. We get it. Here's how we're going to turn the corner. And here's what the next waiver of results are going to look like and how soon you might start to feel them.\par \par \uc2 They can still turn the corner on this, but they squandered a couple of months, they being the broader Democratic family squandered a couple of months. They've got to get back to pointing to those results and helping people understand that they get the problem.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But, Julie, President Biden's answer to how he's going to get an handle on inflation, as he says, let's pass this $1.7 trillion big social spending plan, which he say is going to lower inflation because it will increase worker productivity through a lot of program. But almost anybody who analyzes that bill, if it gets passed, says that in the short term it's going to boost inflation before, in the long term, perhaps it damps it down a bit.\par \par \uc2 PACE: And this is the problem that I think the Biden administration is going to face here. If they can get this bill passed, and we'll see how the next couple of days go here, but if they can get it passed, they are going to have to go out and argue that it was worth all of the time and energy and all of the procedural debate that consumed Washington for the last couple of months. And the reality is that the actual implementation of those policies will take time.\par \par \uc2 And so if you're an American who is not following the ins and outs of the debates in Washington every day, as we are, and thank goodness for most of you that you're not doing that, you know, you're looking at practical measures. You're looking at how much everyday items cost. You're looking at your grocery bill. And you're going to be looking, next year, at those prices and seeing if there's a change.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats will argue that long-term that -- that the positions in this bill are good for the country. But I think the short term impact is what most people are going to be looking for, particularly heading into those midterms.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Doug, and then you've got some other really striking numbers that just came out this week. It turns out, in September, 4.4 million Americans, 3 percent of the total national workforce, quit their jobs.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: What does that say about the state of the economy and the impact for Democrats going forward?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Yes, it says that voters are nervous about what their future might have, that their jobs aren't paying as much as they need to, to, you know, keep their family going. And, ultimately, you know, the word "transitory" is a word that's used in Washington, D.C. It's not used at kitchen tables throughout this country. Those conversations are about the rise in gas prices and how much more it costs to put beef on the table. That's a problem that Democrats need to address and need to understand. And right now they're not showing that they really understand it at all.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Mo, there was an interesting debate in Washington this week -- I think a lot of it sparked by a -- I thought quite an interesting article by two Democrats, although they've strayed in the Republican side at various points, \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2  and Andrew Stein, who argued very strongly that this president needs to make a sharp turn back to the center the way \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  did in 1994 after that midterm debacle.\par \par \uc2 How -- first of all, do you think this president's going to take that advice?\par \par \uc2 ELLEITHEE: I think we obsess over left versus right versus center in this town in a way that nobody else does. I think we keep it not left versus right or center, but it's -- it's front verses back, right? People feel like they are stuck at the back of the line and they can't get ahead. So just talking about people is the way to go.\par \par \uc2 You're starting to see the White House to do this week a little bit. You're starting to see them talk about the immediacy and the urgency for their agenda getting passed, that if it passes, that in 2022 you'll see child care costs come down by x amount, you'll see prescription drug prices come down for seniors by x amount. At least that starts to bring the conversation to the kitchen table that Doug was just talking about.\par \par \uc2 That's what this president does very well and he needs to get back to doing after a couple of months where Washington was just talking about a big pile of money.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I'm not sure, Julie, that I agree with Mo there, because it seems to me that whether it's this spending this huge amount of money or immigration or crime or what goes on in schools, that there is an increase in perception out there. And we certainly saw it played out in Virginia and New Jersey, that Democrats and President Biden have moved to the left.\par \par \uc2 First of all, do you think that's legitimate, that -- that they feel that and, secondly, do you -- I mean do you feel that he's going to take this advice and move back -- tack back to the center, or is that just not who he is?\par \par \uc2 PACE: I think there's just a huge blank space ahead of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as he looks into next year and questions about how he is going to try to fill it. You know, what -- is he going to try to make a run at police reform legislation, at voting rights, two big Democratic priorities, particularly on the left that have been really stalled out in Congress. Does he see that as a way to energize the base heading into the midterms? Does he spend the year campaigning on what he hopes will be a successful package -- successful passage of the Build Back Better plan? I think that's the big dissension for him. You know, what does next year look like. How does he position himself? Because, again, they have spent so much time on these -- on these two big pieces of legislation and there are a lot of promises that were made to a lot of Americans the votive for him that have gone unfulfilled.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But, I've got 20 seconds, do you think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has it in him to triangulate, to move sharply back to the center the way \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  did in the '90s?\par \par \uc2 PACE: I think it's certainly where he's comfortable. And if he feels like that's where he needs to go for the party to succeed, I think that might be where he would want to be.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Thank you, panel. See you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our "Power Player of the Week," Virginia's lieutenant governor- elect, Winsome Sears, on her long shot win and her powerful story.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Glenn Youngkin's victory in the Virginian governor's race was the big story on election night. But there was someone else on the ballot who also won. And she's also creating a stir, taking on tough issues and nailing one-liners.\par \par \uc2 Here's our "Power Player of the Week."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WINSOME SEARS, VIRGINIA LT. GOVERNOR-ELECT: I've just always assumed that whatever room I am in, I belong. Whatever I want to pursue, it's mine for the taking.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Virginia Republican Winsome Sears will soon be taking the office of lieutenant governor.\par \par \uc2 SEARS: How sweet it is.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Once considered a longshot, she'll be the first black woman in that post.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (on camera): What do you think Virginia voters were saying on Election Day when they made you lieutenant governor?\par \par \uc2 SEARS: They're tired of the black against white and the Asian against Latino. They're tired of it and they're tired of politicians who won't let the wounds of the past heal.\par \par \uc2 I think we're going to have to go all around. Yes.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (voice over): We were with \uc2 Sears\uc2  this week as she toured the state capital in Richmond, checking out the senate floor, where she'll preside.\par \par \uc2 SEARS: Wow. How awesome is this.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: The power of the moment was not lost on her.\par \par \uc2 SEARS: A black lieutenant governor was handing off to another black lieutenant governor, and that has never happened.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So that's history.\par \par \uc2 SEARS: That is history.\par \par \uc2 God bless Virginia.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Sears ran at a time when race and education are the focus of charged debate.\par \par \uc2 SEARS: Nobody is denying that we don't want to hear all the history, least of all me. I certainly don't want the sins of the past to be repeated. We don't have to tear one person down in order to build another up. That's no way to be. That's not America.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Sears' love for America is routed in her life story. She immigrated from Jamacia and served in the Marines.\par \par \uc2 SEARS: Everything that I've had, I've had to work for. No one gave me anything.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: She isn't shy about how her lived experience shapes her worldview.\par \par \uc2 SEARS: In case you haven't noticed, I am black and I have been black all my life. But that's not what this is about.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes what happens to you isn't because you're black or because you're woman, it's simply life. I mean some days you're the pigeon and some days you're the statute. It's just life.\par \par \uc2 We need to wake up.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Her victory, her politics, and this photo made headline beyond the commonwealth.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Was elected as Virginia's first black female lieutenant governor. But this is actually a win for Democrats because nothing will get Republicans to support gun control faster than this picture.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (on camera): Did you think the joke was funny?\par \par \uc2 SEARS: Well, I did, because I thought, well, I don't think he knows what he's saying. And the fact that his audience thought it was quite hilarious was also very telling about their character. So, you know, it's a bunch of hypocrisy, but it's all right. I can handle it. I'm a big girl.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (voice over): It's that tenacity that has political insiders asking already if she has bigger plans.\par \par \uc2 SEARS: I just want to serve, you know, in the end. And I really want other to see me, especially the children, to say, well, if Winsome can do it, I can do it, because I didn't do anything special except stay in school and study. And I never let anybody walk over me. And if they did, they didn't get a second chance.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Winsome Sears is ready to go. She takes office on January 15th.\par \par \uc2 And that's it for today. Have a great week and we'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020211114ehbe00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Joe Biden At U.K. Climate Summit As Approval Dives At Home; Rep. Nancy Mace (R-SC) Is Interviewed About Joe Manchin Not Ready To Support Biden Spending Plan; Supreme Court Hears Arguments On Texas Abortion Law; New Jersey Governor's Race Tightens In Home Stretch; Baldwin Eagerly Awaiting Shooting Probe Results; Attorney For "Rust" Assistant Director On Baldwin Shooting Probe; Final Push For Votes In Virginia Governor's Race\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Shannon Bream, Kayleigh McEnany, Guy Benson,  Jonathan Hunt, Rich Edson \par \uc2 8,223 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: There are times to post photos on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and times not to.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: Maybe a little insensitive. We'll see what happens next with the investigation, John, and great to be with you as we begin a brand-new week. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm John Roberts. We will see you again tomorrow for another episode of "AMERICA REPORTS". THE STORY with Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you, Sandra and John. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum in New York right now on THE STORY.\par \par \uc2 President Biden in Glasgow as Senator Joe Manchin says that they're essentially no closer to a deal on the president's agenda. And as Democratic infighting continues, so during this trip abroad, he was asked about the large picture of all of that and the mounting issues back home by A.P. reporter, Zeke Miller, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ZEKE MILLER, REPORTER, A.P.: You've tried to showcase that America is back, but back at home, your poll numbers have fallen, your party's nominee for governor in Virginia is facing a very tougher than expected race, your party spent months trying to negotiate the centerpiece of your Democratic - - of your domestic legislative agenda. So, why should the world, you know, believe that when you say America is back, that really it's here to stay?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Because of the way they reacted. You were here. They listened. Everyone sought me out. They wanted to know what our views were and we helped lead what happened here.\par \par \uc2 I didn't run to determine how well I'm going to do in the polls. I ran to make sure that I followed through on what I said I would do as President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Rough patch to be sure for President Biden, when you look at polls like these, these are brand new numbers that we just got from Marist today. Democrats and Democrat leaning independents were asked, do you think better that Democrats have a better chance of winning the presidency in 2024 if Joe Biden is the president's nominee -- party's nominee, or if it's somebody else? 44 percent said someone else. 36 percent say Joe Biden. 20 percent say they are not sure if they want it to be the current president or another nominee from the Democrat Party when they look ahead to 2024.\par \par \uc2 So, let's bring in Kayleigh McEnany, former White House press secretary and co-host of "OUTNUMBERED" and Guy Benson, political editor at Town Hall, host of the "GUY BENSON SHOW" and a Fox News contributor. Great to have both of you with us today.\par \par \uc2 Kayleigh, obviously, the White House is in one of those difficult phases. And you know what it feels like to stand up at the podium and have reporters sort of come after you and tell you that things aren't going well. Your thought on what you just heard from Zeke there from A.P. and from the president, from Zeke Miller.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANALYST: Yes, that was a -- that was a fantastic question by Zeke Miller of A.P., well said. Factual and really tough for Biden to answer.\par \par \uc2 Look, there's one thing I can say about when I stood at the podium, we never lost the support of our party, President Trump had a base that is famously very loyal even to this day. We never saw a poll like that where you had essentially more than 60 percent of the party questioning whether you should be the nominee. Now, that's one poll.\par \par \uc2 But in other polls, we've seen Democrats give President Biden low marks on the direction of the country, not necessarily tied to him. But that generic right direction, wrong direction, upwards of 40 percent of Democrats saying we're heading in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 When you have those kinds of numbers within your own party and I have not even gotten to independents who he's underwater with that very key group. But when it's within your party, that's not something we experienced in the Trump administration. It's something they're very, very real having to deal with in a -- in a practical way.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, absolutely. So, right track wrong track, which Kayleigh just mentioned, Guy, 71 percent say that we're on the wrong track. I went back to look at the most recent time we've seen a number like that and it was during the heat of COVID.\par \par \uc2 So, as a pandemic was sweeping the entire country, it would be understandable that the country would feel like things weren't going in the right direction. And now we have that in this environment. So, what do you attribute that wrong track number to?\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: So many different factors, Martha, and that comes from the NBC poll. I know the president said he didn't campaign to do well in the polls, but he certainly is not doing that right now.\par \par \uc2 In that poll, he's underwater by double digits overall on approval. One number that I thought was very interesting in the NBC survey, Martha, was strong approve versus strong disapprove. He only has 18 percent of the country, strongly approving of his job performance. 46 percent strongly disapprove, that goes to an intensity gap as well. We'll see if that plays out, for example in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 So, I understand Biden, the president, what else can you say, when your poll numbers are this bad in a given moment, saying I'm not governing for the polls. What he did say, though, was I want to do the things that I promised for the country.\par \par \uc2 Well, he promised to be truthful. He promised to be competent, and he promised to unify the country. And in that exact same poll, he is failing on those metrics as well.\par \par \uc2 So, I don't really know where you go from there, but it's a bit ugly for the Democratic Party. And that's why I think some partisans on that side are starting to look around and saying maybe we need to interchange some different options, looking all the way ahead to 2024? Not a great sign.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, that number that Guy was just referring to Kayleigh, how would you rate Joe Biden -- this is an NBC poll, I should point out on being competent and effective as president, 20 percent give him a very good rating. 42 percent give a very poor rating.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that strikes me is these are the same kinds of things that we heard voters saying during the election period that they wanted, right? They wanted calm, sort of competent, efficient leadership. And, you know, that was one of the major issues that we saw in the last election.\par \par \uc2 But now we're seeing in these numbers that they're watching nine months of this presidency, and they feel a little bit at sea. They don't feel like they're getting what they wanted.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes, that's exactly right. And I think the definite turning point for this presidency was Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 And when I say that, you know, some analysts say, well, foreign policy doesn't really sink into the souls of the American voter. But this did in a real way, because I think it fed into this general understanding or a general, I guess, discernment that this presidency is an incompetent presidency. Only 37 percent gave Joe Biden a four or a five on his ability to handle a crisis.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you, I go all across the country, from Oregon to Washington to Virginia, twice. And Afghanistan is one of the things I hear mainly from voters, it was far bigger than just the collapse of a country. It was the general feeling of an incompetent presidency.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: (INAUDIBLE) quickly here if you can, you know, Americans expect efficiency. We expect stuff to be on the shelves, we don't expect there to be huge backup at the ports, you expect, you know, monetary policy to work in terms of inflation and the border and all of that. And I think there's like an unsettling feeling right now, when people say, well, you know that this is the stuff that happens in other countries, like crazy lines at the airport and all kinds of things that you can't get your arms around and stuff doesn't work.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Joe Biden basically in the campaign against Donald Trump promised normalcy, right? He ran a basement campaign, he popped up occasionally, and he stayed out of sight, out of mind and he won.\par \par \uc2 Does this feel like normalcy right now? Most Americans say absolutely not. And you can't run a basement presidency as much as he might be trying.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Not when there's all this stuff going on. That's for sure. Guy, thank you. Guy Benson always great to see you. Kayleigh McEnany, always great to see you. Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: You bet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, so we are getting some brand-new feedback from the White House responding to Senator Joe Manchin, who says that he cannot support this President Biden spending bill without more clarity on how it would be paid for and he says he doesn't really care about deadlines. He wants to figure out how this will be paid for in an efficient way that they can sell to the country.\par \par \uc2 We're going to show you what the White House is now saying on this, which is very important on that beautiful day, beautiful fall day at the White House.\par \par \uc2 So, still ahead this hour, candidate for Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin on the eve of Election Day in Virginia as Democrat Terry McAuliffe fights to stave off the Republicans advanced. We will speak with Glenn Youngkin clearly a rising star in this Virginia race. We'll talk about how he's feeling tonight before all of this gets underway when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I guess it all depends on who you ask. Moments ago, Senator Joe Manchin said that he is "not ready to support President Biden's social spending bill" that's a paraphrase I should say. Even after the president cut the $3.5 trillion price tag in half.\par \par \uc2 Just a few moments ago, Senator Manchin stepped in front of the microphone. He said he needs to know more about what this bill will do to the economy and to the national debt. Here he is in his own words.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): What I see are shell games, budget gimmicks that make the real cost of the so-called $1.75 trillion bill estimated to be almost twice that amount, if the full time has run out if you extended it permanently.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the White House says that the plan is fully paid for, costs zero, you've heard this a lot. And it will reduce the deficit. They're confident they say that Senator Manchin will end up supporting the legislation.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now Republican congresswoman Nancy Mace from South Carolina. Good to have you with us this afternoon, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 It's interesting to note that we also just heard from Congresswoman Jayapal who said that she wants to get prescription pricing and immigration sorted out in the House bill on your side of the building there. And then she said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-WA): We'll be happy to vote both those bills, both the infrastructure bill and the Build Back Better bill through the House. And I'm hoping that can happen as soon as tomorrow or the next day. We are ready to get this transformational change to people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, sounds like there's going to be a vote in her mind. Is that what you're hearing?\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): We're not hearing that at all. And I think Congressman Jayapal is probably high as a kite right now.\par \par \uc2 Looking at what's going on with Senator Joe Manchin where just a few moments ago, finally, we have a Democrat on the Hill calling reconciliation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you mean she's high as a kite right now? What do you mean?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, they want to have additional immigration in the bill. We haven't had time to make all these modifications, amendments and edits to the spending because clearly, it's not good enough for progressives. I don't know when they're going to do that.\par \par \uc2 I don't see it happening and you have this huge race tomorrow in Virginia. I doubt you're going to get moderates on board if Youngkin wins the governor's race tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 This is a bellwether for the rest of the country on where the country is particularly on these kinds of very touchy issues where you have progressives and socialists against moderates, I don't think it happens.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you know, we know that Nancy Pelosi says all the time that she's not going to bring anything to the floor unless she believes that she can get an up -- yes vote on it.\par \par \uc2 And you know, we have heard Congresswoman Jayapal has been indicating, you know, since the weekend that she felt like this was moving towards a vote. So, if -- on both of the bills.\par \par \uc2 So, do you think that Nancy Pelosi will go forward if she thinks she can get yeses in the House on both of those? And how good do you think your feel is for how close they are to actually having a vote?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, I would never bet against Nancy Pelosi but twice now she's trying to bring both bills, or at least one of the bills or both bills to the floor two different times now and has failed, because the progressives and socialists cannot strike a deal with moderate Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Republicans are totally out in the cold on all of the above.\par \par \uc2 So, seeing what Senator Manchin's comments were today, I don't see how it happens. And he's right, I mean, this is in shell game, when you start digging in the kind of spending that they're talking about, there are limitations and dates on the spinning that end. But there would be need to be additional spending maybe two or three times as much to fulfill the programs that they're talking about.\par \par \uc2 So, it really is a shell game. It really is a gimmick. And finally, I'm really grateful that Senator Manchin is calling it what it is. Inflation will rise, taxes will go up on the middle class, jobs will leave the United States and now they want -- they want illegal immigrants to have mass amnesty, spending billions of dollars on that. I just -- I just it's not going to happen the way that it's going right now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, when you just referenced the election tomorrow, the biggest races that everybody's watching is Virginia, of course and New Jersey, keeping an eye on that as well. In fact, we're going to speak to a couple of those candidates in just a few moments.\par \par \uc2 But what impact you think that might have on the fortitude behind this agenda from the next day?\par \par \uc2 MACE: If Glenn Youngkin wins Virginia tomorrow, then reconciliation crumbles. It is no longer on the table. I don't know how you are a moderate Democrat and support the kind of spending that they're talking about when Republicans may very well and it's looking likely will win the governor's race in a Democrat seat for the first time in over a decade. It means the Build Back Better agenda is dead.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We will see. We'll watch it closely. Thank you for weighing in today. Congressman Mace, nice to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 MACE: As always.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we have a story exclusive this hour with the attorney for Dave Halls, who is the Assistant Director on Alec Baldwin's movie Rust where the tragedy happened just a short while -- short time ago. He handed the gun to Alec Baldwin, who then fired the gun and shot and killed the cinematographer on that set.\par \par \uc2 So, we're going to talk to his attorney because he said some very interesting things to the investigators. And we will ask her about that right after this. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The most strict abortion law in the United States faces two challenges today at the Supreme Court. The Texas law bans abortions after a heartbeat is detected usually around six weeks. It makes exceptions for medical emergencies, but not for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 The question today is not whether the law is constitutional but who has the standing to challenge that law.\par \par \uc2 "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT" anchor and Chief Legal Correspondent Shannon Bream was in the courtroom today watching all of this play out as the justices asked questions and discussed and she joins us live from the Supreme Court who better to answer these questions for us.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, what did you learn in there today?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF LEGAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, you're right, this debate today or the Texas law was much more about procedure than it was substance. But it's a huge deal that this case was fast tracked and put on the schedule today because the justices so far have declined to put that restrictive abortion law on hold, meaning, it is still in effect right now in Texas.\par \par \uc2 About three hours worth of arguments today and there were two groups including the \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2  and a group of abortion providers, they're arguing that they should be able to sue to challenge the law and shut it down.\par \par \uc2 Justice Kagan seemed very skeptical of the Texas laws' unique enforcement mechanism. It allows private citizens to be the ones who go out and bring lawsuits, not state officials or law enforcement. She asked whether this is trying to go around an important precedent.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELENA KAGAN, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, SUPREME COURT: And the fact that after all these many years, some geniuses came up with a way to evade the commands of that decision, as well as the command that the broader -- the even broader principle that states are not to nullify federal constitutional rights and to say, oh, we've never seen this before, so we can't do anything about it. I guess I just don't understand the argument.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The Justice Department seemed to have a tougher hurdle today. Several of the justices expressed concern about what would happen if any administration and any president decide to simply that the feds did not like a law, that the states, any state had passed, here's Justice Breyer.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN BREYER\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, SUPREME COURT: Is it that the federal government, no matter who's in charge, without a statute, whatever party, whatever president, can just go and intervene in any case, can bring a federal case whenever they think a state law affecting private people is unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, no word on when this fast track case when we could get a decision. I've expect it to be pretty quick.\par \par \uc2 Now, on December 1st, a few weeks from now, that's the really big abortion case on substance. That's Mississippi's law banning most abortions after 15 weeks. It will be held and heard here. That case, the ruling is due by the end of June, which of course, Martha, puts it right in the middle of the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, fascinating. Thank you so much, Shannon Bream at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 So, Election Day is upon us as you have seen us talking about this or the past several weeks, and we're going to talk about the two big races that are coming tomorrow. Big one in Virginia. Big one in New Jersey. We have two of the candidates from those races here, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting night for tomorrow night shaping up and one of the states that we're going to be watching very closely is New Jersey. Governor Phil Murphy ahead in the polls, but Republican Jack Ciattarelli may have history on his side, a Democrat governor has not won reelection in New Jersey since 1977.\par \par \uc2 We invited Governor Murphy, he's been on the show before, we invited him to come back and talk to us today. We hope that he'll take advantage of that open invitation to him.\par \par \uc2 But joining us right now is his Republican challenger Jack Ciattarelli who is with us today. Sir, thank you very much. It's good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 You know, 24 hours away here. Where do you think this race is going? We've seen some polls that are tight. And then we see the one that's got you at 39 percent to his 50 right now, how do you see this shaping up?\par \par \uc2 JACK CIATTARELLI (R), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE OF NEW JERSEY: Internal polls, Martha, out of both camps has it as a dead heat. I like our positioning, we're surging. The energy momentum up and down the state as I barnstorm all 21 counties have been phenomenal. We're going to continue to do over the next 24 hours what we've done for the -- done for the past 22 months and I'm looking forward to the outcome.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know taxes has been one of the big issues. You say that Governor Murphy said basically, if taxes are your issue, we're not your state.\par \par \uc2 And you know, we do hear a lot of voters saying that they think it's really expensive to live in New Jersey, which it is and taxes are the number one issue at 27 percent. But he's also tried to paint you as too conservative for New Jersey, which is obviously generally a blue state and sort of purpley at times, but he says that you're against raising the minimum wage. He says you voted against legalizing weed. He wants to loosen the gun laws, he said about you, and that you waffle on whether or not you'll even protect women's health. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 JACK CIATTARELLI, CANDIDATE FOR NEW JERSEY GOVERNOR: Well, if it was up to Governor Murthy, Martha. We mortgage our future with unnecessary borrowing and then we all go home and get high. And he thinks that what New Jersey is all about, it's not.\par \par \uc2 Property taxes is an issue, the worst business climate in the nation, particularly for small business is an issue. A bloated, inefficient state government corrupted by special interests as an issue, a lack of support for police. He's been the most anti-cop governor we've ever had. And then, of course, we want to get our public school curriculum back on track. He's made some drastic changes to the public school curriculum that is of great concern to great many parents across the state.\par \par \uc2 He's the extremist. He's more extreme than Jim Florio, Jim McGreevey and John Corzine combined, and none of them were reelected. He's not going to be reelected tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It'd be interesting to watch. One of the things obviously that's such a hot topic. And we see the closures are the temporary shuttering of fire departments here in New York City. There was sort of an undercover tape done that suggested that somebody in his camp said, we won't put in vaccine mandates until right after the election. Here's what Governor Murphy's folks said about that. Listen,\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PHIL MURPHY, NEW JERSEY GOVERNOR: We have no plans to do that, in terms of mandate. I'm not going to "commit" to something because, as I said, the virus has taken so many turns that none of us expected. It's very humbling. But we have zero, I have zero expectation that's going to happen. We just don't, at the moment, we just don't need to.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What about that?\par \par \uc2 CIATTARELLI: New Jersey is very scared, Martha. This guy isn't being transparent with us. It wouldn't be the first time. He wasn't transparent with us with regard to what happened in nursing homes. New Jersey leads the nation in nursing home deaths because he didn't follow the CDC advice not to send COVID-19 patients there.\par \par \uc2 So it's one of the things that certainly driving the turnout and I think the energy. The fact that they feel it, Phil Murphy is not being transparent with them about what his true intentions are. I've been clear. I'm vaccinated. I promoted my vaccination. I encourage people to get vaccinated. But I'm not going to force a vaccine mandate on any adult or any child here in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it'll be interesting to see what happens tomorrow, Jack Ciattarelli, Thank you very much. Good to have you here today, sir.\par \par \uc2 CIATTARELLI: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we are also going to be joined in moments by Virginia Republican candidate for governor Glenn Youngkin, who is out storming in the barnstorming in the southwestern part of the state. Today, we'll ask him why he's focused there right now, as he looks at tomorrow's big election.\par \par \uc2 But next, an exclusive that you don't want to miss with the attorney for this man, Dave Halls, he was the assistant director on the movie "Rust." He handed the gun to Alec Baldwin that killed crew member Halyna Hutchins. What his attorney wants to tell you about what happened that day when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILARIA BALDWIN, ALEC BALDWIN'S WIFE: You, guys -- what know what, no details.\par \par \uc2 ALEC BALDWIN, ACTOR: I'm mean -- do me a favor, I'm going to ask the question.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, I appreciate that he was probably very upset.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: The guy is overwhelmed with grief. This is something that that, you know, there are incidental accidents on film sets from time to time, but nothing like this. This is a one in a trillion episode, so one in a trillion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Alec Baldwin making his first comments on camera about the movie set shooting that killed cinematographer Halyna Hutchins. Baldwin is also a producer of the movie "Rust" and he defended the crew.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: There are incidental accidents on film sets from time to time, but nothing. This is a one trillion episode, so one in a trillion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting appearance by he and his wife yesterday by him and his wife yesterday. But the LA Times reports that there were three other accidental shootings on this movie set including a woman from the props department shooting herself in the foot. In Baldwin's case, investigators say the assistant director admitted that he should have checked the gun more carefully before saying it was cold, meaning that it was safe, that it had no live rounds in it. The lawyer for assistant director Dave Halls joins me exclusively in a moment, but first to chief correspondent Jonathan Hunt reporting live from LA. Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Martha it's perhaps not surprising that Alec Baldwin and his wife Hilaria seemed tense after they stopped on a rural road in Vermont to talk to journalists looking for Baldwin's perspective on the day he shot and killed cinematographer Halyna Hutchins on the set of the movie "Rust."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: She was my friend. She was my friend. The day I arrived in Santa Fe to start shooting, I took her to dinner with Joel, the director. We were a very, very -- excuse me. We were a very, very, you know, well-oiled crew shooting a film together and then this horrible event happened.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Now, Baldwin has described the shooting as an accident. The LA Times reported this weekend that when the gun went off, he repeatedly asked what just happened. And he told reporters in Vermont he is cooperating with investigators and in regular contact with the Santa Fe sheriff.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: We're eagerly awaiting for the Sheriff's Department to tell us what their investigation has yielded.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Now the LA Times also reported what it says are heard were Halyna Hutchins last words. According to people on the set at the time she apparently said "that was no good, that was no good at all" after being hit by the bullet.\par \par \uc2 The Santa Fe district attorney told us last week Alec Baldwin could yet face criminal charges. He is one of three people investigators want to question further. The others are the armorer, Hannah Gutierrez-Reed, who in a statement released by her attorney blamed lacks safety standards onset. And perhaps most critically, they want to talk to the assistant director, David Halls, once again, who investigators say when they first talked to him, admitted he did not properly check the gun before handing it to Baldwin. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jonathan, thank you very much. So, assistant director David Halls' attorney Lisa Torraco joins me now exclusively. Lisa, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, this is a very tragic situation and you heard in Jonathan's report that what we have learned from the investigation, the early discussion that Mr. Halls had with the investigators, he said that he should have checked, he should have checked that gun more thoroughly to make sure that there were no live rounds in it. Do you stand by that statement of your client?\par \par \uc2 LISA TORRACO, ASSISTANT DIRECTOR DAVID HALLS' LAWYER: Well, before we start on that note, I just want to say that this tragedy is overwhelming to all of us. I mean, it's deeply saddening to my client, it's overwhelming to our community here, the film community, and our condolences go out to the Hutchins family. And I think it's extra hard for my client, because not only is he so overwhelmed with sadness, but now the target of the investigation. People are starting to point fingers at him, and it's overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Isn't it true that there had been a couple of other incidences on other sets where his oversight was questioned in other movies, and will that be relevant in this investigation?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, let me go back to your very first question. And your first question had to do with the responsibility. Anyone to start with the fact that in the affidavits, it states that my client grabbed the gun off of a prop cart, and handed it to Baldwin. That absolutely did not happen. That absolutely did not happen.\par \par \uc2 In addition to me presenting not only what Mr. Halls has told me, but we, our defense team, has interviewed witnesses and some of those witnesses that we've talked to, when we talked to them, they hadn't even been interviewed by the police yet. And those witnesses also confirm that they remember that the armorer, or armors assistant brought in the firearm, brought it on to the set. So this idea that my client grabbed the gun off of a prop cart and handed it to Mr. Baldwin, absolutely did not happen. And I think the needs to be straight right away.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So what you're saying is -- hold on, let me just pause on that for a second. So are you saying that Hannah Gutierrez-Reed handed the gun to him or that another assistant handed the gun to him? And if so, who handed the gun to him?\par \par \uc2 TORRACO: OK. One of the problems that some of the witnesses have had is that most of the crew knew each other, but not all of the crew knew the armorers. So the armorers were relatively new to this particular crew. And both of the armorer and the assistant armorer were women in their 20s. And so some of the witnesses are getting confused whether the armorer came in or the assistant armorer.\par \par \uc2 But regardless whether it was the armorer that brought the firearm on the set, or the assistant armorer that brought the firearm on set, my client did not bring the firearm --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But the point is that someone handed it to him -- did someone -- does he know who handed him the gun before he handed it to Alec Baldwin?\par \par \uc2 TORRACO: Well, I think you're twisting the facts just a little bit. And let me just back up just a little bit if I may. The armorer brings the gun in. And another really important fact that we found out is that there was another member of the crew who also checked the firearm. Because when Mr. Halls gives a safety meeting, he announces to the group, we're going to, you know, there's going to be a firearm on set. Everyone has the right to inspect the firearm as soon as it comes on set before we begin to rehearse or begin to shoot.\par \par \uc2 And so, there was another crew member who our team has spoken to who confirms that he also checked the firearm at the time when the armorer brought the firearm,\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. But I need to just nail something down here. Did Dave Halls hand the gun to Alec Baldwin? That's a very straightforward question. Did he hand the gun that was used and that killed Halyna Hutchins? Did he hand it to Alec Baldwin or not?\par \par \uc2 TORRACO: Here's, here's what I'm trying to tell you. When I answer a question, I want to make sure that it's consistent with all of the facts. I don't want to answer a question to you and later be proven wrong. So we've tried -- that's why we haven't spoken out for over a week because I want to make sure that I've got the facts right.\par \par \uc2 I have received information from crew members that the armorer handed it directly to Baldwin. And then, Baldwin put it inside where his holster would be. And then at some point, he pulled the firearm out and he wanted to adjust the holster. And then he hands the firearm to Mr. Halls, who immediately hands it right back after he's adjusted the holster.\par \par \uc2 Other witnesses have said that the armorer brought the firearm in, that they checked the firearm, that my client checked the firearm. There was another crew member that checked the firearm. And that she handed it to him like a pass through. And that he then handed immediately over to Baldwin because he was between the two.\par \par \uc2 So I can't tell you verbatim what happened. These people are overwhelmed by the grief and the shock. My client went through something that was such a freak accident that he's in shock. I mean, he's having a hard time sorting.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You're saying he doesn't know whether or not he handed the gun to Alec Baldwin? Is that what you're telling us right now? He doesn't know if he handed it to him?\par \par \uc2 TORRACO: No, of course. No, I'm not saying that. But what I'm saying is I want to put all the facts together.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, but you're --\par \par \uc2 TORRACO: I want to deal with evidence.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You said you cannot confirm that it didn't go directly from the armorer to Baldwin. So that's what -- this is a very direct question. Did your client hand the gun to Alec Baldwin right before he fired the gun and shot Halyna Hutchins? Did he hand it to him? Was he the last person to touch it before Baldwin?\par \par \uc2 TORRACO: First of all, I wasn't there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, what did he tell you. What did he tell you.\par \par \uc2 TORRACO: -- what my client remembers --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- about what happened. Did he say I handed it to Alec Baldwin? Or did he say I didn't hand it to him?\par \par \uc2 TORRACO: I've explained what has happened on set. The armorer brought the firearm into the scene. The armorer is responsible to make sure the firearm is safe. The armorer opens the revolver, open surround, my client didn't load the firearm. My client didn't point the firearm at anyone and my client didn't pull the trigger.\par \par \uc2 So the armorer comes in, the armorer opens the firearm, my client looks at it. And one of the other crew members also checks it. Whether or not he handed the firearm directly to Alec Baldwin at that moment, or whether the armorer handed it directly to Alec Baldwin at that moment doesn't really matter because he didn't load it. He's not response --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. That can be your perspective on it but, you know, what we're hearing from all these other armorers and specialists who handle weapons is that every single person in the chain of custody of the weapon has the responsibility to spin it, to look at it, to check it from Alec Baldwin, to the assistant director to the armorer.\par \par \uc2 Now, you're leaving a lot of, I mean, I understand why is his attorney you're leaving, you know things as sort of open as you can as this investigation goes forward. But there's also testimony from the investigators that said that he advised them that he should have checked it. Can you confirm or deny that part of the story before I let you go?\par \par \uc2 TORRACO: OK. What I can tell you is that expecting an assistant director to check a firearm is like telling the assistant director to check the camera angle, or telling the assistant director to check sound or lighting. That's not the assistant director's job. If he chooses to check the firearm because he wants to make sure make sure that everyone's safe, he can do that but that's not his responsibility.\par \par \uc2 It's just like he's not the camera operator. He's not the sound operator. He's not the armorer. So that I disagree with the other reports that are coming out. And I think that this is such a tragedy. This is something that is a deviation of norm. Yes, it's awful.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We got to leave it there. I hope you'll come back because, you know, we obviously have conflicting reports here. And conflicting reports about the responsibility of the assistant director, and what his duty is with regard to these weapons on the set, and why there would even be something on the set.\par \par \uc2 This was not a prop gun. This was a real gun. So why that would even be allowed on the set, why any live ammo would be allowed anywhere near the set, all our questions for your client, for the assistant director on the set. So obviously we're just at the beginning of this investigation, I hope you'll come back as we move forward. Thank you very much, Lisa. Thanks for coming in.\par \par \uc2 TORRACO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Appreciate it. All right. Live look at Richmond, Virginia where Democrat Terry McAuliffe is campaigning before the big day tomorrow, now trailing by two points behind Republican Glenn Youngkin as he gets ready to take the podium there. Mr. Youngkin will join us live as both candidates try to fit in as much as they can today in the sprint to the finish when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Probably the biggest race that we are watching tomorrow night is the battle to for the Virginia governor's seat. Democrat Terry McAuliffe is campaigning right now in Richmond. There's a live shot of the scene where he's spending his time in the last hours here. And he's fallen behind in our latest Fox News poll., interesting to look at all the polls out there. And you see that Youngkin has pulled ahead. In several of them, McAuliffe ahead and a couple. McAuliffe now trails Glenn Youngkin by eight points in this Fox News poll among the likely voters in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 McAuliffe was leading by five just two weeks ago. We invited him here today to speak to us on "The Story." His campaign said that he could not make it. Glenn Youngkin is here joining make us live in just a moment but first a correspondent Rich Edson reporting live from Fredericksburg, Virginia on the campaign trail. Hi, Rich\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good afternoon, Martha. We're waiting on television on Terry McAuliffe to head to the northern part of the state. He spent plenty of time up here. Right now he's down in Virginia. He's running a little bit behind schedule waiting to take the stage at a brewery in that area. He spent earlier today in Roanoke in the central part of the state.\par \par \uc2 This is how McAuliffe is ending it. His schedule is taking him to more of the Democratic areas of the state. He's trying to turn out the Democratic vote. He's been trying to paint his opponent Glenn Youngkin as extreme on social and economic issues. And while he denied to us that he was trying to make this campaign a referendum on former President Donald Trump, McAuliffe seems to mention Trump at nearly every event.\par \par \uc2 Now, the former president lost Virginia by more than 10 percentage points last year, and now McAuliffe finds himself either essentially tied or behind depending on the poll. National Democrats have fanned out across the street. They're trying to boost McAuliffe's chances here. Last week, it was President Biden and Vice President Kamala Harris. Over the weekend, Congressional Democrats joined up like House Majority Whip Jim Clyburn and Senator Tim Kaine.\par \par \uc2 Now, there's another dynamic here, Martha, and that is early in-person voting. It ended on Saturday but it started in mid-September, and about a fifth of the electorate has already cast their votes, much of that time, the polls have changed dramatically. In the beginning, it was a McAuliffe lead. And as you mentioned, those polls have either tightened or McAuliffe is now losing depending on which one you look at. So that's another dynamic with a fifth of the electorate already having voted before election day here with this changing polling situation in the race. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That is a great point, Rich, thank you very much. And that's exactly where I want to start with Glenn Youngkin, who joins us now. As I mentioned, we invited Chairman McAuliffe as well and we are here if he wants to hop on and talk to us as well. Mr. Youngkin, good to see you today.\par \par \uc2 You heard Rich Edson's report. He talks about the early vote, which went all the way back to September 16th people started voting, and about a fifth of the Virginia electorate already voted before you started to take off in the polls. Are you concerned that that could sort of throw things in Terry McAuliffe direction tomorrow night?\par \par \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN, CANDIDATE FOR VIRGINIA GOVERNOR: No. I felt like the early voting was very much representative of our strength. In fact, turnout for my opponent was really light and soft and we had strong turnout, and so we expect to do very well with the early voting. And the momentum that we're seeing on the campaign trail is just huge, I got to be honest. Everywhere we go, the crowds are giant and we have had this 10-day bus door. We've been all over the Commonwealth. And we've been standing room only as people fallen out the doors.\par \par \uc2 And it's so exciting to watch Virginians come together because it's no longer Republicans against Democrats. This is Virginians coming together for a platform that they can believe in, with low taxes and safe schools, and safe neighborhoods, and education that works for our children and, by the way, a job market that's really growing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, we've seen those crowds. It's pretty evident on the video when we see those crowds, but I think it's at 51% of the early vote is Democrat. So why do you say that you feel like, you know, you would have done well in that early portion of the vote?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, historically, early voting has been, you know, 75% to 80% Democrat. And so, for us to be so strong in the early voting just reflects the fact that there's not any enthusiasm on my opponent side, and we've had a lot of enthusiasm. And then we expect to do really well, tomorrow. You know, Virginians are coming together and this is why we see so much momentum going into tomorrow, but also the early voting is going to be, I think, surprisingly good for us.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll see. I mean, it's fascinating to watch. Here's what Terry McAuliffe said in the closing hours here. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCAULIFFE, CANDIDATE FOR VIRGINIA GOVERNOR: We don't want a Trump wannabe, someone who's been endorsed by Donald Trump 10 times tonight, closing his campaign is Donald Trump. I mean, we don't need the hatred, the Division.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Teller rally by the former president, are you -- as he says closing your campaign with President Trump?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: We've had help from everybody and there is a teleconference tonight that that President Trump is hosting and we're going to be off holding a rally in Loudoun County. We've been getting help from everybody. We had governors from all over the United States hosting tella calls yesterday to get the vote out from Idaho and Arizona, and South Carolina, and Iowa. It was fabulous to see the whole Republican Party come together\par \par \uc2 And oh, by the way to see Independents, we're winning, we're winning the independent vote by double digits. We've got Democrats walking across the aisle. This is what unity looks like. And this is why we're going to win on Tuesday, but we got to get the vote out. Polls don't win elections votes do, and so we're encouraging everybody to get out and vote.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And I know you've been doing that in the southwest part of the state today. It's been fun to watch this race, and we look forward to covering it tomorrow. Glenn Youngkin, thank you very much, sir. Good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Thank you so much. You can follow us on youngkinforgov.com and we're going to be in Virginia Beach. And we're going to be, tonight, in Loudoun County.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, we'll be in Loudoun County tomorrow doing our show. Thank you very much, sir. We'll be watching closely.\par \par \uc2 That is THE STORY of this Monday, November 1st. Tomorrow is Election Day, THE STORY goes on. We'll see you there in Loudoun County which has been the center of so much of the debate and discussion in Virginia. So watch for us tomorrow and tomorrow night with Bret Baier election coverage. We'll see you then.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020211102ehb100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Virginia Gubernatorial Nominees Final Campaign Push; Education Top Concern In Virginia Race; Democrats Make Final Push For Black Votes; Experts View Virginia Gubernatorial Race; Joe Biden Highlights Climate Investments In Stalled Bill; Supreme Court Hears Texas Abortion Law Challenge; West Virginia Democratic Senator Joe Manchin Criticizes Framework For Senate Reconciliation Bill; Polls Show Close Race In Virginia Gubernatorial Election Between Republican Glenn Youngkin And Democrat \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2 ; California's Homelessness Problem Examined; Falling Poll Numbers For President Biden Possibly Affecting Democratic Prospects In Virginia Gubernatorial Race\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Chad Pergram, Laura Ingraham, Marc Thiessen, Leslie  Marshall, Kimberley Strassel, Rich Edson, Mo Elleithee, Jacqui Heinrich \par \uc2 7,893 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD J.R., FOX NEWS CHANNEL POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): (INAUDIBLE) guy, didn't have to do this. Donating all of his salary. Made me feel good to see that and read that story yesterday.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Yes, but he yells at all the kids. Did you hear about that?\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : I don't --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Screams at --\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): We need it. It's good.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's right. All right, that is it for us. "SPECIAL REPORT" -- why are you laughing so much? You got to get it together.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): I am sorry.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next with Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Hey, Jesse, we've got a big election here in Virginia. So, I'm outside. It means --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thank you for explaining.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. All right, good evening. I'm Bret Baier. We are coming to you tonight from Leesburg, Virginia, the hub of Loudoun County, the county that might tell us who the next governor will be here in the Commonwealth.\par \par \uc2 Later tonight, Republican Glenn Youngkin will wrap up what he hopes will be a successful come-from-behind campaign against Democrat \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is on the world stage trying to sell his climate change policy, while back home, one moderate Senator, a Democrat, is pumping the brakes on the president's tax and spend social agenda. We'll bring you that.\par \par \uc2 But the race here in Virginia has centered in recent weeks on the issue of education and ties to current and former presidents. Those themes continued in this final day of campaigning. McAuliffe has been in Roanoke and Richmond so far today. He finishes up later in Fairfax.\par \par \uc2 Youngkin was also in Roanoke and Richmond along with a stop in Virginia Beach. He'll be here in Leesburg later this evening. We'll have analysis from our political panel on this race shortly. We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE" tonight.\par \par \uc2 Rich Edson\uc2  is in Fairfax, Virginia with the McAuliffe campaign. But we begin with correspondent Alexandria Hoff with us here in Leesburg, Virginia. Good evening, Alexandria.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. This final rally, its location, its emphasis, it's really a full-circle moment for Glenn Youngkin, who found his footing on the issue of education.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): Tonight, Loudoun Parents Matter Rally here in Leesburg comes on the heels of a 40 plus stop bus tour in 38 counties. But it is here in Loudoun County where many have watched fiery parent versus school board debates play out.\par \par \uc2 Youngkin's message of fighting what he sees as government overreach in schools helped him gain momentum statewide that seemed unlikely early on.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE RUDD, YOUGKIN SUPPORTER: When you have the school systems in jeopardy like they are, you know, if you don't want to inject racism, you should not have it in the -- have it as a subject into school or anywhere else as far as that goes. If you don't want hate, don't teach it.\par \par \uc2 Everybody's upset around here because of the way the school systems --\par \par \uc2 HOFF: And that is a sentiment we have been hearing over and over again. According to the latest Washington Post poll, education has become the number one issue for voters in Virginia. That is above the economy and COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 Today, Youngkin said voter enthusiasm is on his side.\par \par \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN (R), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE, VIRGINIA: Tomorrow, Virginians will go to the polls, and we will make a decision about our future. And let me tell you what's going to happen. We are going to sweep our statewide offices.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: So, if that were to happen, Youngkin would become the first Republican elected statewide in office in Virginia in 12 years, likely helping to write the GOP playbook for running midterm races across the country.\par \par \uc2 Now, absent on this race, former President Trump. He will be holding a tele-rally this evening. But Youngkin is not taking part in that. He has made it a point of keeping big names off of the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, his Democratic opponent \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2 , has taken a different approach. For more on that side, we turn it over to Fox News's \uc2 Rich Edson\uc2 . He is live in Fairfax tonight. Hi, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hey, good evening, Alex. And \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  will have the final event of his election-eve here in Northern Virginia, where he spent a good deal of time campaigning.\par \par \uc2 This is Fairfax, Virginia at a beer garden. They're still setting up the event here. As you mentioned, Northern Virginia is still that area of the state where they had some of those contentious school board meetings as education has become a major issue in this race.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the recent Washington Post poll has found that \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  standing among education voters has fallen dramatically in just the past couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): He's been closing his campaign hitting the Democratic areas of the state, trying to get his supporters to the polls.\par \par \uc2 Just the past few days, he's been in Roanoke, Richmond, the suburbs of Richmond, Hampton, and the Norfolk area. And around here in Northern Virginia. While McAuliffe denied to us he was trying to turn this race into a referendum on former President Trump. He has been mentioning Trump at nearly every stop, including just a couple of hours ago when he borrowed a line from President Biden last week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCAULIFFE\uc2  (D), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE, VIRGINIA: I am running against -- I like to say \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and khakis, or sweater vest. What is he going to do with all those sweater vests at the end of this campaign? But Trump has now endorsed him for the 10th-time today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Several Democratic activists have warned about turnout in this election, especially among African American voters. One of his most high- profile surrogates is South Carolina Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\par \par \uc2 An aide close to Clyburn tells us that he accepted the invitation to campaign for McAuliffe because he knows the black vote is a huge factor in Virginia, and Clyburn wanted to do everything he could to get out that vote.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): A fifth of the electorate has already voted in early in- person voting that some 1.1 million returns. McAuliffe says all of that early voting gives him the early advantage. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hey, Rich, you know, there's a sound bite from McAuliffe that he's been making on the campaign trail actually many times that's getting some pushback. Let's take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCAULIFFE: In Virginia, 1,142 of our precious children have gone to ICU beds and hospital beds with COVID. We just had two 11-year olds die of COVID -- just happened.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. So, according to the \uc2 Virginia Department of Health\uc2 's web site, through October 23rd, you've got 134,000 kids cases, 976 hospitalizations, 10 deaths. That's it since the beginning of COVID. What's the campaign saying about this?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: The campaign has no comment on that, Bret. And the Washington Post looked into this last week and got a response from the campaign that said that sometimes he tends to misspoke or misspeak on the campaign trail when it comes to these COVID statistics that the Washington Post found that it just happens way too often that he tends to inflate them.\par \par \uc2 And as The Post characterizes it, wildly inflated figures for child hospitalizations, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Rich. We'll follow it. Final hours here. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our political panel to get a sense of where things are. Mo Elleithee is executive director, Georgetown Institute of Politics, and former White House Chief of Staff.\par \par \uc2 Former Republican Party chief Reince Priebus joins us as well. All right, gentlemen, where do you see this race? Reince, first to you.\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS, FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: Well, it's going to be very, very close. Obviously, both sides are debating where we're at an early voting. I think most people agree -- obviously, there's been about 1.1 million people that have voted.\par \par \uc2 The average of what people think is going to turn out tomorrow is going to be two more million people maybe a little bit more than that. Most people think that \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  has an early vote, absentee ballot advantage anywhere from 100 to 250,000 votes, let's say 150,000. Take the middle part -- middle ground.\par \par \uc2 That would mean that Glenn Youngkin would need about 54-55 percent of the turnout tomorrow to win. That's very doable for the Republican on Election Day. Glenn Youngkin. Has the momentum in this race.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, Independents are going to Youngkin. He's got the enthusiasm. And the messaging seems to be flowing Youngkin's way on education, on taxes, on all of the things that are being debated right now, Glenn has that momentum.\par \par \uc2 And now, he's got the late money rush too. So, when you have the message, you've got the momentum, you've got the money -- by the way, on this turnout issue for tomorrow, 55 percent is not a huge number for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  got over 60 percent of the vote on Election Day. Much different. Much different, because a lot of people voted early. I get it. But things do look pretty good for Glenn Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 But cautiously optimistic, RNC chairwoman has done a great job. I think it's going to be very, very, very close.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Mo, obviously this election like others will see a lot of mail in ballots. The early vote is a big deal here. And you're over 20 percent of the Commonwealth already voting early. Where do you see ground zero here as your head until tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 MO ELLEITHEE, FOX NEWS POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): Yes, and I think I would agree with just about everything Reince said in terms of where we are. I do think that early vote number is critical. And I do think McAuliffe has a pretty big advantage. The numbers I've seen show that he has a -- that Democrats have a bigger advantage in the early vote this time than they did in the last governor's race. And there are a lot more votes.\par \par \uc2 So, that I think is going to matter quite a bit. But I think it's going to be close tomorrow. I think there's a couple of areas to watch. You're in one of them, Bret. Loudoun County is going to be pivotal. It is a purple County, and it's gone to Democrats for a couple of elections in a row. But by no means is it a Democratic stronghold, and that seems to be ground zero.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to be looking at a couple of other key areas. I'm going to be looking at the Richmond suburbs, Henrico County and Chesterfield County.\par \par \uc2 Not long ago, both of those were solidly red. Henrico is solidly blue now, but Chesterfield can go either way. And so, what those numbers look like? I'm going to be looking at Virginia Beach which is also a pivotal jurisdiction.\par \par \uc2 And what's the turnout?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELLEITHEE: At the end of the night going to look like in Fairfax County. Largest county in the state, about as big of a Democratic stronghold. If turnout is high there, that's a good thing for McAuliffe. If it is low, it's going to be a longer night.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll explain, you know, how this state -- this Commonwealth breaks down. And a lot of times Republicans obviously rely on the southern part of the state to turn out big numbers, but there aren't bigger cities down there.\par \par \uc2 So, around Washington, D.C. has been a Democratic stronghold, Reince, but it seems like this education issue has broken through and has kind of chipped away at some of those suburban voters. At least that's according to polls.\par \par \uc2 But here is the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  in this editorial. "Recent school board wars have been a battle of subcultures. A lot of parents have trouble knowing what's going on in their kids' classrooms. When they challenge what they sense is happening, they meet a few common responses. They are told, as by Virginia's Democratic gubernatorial candidate that parents shouldn't tell schools want to teach. They are told they are racist. Or they are blindly assured that there is nothing radical going on -- when, in fact, there might be. When people sense that those culture power -- cultural power are imposing ideologies on their own families, you can expect the reaction will be swift and fierce."\par \par \uc2 Is that what's happening here, Reince? Is that why some of these blue strongholds are being chipped away at?\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: Well, I think so. But, you know, what Mo said is also I agree. You know, look, when you're talking about how Loudoun County comes down, \uc2 Ed Gillespie\uc2  in 2017 got more votes than any Republican in a statewide race ever got, but he still lost by 20,000.\par \par \uc2 Glenn Youngkin, if he wins, Loudoun County, it's over. If Glenn Youngkin gets one-third of Fairfax County, maybe needs 36-37 percent, Fairfax County, Glenn Youngkin wins.\par \par \uc2 But what's happening to your question, Bret, is that these school issues that the mask and the -- and the -- and the vaccine mandates, and who's teaching our kids and CRT? What's happened is it's nationalizing this race.\par \par \uc2 And what's -- what -- this is why it's important for tomorrow. Because all the early vote is over. McAulliffe's got a slight advantage there. But if the 2020 electorate comes out, meaning the presidential vote people, not the governor voting people, but the people who only vote in a presidential election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: If they turn out -- and this is what juices up that vote. That means that Republicans that normally wouldn't bother in a Virginia governor's race, they turn out. That's why that issue is deadly for \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Mo, I got 20 seconds here.\par \par \uc2 ELLEITHEE: Yes, look, for that level of turnout to happen -- I mean, look, I don't -- \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was able to draw out Republicans that no other Republican was able to draw out.\par \par \uc2 Youngkin has had a tortured relationship here with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Trying to juice his supporters when the cameras aren't on, but keeping arm's length when they are on.\par \par \uc2 So, I question whether or not he is going to be able to excite the Republican base the way it happened in 2020. But McAuliffe is making sure that every Democrat gets excited by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's shadow on this race.\par \par \uc2 So, I -- look --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELLEITHEE: I think it's a roll of the dice, and I think Reince and I are going to be up late tomorrow, poring over these numbers. It's going to be an exciting night.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, hey, we're excited. We're excited.\par \par \uc2 ELLEITHEE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mo, Reince, we appreciate -- we appreciate it. We'll see you tomorrow and see how it all turns out. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden says climate change is an existential threat to human existence, and the cost of inaction increases every day, in his words. He's at the U.N. Climate Change Conference in Scotland.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich is in Edinburgh tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I guess I shouldn't apologize, but I do apologize for the fact the United States, in the last administration, pulled out of the Paris accords and it put us further behind the eight ball.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): President Biden laid blame on the Trump administration for the U.S. not being farther ahead on climate goals. But empty-handed on legislative action himself, Biden instead touted plans in his spending bills stalled again on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We will incentivize clean energy manufacturing, building the solar panels and the wind turbines that are growing energy markets of the future, which create good-paying union jobs for American workers.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Biden's remarks to foreign leaders sounding equally geared toward Democrats in Washington whose votes he needs. The White House says the $555 billion in proposed climate spending would cut greenhouse gas emissions by a gigaton by 2030, while bringing down energy costs, fortifying homes and businesses against natural disaster, and creating jobs.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're standing at an inflection point in world history.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Republican support is out of the question.\par \par \uc2 REP. JOHN CURTIS (R-UT): If we follow the Republican path, we don't need to kill U.S. jobs. We don't need to export our jobs overseas and subject ourselves to our enemies.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Neither Russia nor China, respectively the first and fourth biggest emitters worldwide attended the summit.\par \par \uc2 President Xi delivered a promise over video with no significant new pledges. Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  remotely announced plans to reach carbon neutrality 10 years after scientists desired target date.\par \par \uc2 The GOP also quick to call out hypocrisy of the trip. President Biden's motorcade in Rome was 85 cars long and the U.K. Foreign Minister estimated some 400 private planes flew into Glasgow.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA): Look at the carbon footprint of this Glasgow Summit where they're talking about global warming. And all of them are flying over there in jets, they could have Zoom this thing in.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The White House also touted new agreements to increase climate support for developing countries and halt international investment in coal. But much of it is being overshadowed on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  by video of Biden closing his eyes for a lot longer than a blink, prompting speculation that he was falling asleep.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): And that is the last we'll see of President Biden today ahead of a press conference in Glasgow tomorrow. And here's to hoping we get a question in after he appeared to have a select list of reporters he was instructed to call on in Rome, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live in Scotland. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were up today. The new record closes for all three major markets. The Dow gaining 94, the S&P 500 finishing ahead eight, the NASDAQ jumped 98 today.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  hears arguments over a controversial abortion law in Texas. We'll have a live report from the court.\par \par \uc2 But first, as we head to break throughout the show, we'll talk to voters here in the Commonwealth of 