r \uc2 A special advisory panel to the CDC meets tomorrow to reconsider and consider a recommendation from the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to go ahead with the vaccines.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  justices are weighing arguments tonight on a controversial abortion ban in Texas. It's the start of what could be a pivotal period in the decades long fight over abortion.\par \par \uc2 Fox News Chief Legal Correspondent anchor of "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT" Shannon Bream reports from the Court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The Supreme Court's fast track fight over S.B. 8 Texas has banned on most abortions after roughly six weeks into a pregnancy sparked nearly three hours worth of arguments Monday as both a group of abortion providers and the \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2  argued to convince the justices that they have the legal right to sue to stop the law.\par \par \uc2 Today's arguments were primarily procedural in nature, mostly because of the law's unique enforcement mechanism, giving private citizens and not state officials the power to enforce the laws ban on most abortions after a fetal heartbeat is detected.\par \par \uc2 Justice Kagan questioned whether the law was specifically crafted to get around relevant Supreme Court precedent the case Ex parte Young.\par \par \uc2 ELENA KAGAN, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, SUPREME COURT: Some geniuses came up with a way to evade the commands of that decision, as well as the command that the broader -- the even broader principle that states are not to nullify federal constitutional rights and to say, oh, we've never seen this before, so we can't do anything about it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A majority of justices seem to be leaning in favor of giving the abortion providers the ability to sue, but the Justice Department's claim seemed much less likely to succeed. Here's Justice Breyer.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN BREYER\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, S