e Critical Race Theory, and honestly, church. I'm a follower of Jesus. Everything I do is faith-based. I just pray that a leader like Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It bothers me that Democrats sometimes are lackadaisical. They don't come out and vote during the off-year elections. And that really bothers me, where the Republicans seem like they are always motivated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to Leesburg, Virginia. We are here covering the governor's race tomorrow. And there is a lot happening here as far as people coming out for both campaigns.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Laura Ingraham, host of THE INGRAHAM ANGLE, weeknights 10:00 p.m. eastern here on FOX News. We're going to talk about your special in a bit. But Virginia, this is happening here. There's a lot of people engaged.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: We were laughing, because on the way out to Leesburg we drove from D.C. through McLean, Virginia. McLean, Virginia, Fairfax County, it's a blue electorate there. Overwhelming voted for Biden. There were parents, kids, it was like a party. Was it Halloween or is this Youngkin? But there is an enormous amount of energy. You kind of feel it. You get a sense. You've lived around the area for a long time, Bret. I went to school at UVA, law school. I lived on and off in Virginia for almost 25 years. This is different. And Youngkin has run a really smart campaign, I think.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So that issue of education that we were talking about earlier with Mo Elleithee Reince Priebus. It does seem to cross party lines. It does seem to especially hit suburbs and parents who are concerned no matter what party they're a part of.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The one group that the Democrats could not afford to alienate were the suburban women. They were critical to their election victory in 2020. They were a deficit for Trump in 2020. The Democrats managed nationally, I think, and in Virginia, slowly but surely are eroding their majority base among suburban women.\par \par \uc2 Education, whether you are a minority voter, a nonminority voter, it matters. You want a quality education. To kind of just teach racial polarization and gender bending, it works in college campus seminars on a Thursday night. It doesn't work for Monday through Friday in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There is another state that Republicans have not given up on, and that is California. They haven't had the success that Virginia has seen in the Republican party in the past. You have this special coming up.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, on FOX Nation right now. It's called California on the edge. And I spent the better part of a week going up and down the California coast. And what we saw was both illuminating, a little scary, sad, frightening. But there are pockets of hope, and I think we captured that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We have a clip with your ride along with police. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When you talk about spikes in crime, this is our hotspot. And you see those boxes of food back there?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So you have people, churches, that come here and they give them food, which, I get it. Everybody needs to eat. But the problem is, it encourages them just to stay here. And now you have old food that's trash with the rats.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Everywhere.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And then they go into these houses right here.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh boy.\par \par \uc2 What percentage of the people who are homeless are on drugs? If you had to guess.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If I had to guess 80 percent.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. San Francisco police told me 90.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's pretty amazing.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Ron Kingi is a sergeant there and does a community safety partnership. He has a multiracial, really cool background. He is someone who hasn't given up on his neighborhood. He grew up there. He is in there. And they are making a difference. It's incremental, Bret.\par \par \uc2 And just to bring you back to politics for a moment. I think what we will see tomorrow and then Wednesday is this lesson that Republicans cannot write off entire sections of the country and hope to cobble together a victory. Virginia, Colorado, New England, even California, it's got to be a 50 state campaign in 2024. Youngkin ran a smart campaign throughout the entire commonwealth of Virginia. He didn't give on Fairfax County, Loudoun County where we are tonight, really, really smart campaigner.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's interesting. So you can stream it now on FOX Nation?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Great to see you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the panel on the Virginia governor's race and President Biden in Europe. We are also hearing Virginia, obviously, ahead of tomorrow's big governor's election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Usually I vote for Republicans, but I couldn't do it this time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So the linkage to Trump really stuck with you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. Yes, it did. I thought he wasn't a very nice man.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm a small business owner, so wanting to make sure that Virginia gets back to being active and small business friendly and keep our economy moving.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Can I ask you how you voted?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You certainly can. I voted Republican. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And so do you think that there is a tide changing here? Do you feel something heading that way?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I do. I think that people are tired. I think that people are wanting things to get back to normal.\p