lter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020211030ehat00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Meets With French President Macron; Biden Jokes With \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  At Vatican; Democrats Debate Social Spending Bill; Virginia Gubernatorial Campaigns Trade Accusations; High-Profile Democrats To Stump For McAuliffe; Shots Required For Most City Workers; New York City Braces For Workers Shortage; \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  Riot Involvement Questioned; Biden Administration Considers Compensation; Internal Education Program At \uc2 AT&T\uc2  Instructs White Employees To Read Articles Saying They Are Racist; Media's Coverage Of Profane Criticisms Of President Biden Compared To Coverage Of Same Criticisms Of President Trump; President Biden Meets With French President Macron And Discusses Rift Between Two Countries Caused Nuclear Submarine Deal\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Chad Pergram, Alexandria Hoff, Rich  Edson, David Lee Miller, Jennifer Griffin, Dan Springer, Lauren Simonetti,  Trey Gowdy, Ben Domenech, Harold Ford Jr., Howard Kurtz \par \uc2 7,656 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 October 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS HOST (on camera): It takes a lot of time for her to make each of these, but she called it a labor of love. Then, I just --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS HOST (on camera): Get it? Labor? That's funny.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I do get it, Greg. I didn't -- I didn't -- It's so sweet. Anyway -- and everyone trick or treat, do so safely. Have a great holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Yes, I want to see pics of all of your costumes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I'll see you again at midnight. That is it for us here on THE FIVE. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Good evening, I'm Bret Baier. Breaking tonight, President Biden calls a major diplomatic blunder by his own administration, clumsy. But does not apologize to one of our oldest allies on this the first day of his trip to Europe, where the president will attend major conferences on the economy and the climate.\par \par \uc2 The second Catholic president in U.S. history visited the pope today at the Vatican amid calls from some bishops, that President Biden be denied communion. All of this comes amid the political chaos surrounding the president's domestic tax and spend agenda back home.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Peter Doocy is traveling with the president starts us off in Rome tonight.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 The President is in for the night after a day of meetings all over Rome and Vatican City. On foreign trips like these, he wants his foreign policy motto to be America is back. But today it was more like America did not stab you in the back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: What we did was so clumsy. It was not done with a lot of grace.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Hoping to smooth things over with the French President Macron, the president now claims, when the U.S. made a deal to sell nuclear powered subs to Australia, costing France a valuable contract, he didn't know Paris was in the dark.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I was under the impression that France was informed long before.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Now, the presidents are patching things up.\par \par \uc2 KATE BEDINGFIELD\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: Well, the breach such as it was is healing. President Biden obviously values this relationship tremendously.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But Biden is not saying sorry.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You have to apologize Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Just after 2:00 a.m. local, Air Force One landed, carrying a president whose agenda at home is being held up by his own party's most progressive members.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): To think that, that bill wasn't enough spending for them shows just how far left they are.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The Bidens visited the Vatican.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Thank you. I'm Jill's husband.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And met with \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 , where "The Parties focused on the joint commitment to the protection and care of the planet." Notable because the president arrived in a longer than usual motorcade with dozens of vehicles. Domestic politics crept into Biden's 90 minute meeting with the pope, including the stalled Build Back Better plan.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Did you addressed the cuts to climate in build back better?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What did you discuss about climate and Build Back Better with him?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If you have half an hour, I'll tell you. About the need and moral responsibility we have to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Abortion did not come up. Even though a number of American Catholic leaders believe Biden's position that a woman should have a right to choose to terminate a pregnancy makes him ineligible to receive communion.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: He was happy I was a good Catholic and I should keep receiving communion.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Later, comparing the pope to American servicemen and women.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You are the most significant warrior for peace I've ever met.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The President gifted him a challenge coin.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Next time I see you, you don't have -- you have to buy the drinks. I'm the only Irishman you've ever met you ever had to drink.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: At one point, while the leader of the free world was joking with the leader of the Catholic Church, the President drew upon one of his most familiar Bidenisms to tell the pope something he may have heard a time or two before. God love you, man. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live in Rome. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 While I have you, what are we learning from this updated report about the COVID-19 origins came out late this afternoon?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Yes, it expands on that 90-day review of the COVID origins that the president ordered a few months back, and what they found in this 18-page update is one \uc2 Intel\uc2  agency is saying they believe COVID came from a lab accident. They say they have moderate confidence in that.\par \par \uc2 Four other \uc2 Intel\uc2  agencies say, COVID came from exposure to an infected animal, but they have low confidence. Three other \uc2 Intel\uc2  agencies are saying they need more info. And the director of National Intelligence has concluded they don't think China was making a bioweapon, but the DNI is concluding that unless China comes forward with more information to help them fill in the blanks, then, we will probably never know the origin of COVID for sure. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll keep asking questions. Peter, thank you. Progressive Democrats back here at home are once again preventing the president's social spending measures from getting through the Democrat-run Congress.\par \par \uc2 Congressional correspondent Chad Pergram live on Capitol Hill with that part of the story.\par \par \uc2 Chad, we saw what happened last night, but how much blood -- bad blood is there between moderate and progressive Democrats?\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Well, Bret, progressives are demanding assurances from moderates Kyrsten Sinema and \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  that they will vote for the social spending bill.\par \par \uc2 There is also distrust between the House and Senate that's why liberals are forcing leaders to do the infrastructure bill and the social spending bill in tandem. Both President Biden and House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  failed to close the deal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: What's your response when you hear that sort of dissension in your caucus about where you stand, when you present a very positive picture of where you are?\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): I have respect for all of our members on, in the full spectrum of the big tent that is our Democratic Party. Everybody has the chance to say what they wish.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): Or mad because they believe moderates prevailed reducing the overall cost of the bill. Plus, they nixed family leave and beefed up Medicare. Liberals are flexing their muscles.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL HARDAWAY, FORMER HOUSE DEMOCRAT LEADERSHIP AIDE: They are coming into their power in terms of understanding just how much power they willed on the Hill, and how much power they willed with throughout the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): But which side has the upper hand? Moderates control the policy. That's because Democrats hold narrow margins in the House and Senate. That dictates what can pass. But liberals control the politics. There is strength in numbers, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad, what's the timeline here?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The best case scenario is probably two weeks, even if they had a deal today, then you're into Thanksgiving. They are a long ways off from final language. The other major deadline is December 3rd. That's when lawmakers must fund the government to avoid a shutdown and there will be another Donnybrook over the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 And they could enroll all this into a big bill in early December, the other deadline which -- of course, there's always a big deadline here on Capitol Hill. This Christmas. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Chad. We'll follow it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 An election may many feel is a harbinger of things to come in next year's midterms is just four days away. The Virginia gubernatorial race has seen the Republican overtake the favorite Democrat in recent polling, sending a shiver through much of the bluish purple state.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE" tonight. \uc2 Rich Edson\uc2  is in Norfolk tonight where Democrat \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  has spent much of the day. First up though, correspondent Alexandria Hoff with the Glenn Youngkin campaign in Warrenton, Virginia. Good evening, Alexandria.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Yes. Glenn Youngkin has absolutely been peppering this state. This event tonight will be his fifth of the day. That's about average for his bus tour and all this voter outreach may be paying off.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice over): According to the latest Fox News poll of likely Virginia voters, it shows Republican Glenn Youngkin now leading Democratic opponent \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  by eight points, 53 to 45 percent with two percent undecided. That is a major shift from just two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 The latest Washington Post poll puts Youngkin one point behind McAuliffe well within the margin of error. As part of his 50-stop tour across the state, Youngkin started the day with two stops in Charlottesville, celebrated the poll findings but added this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN (R), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE, VIRGINIA: Polls don't elect governors, voters do. Voters do. So, now it's time for all of us to go to work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: The latest Fox News poll also shows Youngkin has an eight percent lead over McAuliffe when likely voters in Virginia were asked who they trust to do a better job on education, which has been a major talking point for Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 On the economy, a tie from two weeks ago was broken. McAuliffe lost a percent as Youngkin gained 10. And with these new numbers in hand, Youngkin has called McAuliffe's campaign desperate.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, members of McAuliffe's team claimed that a photo taken at a Youngkin event today in Charlottesville showcased white nationalist carrying torches. Youngkin's team fired back, calling it a disgusting and desperate stunt by the opposition.\par \par \uc2 For more on that, let's turn to our \uc2 Rich Edson\uc2 . He has been following the McAuliffe trail and he is live in Norfolk tonight. Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Yes. Thanks and good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  just left the stage here. We're all waiting for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to step up and address the crowd. The McAuliffe campaign has commented on that. Says they had no involvement in that group that met Youngkin's bus earlier today.\par \par \uc2 And now we're hearing from The Lincoln Project, that anti-Trump group, we'll they are now claiming in a statement, "Today's demonstration was our way of reminding Virginians what happened in Charlottesville four years ago, the Republican Party's embrace of those values, and Glenn Youngkin's failure to condemn it."\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): McAuliffe and Democrats are accusing Youngkin of deploying what they call racist dog whistles and trying to tie the Republican to former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Youngkin has countered that he is focused on local issues like education, crime, and the economy.\par \par \uc2 Also, one of our foxnews.com reporters, Tyler O'Neil, he e-mailed a McAuliffe spokesperson for a response to the campaign spending more than $50,000, hiring the Elias Law Group that started by Mark Elias. He is an attorney who previously challenged elections, was \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's campaign general counsel, and hired the research firm that produced the dossier with claims of connection between Russia and Trump.\par \par \uc2 A campaign spokesperson e-mail that reporter back, apparently, mistakenly saying, can we try to kill this? The story is now up on foxnews.com.\par \par \uc2 McAuliffe canceled campaigns this weekend. He'll be with Bruce Smith to be NFL Hall of Famer, also with a number of politicians like Senator Tim Kaine from Virginia, and representatives, Barbara Lee, and \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 . Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Rich, quickly, I see that crowd behind you. But what have the crowds look like for McAuliffe around the state?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: No, it's been hit or miss. He was in Charlottesville yesterday, the crowd at a few dozen people there and that's against a stronghold in the middle of the state, we're they're trying to get people out.\par \par \uc2 This room is largely filled, and say there's a few 100 people here. But as you get to the back, it begins to empty out a little bit. Republicans have been talking at least among themselves about what they perceive as a much smaller group of people who are showing up to these McAuliffe events. They feel like they've got the momentum and the excitement going their way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll see. I mean, that's what the polls show. \uc2 Rich Edson\uc2  on the trail. Rich, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Well, the superintendent of Northern Virginia School District is comparing parental protests at school board meetings to the Capitol riot on January 6th, and his experience seeing people drinking at the beach.\par \par \uc2 The comments by Loudoun County School Chief Scott Ziegler came as part of a court hearing for a man arrested at a June school board meeting. Ziegler's actions in recent months have led parents in that area to demand his resignation.\par \par \uc2 Markets today, three major stock markets closed at record highs today. The Dow gained 89. The S&P 500 was up nine. The \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  jumped 50 today. For the week, the Dow gained four-tenths of a percentage point. The S&P 500 finished ahead 1-1/3. The \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  was up 2-7/10.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  has approved the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine for emergency use in children ages 5 to 11. The kids' doses are one-third the amount for adults and teens. Up to 28 million additional American children could be eligible for vaccinations as early as next week. The process next goes to the CDC for final approval.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, New York City employees who have not yet been vaccinated are in limbo tonight. Really, right now, the mayor is threatening their jobs. City residents are worried about what will happen if they need some kind of emergency assistance in coming days if the numbers dwindle down.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Lee Miller is in New York tonight with the latest. Good evening, David Lee.\par \par \uc2 DAVID LEE MILLER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 New York City's vaccine mandate took effect a little more than one hour ago. That means virtually all New York City employees must now be vaccinated, or on Monday morning, they will be placed on leave without pay.\par \par \uc2 This evening, union leaders for New York City firefighters held a news conference warning that fewer people on the job could endanger lives.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ANSBRO, UNIFORMED FIREFIGHTERS ASSOCIATION: We need everyone we can to keep the city running and keep it safe. We're trying to avoid what is going to be an inevitable disaster by design on Monday morning.\par \par \uc2 MILLER (voice over): New York is bracing for a shortage of first responders and other workers who refuse to comply with the city's vaccine mandate. Throughout the week, city employees opposed to the policy marched and rally.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: We (INAUDIBLE) people will not comply!\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Many in attendance said they were not anti-vax, but anti-mandate.\par \par \uc2 KIERAN BURKE, FORMER NEW YORK CITY FIRE MARSHALL: I think at the root of it, and today especially, it's about people's right to choose.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: The fire department has admit morning, said the mandate compliance rate is 71 percent. The commissioner in a written statement say there is a plan if there is a worker shortage. "We will use all means at our disposal including mandatory overtime, mutual aid from other EMS providers, and significant changes to the schedules of our members."\par \par \uc2 According to the city's police commissioner, yesterday, 1,000 cops received their first dose. Raising the number of those vaccinated to 80 percent. If there is a shortage of cops, the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  says it is ready.\par \par \uc2 DERMOT SHEA\uc2 , POLICE COMMISSIONER, NEW YORK CITY: New Yorkers should not -- should not be worried about this. A contingency plans are in place there. So, this will not affect cops on the street.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Across the country, vaccine mandates have met with opposition. The L.A. County sheriff on Thursday said the Board of Supervisors decision to require vaccinations led to a mass exodus within the department, resulting in an "imminent threat to public safety".\par \par \uc2 And in Chicago, multiple court battles are brewing. Both cops and firefighters are refusing to comply with the mayor's mandate, requiring city workers to disclose their vaccination status and either get a shot or undergo regular COVID testing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MILLER: And back here in New York City, even though the 5:00 p.m. mandate deadline has now passed, city workers who do get a shot over the weekend will be able to report to work on Monday and will not lose any pay. However, they will not be eligible for a $500 vaccination bonus. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Lee Miller in Manhattan. David Lee, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  has rejected an emergency appeal from health care workers in Maine, seeking religious exemptions for a vaccine mandate. That went into effect today. The justices rejected a request for -- from injunctive relief that would have temporarily blocked enforcement of the state mandate for hospital and nursing home employees. Justices Gorsuch, Thomas, and Alito dissented.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a Republican member of the committee investigating the Capitol riot is retiring from Congress. Plus, lawmakers investigating the riot hear from some of the defendants, we'll have a live report.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Illinois Republican Congressman \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  says he will not seek reelection next year. Kinzinger is a critic of former President Trump and he is on the panel investigating the Capitol riot.\par \par \uc2 The announcement came after Illinois announced redistricting plans for his district. The military veteran was one of 10 House Republicans who voted to impeach then-President Trump on a charge of inciting the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  (R-IL): In Congress, I've witnessed how division is heavily rooted. It has also become increasingly obvious to me that in order to break the narrative, I cannot focus on both a reelection to Congress and a broader fight nationwide. I want to make it clear, this isn't the end of my political future, but the beginning.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kinzinger called it the insurrection, the Capitol riot, January 6th. He is the second Republican who voted for impeachment to announce he will not seek reelection.\par \par \uc2 Today, some of the hundreds of people charged in connection with that incident on January 6th, spoke with the committee looking into it. But many of the people lawmakers most want to talk with are not showing up so far.\par \par \uc2 National security correspondent Jennifer Griffin is at the Pentagon tonight with details of one -- of where this investigation is headed.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The January 6th Select Committee conducted more interviews today with some of the 650 defendants charged with storming the U.S. Capitol. The committee has subpoenaed 13 people, but former Trump officials continue to refuse to testify. The Justice Department is now deciding whether former Trump strategist \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  will face jail time after the House voted to hold him in contempt.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY\uc2  (R-WY): He attempted to assert some sort of vague executive privilege which, of course, he doesn't have the right to assert.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: At the center of the investigation, is Bannon's presence at a so- called war room in the Willard Hotel, a block from the White House in the days before the riot.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: So, the war room in the Willard seems to have been established some days before the 6th itself, and was active also on the 6th, and we are, of course, very interested in talking to people who were in the war room.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The committee is seeking documents from John Eastman, a legal scholar who met in the Oval Office with President Trump and Vice President Pence, and outline ways to block the Biden presidency, and obstruct the electoral count.\par \par \uc2 Former top intelligence officials like Marc Polymeropoulos, who served in the CIA for 26 years specializing in counterterrorism in Russia, push back on any suggestion that January 6th was a false flag operation, in which the perpetrators posed as another group to hide their identities.\par \par \uc2 MARC POLYMEROPOULOS, FORMER CENTRAL INTELLIGENCE AGENCY INTELLIGENCE OFFICER: One of the things with false flag operations as well, is you know, is sometimes it's used, you know, by conspiracy theorists to actually hide the truth. Or adversaries or others who want to believe in some conspiracy theories will say, well, no, yes, the government who was behind 9/11. Pretty far-fetched, in no way was January 6th, a false flag operation.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is one of two Republicans who serve on the January 6th committee.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: We have seen already in 100s of cases, pleadings in court from those who were in the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  that day, who have said they were there because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sent them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Former Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  has engaged the committee through his lawyer, but is refusing to testify frustrating committee members who may seek further contempt charges. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, should illegal immigrants separated from family members be awarded hundreds of 1000s of your tax dollars? That story coming up.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 13 in Tampa as official say the search for Gabby Petito and Brian Laundrie has helped law enforcement agencies across the country find the bodies of six other missing people.\par \par \uc2 Authorities found the bodies in Alabama, Wyoming, California, and North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 A newlywed couple was also found shot to death in Utah. Investigators originally thought that case could be connected to the Petito matter, but it was later determined to be unrelated.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Nashville. From our affiliate there, one of the big stories there tonight, babies at St. Thomas Midtown Hospital's newborn intensive care unit celebrate Halloween in style. The newborns were gifted handmade costumes by nurses, they include Cruella de Vil, Colonel Sanders, and Dr. Phil. How about that?\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. Spooky. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tonight, we have new information on a story we brought you last night first reported by The Wall Street Journal, about the Biden administration actually considering payoffs. Hundreds of thousands of dollars of payoffs to migrant families suing over being separated at the border.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Dan Springer is in La Joya, Texas tonight.\par \par \uc2 DAN SPRINGER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Another 173 migrants were rounded up overnight in La Joya, Texas. They were put on buses to begin processing. Those with children will likely be allowed to stay during their asylum claim.\par \par \uc2 For two months in 2018, the policy was to separate minors, put them in detention facilities, and remove the adults. Now, The Wall Street Journal is reporting, "The Biden administration is in talks to offer immigrant families that were separated during the Trump administration around $450,000 a person in compensation." To settle claims that the children's suffered "lasting psychological trauma". Many Republicans expressed outrage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COMER: Every decision \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's made since he's been president has made our border more open to illegals. And once again, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  prioritizes the rights of illegals over the rights of taxpaying working Americans.\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER: Congressman and former Navy SEAL \uc2 Dan Crenshaw\uc2  tweeted, "Biden wants to pay illegal immigrants $450,000 for their hardship while breaking our laws. For perspective, if a service member is killed in action, there's next of kin gets an insurance payment of $400,000. Let that sink in.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ACLU\uc2  says more than 5,000 children were separated from their parents, leading to nearly 1,000 lawsuits. Settling those all at once could cost taxpayers a billion dollars or more.\par \par \uc2 A rancher in Texas says Trump's policy was not cruel because many of the kids were really unaccompanied minors, and paying the families will only create a bigger magnet.\par \par \uc2 BRAD SCHWARZ, TEXAS RANCHER: That has got to be the most asinine thing I have ever heard in my life. I can't believe we are going to reward people that are coming into this country illegally when we do have a process of coming in legally, and then reward them with $450,000?\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER: The Biden administration has also just made a second attempt to undo Trump's so-called "Remain in Mexico" policy. Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas said sending migrants back to Mexico while they apply for asylum led to substantial and unjustifiable human costs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER (on camera): DHS says even as it files a second legal challenge to scrap the Remain in Mexico policy, it will continue working toward complying with that court order and be ready, if necessary, to reinstate it by mid-November. Homeland Security did not, however, comment on the story about potential big payouts to migrant families separated at the border. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's a big story. We'll follow it. Dan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The sheriff of Albany County, New York, is not happy with how the news of a sex crime charge against former Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  was made public. But Sheriff Craig \uc2 Apple\uc2  says his people have done their jobs well. The former governor is accused of putting his hand under a woman's shirt. It is a misdemeanor but still a sex crime. Cuomo denies the allegation.\par \par \uc2 We stay in the New York area now for a story about an internal education program at one of the nation's largest companies, instructing white employees to read an article saying they are racist. FOX Business correspondent Lauren Simonetti joins us tonight from Westwood, New Jersey, with the latest on this. Good evening, Lauren.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SIMONETTI, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. New documents from \uc2 AT&T\uc2 , one of their internal forums, called "Listen, Understand, Act." It was a platform started in the aftermath of the George Floyd murder last year, and it's based on the teachings of Critical Race Theory, open to all of the company's near quarter-million employees. Topics include white privilege, systemic racism, and show company recommendations for listening and reading materials, books like "White Fragility, Why It's So Hard for People to Talk about Racism," and articles such as "White America, If You Want to Know Who is Responsible for Racism, Look in the Mirror."\par \par \uc2 This as more major companies, including \uc2 AT&T\uc2  dip their toes in the political waters. AT&T CEO \uc2 John Stankey\uc2  writing to his staff earlier this year, quote, "We have a moral and business obligation to engage on this issue of racial injustice, to live up to our value to stand for equality for our employees, and to actively participate in helping create an equitable society."\par \par \uc2 We asked \uc2 AT&T\uc2  to comment on this program and to ask them if employees were pressured or required to participate in it. Here's their response -- "Whether an employee uses these resources or not is up to them and does not affect their annual performance rating. We have a long and proud history of valuing diversity, equality, and inclusion, and will continue to do so."\par \par \uc2 Now, the portal, Bret, also direct those employees to materials for some leftwing causes, like defunding the police and reparations. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's quite something. Lauren, we'll follow it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, do the media have a double standard when it comes to profane criticism of presidents? We'll take a look at the evidence.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Queen Elizabeth II is being advised to rest for at least the next two weeks. Buckingham Palace says she is taking doctor's advice to cut back on her busy schedule. The 95-year-old monarch can continue to undertake light desk-based duties during this time, including some virtual audiences.\par \par \uc2 The Navy says it has successfully tested a booster rocket motor designed to power a launch vehicle carrying a hypersonic weapon aloft. One official says the service is on schedule for a flight test of the full common hypersonic missile next year. The news comes amid concern over a pair of Chinese tests involving the next generation of hypersonic weapons, which caught U.S. intelligence officials off guard just weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. is hitting Iran with a fresh set of sanctions. The new penalties are against two senior members of Iran's Revolutionary Guard Corps and two affiliated companies for supplies lethal Jones and related material to insurgent groups in Iraq, Lebanon, Yemen, and Ethiopia. The sanctions come as President Biden is in Europe where he may discuss with allies the possible resumption of nuclear talks with Iran.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we're taking another look at an apparent case of double standard by the mainstream media. Some of the press are lamenting the public criticism involving profanity directed toward President Biden at stadiums around the country. There were no such musings, of course, during the Trump administration. Here is FOX News media analyst and host of FOX's MEDIA BUZZ, Howard Kurtz.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST: President Biden ran into some x-rated protest signs on a recent trip to Michigan.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Notwithstanding some of the signs I saw coming, that's why 81 million Americans voted for me.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: And face an f-word chant when he was in Scranton.\par \par \uc2 "The Washington Post" says Biden is becoming an object of hatred for Trump supporters, blaming that in part on the norm-breaking and vulgarity of the Trump era. But while briefly acknowledging that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was also vilified, "The Post," like other news outlets, didn't loudly object when he was cursed out, such as at the World Cup.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are -- in a -- sports -- we were --\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Or when Robert De Niro dropped the f-bomb at the Tony Awards.\par \par \uc2 And much of the vitriol came not from angry citizens but from journalists, such as CNN's White House correspondent.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't see how you avoid the conclusion that the president of the United States is a straight up sociopath.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: After January 20th, he just goes back to being another crackpot on the Internet.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Conservative rapper Bryson Gray in AN "Impeach Biden" t-shirt has jumped to the top spots on iTunes, knocking off Adele, with a euphemism for f-Joe Biden.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Let's go Brandon.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: "Let's Go Brandon," based on NBC reporter mistaking a cursing Biden chant at NASCAR as a cheer for driver Brandon Brown. Gray sarcastically thanked \uc2 YouTube\uc2  by boosting the song's popularity by banning it, saying the company told him that was because of medical misinformation based on lyrics about the COVID vaccine.\par \par \uc2 A YouTube spokesperson told FOX News Grays' song was removed for falsely claiming the pandemic isn't real and vaccines don't work.\par \par \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  had his birthplace questioned and was interrupted by a Republican during a speech to congress.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You lie.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Actor Mark Ruffalo tweeted that \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  must be brought to justice for the crimes of the Iraq War.\par \par \uc2 Biden, with his folksy uncle Joe image, had been spared most personal attacks. But his current struggles are making him just another target of vitriol.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 KURTZ (on camera): No president is spared in an age of polarized politics, and that's fueled by social media. Blank Joe Biden has become a \uc2 twitter\uc2  hashtag. But some in the media seem more offended now that the target is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's successor. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Howie, thanks. See you this weekend on MEDIA BUZZ.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on President Biden's trip abroad, plus the Virginia governor's race, and Winners and Losers.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. Another record for daily deaths from the coronavirus in Russia. Each day this week more than 1,000 people have died there. Russia's total death toll is the highest in Europe. President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has ordered a lockdown to try to slow the spread there.\par \par \uc2 An undersea volcano causes a massive pile up of pumice pebbles along the coast of Japan, the Japanese islands there. The gray pebbles are causing damage to ports and fishing boats. Ferry services connecting Okinawa to other remote islands have been temporarily suspended because of those pebbles. Officials warn the pebbles are moving farther north and could affect entire Japanese coast lines.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KERRY\uc2 , SPECIAL PRESIDENTIAL CLIMATE ENVOY: He had not been aware of that. He literally had not been aware of what had transpired.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: What happened was, to use an English phrase, what we did was clumsy. It was not done with a lot of grace. I was under the impression certain things had happened that hadn't happened.\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL MACRON, FRENCH PRESIDENT: Now what's important is precisely to be sure that such a situation will not be possible for our future.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It was a major diplomatic blunder, the U.S. not telling France of an end to a submarine deal, a big one, dealing, instead with Australia. And the French were really upset about it, so much so they pulled the ambassador from the U.S., the French ambassador to the U.S. out. Now, the president said it was clumsy mistake. Didn't apologize, but that's the first part of his Europe trip.\par \par \uc2 We'll start there with our panel, Ben Domenech, publisher of "The Federalist," Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, CEO of Empowerment and Inclusion Capital, and \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , former Congressman from South Carolina. Trey, clumsy, and he didn't even know about it is what he told the French President Macron.\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA REPRESENTATIVE: Yes, I'm not sure I would have admitted that, even if it were true. I can tell you, imagine how bad your life is when you are happier meeting with the president of France who you stiffed in a submarine deal, you would rather do that than go meet with the Progressive Caucus in the house. How bad is your life if you prefer that?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So let's turn there, Harold. We saw what happened last night up on Capitol Hill, the punt to next week. There is still some thought that it might be two weeks. Some people are saying some time before Thanksgiving where this all gets ironed out. It's pretty ugly. It may still happen, but it's pretty ugly.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: Thanks for having me on, and I would agree with you. It's not the way, I'm sure, he orchestrated or choreographed it. But sometimes bigtime legislation is not choreographed, is not choreographable in the way you may like.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, if they get this passed, $1.75 trillion. You have $1.2, $1.25 trillion package for infrastructure, that's $3 trillion he would have gotten in the first year in the presidency. Again, to your point, it has not been pretty. But I give credit to some of the -- a lot of progressives, because they kept their word. I think now the question is whether or not Senators Manchin and Sinema can keep their word as well. And if they do, we will pass an historic piece of legislation, again, not pretty the way they did it. We often don't see this kind of sausage-making. We just happen to be seeing it in this instance.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Talk about not pretty, Ben. This story that was from "The Wall Street Journal" and now is moving forward about the possible plans to pay illegal immigrants who have been separated in their families some $450,000 or more. Representative \uc2 Dan Crenshaw\uc2  tweeting out "Biden wants to pay illegal immigrants $450,000 for their hardship while breaking our laws. For perspective, if a servicemember is killed in action, their next of kin gets an insurance payment of $400,000. Let that sink in." I'm not sure I believed this story when I first read it, Ben. But there hasn't been push back yet, and we haven't gotten an official shoot down of this story.\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, PUBLISHER, "THE FEDERALIST": Bret, as a publisher of "The Federalist" people pitch me, occasionally, stories. And when this one came into my email, I reacted by assuming that it was not true. I could not believe that something like this was actually happening. And yet, we are seeing that there is not pushback of this, that this is something that is ongoing. And the amount of backlash that I think you are going to see to this is just going to be unprecedented. People are not going to accept this and just go along with it.\par \par \uc2 And just to circle back to Harold's point. I don't think that this dog's breakfast of a bill or a fire festival dinner, if you want the more current recollection, is going to be something that you can actually sell to the American people. The Biden administration seems to be going down this road of meandering through all of these different topics that are of the utmost importance to the American people, and then just sort of saying trust us, what we're doing is going to be something that you like when they have no major thing, no major thing to point to that's actually going to make a difference. And certainly the fact that they couldn't get this done in time for the Virginia election is something that weighs on the minds of a lot of D.C. Democrats tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, and particularly \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  in the Virginia election.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of which, final days before Tuesday's Election Day, we saw the poll that we had last night, a big shift in that race. Now, you have got a lot of different things happening, the Youngkin communications director saying that "\uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2 's campaign has been the dirtiest, nastiest, most dishonest campaign in Virginia history. This morning they are closing out his career by going even lower."\par \par \uc2 There was this moment this morning where there were these guys with tiki torches, Trey, in front of a Youngkin bus saying we are all in for Glenn, suggesting -- hearkening back to Charlottesville and the Nazis and the white supremacists who marched in that march. Late today -- there was a lot of allegations that they were Democrats, actually. The Lincoln Project said that they are taking credit, or saying that they were behind it. The Youngkin people are saying they think they are Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line, you have got a lot of stuff going on. Then you look at this. This is a flier that came out in which you would think that it's Youngkin just being endorsed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and that's a Republican thing, and Trump next to Youngkin and thumbs up. But if you look closely, on the back of this flier it's the Democratic Party of Virginia and specifically the McAuliffe campaign that really wants that tie between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Glenn Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Yes, Bret, if people say that it was Democrats and the Lincoln Project, they are both right. The Lincoln Project is a bunch of Democrats. So this is why people hate politics. There may be reasons to not vote for Glenn Youngkin, but he's not a white supremacist. So the fact that Democrats are on the verge of losing in Virginia for, what, the first time in 20 years, when you run a candidate who has been around since the Hamilton-Burr duel, and he is a Clinton acolyte that no one is excited about, Virginia Democrats found a way to make that state competitive.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I will give you the last word, Harold. We're going to do without Winners and Losers, but you get the last word here.\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : If Glenn Youngkin, who has, I think, run a great campaign so far, who reminds me a little bit of \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2  and some of the Republicans that I served with whom I respected to greatly, if he decides to bring in a guy, which I think he is going to bring in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the last few days, who lost that state by 10 points, maybe he doesn't have it locked up yet.\par \par \uc2 This campaign has not been the prettiest campaign, but we'll have an outcome Tuesday. And I agree, it will portend what will happen in next year's midterms. But I wouldn't count \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  out, and I certainly, for Glenn Youngkin to bring \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in, it leads me to believe he doesn't have this locked up yet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Ben. I owe you next time. Thank you, panel. Have great weekend.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, it's Friday, you know what that means -- "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You know, when you're president -- see all these people here? They are with you all the time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Bongiorno, Bill.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, no, doing the hokey pokey, one foot in, one foot out. So we need certainty.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No one got everything they wanted, including me.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITT ROMNEY\uc2 , (R-UT): A trillion seconds ago Neanderthals walked the earth. So a trillion is an enormous amount of money.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If you get a chance, go vote. Because we're chasing you. We are calling you. We are texting you. We're knocking at your door.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I believe this election will be fair. I believe this election will come out big time in our favor.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Extremism can come in many forms. It can come in a smile and a fleece vest.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN KENNEDY\uc2 , (R-LA): Somebody at the White House has been smoking the devil's lettuce.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You think we have a problem in a caravan coming towards the border now? Wait until the word gets out that you actually get paid for breaking the law.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't believe it's reasonable to read this memorandum as killing anyone's rights. It's about threats of violence.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank God you are not on the Supreme Court. You should resign in disgrace, Judge.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Live long and prosper with candy.\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : Your book "To Rescue the Republic" is number one on the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " bestseller list. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I still owe Harold for that one. And smoking the devil's lettuce, that's a new one for Senator Kennedy. Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, we'll talk with Laura Ingraham about the news of the day, plus, her new FOX Nation special about California. Also have our political panel discussing the Virginia's governor's race on the eve of Election Day. We have a special Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  in Florida, Republican Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  will be guests on FOX NEWS SUNDAY with Chris Wallace this weekend. So check your local listings for that.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for the SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "FOX NEWS PRIMETIME" is still hosted by Brian Kilmeade. It starts right now. Brian?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020211030ehat00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 1.7 Million Migrant Apprehensions In 2021 Fiscal Year; White House Officials Clarify Multiple Biden Comments Made During Live Town Hall; Supreme Court Moves Quickly To Hear Texas Abortion Case; Crew Member Dies After Alec Baldwin Discharges Prop Gun; Warehouse Backups Contributing To Situation; Doctors Use Nonhuman Organ In Transplant; 98,000 On National Wait List For Kidney Transplant; McAuliffe Kicks Off Virginia Bus Tour; Recent Polls Show Tight Race For Virginia Governor; President Biden's Comments On America's Commitment To Defend Taiwan From Attack From China Draw Controversy; President Biden Holds Town Hall On Possibly Supporting Changing Senate Filibuster And U.S. Foreign Policy; Polls Shows Close Race Between Democrat \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  And Republican Glenn Youngkin In Virginia Gubernatorial Election.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jonathan Hunt, Shannon Bream, Peter Doocy, Bill  Melugin, William La Jeunesse, Marc Siegel Alexandria Hoff, Greg Palkot,  Harold Ford Jr., Ben Domenech \par \uc2 8,059 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 October 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DAGEN MCDOWELL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: She had a bottle of bourbon in her backpack and I had two cans of Seltzer in my sweatpants pockets.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: To see a very important movie though.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: The Silence of the Lambs.\par \par \uc2 LISA KENNEDY MONTGOMERY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: The Silence of the Lambs 30th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Wait, you guys drink hard seltzer smuggled to watch Silence of the Lambs together?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Maybe.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's crazy. I'll see you guys here on Monday and get to the bottom of it.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Coming to you tonight from Dallas, about 460 miles from the U.S. Mexico border. We will go there live shortly.\par \par \uc2 But breaking tonight, it's official, a record 1.7 million apprehensions of illegal immigrants along the southern border according to just released Customs and Border Patrol statistics. That's in one year.\par \par \uc2 Again, that's the most on record. The next highest number of apprehensions happened 35 years ago. That does not include the hundreds of thousands of so called got-aways that evaded apprehension.\par \par \uc2 The stats paint a dire picture of the border crisis, something former and current border officials have been raising an alarm about for months.\par \par \uc2 This occurs as President Biden says he has been to the border and probably should go back. Fact checkers then went into action after his town hall last night.\par \par \uc2 And today, the White House press secretary attempted to clarify her boss's remarks on the border issue and several others.\par \par \uc2 We have Fox team coverage tonight, Peter Doocy is at the White House with efforts to set the record straight the day after that presidential town hall.\par \par \uc2 But we start off with correspondent Bill Melugin back in La Joya, Texas tonight with staggering new immigration numbers that paint a bleak picture for Border Patrol officials. Good evening, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening to you. Those numbers are staggering and literally just a couple of minutes ago, you can take a look at our Fox drone right now, we had groups of migrants just come walking right past us right now. They just crossed the Rio Grande and they have officially entered the United States illegally ready to turn themselves in.\par \par \uc2 But late this afternoon, CBP officially released their September encounter numbers for the month of September that were just over 192,000 encounters with migrants here at the southern border. That adds up for the grand total of the 2,021 fiscal year, a staggering 1.73 million, that is an all-time record high, beating the previous high record of 1.69 million back in 1986. This border crisis is making history and it is not slowing down.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): Another day, another human smuggling pursuit here at the border. This time, near Del Rio, Texas where DPS troopers give chase. The driver eventually loses control and slams into a parked vehicle, pinning the passengers inside. The driver was arrested and four illegal immigrants inside were turned over to Border Patrol.\par \par \uc2 In Mission, Texas early Friday morning, another suspected human smuggling crash left two people dead and five others hospitalized.\par \par \uc2 Texas DPS tells us a local police department was chasing this vehicle when it rolled over while filled with suspected migrants, two men were ejected and both died on scene.\par \par \uc2 In La Joya Friday morning, we witnessed this group of single adult migrants being taken into custody by Border Patrol. Some of the men were wearing camouflage, something we often see with runners.\par \par \uc2 That includes this group of illegal immigrants arrested by Texas DPS for criminal trespassing on a ranch in Kinney County, all of the men wearing camouflage trying to evade.\par \par \uc2 And in Border Patrols Tucson sector, agents arrested Mexican national Felix Perez-Torres after he crossed illegally. He was previously convicted of felony sexual assault in the State of Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 The vice president of the Border Patrol union tells Fox News it's the hundreds of thousands of illegal immigrants who aren't caught who are the biggest concern.\par \par \uc2 ART DEL CUETO, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: When you have so much lawlessness going on in the border and you have an administration that pretty much has opened the door for criminal behavior, those that are going that extra step not to get apprehended, not to get detected, what kind of criminal backgrounds do they really have?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, that criminal element is constantly trying to make it through the border here.\par \par \uc2 Just here in the Rio Grande Valley alone on Wednesday, Border Patrol reports that just in a matter of a few hours, they arrested three confirmed gang members here after they crossed illegally. That includes two MS-13 gang members, one of whom had homicide charges in the state of New York. The other has a homicide arrest down in El Salvador. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Bill, I just want to clarify this, 1.7 million apprehensions but we don't really have a number on the so called got-aways. It could be 300,000 400,000 according to former Border Patrol Chief Rodney Scott.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Exactly, former Chief Scott says their latest numbers are there are at least 400,000 known got-aways but the key part of that is known, those are the ones they see on the cameras, they see on the sensors and they're just never able to get to.\par \par \uc2 The key question is, what about the unknown got-aways? How many of those are there because agents can't be everywhere at once out on this border, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Bill Melugin along the border tonight. Bill, thanks.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is spending the weekend at his home in Delaware and his staff may spend a lot of time during that weekend trying to continue to clean up from a series of mistakes and misstatements during a cable news town hall Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 It all occurs as the president's domestic agenda really hangs in the balance because of continued Democratic Party infighting.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy has that part of the story tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. The president is trying to keep people focused on $2 trillion worth of progressive proposals and not on issues he says are out of his control. There at the border and also at the gas pump where he admits prices will remain high through next year.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I don't have a near term answer.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Staffers don't either.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: There are limitations to what any president can do as it relates to gas prices.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Same for the supply chain.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: 40 percent of all products coming into the United States of America on the West Coast go through Los Angeles and -- what am I doing here?\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER, CNN HOST: Is it Long Beach or--\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Long Beach. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Biden floated activating the National Guard.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Would you consider the National Guard to help with the supply chain issue?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes, absolutely, positively. I would do that.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: It turns out that's not true.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It's not something under active consideration.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The president's COVID policy could make things worse. A letter published by Politico from the leader of a union representing UPS, \uc2 FedEx\uc2  and DHL workers warns: We have significant concerns with the employer mandates announced on September 9th 2021 and the ability of industry members to implement the required employee vaccinations by December 8th 2021.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: We don't actually anticipate these disruptions.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The President's top priority is finalizing a deal with trillions of taxpayer dollars for social programs.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But you feel like a deal is close?\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA): I think it's very possible.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The vice president pitched the plan in the northeast today.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have a motto. I drink, I eat and drink no for breakfast.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: She's been much less visible handling her most high-profile assignment, immigration.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you have any plans to go to