swer the call.\par \par \uc2 They're basically saying that if they tap the Strategic Petroleum Reserve here in the United States to get some more gas into the interior of the country, he thinks that it could lower prices by about just a little bit in the near term.\par \par \uc2 But he says through 2022, expect some pain at the pump. Not his word pain at the pump but higher prices.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right, Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thanks. More on this with the panel.\par \par \uc2 Now to a story that is getting a lot of attention here in Texas, but obviously has implications for the nation. The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  is allowing the Texas law banning most abortions to remain in place for now. But justices have agreed to hear arguments in the case November 1st.\par \par \uc2 Fox News Chief Legal Correspondent, anchor of "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT" Shannon Bream is here with the details. Good evening, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHIEF LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. It is fair to characterize what happened today as rare and unusual for a body (PH) that is very deliberative with a detailed framework about how these cases proceed, this one is on the fastest of the fast tracks.\par \par \uc2 The court has ordered initial briefs to be in by Wednesday, reply briefs due by Friday and then arguments that following Monday. Texas law SBA bans most abortions after a heartbeat is detected around roughly six weeks into a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 And though the justices will hear a dispute over the law on November 1st, it's much more procedural in nature about whether the Justice Department can actually sue a state, state officials or private individuals in an attempt to keep the law from being enforced. It's not about the merits.\par \par \uc2 White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  saying today, she reiterated the president's position on this that the Texas law they say is "blatantly unconstitutional".\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the justices elected not to put the law on hold. Justice Sotomayor objected to that today writing this "These women will suffer personal harm from delaying their medical care and as their pregnancies progress, they may even be unable to obtain abortion care altogether".\par \par \uc2 She added, every day the court fails to grant relief is devastating, both for individual women and for our constitutional system as a whole.\par \par \uc2 While pro-life group by Live Action called the court's decision not to block the law "A victory for life that will save the lives of innocent preborn boys and girls."\par \par \uc2 The as for actually getting to the abortion debate on the merits, that's going to happen December 1st, the date the justices will hear arguments over Mississippi's law banning most abortions after 15 weeks. A case -- a decision in that case is going to be due in June, late June.\par \par \uc2 And Bret, of course, that puts it right in the middle of all those midterm elections and campaigning.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, a lot of things to watch at the Supreme Court. Shannon, as always, thank you. We'll see you tonight.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today, the Dow gained 74 to close at a new record. The S&P 500 lost five, the \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  gave back 125-1/2 today.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow rose about one percentage point, the S&P 500 gained 1-2/3, the \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  picked up 1-1/3.\par \par \uc2 Investigators in New Mexico are looking for answers tonight about what caused the tragic fatal accident on a movie set. Actor Alec Baldwin apparently discharged a prop firearm, resulting in the death of one crew member and wounding another.\par \par \uc2 Chief Correspondent Jonathan Hunt tells us what we know tonight from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We have a -- two people have been shot accidentally.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CORRESPONDENT (voice over): That 911 call brought police to the Bonanza Creek Ranch outside Santa Fe New Mexico Thursday afternoon after Alec Baldwin apparently fired a prop gun on the set of his new Western Rust hitting two people. 42-year-old Halyna Hutchins, the director of photography on the movie was killed and director Joel Souza who's 48 was injured.\par \par \uc2 Baldwin made his first public comments today via social media, saying his heart is broken and tweeting, "There are no words to convey my shock and sadness regarding the tragic accident that took the life of Halyna Hutchins, a wife, mother and deeply admired colleague of ours. I'm fully cooperating with the police investigation to address how this tragedy occurred.\par \par \uc2 The Sheriff's Department issued a statement saying in part "This investigation remains open and active. No charges have been filed in regard to this incident. Witnesses continue to be interviewed by detectives."\par \par \uc2 Those witnesses include the 63-year-old Baldwin, who was seen seemingly distraught and in tears outside the sheriff's office yesterday.\par \par \uc2 The shooting recalls a similar tragedy back in 1993, when 28-year-old Brandon Lee, son of the martial arts icon Bruce Lee was shot and killed with a prop gun. Those who handled weapons on movie sets say safety protocols introduced since then should now prevent any accidental shooting.\par \par \uc2 SAM DORMER, SUPERVISING ARMOURER AND DIRECTOR, ALAMO ARMS: There are lots of checks in place, for instance, in the Brandon Lee incident, there was an obstruction in the barrel which came out once the blank was put behind it. And nowadays, all weapons are checked, the barrel is checked before any blanks are put into the weapon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT (on camera): And tonight, the L.A. Times is reporting that the prop gun fired by Baldwin had previously misfired several times on set, also reporting that there were safety concerns among crew members and that hours before the shooting, a half dozen camera crew workers had walked off set to protest working conditions, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tragic. Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the ships keep coming but there's no place for them to offload. We'll take you to the supply chain crisis.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 5 in New York where real estate mogul Robert Durst is charged with the death of his wife, Kathie Durst who vanished in 1982. Durst is accused of second degree murder.\par \par \uc2 Durst was sentenced last week in California to life in prison for killing a friend in 2000.\par \par \uc2 Fox 2 in Detroit as hundreds of filters are distributed to residents of Hamtramck due to high lead results in drinking water samples. The mayor there says outdated service lines are leaching lead into the water of individual homes. The city says another 900 water filters will be distributed next week.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look in Seattle from Fox 13. One of the big stories there tonight. A record number of humpback whales have been born this season. Whale watchers and researchers have recorded 21 calves documented throughout inland Washington and British Columbia. One expert says it is the highest annual number on record.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We're outside the beltway too. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A New York jury has convicted a former associate of \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  of making illegal campaign contributions to influence politicians, and advance his business interests.\par \par \uc2 Lev Parnas\uc2  was accused of using other people's money to pose as a powerful political broker and influence some of the nation's star Republican political figures. The case had drawn interest because Parnas has had been involved in Giuliani's efforts to get Ukrainian officials to investigate President Biden's son, Hunter. Giuliani was not charged in that case.\par \par \uc2 Fingers are being pointed tonight as the supply chain crisis worsens. It is a complex problem with many possible culprits. National correspondent William La Jeunesse shows us from the \uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Being a truck driver, I think it's all in the ports.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): 10 days after President Biden promised to speed things up, congestion at the ports is worse.\par \par \uc2 GENE SEROKA\uc2 , EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, PORT OF LOS ANGELES: That's like compressing 10 lanes of freeway traffic into five.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: On the water and off, that traffic piles up at America's largest port. A record 109 ships, wait to offload their cargo, half don't even have an assigned place to anchor just outside the ports, forcing the rest to simply drift in waters well outside San Pedro Bay.\par \par \uc2 Going to 24/7 operations was supposed to jumpstart improvements in the supply chain, right now, there's no evidence it has.\par \par \uc2 LANCE CUNNINGHAM, FREIGHT COMPANY PRESIDENT: 24/7 is just basically placating the public to saying that we're actually maybe doing something.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Truckers say a shortage of warehouse workers is one problem. The other, a lack of chassis to haul containers.\par \par \uc2 CUNNINGHAM: The extra, you know, four or five hours of the -- that the ports are being open, they're not -- I can't return an empty when that -- when that -- in that extra time that we're doing there. They're only allowing -- they're only allowing containers to go out that are already on chassis on wheels. That's my whole problem, I don't have a chassis to bring in.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: While containers stack up at the ports, the economy slows, businesses get hurt.\par \par \uc2 KURT GEORGE, SUPPLY CHAIN EXPERT: Companies are losing money, because they're not able to get products on the shelves.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: As losses increase, so do insurance claims on spoiled food and time-sensitive deliveries.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE: There is no creative way or quick fix to this problem right now. Until the goods, both the end-user goods and the components get into the country and get into the factories and the manufacturers' hands. Until then, we're going to start running out of things.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Who do you think can do more?\par \par \uc2 DAVID CRUZ, TRUCK DRIVER: I leave it up to the ports and Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): So, the container ship really revolutionize the way the world moves cargo. It fueled the rise of China and our dependence on it. What we're seeing right now is the global supply chain overwhelmed and there are no silver bullets, we're told, to end it anytime soon. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: William La Jeunesse, in the water off \uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2 . William, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Now, to some good news for a change. There is renewed hope tonight for people with kidney problems. Doctors in New York have pulled off a breakthrough procedure involving the use of a non-human organ.\par \par \uc2 Fox News contributor Dr. Marc Siegel has the breaking details tonight on this. Good evening, Dr. Siegel.\par \par \uc2 DR. MARC SIEGEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): Hi, Bret. Dr. Robert Montgomery, chief of surgery and founding director of the Transplant Institute at NYU, and my colleague at NYU, has a long passionate history as a kidney transplant pioneer, driven by his own personal need for a heart transplant.\par \par \uc2 We could up -- we caught up with Dr. Montgomery and got a look at the groundbreaking surgery that was performed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT MONTGOMERY, DIRECTOR, TRANSPLANT INSTITUTE, \uc2 NEW YORK UNIVERSITY\uc2 , LANGONE MEDICAL CENTER: We want to make sure that as many people as possible have that opportunity.\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: Dr. Montgomery pioneered a lottery system for mixing and matching donors and recipients that has saved 1000s of lives.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: Unfortunately, nearly half of the people who are waiting for an organ won't make it. We came up with this idea that, that person would give their kidney to someone who had been living but incompatible donor, and then that donor would give to someone else. And so, you can form this single, you know, act of altruism.\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: But the massive organ shortage continues. So, the first 2-1/2 day successful transplant of a genetically altered pig kidney to a human recipient is clearly groundbreaking.\par \par \uc2 Montgomery says the surgery was no more difficult than a human operation, with the pig gene that would have caused rejection deleted. Immunosuppression, then, worked.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: We can say that -- you know, it's -- it functioned well, it did all the things that a kidney is supposed to do. It acted like a human kidney transplant. And we didn't see any evidence of you know, a rapid rejection.\par \par \uc2 So, the kidney did make urine almost immediately like moments after we joined blood vessels.\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: Montgomery says that the next phase will be studying longer periods after transplant to make sure that the pig kidney isn't rejected. I asked him which organ came next.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: I think you know, the closest organ to the kidney in terms of being kind of ready for primetime is the heart.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL (on camera): He calls it a game-changer, pig kidney, and then the heart. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's a great story. Dr. Siegel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, we're entering the home stretch in the Virginia governor's race and it's down to the wire. We'll have