iasm among Democrats, President Biden is set to hit the campaign trail in Virginia next week, despite low approval numbers.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  lobbied for McAuliffe last night.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This race is tight. And what happens here, I promise you is about the people of the state and the people of our country.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Harris also lent her voice to a video message playing out in black churches across the commonwealth.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I believe that my friend \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  is the leader Virginia needs at this moment.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Critics have questioned the legality of the video given that \uc2 IRS\uc2  rules prohibit church involvement with political campaigns. On the push for minority voters, Democrat Doug Wilder, Virginia's only black governor, wrote this when McAuliffe announced his run, quote, "Why should he expect that vote to be there for him in 2021 having shown such little appreciation for it."\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  will campaign with McAuliffe in Richmond tomorrow as Glenn Youngkin's bus tour will kick off as well some 115 miles away in northern Virginia's Fairfax County.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): New early voting data shows that nine-and-a-half percent of registered voters have so far returned their ballot, with registered Democrats largely outnumbering Republicans. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A religious organization whose 17 members were kidnapped in Haiti is asking supporters to pray tonight and share stories with the victims' families of how their faith helped them through difficult times. Ohio based \uc2 Christian Aid\uc2  Ministries issued the statement a day after a video was released showing the leader of the kidnappers threatening to kill those abducted if his demands are not met. Haitian officials have said the gang is seeking $1 million ransom per person.\par \par \uc2 China is urging the U.S. to avoid what it calls sending any wrong signals about Taiwan. This comes after President Biden said at that town hall the U.S. would come to the defense of Taiwan in the event of a Chinese attack. The White House clarified that as well today, too.\par \par \uc2 Senior foreign affairs correspondent Greg Palkot tells us where things stand tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: Has the U.S. taken one step closer to being involved in a clash between China and the island of Taiwan. President Biden appeared to last night.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I don't want a cold war with China. I just want to make China understand that we are not going to step back. We are not going to change any of our views.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ROY COOPER, (D) NORTH CAROLINA: So are you saying that the United States would come to Taiwan's defense if China attacked?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes, we have a commitment to do that.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Taiwan officials who have seen Chinese military aircraft enter their region cited America's rock-solid support. China, which assert Taiwan as its own, was also quick to react.\par \par \uc2 WANG WENBIN\uc2 , CHINESE FOREIGN MINISTER: We urge the U.S. to be cautious with its words and actions on the Taiwan issue and not to send any wrong signals.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: America's Taiwan relations act is purposefully vague, stating "the United States shall make available to Taiwan such defense articles and defense services in such quantity as may be necessary to enable Taiwan to maintain a sufficient self-defense capacity." Experts say tampering with this strategic ambiguity risks war with China.\par \par \uc2 DEAN CHENG, HERITAGE FOUNDATION: Providing a blank check, so to speak, creates then a misimpression on Taiwan that maybe they can declare independence, which would absolutely destabilize the Taiwan Straits situation.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: At a \uc2 NATO\uc2  meeting in Brussels, Defense Secretary Austin appeared to be doing damage control.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN, DEFENSE SECRETARY: No one wants to see strait issues come to blows, and certainly not President Biden. And there is no reason that it should.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: For the immediate future, analysts say Chinese President Xi will hold back on any Taiwan invasion, not wanting to disrupt next year's Winter Olympics in Beijing and the Chinese Communist Party conference which Xi should get a new mandate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): White house spokesperson \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  also said today President Biden was not announcing any change in America's Taiwan policy. Experts say it was China threatening the island, that's a safe way to go. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Greg Palkot in London. Greg, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on why White House officials would not mind a few do- overs on some of President Biden's answers last night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. ROY COOPER, (D) NORTH CAROLINA: Do you have plans to visit the southern border.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have been there before, and I haven't -- I know it well. I guess I should go down, but the whole point of it is I haven't had a whole hell of a lot of time to get down.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: There has been reporting that he did dry through the border when he was on the campaign trail in 2008. He does not need a visit to the border to know what a mess was left by the last administration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The White House saying he drove past the border during the 2008 campaign, and saying even though he said that maybe he should go down, that he didn't really mean, the president didn't in that town hall, that he would go down. Town hall needed to be cleaned up today by the White House press secretary in a couple of different points.\par \par \uc2 Meantime Gallup, the approval analysis for Gallup says that the Biden's average third quarter job approval rating is down eight points from his second quarter 53 percent, 11 points from his first quarter rating of 56 percent. This 11-point decline is larger than any prior president registered between first and third quarters, although it is similar to those for the last three Democratic presidents, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , Clinton, and Carter.\par \par \uc2 So let's bring in our panel, Ben Domenech, publisher of "The Federalist," Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, CEO of Empowerment and Inclusion Capital, and Jason Riley, "Wall Street Journal" columnist and senior fellow at the Manhattan Institute. Jason, there were a number of incidents that the White House had to clarify. We pointed them out here. The border, the situation about gas prices, and maybe most importantly, about Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 JASON RILEY, COLUMNIST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": Yes, there are a number of issues going on here. I would add to that, Bret, the spending debate going on in Congress right now. And I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's problem is that he is trying to present as a Democrat versus Republican issue when, in reality, particularly with regard to this spending bill, it's a Democrat versus Democrat problem. He can't get his own ducks in a row. And you have a debate going on between progressives in the Democratic Party and more moderates in the Democratic Party on how to spin this spending bill that they want to unleash.\par \par \uc2 And it frankly boils down to their ambitions being greater than their actual mandate, that the American people did not elect a progressive president to pursue a progressive agenda, and Biden is trying to slip one by the American people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "The Washington Post," Harold, writes it this way about that town hall, "President Biden had one central message for disappointed progressives at his CNN town hall last night -- please be patient. The problem with Biden's promises to come back to progressive priorities is that his razor thin Democratic majorities in the House and Senate won't be any bigger and Republicans won't be any more inclined to go along, which is why progressives have been urging him for months to fight to get rid of the filibuster," something he said he might actually be for now.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: Well, thanks for having me on. This is -- I think Jason nailed it with the frame. Progressives didn't and don't have a massive majority in the House or even a comfortable majority in the House, for that matter, the Senate. I think when we elected President Biden, the country did, they were electing someone they thought would bring a level of stability and maturity and experience to decision- making and to the White House. And to Jason's point, the president has spent most of his time trying to referee disputes between and amongst his own party members.\par \par \uc2 I have said for some time, Democrats take the victory. Give the country what they have asked for, which is an infrastructure bill. Demonstrate that you can govern and lead and then come back and do the other things, which I think at some point President Biden is going to have to say to Democrats in a forthright way. I hope he doesn't have to say in a forthright way with an outcome in Virginia that is detrimental to my party. \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  has asked for Democrats to give us this victory and give us a chance to lead and to govern in the lead majority, which is the way you actually enact policy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : Until they do that, I think it's going to be hard for my party.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I don't see how it's going to get done before that Virginia election, but we'll see