tened by that.\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL: Yes, because children are very threatening, because families are threatening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well exactly. If you find yourself against families, you're on the wrong side.\par \par \uc2 That conversation went on for an hour. You can watch the whole thing, our interview with Ian Samuel on "Tucker Carlson Today." It's on foxnation.com along with a growing library of hours of interviews. Well, they are good.\par \par \uc2 By the way early this hour, we did a segment on UFOs. The government apparently is going to make a permanent office to investigate these things. Our apologies by the way, we do a segment on this topic once a month maybe, usually on a Friday.\par \par \uc2 This is the largest story of our lifetime. The government has determined these are not of human origin. They're not actually Russian or Chinese. We don't know where they are from.\par \par \uc2 They behave in a way that defies physics apparently and they're constantly buzzing our military installations. Why is this not huge? It is huge.\par \par \uc2 And without shame, we're going to stay on it for months to come.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. Have the best weekend with the ones you love. We'll see you 8:00 p.m. Monday.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020211002eha100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Trying To Salvage Domestic Agenda Legislation; Social Spending Bill Cost Still Uncertain; New Subpoena Issued To Clinton- Connected Law Firm; Polls Show Glenn Youngkin And \uc2 Terry Mcauliffe\uc2  In Tight Race; McAuliffe And Youngkin Spar In Fiery Debate; Youngkin Faces McAuliffe In Virginia Gubernatorial Race; Youngkin Relationship With Former President; Schools Nationwide Deal With Staff Shortages; Defense Department Officials Testify Before Senate Concerning Intelligence On Imminent Collapse Of Kabul Government In Afghanistan After U.S. Troop Withdrawal; North Korean Leader Kim Jong-un Makes Diplomatic Overtures To South Korea; Democrats Continue Negotiations Over Passing Bipartisan Infrastructure Bill And Budget Reconciliation Bill\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jennifer Griffin, Greg Palkot, Trey Gowdy, Mara  Liasson, Ben Domenech, Chad Pergram, Jacqui Heinrich, David Spunt \par \uc2 7,972 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 September 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hello there, Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I don't know what I would do if I saw that coming down the aisle. I'm not sure. All right, Martha, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, welcome to Washington, I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, does she have the votes tonight or not? A question frequently heard on Capitol Hill in the past few hours, all about House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and a potential vote on a bipartisan infrastructure bill $1.2 trillion without a massive reconciliation spending bill at $3.5 trillion.\par \par \uc2 Frantic phone calls, meetings at the White House and on Capitol Hill, progressives versus moderates. But Democratic Senator Joe Manchin from West Virginia put his foot down today, a $3.5 trillion bill will not get his needed vote in the Senate. So, what will? That's where we are at this hour at this minute.\par \par \uc2 There was a victory for leadership today in the form of legislation to avoid a partial federal government shutdown at least until early December, punting that fiscal cliff a few months. But most things including a debt ceiling increase and the future of the Biden legislative agenda hanging in the balance tonight as SPECIAL REPORT kicks off.\par \par \uc2 We have \uc2 Fox\uc2  team coverage, Jacqui Heinrich at the White House tells us what the administration is doing to try to get the key votes. But we begin with Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill with where things stand. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. It's Democrat against Democrat, the left battling centrist. Moderate Democrat Joe Manchin calling 3.5 trillion in social spending "fiscal insanity".\par \par \uc2 For days Manchin said he was not negotiating a price tag. But a memo obtained by \uc2 Fox\uc2  says Manchin has been committed to only 1.5 trillion since summertime. Manchin says he worries about the nation becoming a welfare state.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): I don't fault any of them who believe that they're much more progressive and much more liberal. God bless them. And all they need to do is we have to elect more -- I guess for them to get theirs, elect more liberals.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): That inflame the left, protesters heckling Manchin at the Capitol .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Joe Manchin is a traitor, (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): So, this is really about the deficit, then maybe we go after our defense spending and find our savings there instead of going after people's childcare.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Liberals now threatened to yank their support from the bipartisan infrastructure bill scheduled for a vote today.\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-WA): We reiterated this again to the speaker and we're in the same place that we will not be able to vote for the reconcile -- for the infrastructure bill until the reconciliation bill has passed.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: As many as 50 progressives could vote no, more than 10 GOP members could vote yes. But the math doesn't add up. It begs the question, is the president's domestic agenda too ambitious in a split Senate and a House with a three-vote margin?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  , (D-CA): From what I imply from -- infer from what you say is that we should have just done nothing because we had a slim majority? That's not who we are.\par \par \uc2 No, it's not too big. No, it's not too big. In fact, it will be a compromise.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Pelosi believes the bill will pass maybe later tonight. The House Majority Whip \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  says he hasn't whipped the vote and some good news Congress voted to avert a government shutdown. Tonight, the lights remain on until December 3rd, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad Pergram up on the Hill. Chad, thanks. We'll head back for any breaking details.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has taken a hit as we've told you in the polls recently, but that has not stopped him and his staff from working the phones, trying to cut deals and get those key votes on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 What we do know tonight is that the price tag for his agenda appears to be dropping overall. Here's White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): After days of talks brokered by the White House, a new but fluid price tag on President Biden's social infrastructure package, no longer $3.5 trillion, now somewhere in the low to mid $2 trillion range with the caveat that nothing appears to be final.\par \par \uc2 Perceived progress was drowned out when a leaked memo Senator Joe Manchin sent to \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  this summer put with the West Virginia Democrats top line number at 1.5 trillion in social spending.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I've never been -- I've never been a liberal in any way, shape or form. There's no one has ever thought I was.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Progressives patience wearing thin, feeling emboldened to tank support for the hard infrastructure bill that still needs to pass the House. The roads and bridges funding touted by the president as a bipartisan win in front of the Oval Office now hanging in the balance, adding pressure for the president who campaigned on his ability to unite people to pull his own party together.\par \par \uc2 Some people say that it appears that progressives are running the show, they're banding together and making their demands. Other people are saying looks like Joe Manchin is playing president. So, who is in charge?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2  , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Well, this is how democracy works. We're not trying to paint over how messy it looks from the outside, we know that. But what the good news is, is that there is agreement that among most Democrats, if not every single one of them that we need to get something done.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Republicans say the food fight that's been playing out is no surprise.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): They have these radical left-wing nut cases who basically want to -- they want it to be seven trillion, not 3.5 trillion, and they think 3.5 trillion is a compromise.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wasn't elected because he was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  . He was elected as a vessel, right? He was -- he's there because he became sort of an instrument for the far left and now dominates the Democratic Party to pursue their agenda.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Now, there was some talk at the White House today that the president might go to the Hill at some point. At this point, it doesn't appear that that's going to be happening.\par \par \uc2 White House aides were huddled in Speaker Pelosi is office for hours trying to strike some sort of a deal and we haven't gotten any updates from that, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, we'll follow it. Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thank you. Stocks were off today. The Dow lost 547, the S&P 500 fell 52, the NASDAQ dropped 64.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, there is more movement in the investigation into the origins of the Trump Russia collusion probe. The special prosecutor \uc2 John Durham\uc2  is going after another individual with connections to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt is live tonight from the Justice Department with the latest specifics. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening to you. The announcement of this subpoena tonight comes just a couple of weeks after a former Clinton campaign attorney from 2016 was indicted by special counsel Durham for lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Fox News can confirm tonight that this subpoena specifically involves a man named Rodney Joffe according to a source with knowledge of the investigation.\par \par \uc2 Our sources Bret, they tell us that Joffe's name appears in the indictment of Michael Sussman. Sussman is the former \uc2 Perkins Coie\uc2  attorney who the special counsel says brought information to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  about an alleged server the Trump campaign used to communicate with the Kremlin through a Russian bank. Information Bret, that just did not pan out.\par \par \uc2 Joffe's attorney denounced the allegation of Fox News saying in part, this indictment of cherry picked portions of e-mails and selective facts that gratuitously present an incomplete and misleading picture of his actions and role in the events in question.\par \par \uc2 It is not clear in what capacity Michael Sussman was acting as against the Trump campaign. According to special counsel Durham, Sussman claimed he was doing it as a good citizen when he delivered the information.\par \par \uc2 In 2-1/2 years, \uc2 John Durham\uc2  charged two people in the probes, Sussman and this man Kevin Clinesmith, a former \uc2 FBI\uc2  attorney who pleaded guilty to falsifying a document used to apply for the application to surveil Trump campaign aide \uc2 Carter Page\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): While Clinesmith pleaded guilty, there's no indication that Sussman wants to do so. He plans to fight this case.\par \par \uc2 But Bret, this subpoena tonight is significant because it shows that \uc2 John Durham\uc2  is still hard at work at this case. More than 2-1/2 years later, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow it. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Former Trump campaign manager \uc2 Corey Lewandowski\uc2  is out of a job tonight. This follows an accusation he inappropriately touched a female Trump fundraiser during a recent charity dinner.\par \par \uc2 He will no longer be leading former President Trump's Make America Great Again political action committee.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we will talk with the Republican gubernatorial nominee in Virginia who's giving a former governor -- Democratic governor a tougher than expected fight for his old job. We'll also have brand new Fox News polls on this bellwether race.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 4 in Dallas-Fort Worth were four firefighters and four civilians were taken to the hospital after a gas explosion at an apartment complex. The blast shattered windows on neighboring buildings, throwing debris into a nearby field. Nearly 300 people are out of their homes as firefighters investigate that incident.\par \par \uc2 KHON in Honolulu where the Kilauea volcano erupts for the second time in a year. Lava fountains cover the floor of the crater on the Big Island as clouds of volcanic ash and gas rise into the air.\par \par \uc2 Official say the eruption is contained to that crater and no residents are in danger there.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Cleveland from our affiliate Fox 8, the big story there tonight, are you feeling lucky? A gas station in Powell, Ohio sold a $2 million winning ticket in the latest Powerball drawing. The winner has 180 days from now to claim the prize, so check your tickets on that.\par \par \uc2 But the Powerball jackpot is now $620 million for Saturday's drawing, the sixth highest ever. So, there you go.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Virginia governor's race appears to be much closer than many people had expected. Our latest Fox polls has Republican Glenn Youngkin trailing by only four percentage points to former governor Democratic nominee \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  . The margin of error there you see is plus three, minus three.\par \par \uc2 Both men have 79 percent certainty among their supporters. The top issue for McAuliffe's people, the coronavirus pandemic. Youngkin's supporters are most concerned about the economy.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we're talking with Republican nominee Glenn Youngkin. Thanks for coming in.\par \par \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN (R), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE OF VIRGINIA: Bret, thank you for having me. And I just appreciate the fact that this race is front and front and center on your mind because it's front and center in Virginian's minds and we have a chance to chart a different direction in Virginia and I'm looking forward to having voters come to the polls over the next 32 days and decide this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, a lot of people look at it as a bellwether, especially in this current environment. You've had a couple of debates now, one just the other night which we -- which we covered.\par \par \uc2 Polls are one thing, but where do you see the race and what issues are most important from your point of view?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, the race is a toss-up right now. I feel amazing amount of momentum, particularly over the last six to seven weeks as we've been talking to voters and listening to voters about cutting taxes and fixing our schools and making our communities safe again, and getting our economy moving.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of Virginians who are really concerned about whether their job is going to be there for them. And so, those are the areas that we're focused most on. And that is where Virginians are focused most on.\par \par \uc2 And you're right, Bret, this race has national importance because what happens in Virginia is going to have a huge impact on what happens in congressional elections next year, and this is our chance for Virginians to come together and speak for the whole country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, one of the issues, I want to talk to you about schools, because that got a lot of attention. And you made one answer into an ad right away, but I want to talk about another issue that wasn't covered as much, and that's abortion and the difference between you two. Let's take a listen to this exchange.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: I am pro-life. I do believe in exceptions in the case where there's rape or incest, or the mother's life is in jeopardy. My opponent, on the other hand, is the most extreme abortion candidate in the country.\par \par \uc2 He actually has said he's going to promote Virginia businesses to come. For companies to come to Virginia because it's the easiest place to get an abortion. I think that's shameful.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCAULIFFE\uc2  (D), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE, VIRGINIA: I want every woman in Virginia to listen to me closely. I was a brick wall to protect women's rights when I was governor.\par \par \uc2 The businesses that I brought to Virginia, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  , \uc2 Google\uc2  , \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MCAULIFFE: They are not coming to a state that discriminates against women.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MCAULIFFE: They're not.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. --\par \par \uc2 MCAULIFFE: Half the stuff he said came from outer space tonight. I mean, he literally could have had a tinfoil hat on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, it seems like there's a big chasm on the issue of abortion. But what about his charge that, that what you were saying is not right?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, it's -- he's -- Terry does one thing this entire election, which is combat truth with his untruths. I mean, he said that he would sign the bill that would use taxpayer money to fund abortions up through and including birth. He said it was common-sense legislation and that he would sign it.\par \par \uc2 And now he wants to try to walk away from that. This is what he does. This is what a 43-year career politician who's been working in the swamp his whole life does is defend something by saying it's untrue.\par \par \uc2 The reality is that \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  is the most pro-abortion candidate in America. The vast majority of Virginians disagree with his position. And to say that companies are going to come to Virginia because we have the most liberal abortion laws is absolutely insanity.\par \par \uc2 We just watched \uc2 Ford Motor Company\uc2  make a decision with four plants. They put two in Kentucky, one in Tennessee, and one in Georgia. And let me tell you, \uc2 Ford\uc2  was making a decision based on workforce, on site of male availability, on the ability to make sure that that state is going to maintain their right to work status.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  has threatened to get rid of right to work. He's told everybody he'll sign the bill. That will be a death blow for Virginia's business community.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: And I'm going to protect right to work. And this is absolutely on the ballot, and \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  's on the wrong side of it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, listen, the most important issues according to our latest Fox poll, number one is the economy, number two is the coronavirus, and what happens with that down the line, you get to abortion, crime, education.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to coronavirus, will there be an effort, if your governor to repeal rollback vaccine mandates, mask mandates that Governor Northam in Virginia has put in place?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Yes, I believe that everyone in Virginia should get the vaccine, and I'm going to work tirelessly to help people get the best information so that they can make a decision for themselves to get it.\par \par \uc2 Of course, what \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  wants to do is have companies fire people who don't get the vaccine. I mean, here we are coming out of a pandemic. And Virginia is ranked 44th in the nation when it comes to job recovery.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  wants companies to fire their employees if they don't get the vaccine. I mean, this is what dictatorial leadership is all about. You know, if he doesn't like what the sheriff says, he yells at him the other day. If he doesn't like what she's -- what she -- what parents have to say about schools, he tells that he doesn't want you involved in their education.\par \par \uc2 And in this case, when you actually want to make a decision about whether you get the vaccine or not, if you don't make the right decision for \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  , he wants your employer to fire you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, in some places --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: This is not leadership. This is not leadership.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In some places, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his approval is a factor. In Virginia, according to our poll, it's about even: 50 to 49 approve disapprove. How much do you think what's happening federally, with the potential tax increases, other proposals affect your race come November?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, I think Virginians, just because of our proximity to Washington, D.C., pay a lot of attention to what's happening in Washington. But as I said earlier, I've talked to tens of thousands of Virginians and listen to their concerns. And they're worried about their jobs and the economy?\par \par \uc2 Yes, they're worried that we have to manage our way through this pandemic. We want to do it together so we can protect both lives and livelihoods.\par \par \uc2 They're worried about their kids' education. I mean, parents want their children to receive a great education, and they want to be involved in it. And what we've heard from \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  is he doesn't want them involved in it. And they're worried about public safety.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we're at a 20-year high in murder rate in Virginia, shouldn't shock Terry because when he was governor, the murder rate went up 43 percent. And that's why all of the sheriffs in Virginia, I mean, 50 sheriffs have stood up for me.\par \par \uc2 I was endorsed by -- I was endorsed by the FOP, I was endorsed by the Police Benevolent Association. They understand that I'm going to help Virginia have our community safe again, we're going to have the best jobs, we're going to have the best schools, we're going to cut taxes, we're going to lower the tax burden on Virginians, and that's what they're most, most important to them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing quickly, \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  and his campaign spent a lot of time trying to link you to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . And he did that in the debate saying that you -- he was endorsed -- you were endorsed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , he said four times. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  comes to Virginia, are you going to come campaign with them?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, first of all, when President Trump endorsed me, I thanked him for it. I was -- I was grateful for it. I've been endorsed by lots of Republicans, and we're bringing people together.\par \par \uc2 This is a moment for us to bring Virginians together, forever Trumpers, folks that were never Trumpers, single-issue voters, but also Independents and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 I think what you'll see in your poll is I'm winning amongst the Independents because they trust me. This is a moment for Virginians to come together and decide who they trust.\par \par \uc2 They do not trust \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  . He's a 43-year-career political operative. He changes his mind every other week. He actually lies every other day or if not every other minute.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: And this is what's on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: This is why Virginians are coming together around me as the candidate. And I know Terry is seeing the polling numbers. He wishes he was running against somebody else.\par \par \uc2 But it's \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  against Glenn Youngkin, and we are surging in momentum. And that's why I feel so good about the fact that we're going to win this fall and get Virginia move and come January.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, Glenn Youngkin, we appreciate your time. You're right, President Trump is not on the ballot. We will cover this race very closely. We've asked \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  to come on. We'll see if he does. We appreciate your time today.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Thank you so much for having me. I hope you have a great day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, pandemic vaccine mandates are creating staff shortages in schools. We'll take a look at that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL CARDONA\uc2  , UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF EDUCATION: It's all hands on deck. I think we have the right policy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOMMY TUBERVILLE (R-AL): We can't shut down Alabama or some of these other states.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 PATTY MURRAY\uc2  (D-WA): They are making denial a badge of honor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tomorrow is the deadline for New York City school staff to be vaccinated or risk losing their jobs that could lead to a major personnel problem. Many school districts throughout the country are dealing with similar issues because of the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Rich Edson shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON , FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Teenagers on a party bus with neon lights and a stripper pole. The only ride earlier this month that a group of Boston high school students had for their field trip. Their neon substitute is just part of a nationwide campaign of impromptu fixes to major school staff shortages.\par \par \uc2 Also, in Massachusetts, Governor \uc2 Charlie Baker\uc2  deployed about 250 National Guardsmen to drive school buses.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARDONA: It's all hands on deck. I think we have the right policy.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: This morning on Capitol Hill, Education Secretary \uc2 Miguel Cardona\uc2  told the Senate committee that part of the solution is federal money for better salaries and training.\par \par \uc2 CARDONA: If we're serious about reopening schools and making sure our children have the best opportunity to engage not only in the classroom, but extracurricular activities or getting to school on time, we have to make sure that we're addressing these workforce gaps boldly.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: In Brooklyn, reports of staff shortages, forcing a school cafeteria to close. And starting Monday, the nation's largest school system, New York City, is requiring the bulk of its workers to show proof of vaccination.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders there are warning of employee shortages. Others from states with lower vaccination rates say mandates there would create major problems in school districts.\par \par \uc2 TUBERVILLE: We can't shut down Alabama or some of these other states simply for the fact that we might not be taking as many vaccines.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: City officials in New York say they'll have 1000s of substitutes standing by for teachers who fail to present vaccination cards. Union officials say they're also concerned about staff beyond teachers like custodians, cafeteria workers, and safety officers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): Just this afternoon, a federal appeals court ruled New York State must temporarily allow vaccine exemptions for healthcare workers with religious objections.\par \par \uc2 The judge will hear arguments in that case in a couple of weeks. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Just in the past few minutes, we got word from our Supreme Court team Shannon Bream, Bill Mears, that some New York City teachers have filed an emergency request with the Supreme Court to block the vaccine mandate that is about to kick in based on geography alone.\par \par \uc2 This filing would go to Justice Sotomayor, and she can decide alone or refer it to the full court. We will continue to watch this as it develops.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  is deadlocked tonight over President Biden's nominee for U.S. Attorney in Massachusetts. Suffolk County District Attorney \uc2 Rachael Rollins\uc2  is being opposed by Republicans who insist she is soft on crime.\par \par \uc2 Senate Democrats must now hold a vote in the full chamber to bring up her nomination.\par \par \uc2 The regional director of the International Red Cross is warning of a major humanitarian crisis in Afghanistan, because of severe financial shortfalls and the coming of winter. The U.S. withdrawal from that country has been a foreign policy crisis for President Biden and his team.\par \par \uc2 The smoke is still clearing tonight after explosive testimony this week from the president's top defense advisors on Capitol Hill. National security correspondent Jennifer Griffin looks at the fallout from the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 MARK MILLEY\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, JOINT CHIEFS OF STAFF: It's a real possibility in the not too distant future, 6, 12, 18, 24, 36 months, that time and timeframe for reconstitution of al-Qaeda or ISIS.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice- over): Just one of the stunning admissions from this week's hearing. Signs of collapse began in June, yet the White House did not halt the withdrawal.\par \par \uc2 Documents obtained by Reuters show the \uc2 Afghan Central Bank\uc2  depleted its U.S. dollar reserves in the months before the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  took Kabul.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN\uc2  , DEFENSE SECRETARY: We didn't fully comprehend the depth of corruption.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: And yet that corruption was documented for more than a decade by the special inspector general for Afghan reconstruction in quarterly reports required by Congress. Defense officials testified no one anticipated the fall of Kabul in 11 days, but they had intelligence suggesting the Afghan government would fall by Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 MARK MILLEY\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, JOINT CHIEFS: We knew that when we pulled the advisers out, when we pulled the money out, that at some point in the future, most said it was in the fall, that the Afghan security forces were going to fracture and the government would collapse.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Asked why they did not begin evacuating Americans and civilian allies sooner --\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: How to do that and when to do it is really a State Department call.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Today another private charter plane landed in Chicago with American citizens and green card holders on board. Eight military bases in the U.S. are housing 53,000 Afghan evacuees awaiting further screening.\par \par \uc2 GEN. GLEN VANHERCK, U.S. NORTHERN COMMAND AND NORAD: One-hundred percent of the Afghans are COVID tested, and 84 percent have received COVID vaccinations to date.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: All received the MMR measles vaccine, 4,000 are ready for placement in American communities, among them this Afghan air force pilot currently at Fort McCoy Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 HABDUL, FORMER AFGHAN AIR FORCE PILOT: I got my pilot training in the United States. I was in San Antonio one year, one year in Alabama. Wherever I find a job, I want to live in the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): Hundreds of U.S. military veterans have volunteered through private veterans' groups, like Save Our Allies, to provide services to these Afghan evacuees at eight U.S. military bases. Some of these Afghans may be on the military bases until spring. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Republicans are celebrating a big win over Democrats in the congressional baseball game. The GOP side won the back and forth affair by a score of 13 to 12. President Biden was on hand. He was one of four former players inducted into the congressional baseball hall of fame which also enshrined a late president George H. W. Bush. Last night's contest raised $1.2 million for local charities.\par \par \uc2 Up next, North Korea's leader wants to talk with the south and wants some big changes from the U.S., but is still being provocative.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Two FBI agents entered the home of Brian Laundrie and his parents today in North Port, Florida. Laundrie has been identified as a person of interest in the death of his former girlfriend, Gabby Petito. He is also a federal fugitive on a charge of bank fraud. The agents, we're told, stayed for approximately 30 minutes and briefly explored the camper in the driveway at that home before leaving with a bag. After the agents departure, the Laundrie family attorney said the agents collected some of Brian's personal items to assist canines in their search for him.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, police records obtained by \uc2 FOX\uc2  show at least 46 911 calls or response requests related to that home between September 10 and the 27th.\par \par \uc2 Overseas now, North Korea's leader says he is willing to start talking with South Korea again, but Kim Jong-un 's regime continues to conduct weapons testing, including a reported launch of a newly developed anti-aircraft missile just hours ago. Kim is also calling out the U.S. for what he says is its hostile policy. Senior foreign affairs correspondent Greg Palkot has details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: North Korea's Kim Jong-un making nice with his neighbor to the south. A year since Pyongyang destroyed an office aimed at facilitating ties with South Korea in a North Korea border town. Later Kim said this week he wants to restore severed communication between the North and South, moves perhaps aimed at nudging his new nemesis, President Biden, reacting to diplomatic offers like one from Biden at the U.N . last week --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We seek serious and sustained diplomacy to pursue the complete denuclearization of the Korean peninsula.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Kim is reported to have said "It is no more than a petty trick for deceiving the international community and hiding its hostile acts." Kim also said he'd continue to bolster his nuclear arsenal, underscoring that Pyongyang continues to test nuclear capable weaponry. Just this month there's been a cruise missile launch, then train launch mobile missiles, and, this week, what the North claims is a superfast, maneuverable hypersonic missile. Experts are doubtful and say the launch was less than a success. Still, it could be a game changer.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS EBERSTADT\uc2  , AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE : It is significant. Exactly what the significance is I think we'll have to assess in the next few days.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: There's even talk of another summit between Kim Jong-un and South Korean President \uc2 Moon Jae\uc2 -in . The last time they met was at the DMZ in 2019 with then President Trump. But North Korea hit by COVID-19 restrictions, natural disasters, and U.S. backed sanctions, neighbor China is hoping for the best.\par \par \uc2 HUA CHUNYING, \uc2 CHINESE FOREIGN MINISTRY\uc2  : We hope the relevant measure can play a positive role in improving and developing North-South relations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): South Korean President Moon has to constitutionally step down next March, one more reason for him to push his new ally, President Trump, to make some kind of peace with longtime rival Kim Jong- un, no easy feat. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Greg Palkot in London. Greg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on scaling back expectations for the Biden agenda, where things stand at this minute.\par \par \uc2 First Beyond our Borders tonight. High level Russian and U.S. diplomats meet in Geneva as part of strategic talks revived by President Biden and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  at a June summit. Deputy Secretary of State \uc2 Wendy Sherman\uc2  held several hours of discussions with her Russian counterpart. They addressed, we're told, contentious issues such as nuclear weapons, artificial intelligence, and cyberspace.\par \par \uc2 Coronavirus cases surged to a record high in Melbourne, Australia. Australian officials blame the spike on illegal home gatherings to watch a major soccer match. Australia's two largest cities, Sydney and Melbourne, are in a weeks-long lockdown that has resulted in mass protests against the strict virus measures.\par \par \uc2 And Swedish police look for a man in connection with an explosion and fire at a large apartment building in Sweden. Sixteen people were injured in the blast, four of them seriously. Swedish media are reporting the suspect is a man in his 50s who lived with his mother in that building. A local newspaper reports the property owner had been trying to evict them from that apartment.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  , (D-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: Doubt in anyone's mind that we will not have a reconciliation. We will have a reconciliation bill. That is for sure.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRANHOLM\uc2  , ENERGY SECRETARY: It is not some major cataclysm if there isn't a vote today.\par \par \uc2 Mark my words, the infrastructure bill will be passed, and a version of the reconciliation bill will be as well.\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL , (D-WA) HOUSE BUDGET COMMITTEE: We will not be able to vote for the infrastructure bill until the reconciliation bill has passed. So we're in the same place.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN, (D-WV): It's a shame for that, because no two bills should ever be linked together to the point that we're going to let the perfect be the enemy of the good.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Joe Manchin there. At this hour we are told that the House will not have any votes before 9:00 p.m. We don't know the status of whether the infrastructure bill is coming up, whether there's a deal on the reconciliation bill.\par \par \uc2 We do know this -- Joe Manchin laid down a marker today, and "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " took note, saying "Joe Manchin delivers a dagger to the heart of the progressive cause. They can huff and puff all they want, but they won't blow down the West Virginia Democrat's House because he correctly read public opinion. Reengineering the fabric of American life is modern progressivism's whole point. Its adherents believe that the nation is in crisis. Crises demand immediately and wide-ranging action, not incremental change or tinkering around the edges. Their ambitions, however, can only prevail in a democracy if they are supported by public opinion."\par \par \uc2 That's where we are at this hour. Let's bring in our panel, Ben Domenech, publisher of "The Federalist," \uc2 Mara Liasson\uc2  , national political correspondent of National Public Radio, and \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2  , former Congressman from South Carolina. Mara, we don't know where this is going. We do know that it's going to be less than $3.5 trillion in the reconciliation bill, if it happens.\par \par \uc2 MARA LIASSON\uc2  , NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, NATIONAL PUBLIC RADIO: Yes, it's definitely going to be less. I think we've known that for quite some time. But we just didn't know the actual parameters of the negotiation. And I think the big progress that was made today is that Joe Manchin said in public before a camera what he's been saying in private and actually on paper in this memo that he and \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  signed, that his number, top line number is $1.5 trillion. He has some other substantive demands. He wants new entitled programs, social safety net programs to be means tested. He wants to roll back the Trump tax cuts, including carried interest. So he has a whole lot of things that he cares about.\par \par \uc2 Now, he did not say that this was a hard and fast number and he wasn't flexible at all. He said, we're going to negotiate. So, somewhere between $3.5 trillion and $1.5 trillion is where they'll end up if they can get a deal among themselves.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And that's a big if, Trey, listening to progressives saying, listen, we're going to vote against this, the infrastructure, bipartisan infrastructure bill, if we don't have a deal, a vote on reconciliation.\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2  , FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA REPRESENTATIVE: Yes, Bret. I give credit to the progressives in the House. They watched very carefully as the Freedom Caucus on the Republican side proved that you can get a lot more famous opposing your own leadership than you can fighting with the other side. And fame has become the ultimate political virtue.\par \par \uc2 I will say this. \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  used the word "democracy." "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " used the world "democracy." Let's lay aside the fact that they tried to ram it through with a parliamentarian and put everything in one bill. Let's give them credit for liking democracy.\par \par \uc2 Bret, you and I won the same number of counties in West Virginia that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did. And Arizona was less than one half of a percentage point. So don't be lecturing Joe Manchin and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  about democracy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaking of \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  , she was asked about the White House potentially losing control of this whole deal today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2  , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: This is how democracy works. I know it feels foreign because there wasn't much that happened over the last couple of years. But how it works is the American people elect their elected officials. The president of the United States puts forward a bold and ambitious proposal, and everybody negotiates about it. And they have different points of view. That's how democracy should work. We're in the midst of it right now. We're not trying to paint over how messy it looks from the outside. We know that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ben?\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, PUBLISHER, "THE FEDERALIST": Well, we're not actually a democracy, just to point that out for a point of clarification. If we were, then we would be fine with majorities running pell-mell toward all of these types of extreme steps, even if they were slimmest of majorities. There's an important reason, and the founders really believed in it, that we are not that way, and our government does not work that way.\par \par \uc2 And that's why Joe Manchin is so important in this moment. Look, he reads the politics very well. Not just in his own state, but in, I think, the broader dynamic. And I think in this moment there ought to be a lot of critiques of the approach that \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  used, because at the end of the day, she may have missed an opportunity to achieve a package that could be much more perhaps than what Manchin's number would be, but far less than what progressives are currently demanding. Taking the step that she did in terms of linking these measures together in the way that I think was politically unwise is coming back to bite her in a big way today.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We always knew that Senator Manchin was going to be crucial in this whole thing. We've talked to him here on this show many times and asked him specific questions. Today, Mara, you're right. With the $1.5 trillion top line that he talked about, take a listen to this and reaction on the progressive side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN, (D-WV): I guess for them to get theirs, elect more liberals. But I'm not asking them to change. I'm willing to come from zero to $1.5 trillion.\par \par \uc2 My top line has been $1.5 trillion because I believe in my heart that what we can do and with the needs we have right now and what we can afford to do without basically changing our whole society to an entitlement mentality.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ, (D-NY): Which senator are we negotiating with? Is this June Manchin? Is it September Manchin? Is it August Manchin? Will December Manchin be a different -- will this be a different Senator that pops up?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: It's actually July 28th Manchin. That's the date of the memo.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, that's right.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: And it hasn't changed since then.\par \par \uc2 But look, what he said was just basic facts. The reason Democrats are in this fix is because they have a gigantic agenda and a miniscule majority. And they failed to win the extra seats in the House and Senate that they thought they were going to win in 2020, that Republicans thought they were going to win in 2020, and that's just reality. And as Joe Manchin said today, I have never been a liberal. He's representing a state that Trump won by, what, 29 points? So like that song in "Hamilton," they don't have the votes, and they're going to have to compromise. And that's just the way it is.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey, you've seen this play before. And Speaker Pelosi has pulled rabbits out of hats legislatively before. We don't know what's going to happen tonight. Do you think she could cobble this together?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: It depends. It depends on how many Republican senators continue to call Republican members of the House and ask them to vote for the infrastructure bill. So Kevin McCarthy has a job to do here, too, which is keep the Republicans coalesced. I love AOC's political strategy, which is insult the person whose vote you need. I just don't think it's going to work.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But let's take you to the flipside, Ben. What about those moderate Republicans who look at the $1.2 trillion infrastructure bill and say, this is a chance to finally build that bridge, to finally fix that road that I have been talk about fixing to my constituents forever. Maybe I should vote for it. Is the politics of the moment more important than the bridge at home?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: I think that's the big question, but headed into a midterm year, delivery of that bridge is going to become less and less of their priority.\par \par \uc2 And one more note on this. The kind of jabs that we've seen not just at Joe Manchin but today from some progressives at Arizona Senator Sinema have really become more and more aggressive during this point. The idea said by one member today that she hasn't shown us anything but a designer purse. That's not the kind of insult that's going to go across very well with someone who is navigating a situation and who, last I checked has, 40 percent popularity among Republicans in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And either one of them could become Republicans and change the dynamic. You get Senate Majority Leader McConnell. All right, thank you all. When we come back, a look at the newly renovated Washington Bureau.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Fancy. Finally tonight, FOX News is marking the 25th anniversary this year. We've covered a lot of stories in that time, and a lot have come from here in our Washington Bureau, where this program originates. As we look back on the past quarter century, we're also moving forward with a special ceremony here just a short time ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A state-of-the-art, all-digital newsroom, the FOX News Washington Bureau reopened after a major renovation. Two new flagship studios, a super high resolution technology platform for video and graphics, and our newsroom.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: David is replacing on two, right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Reconfigured to allow producers and editors to easily monitor the ever-growing number of satellite video feeds on 75-inch TV screens. \uc2 FOX Corporation\uc2  CEO \uc2 Lachlan Murdoch\uc2  calls it an investment in the future for the top-rated news channel.\par \par \uc2 LACHLAN MURDOCH\uc2  , \uc2 FOX CORPORATION\uc2  CEO: And the reason is to give all of you the space and environment to continue to do your very best work.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Another behind the scenes feature of our new bureau, two very special places honoring two very special people. First, the Charles Krauthammer Green Room. If you've watched SPECIAL REPORT, you know how important he was to the show and the channel. We miss his words of wisdom every day. This plaque says in memory of our beloved colleague, friend, and patriot.\par \par \uc2 This is the Tony Snow Radio Studio in honor of our friend who passed away 13 years ago, the host of FOX News Sunday. This is to remember him every day.\par \par \uc2 JAY WALLACE, FOX NEWS MEDIA PRESIDENT: The heartbeat of the news operation since FOX News Sunday kicked off 25 years ago this past spring.\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE SCOTT\uc2  , FOX NEWS MEDIA CEO: This is also a moment to remember an extraordinary voice and gentle giant in our business.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This renovation was long in the planning. The original FOX News Washington Bureau was our home through the major news events of our time -- impeachments, wars, congressional fights, Bush v Gore, and 9/11. But an ever-evolving news environment has inspired us to redesign our new offices bigger and better to complement the latest technology along with our solid storytelling and reporting.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's great digs, 25 years later, we'll be looking back over the next couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we'll talk about the coronavirus pandemic with former \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  commissioner Dr. Scott Gottlieb .\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching us. Thanks for the SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "FOX NEWS PRIMETIME" hosted this by Brian Kilmeade starts right now. Brian?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020211001eh9u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Left Loves To Lie When It Benefits Them; Team Biden Embraces China & Criminals; Border Czar Kamala Lying About What's Happening; Biden Repeats Lies About Border Agents; DHS Sec Takes No Responsibility For Border Crisis; DHS Sec Can't Keep His Border Story Straight; CDC Director Rewrites History On Booster Decision; Dems Stash Radical Policies In Defense Bill; Democratic Defense Bill Includes Processes for Military Courts to Issue Protective Orders Making Accessing Firearms Illegal; Late Night Talk Show Host Discuss Climate Change; Nancy Pelosi Defense Her Pro- Choice Position; College and University Campuses across U.S. Promulgate and Attempt to Enforce Speech Codes.\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Matt Finn \par \uc2 7,369 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 September 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Laura Ingraham and this is the INGRAHAM ANGLE rom Washington tonight. Let's start right off. Biden goes full Sharpton. That's the focus of tonight's "Angle".\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: That was horrible to see, as you saw, to see people treated like they did, horses nearly running them over and people being strapped. It's outrageous. I promise you