 \uc2 You've got to ask yourself, Laura, do we really want to trust the administration with this kind of power? We've seen them prosecuting hardworking Border Patrol people who are on videotape not committing a crime, yet they're being persecuted. Are we going to trust them with our soldiers when they said they want to enforce --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They are obviously in the process of surveilling social media posts, watching what people are saying, how you're operating, and that's where this will all kick in. There's a really weird part of the language of this bill, the secretary of the department, each department, shall develop and implement training and education programs to assist members of the armed forces and civilian employees in identifying, preventing, responding, or reporting, mitigating the risk of extremism. The question is, Kurt, always, how do you identify -- what is extremism to you might not be extremism to anyone else. Being conservative, to them, is extremist. That's the problem, is it not?\par \par \uc2 SCHLICHTER: That's exactly the problem. And real extremist, the ones who really cause damage, like the shooter at Fort Hood, everyone was afraid to raise the red flag because they didn't want to be accused of bigotry. It wasn't bigotry. It was taking care of your brother and sister soldiers. This, Laura, we have seen is part of a trend, and it's part of a trend to impose a uniform political view within the uniformed services. And that is so wrong on so many levels. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Good luck finding people to sign up for the armed forces under these rules. Kurt, we've got to go, but great to see you tonight. And I'm glad we flagged that.\par \par \uc2 And up next, the late-night comics go woke, but is it funny? Raymond Arroyo has it all, Friday Follies next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's Friday, and that means it's time for Friday Follies. And for that we turn to FOX News contributor Raymond Arroyo. Raymond, the late- night hosts coordinated their shows this week with the purpose, which you know, you knew ahead of time was going to be just so lame. So what the heck was it?\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: The purpose may have been, Laura, to prove that climate change makes a lousy late-night guest. Extreme weather is not exactly Don Rickles. Eight late-night shows committed to hector their audiences about climate change. And I'm sure, this evaporated all the Co2 emissions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL, LATE NIGHT TALK SHOW HOST: If you are a person living on this planet, your future is in peril. That's a scientific fact. The IPPC -- IPPC says. I can't even say their name, that's how serious this is.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FALLON, LATE NIGHT TALK SHOW HOST: Of course, some people are sad that summer is over, but good news, thanks to climate change, it's not.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT, LATE NIGHT TALK SHOW HOST: Fifth-six percent of young people agree with the statement that humanity is doomed. How do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's always been dying. It's always been doomed. I empathize with the doom, I feel the doom.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I feel the doowm.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I feel the doom, too, Laura. Right. It's why their audiences are fleeing, and these shows barely break 2 million people a night. Remember, Johnny Carson had 10 million people watching when he left late night. These people have decimated the timeslot.\par \par \uc2 Over at \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2 , Trevor Noah kept the good times rolling with the hilarious and charming Greta Thunberg.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRETA THUNBERG: I understand that the climate emergency is very uncomfortable. It's not something that makes you popular. It's sort of an icebreaker. It doesn't, people don't like it if you talk about the climate crisis. I'm so tired of talking about the climate by now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: We are tired of hearing her talk about it, too. But don't worry, Laura. Seth Meyers kept the late-night excitement going. Remember the other night we reported on the boy band BTS doing that musical number at the U.N.?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, that was fun.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Seth got into that with the scintillating climate czar, John Kerry.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS, LATE NIGHT TALK SHOW HOST: BTS performed.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KERRY, SPECIAL PRESIDENTIAL CLIMATE ENVOY: I know. I know. They're great.\par \par \uc2 MEYERS: John Kerry is a BTS fan.\par \par \uc2 KERRY: I watched the video of their, what is it, permission to dance, or something. It's fantastic.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 KERRY: They are really good. They're good.\par \par \uc2 MEYERS: And they are taking this seriously.\par \par \uc2 KERRY: Kids are absolutely correct to be furious, to be frustrated, to scold us, and say adults, hey, be adults.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Welcome to late night in America, Laura. This as a man who takes a private plane almost everywhere he goes, and according to a FOX analysis, he's expended 30 times the emission of a normal private plane. And he's going to lecture everyone about Co2 emission in the environment? Come on. He should be the adult.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Every time I look at John Kerry, I go back to the old radio days when we used to play Lumiere from "Beauty and the Beast." He looks like he is melting. Maybe it is the climate.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It's that --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, but the idea that John Kerry is going to outmaneuver Xi Jinping is about the most hilarious thing I have ever heard. The man needs to stay away from any serious negotiations. He's going to sell us down the river while he flies the G5 over to Asia.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, Kimmel kept warning about flash floods and wildfires and how we have to stop those. There's a reason we call them wildfires and flash floods, because you don't control them. It's called climate and weather and cycles of weather, that's what happens on the earth. And it happens whether we're here or we're not here, and long before we were, and long after we are.\par \par \uc2 But look, as long as we're speaking about doom and future issues, they archbishop of San Francisco Sal Cordileone, has called for people to pray and fast against Nancy Pelosi's bill that would codify Roe v Wade into law. He called it child sacrifice. And Pelosi responded this way.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI, (D-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: I'm Catholic. I come from a pro-life family. Not active in that regard, different in their view of a woman's right to choose than I am. In my right to choose, I had five children in six years and one week, and I keep saying to people who say things like that, when you have five children in six years and one day, we can talk about what business it is of any of us to tell anyone else to do. I believe that God has given us free will to honor our responsibilities.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Move over Thomas Aquinas. That was quite a dissertation. That made no sense whatsoever, period.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But who says I'm pro-life -- and I want to quote this, I'm pro- life, I come from pro-life family, but I'm not active in it. Being a Catholic who is pro-abortion and pushing this bill, not just pro-choice, but pro-abortion in this way, it's like a vegetarian saying I will have the New York strip steak, please. Just be what you say you are. If you're not Catholic, that's fine, that's OK. But you can't pretend to be something and then legislate and carry on in your public life in total opposition to your professed belief.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: See you Monday.\par \par \uc2 Universities woke speech codes have gone from just being recommendations to really hard and fast rules. And now they want to punish those who don't adhere. The disturbing story from one college student is next. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Across the country colleges and universities, they are not just creating absurd new speech codes. They are now punishing people anyone who deviates from the woke orthodoxy. For more on this story, we turn to FOX's Matt Finn in our west coast newsroom. Matt?\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Laura, a survey of more than 150 colleges reveals just how freely people can actually speak on our college campuses. The 2021 college free-speech ranking show Claremont Mckenna College in California ranks as the most open to free speech. Marquette and \uc2 DePauw University\uc2  tied for last.\par \par \uc2 These rankings are based on more than 37,000 students who we're surveyed about their school's free speech climate. Some quick highlights, this year 66 percent said they found it somewhat acceptable to shout down a campus speaker, and 23 percent said it was acceptable to use violence to stop a campus speech.\par \par \uc2 And speaking as free speech, California State University Sacramento has now set up a new bias incident reporting website where anyone in the Sac State Community can report a person to be investigated for an alleged incident of bias. That includes, quote, "a person does, says, or expresses anything that is motivated by bias related to one or more identities such as citizenship, disability, ethnicity, gender, national origin, race, sexual orientation, or more."\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Penn State University\uc2  says it has, quote, grown out of a male-centered world and stopped using male-centric terms like "freshmen" and "upperclassmen" because they can be interpreted as sexist. Instead students will be referred to the first-year, second-year, and beyond.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Brandeis University\uc2  made headlines with its oppressive language list, urging students and faculty to stop using words like "picnic," "trigger warning," and "rule of thumb" because of their links to violence and oppression.\par \par \uc2 And at the elementary level, a P.E. teacher at Leesburg Elementary School in Virginia was suspended, saying he loves all have his students but would not comply with the district's rules of using students' preferred pronouns or allowing transgender students to use the bathroom or play on a sports team of the gender they identify with.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TANNER CROSS, LEESBURG, ELEMENTARY SCHOOL TEACHER: I'm a teacher, but I serve God first, and I will not say a biological boy can be a girl and vice versa, because it's against my religion, it's lying to a child. It's abuse to a child, and it's sinning against our God.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FINN: That man sued the school for violating his First Amendment rights. The case is still playing out for courts. But for now, Laura, the Virginia Supreme Court ruled that he can be reinstated and keep his job. Laura?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Matt, and you taught me something tonight. I didn't know "picnic" was also on the list of prohibited words, so I learned a lot. Matt, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And this tyranny has also spread to Point Park University in Pennsylvania where students were told action could be taken against them for using the wrong gender pronouns regardless of intent. Now, this is an obvious free speech problem, and at least one student is speaking out. Logan Dubil is a junior at Point Park University. Logan, is there any indication of what the punishment will be from the university against people who violate this policy? What is it? What is the punishment?\par \par \uc2 LOGAN DUBIL, JUNIOR AT POINT PARK UNIVERSITY: As of now, no one knows what the punishment could be. On September 13th, the student body received an e- mail saying if they were to misgender, misuse someone's pronouns, or even use someone name against another student, action could be taken against them. I reached out to the student government president along with the president of the university itself, and no concrete answer has been received so far.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I want to read a quick example of what the university suggests that you should use as non-gender specific pronouns. OK, this is what they have a lot of time on their hands to do all this. Here's an example. "Those shoes belong to "hir," h-i-r. "Ze made them hirself," h-i-r. Are you going to have to start actually speaking like that? This is just stupid. I'm sorry. You guys have got to get together and just say this is really dumb and we sound really stupid speaking this way, and heaven forbid, writing this way.\par \par \uc2 DUBIL: One-hundred percent, I couldn't agree with you more. A lot of conservative students on campus have reached out to me, going along with what you said, how stupid this is. This is not normal in any sense, but God forbid as student of the liberal descent wants to be respected on campus. The organizations and the university bow head over hell to make sure that they're safe. But when it comes to conservative campuses, we don't feel that same love. Freshman year I dealt with threats of violence, my door was violated. Sophomore year, I dealt with being threatened to be doxed from other students, and nothing has been done to this day.\par \par \uc2 And it is really scary how easily and how ready the school is to jump to make sure that one side is protected and on side feels safe, but the other doesn't.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Which piece of gear is most popular? I will tell you when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All week long we've been saying how low can Biden go? He keeps going lower, and we'll track it all for you. We have big shows on tap next week as well.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of you want to know which piece of gear on LauraIngraham.com is the most popular of the Freedom Matters gear. And I can tell you just this last week, made in the USA gear, this camouflage trucker hat is the most popular item on the site. You guys like to hunt in the fall, and I guess you like this hat. The second most popular, the mug, OK, made in the USA, Freedom Matters. And third, the t-shirt. All of these make great housewarming gifts. And all proceeds of mine go to charity, and this month to the Stephen Siller Tunnel to Towers Foundation through the end of the month. A new charity in October. We'll reveal that next week.\par \par \uc2 I want to thank you for watching, and always remember, it's America now and forever. Greg Gutfeld takes it all from here. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020210925eh9o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Pentagon Says Kabul Drone Strike Killed Civilians; \uc2 FDA\uc2  Rejects \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Plan For Booster Shots; Virginia Gubernatorial Candidates Democrat \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  and Republican Glenn Youngkin Hold First Debate; Biden Administration States Drone Strike in Afghanistan Aimed at ISIS-K Member Instead Killed 10 Innocent People Including Seven Children\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Rich Edson, Leo Terrell, Peter Doocy, Bill Melugin,  Trey Yingst, Jonathan Serrie, Alexandria Hoff \par \uc2 7,721 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: That's it for us. Have a great weekend, everybody. We'll see you on Monday.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Good evening, welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, three major stories developing tonight. A \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  advisory board has rejected the president's plan to have all Americans take a booster vaccine shot against COVID-19, at least not yet.\par \par \uc2 The board is recommending Americans 65 and older and those immunocompromised get a booster shot, further confusing the administration's announcements on booster shots.\par \par \uc2 More than 11,000 illegal immigrants, many of them from Haiti surged toward the southern border of the U.S. under a bridge in Del Rio, Texas. Customs and Border Patrol officers call it a situation that is out of control tonight.\par \par \uc2 And the president's so-called over the horizon counterterrorism strategy in the wake of the Afghanistan withdrawal is in question this evening, real question.\par \par \uc2 Senior Pentagon officials now admit a drone strike the White House celebrated at first as a major victory against the ISIS actually did kill almost a dozen civilians including an aid worker and seven children but no terrorists.\par \par \uc2 Lucas Tomlinson starts us off at the Pentagon with the tragic details of this strike. Good evening, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. An American reaper drone orbiting high above Kabul had been tracking and shadowing a Toyota Corolla -- a white Toyota Corolla for eight hours before launching a hellfire missile.\par \par \uc2 The military thought it destroyed a car filled with explosives and ISIS fighters. Instead, 10 innocent civilians including seven children were killed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice over): The head of U.S. forces in Afghanistan General \uc2 Frank McKenzie\uc2  coined the strike on August 29th, one day before all U.S. troops left Afghanistan, a tragic mistake.\par \par \uc2 The strike took place three days after 13 American troops were killed in a suicide attack in Kabul, General McKenzie said more attacks were anticipated.\par \par \uc2 GEN. KENNETH "FRANK" MCKENZIE, COMMANDER, U.S. CENTRAL COMMAND: It's important that I emphasize that we did not have the luxury of time to develop pattern of life and to do a number of other things. We struck under the -- under the theory of reasonable certainty.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Did you have forces on the ground that helped you conduct this strike?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: No, we did not. As you know, no time where our forces really away from Hamid Karzai International Airfield. So, there was nobody there -- nobody there on the ground to have to play that role.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: American drones must fly for hours from distant bases in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Two days after the drone strike, President Biden touted the military's ability to strike over the horizon.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have what's called over the horizon capabilities, which means we can strike terrorists and targets without American boots on the ground, or very few if needed.\par \par \uc2 We've shown that capacity just in the last week. We struck ISIS-K remotely days after they murdered 13 of our service members and dozens of innocent Afghans.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The U.S. military's top officer General Mark Milley spoke earlier this month about the strike.\par \par \uc2 GEN. MARK MILLEY, CHAIRMAN, JOINT CHIEFS OF STAFF: Because there were secondary explosions, there's a reasonable conclusion to be made that there was explosives in that vehicle.\par \par \uc2 We think that the procedures were correctly followed and it was a righteous strike.\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: We knew from the very beginning, there was a possibility of civilian casualties.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): General McKenzie also said those explosions were likely the result of a propane tank located behind the car. That's not what the military said after the strike. Officials tell me there'll be no disciplinary action taken, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Quite something. Lucas, before we learned about this drone strike, the focus really was on General Milley's calls to the Chinese in the wake of that Bob Woodward, Bob Costa book. He spoke for the first time publicly today. What did he say?\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Well, General Milley claims he didn't do anything wrong. Telling the Associated Press while in Greece that his calls to the Chinese was nothing out of the ordinary.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MILLEY: These are routine calls in order to discuss issues of the day, to reassure both allies and adversaries in this case, in order to ensure strategic stability.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: General Milley will appear before the \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2  on September 28th to discuss these calls to the Chinese and also Afghanistan including this tragic drone strike, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Number of questions there. Lucas Tomlinson at the Pentagon. Lucas, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Trey Yingst is coming up with a live report from Kabul, Afghanistan, the story of an American man who traveled there to try to save his family.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, a blow to President Biden's effort to have all Americans take a third vaccination shot or a booster. And \uc2 FDA\uc2  advisory committee is overwhelmingly rejecting a broad booster vaccine plan, but is recommending narrowing the scope who should get a third shot.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy joins us live from the North Lawn with murky messaging on boosters from this administration. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. President Biden likes to say the pandemic is not political but the layout of today's \uc2 FDA\uc2  booster hearing was because doctors debated the science for hours and then, took a vote and the result was no boosters yet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This vote did not pass since the majority voted no.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Just two \uc2 FDA\uc2  advisors voted in favor of a third shot for the general population right now, an overwhelming 16 voted against it. Three days before the booster program was to be launched.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEG SEYMOUR, SENIOR FELLOW, NATIONAL CENTER FOR HEALTH RESEARCH: It is unfortunate that the White House announced the need for an availability of boosters prior to \uc2 FDA\uc2 's assessment of the data.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And that is exactly what President Biden did.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Just remember, as a simple rule, eight months after your second shot, get a booster shot.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Biden officials were so confident in their plan, they bought boosters before scientists signed off.\par \par \uc2 JEFF ZIENTS, COVID COORDINATOR, WHITE HOUSE: We have supply in inventory and we have also supply on order. So, supply is in good shape.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Moderna and \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  boosters were not considered, only reps from \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  were invited to argue in favor of a third dose.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There is clear erosion of vaccine protection over time against COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But experts were generally unimpressed with the presentation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And I just don't think we're there yet in terms of the data.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Today was not a condemnation of COVID vaccination, more just raising questions about boosters.\par \par \uc2 DR. CODY MEISSNER, MEMBER, \uc2 FDA\uc2  ADVISORY COMMITTEE: Everyone has to get the primary series. This booster dose is not going to make a big difference.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: One of the most common issues flagged is the risk of cardiovascular problems in young men who got the booster already.\par \par \uc2 DR. JAMES HILDRETH, \uc2 FDA\uc2  ADVISORY COMMITTEE: I have a serious concern about myocarditis in young people.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: One advisor Marion Gruber participated today despite announcing a plan to resign amid a dispute about boosters.\par \par \uc2 MARION GRUBER, OFFICE DIRECTOR OF VACCINES RESEARCH AND REVIEW, FDA: It has been a privilege to serve you.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: For now, there is unanimous agreement that the immunocompromised and senior citizens are helped by third doses.\par \par \uc2 DR. ARCHANA CHATTERJEE, MEMBER, \uc2 FDA\uc2  ADVISORY COMMITTEE: I'd like to see more data before I would recommend it for a younger age group. But over 60 is probably OK from my standpoint.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But not the general population. Despite the president's announcement that starting Monday, it would be easy for everybody.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It will be easy, just show your vaccination card and you'll get a booster.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): Not unless you're 65 and up, the only age group this advisory board is recommending vaccine boosters for right now.\par \par \uc2 And this is the first time White House officials have been bopped (PH) by the scientists when it comes to COVID.\par \par \uc2 When White House officials pushed mask wearing, scientists said great. When the White House officials pushed vaccines, scientists said great but when they started to push boosters here, the scientists said wait, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But Peter, internally, are they now acknowledging that, you know, follow the science they got over their skis here on this one?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That would have been a great question for the president today but he did not take any and he is now at his beach house. It would have been a great question for \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , but there was no White House briefing and it would have been a great question for any of the White House COVID advisory board but they decided today to speak before this announcement, before this vote was taken.\par \par \uc2 And so, it's hard to get them on the record on camera about something that hasn't happened yet, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, good try. Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 We have some startling exclusive images tonight from the southern border. Take a look at these images, mass of illegal immigrants, thousands of them assembling along one key entry point.\par \par \uc2 The numbers are really staggering and according to the Customs and Border Patrol, the fact that virtually none of them are vaccinated and they are now setting up as you see here a tent city under a bridge is concerning to authorities there.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bill Melugin reports tonight from Del Rio, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My God, look at that.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): From a Texas DPS helicopter, Fox News is getting an exclusive look at the deteriorating situation along the border here in Del Rio, Texas and the footage is stunning.\par \par \uc2 Close to 11,000 migrants all crammed under the International Bridge waiting to be processed by a completely overwhelmed Border Patrol. Some already building makeshift shelters out of grass and sticks, others erecting tents. A majority of these migrants from Haiti which has a COVID vaccination rate of under one percent.\par \par \uc2 From the air, we watched as a constant stream of hundreds of people left Mexico by crossing a dam in the Rio Grande and then walked illegally into the United States, masses of them gathering on the shore after they cross.\par \par \uc2 From there, we could see a lengthy line of migrants walking a dirt path towards the International Bridge where they will join the constantly growing mass of people there.\par \par \uc2 Nearby, a bulldozer clears a field apparently making space for potential emergency shelters, or maybe a processing tent.\par \par \uc2 Sources on the ground sent us video showing the situation from their perspective as hundreds of migrants cross illegally into the U.S. without any resistance on either side of the border. With more in wait on the Mexican side of the river, and it happens all day long.\par \par \uc2 The Del Rio mayor, a Democrat declaring a local state of disaster as a result.\par \par \uc2 BRUNO LOZANO, MAYOR OF DEL RIO, TEXAS: There's people having babies down there, there's people collapsing out of the heat. It's something that it's very challenging to describe in words, but it's extremely chaotic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, last night, we learned that the \uc2 FAA\uc2  had suddenly issued a TFR, a temporary flight restriction in the area around the bridge right here.\par \par \uc2 What that meant was that our Fox News drone could no longer fly in the area and bring everybody those images of the migrants under the bridge, at the time the \uc2 FAA\uc2  said it was for a special security reason.\par \par \uc2 Well, I'm happy to give an update today. This morning, Fox News filed a waiver with the \uc2 FAA\uc2  to try to get our drone back in the air. Happy to report, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  cleared us this afternoon. We have been given clearance to fly our drone once again here at the bridge. We will continue to do so in the coming days. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin live in Del Rio, Texas. Keep it up. Thanks, Bill.\par \par \uc2 The Dow lost 166 today, the S&P 500 fell 41, the \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  was off 138. For the week, the Dow dropped seven hundredths of a percentage point, the S&P and the \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  lost about a half point.\par \par \uc2 America's oldest foreign ally is furious tonight with the Biden administration. French officials are livid over a military deal between the U.S., Australia and Great Britain.\par \par \uc2 So much so they have pulled their ambassador from Washington D.C. back to Paris and extremely rare move in diplomatic circles especially between allies.\par \par \uc2 State Department Correspondent \uc2 Rich Edson\uc2  explains.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Allies and partners, the foundation of the Biden administration's foreign policy and a strategy openly cheered months ago in European capitals. Though a new security partnership just announced between the United States, United Kingdom and Australia has allies in Europe, especially France, irate.\par \par \uc2 PHILIPPE ETIENNE, FRENCH AMBASSADOR TO U.S.: We have a feeling, yes, of not being treated like an ally. France play a key role in the Indo-Pacific Ocean.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: The U.S. and U.K. agreed to provide nuclear submarine technology to Australia as American allies bolster their presence in the Indo-Pacific to counter China.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European Union\uc2 's foreign policy chief complained the United States failed to even notify the Europeans about this deal.\par \par \uc2 The French also lose out on an agreement they signed with Australia in 2016, to provide tens of billions of dollars in submarines.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN HADDAD, DIRECTOR OF THE EUROPE CENTER, ATLANTIC COUNCIL: I do think there is a sense of disappointment right now in Europe towards the Biden administration, especially after four years of disagreements and conflicts under the Trump administration, there was a hope for sort of a new age in transatlantic relations, and we've seen very little substance to show for it.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: A French diplomat says France's Embassy in Washington has canceled a reception tonight to commemorate the 240th anniversary of the Battle of the Capes, a French naval victory over the British, resulting in a successful Siege of Yorktown and the creation of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: France in particular, is a vital partner on this and so many other issues, stretching back generations and we want to find every opportunity to deepen our transatlantic cooperation in the Indo-Pacific and around the world.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Blinken says the administration had spoken with the French government ahead of the Australian partnership announcement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): The French government has just announced it is recalling its ambassadors to the United States and Australia, saying this submarine deal represents unacceptable behavior among allies and partners.\par \par \uc2 The White House official says the U.S. had been in close touch with French leaders, and we'll continue to try to resolve these differences, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But Rich, for people sitting at home, I mean, it's a big deal, right? They pull the ambassador.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: That is a big deal. Anytime you do this, it says an awful lot about a relationship when you bring an ambassador back. It's happened with the U.S. and Russia in a number of different times. And it really is a strong diplomatic message of displeasure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, \uc2 Rich Edson\uc2  live at the State Department. Rich, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Trey Yingst reporting from inside Afghanistan brings us a remarkable story of one man's journey to try to save his family.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 40 in Sacramento, where old growth trees and other structures in Sequoia National Park are threatened by another fast-moving wildfire in the area. That blaze could reach a grove of 2,000 sequoias in a matter of days.\par \par \uc2 Fire crews have wrapped the base of the tallest, the 275-foot General Sherman sequoia in a flame-resistant aluminum blanket.\par \par \uc2 California wildfires have scorched more than 3,500 square miles this summer, destroying hundreds of homes.\par \par \uc2 Fox 5 in New York where the latest crime spree there involves victims wearing expensive jewelry, and a pair of suspects using a Mercedes getaway car.\par \par \uc2 One person was shot while another had a Rolex watch stolen at an upper Eastside restaurant this week. Police are looking for links to several similar attacks in recent days in other high-end and trendy areas.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at St. Louis from our affiliate Fox2, you see the arch there, the big story there tonight.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  picks MidAmerica St. Louis airport as the site to build the Navy's first carrier-based unmanned aircraft. The 15-year $200 million project is expected to bring 300 jobs or so to that region.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Police are warning of threats of violence ahead of a rally tomorrow here in Washington by a group supporting people arrested during the January 6th riot. Hundreds of demonstrators are expected for what is being called the Justice for J6 event.\par \par \uc2 Law enforcement is being beefed up around the capital city and 100 National Guard troops are on standby nearby. A temporary fence has been installed around the \uc2 Capitol\uc2 . Several streets will be closed in the area and members and staff are encouraged to avoid the conflicts.\par \par \uc2 There are new revelations tonight about \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's unwillingness and inability to enforce its own rules, and its awareness that its products routinely fail and are harmful to consumers.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal says \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's own research confirms it rules favor -- its rules favor VIP clients and its platforms have negative effects on the health of their users. More with this story coming up.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we are hearing from an Afghan American who left his home here in the U.S. to try to rescue his family following the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  takeover. Correspondent Trey Yingst has the remarkable story tonight from Kabul, Afghanistan, where Americans and allies are still trying to get to safety.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): One citizens wait outside the Serena Hotel in Kabul, passports in hand. They're trying to get inside the grounds that will act as a staging point for evacuations.\par \par \uc2 A Qatari official tells Fox News, 170 people left Afghanistan today on a chartered flight. The passengers included people from eight different nationalities, including the United States.\par \par \uc2 Onboard was U.S. citizen Ehsanullah Muhsen, his wife, and 2-year-old daughter, Miriam.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): You're in St. Louis last month.\par \par \uc2 EHSANULLAH MUHSEN, UNITED STATES CITIZEN: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: And your wife and daughter are here for a wedding. And then, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  takes over, and you say to yourself --\par \par \uc2 MUHSEN: I'm going to get my family out. As simple as that, you know, because I knew one way or another, especially when the explosion happened with ISIS, and then the consequent firing upon the protesters, or whatever happened. You know, I just knew I got one way or another, I got to get my family out.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice-over): After watching the chaotic scenes outside the airport and getting limited help from the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , Ehsanullah flew to Pakistan, and then crossed into Afghanistan by land.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration said there were contingency plans in place for the U.S. withdrawal. You experienced this firsthand. What's your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 MUHSEN: It's not true. It's not true. I mean, this -- it could have been avoided, but it's just never, you know, it's too much hectic, too much disorder.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Across the street, the reason so many are fleeing Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): People are headed now to Friday prayers in the Afghan capital of Kabul. The imam inside this mosque chanting death to America, declaring victory for the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and the Afghan people.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice-over): Inside Muslim prayers are recited as some worshippers thank God for the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  victory over U.S. forces. Just over a month ago, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  entered Kabul. Today, its fighters are being praised in mosques throughout the city. There's no way to tell who willingly accepts the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and who is being forced.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is already going back on their promises about what life will look like for the Afghan people, like you saw in that mosque, a dangerous preview about what is to come. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey Yingst, early Saturday morning, live in Kabul Afghanistan. Trey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A former campaign lawyer for \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  is facing a federal charge tonight in what is the first legal action taken in the investigation into the origin of the Trump-Russia probe, the \uc2 John Durham\uc2  investigation.\par \par \uc2 Michael Sussmann made his first court appearance today after being charged with lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . He pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we are finally hearing from the boyfriend's family in the case of the young woman who disappeared during a cross-country track.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE PETITO, FATHER OF GABBY PETITO: Whatever you can do to make sure my daughter comes home, I'm asking for that help. There is nothing else that matters to me now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now, to an update on the case of the missing young woman, Gabby Petitto, who was traveling cross-country with her boyfriend. Brian Laundrie's sister now says the family wants Gabby Petito to be found safe. Yet, her brother, who police consider a person of interest has still not been cooperating.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Jonathan Serrie has the latest tonight from North Port, Florida.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): More than 1,000 tips have flooded in this investigators, trace Gabby Petito's movements on a cross-country road trip prior to her disappearance.\par \par \uc2 Her boyfriend, Brian Laundrie silence, and his solo trip back home and the couple's van are raising suspicions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSH TAYLOR, SPOKESMAN, NORTH PORT POLICE DEPARTMENT: Obviously, that vehicle drove a couple of 1,000 miles to get back here to North Port. It didn't do that on one tank of gas.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Brian's sister broke her family silence on the case in an interview with ABC News.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE LAUNDRIE, SISTER OF BRIAN LAUNDRIE: Me and my family want Gabby be found safe. She's like a sister and my children love her. And all I want is for her to come home safe and sound, and this be just a big misunderstanding.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Police confirm Cassie Laundrie also spoke with law enforcement but was unable to provide information useful to the investigation. Gabby's family members believe Brian holds the key to her whereabouts and are increasing the pressure on him to break his silence.\par \par \uc2 FLORA ROCCO, GODMOTHER OF GABBY PETITO: It's not that you're not even helping anymore, it's now hindering this investigation. It's