 branch, and we may have become the most dangerous. And I think that's problematic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thomas says judges venturing into areas that should not be entered into is part of the reason why the nomination process is so contentious nowadays.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Virginia voters are considering what they saw last night during the first debate of the governor's race. Correspondent Alexandria Hoff takes a look at some of the highlights from the spirited session.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Debate night in Virginia's governor's race --\par \par \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN, (R) VIRGINIA GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE: My opponent wants to be the abortion governor, and I want to be the jobs governor.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCAULIFFE\uc2 , (D) VIRGINIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: He is a Trump wannabe.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: -- could be a sign of what's to come in next year's midterms. Virginia's former governor Democrat \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  is facing off against Republican newcomer Glenn Youngkin, who headed up the private equity firm the Carlisle Group. The two sparred Thursday night in their first official gubernatorial debate and the last before the polls open for early voting today.\par \par \uc2 MCAULIFFE: He is not requiring vaccinations. That is the difference between the two of us.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: I don't believe that President Biden has the authority to dictate to everyone --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So that's a yes?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: -- that we have to take the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Vaccine mandates, policing, jobs, the environment, the hot button issues in this extremely tight and heavily funded race are likely the same as what will drive voters to have the polls next November, making this gubernatorial race a case study of sorts for voter sentiment ahead the midterms. Take Critical Race Theory -- Virginia has been at the epicenter of the CRT debate.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: To think that it's not in our schools is absolutely bogus. Now, the problem with Critical Race Theory is that it teaches our children to actually view everything through a lens of race.\par \par \uc2 MCAULIFFE: It's not taught in our schools. But what I hate is this is a big dog whistle. I really hate it. It divides people. I'm about bringing people together.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: On abortion, Youngkin and McAuliffe follow party lines.\par \par \uc2 MCAULIFFE: I believe a woman ought to make a decision about her own reproductive right, and I will support those.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: I'm pro-life. I believe in exceptions in the case of rape and incest and when the life of the mother is in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Youngkin added that he would not support a bill similar to Texas' controversial new post six week ban.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): Just like we saw in California, Bret, a constant in this race has been the Trump card. The Democratic candidate criticizing his opponent for having the former president's endorsement, and a Republican trying to emerge as their own person. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex, thank you. And welcome to our team.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Thank you so much. I'm proud to be here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the panel on what has turned out to be a bad day, a bad week, really, for President Biden and his administration.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. Scientists in Spain are monitoring a so- called earthquake swarm in the Canary Islands. Intense seismic activity detected off the northwest coast of Africa Thursday prompting fears of a possible volcanic eruption. Authorities say molten rock pushed into a dormant ridge in La Palma, an island of 85,000 where a volcano erupted some 85 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Apple and \uc2 Google\uc2  agree to delete a social media app linked to jailed Russian opposition leader \uc2 Alexei Navalny\uc2 . The so called smart voter app was designed to spotlight most likely to defeat Kremlin backed candidates. The move comes as polls open for three days of voting in parliamentary elections there.\par \par \uc2 And the longest mission aboard China's first space station is now over. A three-man crew touched down in the Gobi Desert after three months in space during which they conducted two spacewalks. China has also landed a space probe on Mars and a rover on the far side of the moon.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories beyond our borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have what's called over- the-horizon capabilities, which means we can strike terrorists and targets without American boots on the ground. We struck ISIS-K remotely days after they murdered 13 of our servicemembers and dozens of innocent Afghans.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Our over-the-horizon capacity can work and has worked in going after ISIS targets and killing people who went after our troops.\par \par \uc2 GEN. MARK MILLEY, CHAIRMAN, JOINT CHIEFS: At least one of those people that were killed was an ISIS facilitator. So were there others killed? Yes, there were others killed. Who they are we don't know.\par \par \uc2 GEN. KENNETH "FRANK" MCKENZIE, U.S. CENTRAL COMMAND: I am now convinced that as many as 10 civilians, including up to seven children, were tragically killed in that strike. It is unlikely that the vehicle and those died were associated with ISIS-K or were a direct threat to U.S. forces. It was a mistake, and I offer my sincere apology.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A tragic mistake, no ISIS terrorists killed despite all of the statements by the administration and the president himself in the wake of the dead 13 American servicemembers around the Kabul airport.\par \par \uc2 We'll start there. Let's bring in our panel, Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, CEO of Empowerment and Inclusion Capital, \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , former Congressman from South Carolina, and Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner."\par \par \uc2 Trey, I covered the Pentagon for years. Tragic things happen. They happen in dangerous situations where the military is trying to protect its open people and civilian populations. Nobody wants to see civilians die. However, when the administration came out touting this as an example of over-the-horizon and going after ISIS terrorists in the day after that, and then to find out what happened today, your reaction?\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA REPRESENTATIVE: Yes, "over-the-horizon" apparently is a euphemism for infanticide. I want to know the layer of review before this country, the world's greatest country, decides to kill seven children. Who gets to approve it? Who reviews it? What's the quantum of evidence?\par \par \uc2 And it's not lost on me, Bret, that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was slow to pull the trigger on \uc2 Usama bin Laden\uc2  but quick to pull the trigger on this drone strike while he was having really bad press that resulted in the death of seven children. So, Trump gets criticized for killing the right people. Let's see what happens to Biden for killing seven children.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I will say, Harold, it was interesting to see this announcement come on a Friday afternoon. The president had already left for vacation for the weekend. The White House had pretty much shut down because of the -- there were no briefings after that, and we got this announcement from the Central Command general.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: There's no question our prayers and thoughts go to the family and families that lost loved ones. This is tragic. I give the White House credit for being transparent. Questions can be asked, Bret, about whether the timing of the announcement of this, of the admission of this had to do with the fact it was Friday evening.\par \par \uc2 I do think Trey raises an important point about the process has to be evaluated about how they reached this decision, and try to ensure it doesn't happen again. I would caution us although to remember the intentions of our -- of any \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , U.S. military is to protect us. They made a mistake here, and I hope they learn from it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Byron?\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": Well, the over-the-horizon idea was really the foundation of the Biden administration's post withdrawal strategy in Afghanistan. Remember, the president explained that the United States does fight terrorism in many countries in which it does not have a military presence. And so the first run of this over-the-horizon capability in Afghanistan is just an absolute disaster.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing is, as the president just said in that clip you played, clearly this was seen as vengeance for, or a strike back after this bombing that killed -- suicide bombing killed 13 Americans. So now we strike back afterwards, and we hit the wrong person. We hit 10 of the wrong people. So, it's just absolutely wrong all around. And I heard in \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 's voice the sound of a former congressional overseer. There are going to be a lot of people in Congress, hopefully Democrats and Republicans, who are going to want a detailed accounting of this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, again, chairman of the Joint Chiefs facing a lot of questions on a lot of different fronts September 28th up on Capitol Hill, and we'll carry that live.\par \par \uc2 Next up, the booster back and forth. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We bought enough boosters, enough booster shots, and the distribution system is ready to administer them. As soon as they are authorized. Those eligible will be able to get a booster right away.\par \par \uc2 DR. VIVEK MURTHY, U.S. SURGEON GENERAL: Our goals were to stay ahead of the virus, to lay out an additional plan based on our clinical judgment, and to ultimately ensure that the final plan would be based on the independent assessment and recommendation of the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and CDC.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This vote did not pass since the majority voted no.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It wasn't even the majority, Trey. It was 16 to two against a broad booster shot for the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine for the three shots, at least not yet. They did say if you are 65 and over or immunocompromised you should get it. But clearly the administration, the president was ahead on this, ahead of the science.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Yes. We were told to follow the science. We weren't told to kind of predict it and be off by an overwhelming vote. I was surprised at the vote. I thought that I would be in line for a booster. This is what vexes people, though, Bret. I'm not a scientist. I have to rely on people to tell me whether or not the risks outweigh the reward when it comes to boosters. And they say, if you are 65 and above or immunosuppressed, go ahead and do it. But if you are looking for a job, fact checking \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's COVID press conferences would be a really good place to start looking, because there's a lot of information.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Again, Harold, we did not hear from the COVID team today on this decision. But it is frustrating for some people, we get told again and again and again follow the science, and then it's kind of murky on the messaging. I know some of this is evolving as we learn more, but that's where people's heads are as they're waiting for what to do.\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : Look, if you had told me a year ago today that we would have a vaccine, two shots on two of them and one shot on another, that would pretty much immunize and protect us from hospitalizations, not all together, and certainly death, and that only 54 percent of Americans would have taken it, I would have probably taken the other side of the bet, saying there is no way with President Trump leading this effort that every American, in spite of what some Democrats may have said, we all would have gotten this. And we didn't.\par \par \uc2 The science and \uc2 FDA\uc2  can guide us on the third shot, but ultimately everyone has to get the first and second shot, and we should follow the science, too. I know I will.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, a lot to talk about with this in coming days. Up next, the Friday Lightning Round. It will be lightning.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCAULIFFE\uc2 , (D) VIRGINIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: You said you want to give the it 2.6 back in tax cuts. Let me tell you how Virginia law works.\par \par \uc2 RYAN YOUNG, CNN NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: I understand how it works.\par \par \uc2 MCAULIFFE: Half of it has to go into a rainy day fund.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: I understand that theory.\par \par \uc2 MCAULIFFE: It's not your money to give back. You should learn the law.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: The $2.6 billion would --\par \par \uc2 MCAULIFFE: Excuse, it's my turn.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: You were talking when I was talking.\par \par \uc2 MCAULIFFE: This isn't a --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, you can get a feel there for the debate in Virginia, \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  and Glenn Youngkin. If you look at the latest Real Clear Politics average of politics in this governor's race, which is really an interesting race when it comes to races setting the table for 2022, you can see it's pretty tight, margin of error, that sort of thing.\par \par \uc2 We're back with the panel. Harold, this really will be an interesting race that will tell you where a lot of people maybe stand, because Virginia is pretty purple, bluish-purple, but purple.\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : Look, \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2 , I think, is the best retail politician in politics, certainly, in the Democratic Party, I would argue in politics. But this Virginia Republican is a strong opponent. Terry has got to continue to focus on the economy. I think he has been given a good, good issue here as the abortion issue has come to the forefront to subordinate some of the others, but this will be a tight race. And I think Virginians are going to have to tell, they're going to have to determine, do they want to go forward with the economy or not.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Glenn Youngkin is arguing that the history is not there, Byron, as far as the economic success, and that's part of the squabbling back and forth. But it really will be a test. And there's a number of issues like Critical Race Theory and the Biden administration, all kinds of things.\par \par \uc2 YORK: And over it all looms \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I think it's going to be a test of whether the Democrat McAuliffe can accuse credibly Glenn Youngkin of wanting to be a Trump wannabe or an imitator. And it's also about COVID. Their first fight last night was about COVID, with McAuliffe kind of implying that Youngkin might be a vaccine denier or something, and Youngkin came out with a strong statement in support of vaccines. So it's really going to be really interesting to see whether that label can stick.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey, I wanted to turn to this French response to this U.S.- Australia-U.K. deal on nuclear subs. They pulled the ambassador. It's kind of a strong letter to follow here.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Well, you can argue there would not be a United States if it were not for France, so I'm sure they got their feelings hurt. I'm not sure if their feelings were hurt mainly at the result or the fact that they had to hear it on the radio. What struck me as weird, Bret, is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was supposed to repair all of these relationships and \uc2 Tony Blinken\uc2  was supposed to be a Francophile. France has been a great ally to us. We've got to get this fixed, and we can't communicate over radio and television with our allies.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some lightning winners and losers. Here we go, Harold, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : My winner is the U.S. Our strategic national interest are advancing because of what you and Trey just talked about. This agreement between the U.K. and Australia will help us counter China.\par \par \uc2 And my loser is anyone who protests violently tomorrow. You can protest on any issue you want, but just be nonviolent in how you do it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Byron, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 YORK: Loser, San Francisco Mayor London Breed. The city has a mask mandate, but that did not stop her from partying in a nightclub without a mask on Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 Winner is Daniil Medvedev. The Russian tennis player won the U.S. Open, but more importantly, he stopped the Grand Slam of Novak Djokovic. That's a historic thing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There you go. Winner and Loser, Trey?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Winner, U.S. women's tennis who outdrew the match with Djokovic and whoever Byron just mentioned, because I wasn't watching either. Loser is the \uc2 FBI\uc2  for botching yet another serious investigation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, guys, make it a great weekend. We will see you next week.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, it's Friday. You know what that means, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm not out to punish anyone. I'm a capitalist.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Pinocchio won't tell you the truth, but I will, and so will the American public.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We had great growth under the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: She wanted to make sure that this message was brought into that room.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We said yes to the diversity. We said yes to inclusion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: To be clear, I blame Larry Nassar, and I also blame an entire system that enabled and perpetrated his abuse.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's heart wrenching. She's my only daughter. I can't give her a hug. I know she needs it, she needs help, and she is nowhere to be found.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The guy the Biden administration droned, was he an aide worker or an ISIS-K operative?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: As many as 10 civilians including up to seven children were tragically skilled in that strike.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They said it was a 15-year struggle to take over Afghanistan and that they're happy they now control the country.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Doing the right thing in the wrong way can end up being the wrong thing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We inherited a deadline. We did not inherit a plan.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  gets \uc2 Blackhawks\uc2  and the unvaccinated get fired.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I have great confidence in General Milley.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This whole narrative that there was this great risk that we were going to do something crazy is just fundamentally false.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is my 10th trip out here to the border. The border agents love our drones.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The Etruscans are the Democrats, and Horatius here is Joe Manchin. By the way, Horatius was even pre Brit Hume.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One week in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, the clock ticks down on lawmakers as they consider raising the federal debt ceiling. So what will happen, and what does it mean for you at home most importantly?\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL R