ishah Hasnie, Jacqui Heinrich, David Spunt, Mike Emanuel \par \uc2 7,589 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, great show. That's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is up next with Bret Baier. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: You sure? You sure Greg doesn't want more time?\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Nobody wants me to stay here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. We do. All right, thanks, Dana. Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, we're learning new details about the bombshell allegations concerning then President Trump's top military adviser, and what the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff did or did not do to try to calm the Chinese, assuring them that the U.S. was not planning any military action against them in the wake of the instability after the election. And what he did or did not do to insert himself into the process for launching nuclear weapons. A decision the President of the United States makes.\par \par \uc2 All of this in the wake of the January 6th riot on Capitol Hill and concern from the Speaker of the House and General Mark Milley that President Trump was acting erratically.\par \par \uc2 Some Republican lawmakers are already demanding answers now. Others say General Milley should be brought in front of a military tribunal. The Pentagon is pushing back tonight essentially saying there's nothing to see here and it's out of context.\par \par \uc2 National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin joins us from the Pentagon with the latest. Good evening, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. The two calls made by General Milley to his Chinese counterpart were not secret. They were done with 15 people in the room including note takers in coordination with the civilian leaders of the military at the time and the interagency I'm told by current and former U.S. Defense officials.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): Unnamed sources in the Woodward and Costa book Peril contend General Milley told his Chinese counterpart General Li that he would give him a heads up if the U.S. plan to strike.\par \par \uc2 Colonel Dave Butler, the Joint Staff spokesman issued the following statement: "His calls with the Chinese and others in October and January were in keeping with these duties and responsibilities conveying reassurance in order to maintain strategic stability."\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PRESS SECRETARY, PENTAGON: I can tell you, frequent communication with two countries like Russia and China is not a typical at all for a chairman of the Joint Chiefs. And those communications are their routine. They're staffed, they're coordinated, and they're transparent, as transparent as they can be.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The White House defended General Milley.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have great confidence in General Milley.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president's experience with him is that he is a man of honor. He is a man who's -- the president is confident in his leadership, his patriotism, his fidelity to the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: General Milley will address these accusations under oath during testimony on September 28th.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): The idea that an American military general is going to warn an adversary if an attack is coming. As you say, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was never even thinking about a military attack against China, the whole thing is pretty far-fetched.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: A former senior Trump defense official says the phone call to the Chinese was done after then-Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  requested his civilian policy officials to do the same. It was designed to lower the temperature and prevent the U.S. and China from stumbling into a conflict.\par \par \uc2 Then-Defense Secretary Esper was so concerned about not spooking the Chinese that he asked for a delay in sending a significant number of ships and war planes to the region around the time of the election for a December exercise.\par \par \uc2 Regarding allegations that General Milley attempted to usurp power from the president when it came to launching a nuclear weapon, the Joint Staff pushed back.\par \par \uc2 The meeting regarding nuclear weapons protocols was to remind uniformed leaders in the Pentagon of the long-established and robust procedures in light of media reporting on the subject.\par \par \uc2 A reference to Speaker Pelosi's phone call to General Milley on January 8th.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): Officials here at the Pentagon scoffed at the suggestion today from former acting Defense Secretary Chris Miller that he did not know about the call with Milley's Chinese counterpart.\par \par \uc2 Not only was he briefed, I'm told, so were senior State Department and White House officials who General Milley called after his January 8th call with his Chinese counterpart, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow this. Jennifer Griffin live at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we take you inside Afghanistan and inside a prison inside Afghanistan from which the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  has released thousands of its fighters, along with criminals since taking over that country.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Trey Yingst reports tonight from Kabul.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Right now, we are entering the Pole Charkhi Prison with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . When the group entered the Afghan capital, they came into this facility and freed thousands of their fellow fighters.\par \par \uc2 These fighters came from the Logar province, and they said it was a 15-year struggle to take over Afghanistan and that they're happy they now control the country. And they say they are bringing security to the Afghan people.\par \par \uc2 Tiny prison cells, handcuffs chained to bars and long dark hallways make up the grounds. The only people left here are petty criminals in jail and \uc2 Taliban\uc2  guards.\par \par \uc2 Niaz Mohammed Halim spent four years behind bars here as a prisoner. He came back today to visit. He's now a top \uc2 Taliban\uc2  intelligence official.\par \par \uc2 Halim thank God for the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 's takeover, he then talked about reports of division among the ranks of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  over how to divide power in the country.\par \par \uc2 NIAZ MOHAMMED HALIM, \uc2 TALIBAN\uc2  INTELLIGENCE OFFICIAL, AFGHANISTAN (through translator): From individual people to top level officials, everyone obeys the rule of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  Emirate. There is no infighting. What we order here in Kabul is implemented in all provinces, that is propaganda of the enemy.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  has started to implement a system of justice based on Sharia law. It bans men and women from working together, does not allow music to be played and adheres to violent punishment for crimes.\par \par \uc2 This as the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  today expressed concern that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  could target people who used to hold them accountable under the previous Afghan Government.\par \par \uc2 URSULA VON DER LEYEN