y should stay and making clear he didn't know what the end timeline would be.\par \par \uc2 But he strongly dissented with the intelligence believe Afghanistan would fall to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  in one to three years. Miller saying it would go much, much faster.\par \par \uc2 INHOFE: And he did not tell the president of that because the president wasn't there.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Fox has also learned Miller did have the chance to share his views with President Biden several times before the draw down and his assessments were included in the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs presentations to the National Security Council before Biden's decision.\par \par \uc2 Senators were unclear if the president ultimately saw Miller's recommendations wanting to hear from Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2 , about how much weight they were given.\par \par \uc2 Was the president support Secretary Austin appearing before Congress? Would he encourage it?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: He is appearing before Congress at the end of the month. And he's already agreed to that.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Miller told senators once his recommendation was turned down, it became his job to execute on the withdrawal order.\par \par \uc2 And eventually, decisions like abandoning Bagram were forced by constraints through the president's orders, like troop caps.\par \par \uc2 Pressure is now ramping up on the Hill for more hearings.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB MENENDEZ\uc2  (D-NJ): The Congress has either been lied to, misled, or gave overly rosy projections.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Tonight, President Biden announced new trilateral agreement between Australia, the U.K. and the U.S. known as AUKUS. It will help Australia acquire nuclear powered submarines in an apparent challenge to China's activities in the Indo-Pacific.\par \par \uc2 U.S. has only ever shared nuclear propulsion technology with one other country, the U.K. This will bolster alliances and it comes after China taunted the U.S. withdrawal from Afghanistan and also targeted U.S. allies like Taiwan, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Long military relationship with Australia but this is a big deal. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were up, today, the Dow gained 237, the S&P 500 finished ahead 38, the \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  jumped 124.\par \par \uc2 Some of America's greatest young athletes were reduced to tears today on Capitol Hill describing incidents of sexual abuse at the hands of their team doctor.\par \par \uc2 Olympic gold medalist Simone Biles and several others are accusing the \uc2 FBI\uc2  of deliberately covering up abuse by the physician during the Bureau's widely criticized investigation. They're also very upset that no one from the Justice Department came to today's hearing.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie has some of their dramatic testimony tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SIMONE BILES, OLYMPIC GYMNAST: I blame Larry Nassar and I also blame an entire system that enabled and perpetrated his abuse.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tearful testimony today from Olympic superstar Simone Biles, Aly Raisman, McKayla Maroney and Maggie Nichols, as they told members of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  how the \uc2 FBI\uc2  failed them when they tried to report sexual abuse by \uc2 USA Gymnastics\uc2  team Dr. Larry Nassar.\par \par \uc2 But tears turned into anger when Department of Justice officials who can actually bring charges didn't turn up.\par \par \uc2 MCKAYLA MARONEY, OLYMPIC GYMNAST: Deputy Attorney General Lisa Monaco couldn't even bring herself to be here today. And it is the Department of Justice's job to hold them accountable.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Several senators also took issue with DOJ's no show.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is outrageous. When we asked them to bring someone in to explain this today, they refused.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The comments come as lawmakers dig into a scathing Department of Justice report in June, which revealed the Indianapolis FBI office botched the case by failing to respond with urgency, falsifying statements and failing to notify state or local authorities.\par \par \uc2 The report found this led to a yearlong delay that ultimately allowed Nassar to abuse dozens of more girls.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This man is on the loose molesting children.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, FBI: I don't have a good explanation for you. It is utterly jarring to me.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Wray said he's implementing all of the inspector general's recommendations.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL HOROWITZ, JUSTICE DEPARTMENT INSPECTOR GENERAL: We keep following up and we will keep working on this.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And the \uc2 FBI\uc2  recently fired a supervisory agent for not properly pursuing tips about Nassar.\par \par \uc2 But former Special Agent in Charge Jay Abbott, who the DOJ found applied for a job with \uc2 USA Gymnastics\uc2  while the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was investigating Nassar wound up retiring, leaving little justice for the women.\par \par \uc2 WRAY: It is extremely frustrating that we are left with little disciplinary recourse when people retire before their cases can be adjudicated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Bret, a DOJ official tells Fox tonight that A.G. Garland will appear before this very same committee next month to take questions. And that same official is telling us also that several career prosecutors have looked over this case more than once and decided that federal charges just would not be successful, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie live on Capitol Hill. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, how data that could shed light on the origins of the coronavirus disappeared from the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  archive. You'll be surprised to hear who made that request.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 Fox 26 in Houston where 98,000 customers are still without power as the remnants of Hurricane Nicholas move into Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 Storm was downgraded today to a tropical depression but it is dumping heavy rain across the area still recovering from Hurricane Ida.\par \par \uc2 Fox 9 in Minneapolis where the Minnesota Supreme Court has overturned a third-degree murder conviction for a police officer charged in the shooting death of an Australian woman.\par \par \uc2 Mohamed Noor testified he feared he was being ambushed when Justine Damond approached his squad car. She had called earlier to report a possible assault. Noor's partner says Damond made a loud noise slapping the back of their squad car before Noor fired his gun ultimately killing the woman.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Cape Canaveral from Fox 35. Big story there tonight, the world's first all civilian space crew is preparing for liftoff. The four-person squad will ride aboard a \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  Falcon 9 rocket and Dragon Resilience spacecraft.\par \par \uc2 They'll conduct research on advancing human health on earth during their three-day trip. Launch window opens just about two hours.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Los Angeles County is -- Los Angeles County is planning to require proof of vaccination or negative COVID test to attend indoor bars, nightclubs, and lounges. That's a big place, L.A. County.\par \par \uc2 The order also covers sporting events. It will strongly recommend but not require verification to attend indoor restaurants.\par \par \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  says U.S. regulators should approve a booster dose of the COVID-19 vaccine because of decreasing effectiveness overtime of the initial shots. The company is recommending the third shot six months after the second. And \uc2 FDA\uc2  committee of outside experts will vote on this proposal Friday.\par \par \uc2 President Biden met today with the CEOs of \uc2 Walt Disney\uc2  and other companies to discuss his recently announced vaccine requirement for firms that employ at least 100 people. President says he looks forward to building on that policy.\par \par \uc2 And the man considered China's most famous defector reportedly warned American intelligence agencies about the coronavirus in November of 2019. That's according to a report in the New York Post.\par \par \uc2 It cites a portion of a book about the Wuhan lab. The defector says he felt U.S. intelligence agencies were not taking his information seriously. China did not alert the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  to the coronavirus until December 31st of 2019.\par \par \uc2 Well, the mystery surrounding the origins of the coronavirus takes another strange turn tonight. It involves the disappearance of information that could shed some light on this issue.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt picks up the story.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): The \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  is reviewing results of an investigation into deleted coronavirus data from the early days of the pandemic. Researchers store genetic sequencing data in an archive run by the \uc2 NIH\uc2 . It's accessible to scientists around the world.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. MARTY MAKARY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): Those sequences are essentially the Rosetta stone that enable virologists to trace the drift or manipulation of a genetic code.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: According to the \uc2 NIH\uc2 , the sequences from Wuhan, China were submitted to the database in March 2020. By June of that year, a Chinese researcher asked the \uc2 NIH\uc2  to remove the data.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: It's very unusual for any data of genetic information in that database to be deleted.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The \uc2 NIH\uc2  considers the sequences to be the property of researchers and obliged, but keeps a backup. It is not available to the public without the researcher's permission.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal reported the deleted sequences in June, prompting three Republicans to question NIH Director Dr. Francis Collins about the data.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN\uc2  (R-TN): The Chinese Communist Party once again, what are they trying to cover up? What do they want to make sure we never know?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The \uc2 NIH\uc2  just days ago responded to Blackburn, announcing an investigation to "determine whether the appropriate steps were taken to assess this withdrawal request. Withdrawal makes the data undiscoverable but does not erase it."\par \par \uc2 And NIH spokesperson confirms the investigation is complete, though the results are not yet public.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: We don't consider it case closed until we get the results of that investigation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: This news comes just weeks after the U.S. intelligence community fail to find a definitive origin of the virus. China says the United States should look at other countries for origin information. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We continue to follow this. David, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department is asking a federal judge in Texas to temporarily halt the controversial new state law banning abortions after about six weeks. The emergency motion comes days after the agency sued Texas, claiming the law was enacted to prevent women from exercising their constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 The Texas governor's office says it is confident the court will uphold the law. The new rule went into effect September 1st after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  refused to block it.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors say former University of Miami professor, his wife, and his sister, are facing federal charges related to purchasing genetic sequencing equipment from U.S. manufacturers, and illegally shipping it to Iran. The suspects made their initial appearances Tuesday in Miami federal court.\par \par \uc2 A criminal complaint says a company operated by the family received numerous wire transfers from accounts overseas totaling almost $3-1/2 million dollars between 2016 and 2020.\par \par \uc2 Up next, why Republicans think one key issue could be a game-changer in the Virginia governor's race. Plus, how Democrats are trying to make immigration reform an economic issue and bend the rules on the Senate filibuster.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): Fate is being decided by the Senate parliamentarian. I think we have a strong case.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): They are trying to do something on a purely partisan basis using the budget reconciliation process.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: California Democratic Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  easily defeated a Republican recall effort against him. Newsom is ahead by a 30 point margin with about two-thirds of the ballots counted so far in California. Newsom gets to keep his job, at least for a while. He is up for re-election next year.\par \par \uc2 Well, this year the governor's race in Virginia is expected to be very close. The Republican nominee and his supporters have made critical race theory which links racial discrimination to America's foundations and legal system a key issue.\par \par \uc2 The first candidate debate in this race is tomorrow. Here is Chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Critical race theory is on the ballot in Virginia and Republicans see it as a potentially defining issue in the governor's race. The RealClearPolitics average of recent poll shows Democrat \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  leading Republican Glenn Youngkin by 5.2. 46 to 40.8, likely closer than the former Governor McAuliffe would like it to be since President Biden won Virginia by 10 points. McAuliffe is called critical race theory, "Another right-wing conspiracy."\par \par \uc2 At a school board meeting in Loudoun County, Virginia, which has been ground zero in the CRT fight, parents sounded off again.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You have implemented programs that are not creating high academic achievers. It is implementing progressive political ideologies, it's not academic.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Youngkin is hitting education hard, including at a recent save our schools event with former HUD Secretary Dr. Ben Carson.\par \par \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN (R), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE, VIRGINIA: Our curriculum has gone haywire. So, on day one, we are going to ban teaching critical race theory in our schools.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: \uc2 Larry Sabato\uc2  from the \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2  says critical race theory could energies GOP voters in this off-year election.\par \par \uc2 LARRY SABATO\uc2 , DIRECTOR, CENTER FOR POLITICS, \uc2 UNIVERSITY OF VIRGINIA\uc2 : They see these issues, these curriculum issues and some of the controversy about masks, for example, is helping them to reduce the democratic margin. They might be influenced by this and might well motivate more Republicans to turn out or at least at a greater rate than Democrats turnout. That's the Republican hope.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: This is a critical stage in the race with that first debate tomorrow night, and early voting starting on Friday, in a race that could create a playbook for next year's midterms. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's one to watch. Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 House Democrats are under pressure from Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  tonight to have language specifically ready for their massive $3.5 trillion tax and spending bill. They call it human infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi wants to pass that measure before the end of the month. Well, today, White House officials, including President Biden are putting their own pressure on too moderate Democratic senators, who so far have said they won't support the bill as proposed.\par \par \uc2 Congressional correspondent Chad Pergram tells us where things stand tonight from Capitol Hill. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Well, Kyrsten Sinema and Joe Manchin went to the White House today. They're trying to get an agreement to keep moderates on board here.\par \par \uc2 The other thing to watch for is whether or not immigration reform can be a part of this reconciliation bill. Listen.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): Democrats are trying to stuff as much as they can into their big social spending bill. It's just not environmental policies and tax hikes. Liberals could use the multitrillion-dollar bill to pass immigration reform and create pathways to citizenship for millions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): That is too precious and too important of an opportunity for us to allow to sail by.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Budget bills are reserved exclusively for money in taxes. Democrats are already using the special process to avoid a filibuster. The Senate can't pass immigration the regular way because of the filibuster, but it's different if Democrats can jam immigration into this bill. So, they are working the ref.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN, (D-IL) SENATE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: Our fate is being decided by the Senate parliamentarian. I think we have a strong case. I hope she agrees.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Senate Parliamentarian \uc2 Elizabeth MacDonough\uc2  determines what's in and what's out. If Democrats persuade MacDonough that immigration is a money issue, it could be in. Even Senate Minority Leader Mitch McConnell admires the audacity of the Democrats, and he is resigned to an eventual McDonough decision.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: I can't blame the other side for trying. That's always the temptation of the majority using the reconciliation process. But abiding by the ruling of the parliamentarian is essential to the function of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Democrats could use revenue from new citizens as new taxpayers to cover the cost of the bill.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN, (D-SC) HOUSE MAJORITY WHIP: If we do the Dreamers and get protected workers on a pathway to citizenship, it would be, some estimate, $121 billion benefit to the GDP.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But some Republicans think the Democrats effort to wedge immigration into the bill shows desperation.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN CORNYN\uc2 , (R-TX) \uc2 SENATE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE\uc2 : I think it speaks to their lack of seriousness at dealing with things like the border crisis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): If they cut the size of the bill to please moderates, the left would be hard pressed to oppose the plan if they include immigration. Also, the House has just finalized tax policy in the bill. The corporate rate 26.5 percent, and those earning more than $5 million a year are pegged with a three percent upcharge. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, without Manchin and Sinema, this does not get through. There are other moderates that possibly are not voting for this because of other pieces of it, right, Chad?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Or is this all negotiated down to something Schumer says, OK, we won't do this, but we are going to pass this?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Two things to look at here. There is so much attention focused on Kyrsten Sinema and Joe Manchin. Today in the Ways and Means Committee when they marked up the tax portion of the bill, the blue dog leader Stephanie Murphy, a moderate Democrat from Florida, she voted no. She left the door open to maybe voting yes later. And also \uc2 Tom Suozzi\uc2 , a Democrat from Long Island, he raised questions about whether or not they would have SALT, the state and local tax reduction, whether or not that would be in this bill. It's not in there yet. But he has said he might oppose this if they don't fix this in the end. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's a good update. We're going to talk about more of this with the panel. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, that new car smell getting harder and harder to find.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: State police in South Carolina say a prominent lawyer tried to arrange his own death earlier this month so his son would get a $10 million life insurance payment. But authorities say the plan's fatal shot only grazed Alex Murdaugh's head. The state law enforcement division says it has charged the shooter with assisted suicide, insurance fraud, and several other counts. Murdaugh has not been charged, but agents say that may change. Two days after the shooting, Murdaugh said he was resigning from his law firm and entering rehab. Murdaugh's son and wife were killed in a double shooting in June.\par \par \uc2 Police investigating the disappearance of a 22-year-old woman who was on a cross-country trip with her boyfriend now say that man is a person of interest in this case. The family of Gabby Petito says Brian Laundrie is refusing to tell them where he last saw her. He has hired an attorney but so far has not spoken to police. Meanwhile, police in Utah say officers were called for an incident involving the two, but no charges were filed, and they are still following up on all leads there.\par \par \uc2 If you are trying to find a new vehicle, you know prices are high, supply is scarce. Part of the reason is that shortage we have been telling you about of a key electronic component. FOX Business Correspondent Lydia Hu is at a car dealership in New Jersey to explain. Good evening, Lydia.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. The global shortage on semiconductor chips continues as industry groups say shipments and production are reaching an all-time high. The world's few chip manufacturers, located mostly overseas, struggle to keep up with surge in demand as supply chain bottlenecks delay shipments. Taiwan is home to the world's leading chip producer, and its tense relationship with China adds concern about America's ongoing access to chips.\par \par \uc2 The shortage is pitting the auto industry against consumer electronics for the limited supply available. The car manufacturers are feeling the pinch with constrained car inventories. Estimates show more than 4 million vehicles have been lost due to the chip shortage. Those losses could reach 9.5 million as some automakers stall production without needed chips.\par \par \uc2 And vehicle prices are already surging. Prices for new cars continue to climb in August, up 7.6 percent year over year. The average new vehicle transaction price for last month was more than $43,000. That's a record high for the fifth straight month. And prices on used cars and trucks came down slightly in August but are still up nearly 32 percent year over year. Average transaction prices for used cars at dealerships hit more than $28,000 last month.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOM MAOLI NEW JERSEY DEALERSHIP OWNER: I think it's going to stay this way for a long time because, as they start producing, the pent-up demand is going to try dry up the inventory that's being produced, and you're going to have another supply and demand issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: Now, the chip shortage is expected to stretch into the beginning of next year. In the short-term car prices are expected to surge again in the aftermath of hurricane Ida as widespread flooding is believed to have destroyed thousands of cars. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lydia, thank you. And that's not just cars. A lot of different items. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on a bombshell report of then President Trump's top military adviser, what he did or didn't do, plus the reaction. Plus, what are Capitol Hill's plans for taxes? We are going to go into that.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. A post monsoon fever outbreak kills at least 115 people in the most populous state of India. The state's health minister says most cases were caused by dengue, a seasonal viral infection spread by mosquitoes.\par \par \uc2 The sister of North Korean leader \uc2 Kim Jong-un\uc2  threatens a complete destruction of bilateral relations with South Korea. The criticism of Kim Yo-jong comes after both countries tested ballistic missiles hours apart. The rival launches underscore a return of tensions amid stalled nuclear talks there.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have great confidence in General Milley.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: His experience with him has been that he is a patriot. He is somebody who is -- has fidelity to the Constitution. I can't speak to anonymous, unconfirmed reports about prior conversations during the last administration.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: It is completely appropriate for the chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff as the senior military adviser to both the secretary and the president to want to see those protocols reviewed. I see nothing in what I have read that would -- that would cause any concern.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAND PAUL\uc2 , (R-KY): If it happened, he should be immediately relieved of his dues and court-martialed. If it is true, absolutely immediately needs to be removed. This is very, very dangerous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, that's another day in Washington after a Bob Woodward book. This time Bob Woodward and Bob Costa with the book "Peril," reaction to charges, allegations in this book of what Chairman of the Joint Chiefs General Mark Milley did in the wake of the January 6th riots on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Here's a voice we hadn't heard from in a while, retired lieutenant colonel Alexander Vindman of impeachment fame tweeting out "If this is true General Milley must resign. He usurped civilian authority, broke chain of command, violated the sacrosanct principle of civilian control over the military. It's an extremely dangerous precedent. You can't simply walk away from that. Do the right thing in the right way."\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Ben Domenech, publisher of "The Federalist," Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, CEO of Empowerment and Inclusion Capital, and \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , former Congressman from South Carolina. Trey, a lot of push back from the Pentagon today. We had our own reporting saying what General Milley did with two calls to the Chinese was to try to calm fears, and what he did, they say, with the nuclear process was to make sure the process was intact and everybody knew what was going on. The book alleges something different. Where are you on this and the significance of it?\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA REPRESENTATIVE: Bret, I have never liked anonymous source. We certainly didn't use them in the courtroom, and there is a reason for that. And I don't like starting things by saying "if it's true," but this is television and not court. I find the whole thing bizarre that you have a U.S. general who thinks an American president is more of a threat to the country than China. I think it's really bizarre that he didn't tell anyone who could have actually done something about it, if that's what he believed. And I think it's bizarre that we call people ahead of time and warn them, hey, we may attack you. My dominant thought, Bret, is I bet that Afghan driver who was bringing some water to folks would have appreciated an advance call before an attack.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold, this comes, stems from in a book a transcript actually of a call between Speaker Pelosi and General Milley in the wake of the January 6th riot on Capitol Hill. I assume it's Pelosi's office who records that call and gives Woodward the transcript. But in it she says how do we prevent this president who losing it from using the nuclear football, from authorizing a nuclear strike, which, by the way, no one in the Trump administration or around them ever said that the president was even contemplating any action like that.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: Look, first, thanks for having me on. I think Trey, again, has the frame about right. We have to remember, though, the last two months of President Trump's presidency was pretty chaotic. In fact, the last two weeks were the most remarkable -- the most remarkable I have seen in politics since 9/11. It appears that according to the reporting in the book, and I have not read it, just what reports are about the reporting in the book, that Senators Graham and McConnell also wanted to try to erect some guardrails around the president, and including Attorney General Barr, according to the reporting.\par \par \uc2 General Milley will have to answer these questions before the Senate in a couple of weeks. And I don't know him, but what I do know of him from seeing him on television, reading his comments, he seems like a pretty straightforward guy who is not afraid to answer questions honestly. So we will see if the reporting is true.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, it's important to have that context in the wake of January 6th and leading up to January 20th, Ben. There were a lot of people inside the administration who was saying the former president, now former, was acting erratically. But the actions of the chairman of the Joint Chiefs if as reported is true, and again, there is a lot of pushback, would go outside his line of command.\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, PUBLISHER, "THE FEDERALIST": One think I can tell you, Bret, is that members of Congress, certainly the ones that I have spoken to, do not believe the pushback. They do not believe that this is an accurate reflection of what went on there. And they have raised significant questions, particularly those members of the Republican caucus who are themselves veterans. And I expect that we will see a continued focus on this in the days to come, particularly given that this is something that exists outside of the chain of command. This is not a situation where General Milley would ever be authorized to do something along these lines.\par \par \uc2 And there are questions that are raised by this that go beyond just the normal slate of things. This is the kind of thing that can be used in many ways to run down the expectations and the respect that military members have for not just the chain of command but for the highest officers, those who are met with the greatest challenges. It does no service to them in order to see this kind of thing happen. And I think you are going to see an investigation into this, perhaps a classified one from the Intel Committee or from the Armed Services Committee in the House that continues and that digs down and that tries to get to the accurate estimation of what really was said, because I think we all deserve to know it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Again, Milley is scheduled to testify September 28th. We have invited the chairman of the Joint Chiefs on the show if he wants to come talk about before that, and we will see how that testimony goes.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, up on Capitol Hill, dealing with what is in the bill, the massive bill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president encouraged members of Congress to put things forward, things they would like to see in the package.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON\uc2 , (R-LA): This $3.5 trillion spending palooza includes amnesty for somewhere between 8 and 10 million illegal immigrants.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN HORSFORD, (D-NV) HOUSE WAYS AND MEANS COMMITTEE: It's about making the American worker competitive.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN CORNYN\uc2 , (R-TX): Permanent welfare with no work requirements, green new deal climate mandates.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY GOMEZ, (D-CA) HOUSE WAYS AND MEANS COMMITTEE: We invest the largest amount in the history of this country in the green economy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROB PORTMAN, (R-OH): It increases taxes on retirement accounts. It increases income taxes.\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA\uc2 , (D-CA): The plan means that seniors are going to get dental. They are going to get vision.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE KELLY, (R-PA) HOUSE WAYS AND MEANS COMMITTEE: This ain't no open bar. Somebody's paying for it. Somebody is going to pay for it down the road.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So some different takes about what's in the bill. Trey, we have got moderate senators, Kyrsten Sinema and Joe Manchin up on the White House. I'm sure they are doing the hardcore press, full-court press on trying to get those moderates to come along.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: I don't know Joe Manchin, but I can tell you it won't work with Kyrsten Sinema. She ain't afraid of anything. My two dominate thoughts are, number one, how embarrassed I am. I never thought to get the parliamentarian to tell me I could put whatever I wanted in a bill and just have one bill for the entire year. Never entered my mind. We used to call that a queen. Now it's called a parliamentarian.\par \par \uc2 The other thing I can't get my head around, Bret, is we are a 50-50 country. The House is close. The Senate is evenly divided. That is not the time to pursue transformational change on either side. It won't work.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Massive immigration reform in a budgetary item is really pretty brazen. But we'll see where that goes.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Wall Street Journal" on the tax plan. I just want to give a couple second to this. Expected to raise -- this is what passed out of panel today, expected to raise more than $2 trillion in new revenue over the next decade from high income households and U.S. companies, raise the corporate tax rate to 26.5 percent from 21 percent, basic top income tax rate would increase from 239.6 from 37 percent. The top bracket starting at $400,000 for individuals, $450,000 for married couples. A three percent surtax for household incomes above $5 million. It would increase long-term capital gains and dividends to 25 percent from 20 percent, prevents people from contributing to retirement plans that's Roth IRA if value exceeds $10 million, $78 billion in funding for \uc2 IRS\uc2  to ramp up tax enforcement. That's a lot more tax agents. Doubles the current tax rate on cigarettes, small cigars, and roll your own tobacco, just to name few. Harold, there is a lot in here.\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : There is. Look, I am for immigration reform. But this is not the way to do it in a reconciliation package. And I would hope the parliamentarian might feel the same way, but we shall see. I would agree with Trey. Senator Manchin and Senator Sinema are probably not going to agree to too much of this.\par \par \uc2 But I've got to tell you, a tax code and spending on the part of government ought to be organized around how we make America stronger and more competitive. And I think some of the ideas in this plan are not as objectionable I think to a majority of Americans as some may think, particularly when you consider that it's going to raise taxes on the highest of earners. I think the $400,000 limit the White House may have, they are going to have to shelve that. If what this plan says you are going to tax those earning over $5 million over a year a little bit more to pay for some things, I think a lot of Americans may come along with that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. I think there may be a lot of horse trading here and Chuck Schumer may know that it's not going to be $3.5 trillion. It may be $2 trillion and a lot may go in the negotiation. But they might still get something through.\par \par \uc2 Ben, you will start on tomorrow's headlines when we come back after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally tonight, a look at tomorrow's headlines with the panel. As promised, Ben, first to you.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Hilarious Canadian man fights cancer to a draw. RIP to the great Norm MacDonald, the best news anchor that I know not named Bret Baier for his fight and battle against cancer. He pointed out that you never actually lose to cancer. You take it with you. So he fought it to a draw. And congratulations to him and all the happiness he has given us over the many years of life he had on this earth.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rest in peace. Trey?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  botches another major investigation, this one involving sexually abused young girls.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tough one. Harold?\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : I agree with Trey.\par \par \uc2 Woodward has done it again, this time with Bob Costa. He has gotten Washington's most powerful to talk about things that sell books.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That is true. Thanks, panel.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, Democrats push federal voting rights in the latest attempt to federalize elections. Will they have the votes?\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for the SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "FOX NEWS PRIMETIME" hosted by Lawrence Jones starts right now. Hey, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020210916eh9f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with Governor \uc2 Pete Ricketts\uc2  of Nebraska\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Wallace, Mark Meredith, Karl Rove, Charles Lane \par \uc2 8,414 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Chris Wallace.\par \par \uc2 President Biden faces a barrage of legal challenges to his sweeping vaccinate mandates, impacting tens of millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is not about freedom or personal choice. It's about protecting yourself and those around you.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (voice-over): As the delta variant surges, the latest offensive in the fight against COVID faces pushback from Republicans, accusing the White House of big government overreach.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KRISTI NOEM (R), SOUTH DAKOTA: I'll fight to protect my people and to defend their freedoms.\par \par \uc2 GOV. PETE RICKETTS (R), NEBRASKA: The president has forgotten we live in America.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: These pandemic politics, as I refer to it, are making people sick.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: We'll ask Nebraska Governor \uc2 Pete Ricketts\uc2  about the battle over federal mandates as the president gets tough with the unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Then, our exclusive sit-down with Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 . We ask about calls for Breyer to step down while Democrats still control the White House and Senate.\par \par \uc2 Why didn't you retire?\par \par \uc2 And the push by progressives to pack the court.\par \par \uc2 What you think of the idea of increasing the number of justices on the court?\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , only on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 And -- America marks 20 years since the 9/11 attack shook our nation and kicked off the war on terror. Now with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  back in control of Afghanistan, we'll ask our Sunday panel if there's a new threat to the homeland.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (on camera): And hello again from FOX News, today from our brand- new studio here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 On this 20th anniversary weekend of 9/11, I'm sure many of you are still reflecting on that roll call of names we heard again yesterday, people of all races and religions that fell like teardrops on our hearts. It never gets easier, and maybe that's the point. We'll have much more on 9/11 20 years out and where we stand in the war on terror later this hour.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with breaking news. President Biden's new mandate that millions of workers get vaccinated or test for COVID weekly has created new battle lines in the fight over the virus buried. Amid accusations of federal government overreach, some 19 Republican governors are now threatening to -- as one of them put it -- fight to the gates of hell.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll speak with one of the governors, \uc2 Pete Ricketts\uc2  of Nebraska.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's bring in Mark Meredith at the White House with a look at the brewing battle -- Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Chris, the president insists sweeping new mandates are necessary to convince some 80 million unvaccinated Americans that now is the time to get the shot. But critics argue the president is overstepping his authority and they're vowing to fight back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We are playing for real here. This isn't a game.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice-over): President Biden is brushing off looming legal challenges to new vaccine mandates. He says, starting soon, OSHA will require employers with more than 100 people mandate vaccinations or require employees who opt out to undergo weekly testing. More federal workers and contractors will be required to be vaccinated in order to keep their jobs, and the TSA is doubling the minimum fine for travelers who violate the mask mandate.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: My message to unvaccinated Americans is this: what more is there to wait for? What more do you need to see?\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: But several GOP governors claim the president is going too far, arguing American businesses should not be forced to police employees' medical choices.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA: I do not believe that people should lose their jobs over this issue, and we will fight that.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: The White House admits there are limits to what it can mandate for schools. It's urging governors require school employees be vaccinated. In Los Angeles, the nation's second-largest school district, officials going even further. Soon, every student 12 and older will need to be vaccinated to attend in person classes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): Tomorrow, the president is going to be hitting the road off to Idaho to get an update on how the government is battling wildfires. Then he's going to California to campaign for embattled Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , whose recall election, Chris, has been dominated by his response to the pandemic -- Chris.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Mark Meredith reporting from the White House -- Mark, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now, the governor of Nebraska, \uc2 Pete Ricketts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Governor, welcome to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 GOV. PETE RICKETTS (R), NEBRASKA: Good morning. Thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I want to start with something that you said this week about President Biden's new vaccine mandates. Here you are, sir.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICKETTS: The president has forgotten we live in America. He thinks we live in the Soviet Union, and the hypocrisy of this is just unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Governor, what's so objectionable about Biden's vaccine mandates and what are you going do about it?\par \par \uc2 RICKETTS: Well, first of all, we have been encouraging people to get vaccinated. We've been providing information and encouraging people to reach out to their neighbors because vaccines work and they will help people.\par \par \uc2 But it should be a personal health care choice. This is not something that the government should mandate and somebody shouldn't have to make the choice between keeping their job and getting a job in the arm. I mean, it's just wrong. I've talked to people, a number of people who have told me if they made me take the vaccine, I'm just going to be fired. I'm not going to -- I'm not going to do it.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But you say it's a personal choice. In fact, to attend school in your state of Nebraska, children must be vaccinated against a number of diseases.\par \par \uc2 Let me put them up on the screen. They must be vaccinated against diphtheria, tetanus and pertussis, polio, measles, mumps, and rubella, hepatitis b, and chicken pox.\par \par \uc2 Why are those mandates that parents and your state must comply with and do comply with routinely -- why is it that they are not so objectionable and such a violation of personal freedom, but Biden's vaccine mandates are?\par \par \uc2 RICKETTS: Well, for all those that you just listed, there's a long history that parents have had the opportunity to see how those things have been implemented. And there's still a lot of people out there who don't know what to trust and in fact this is really an outcome of what the CDC has done because they flip-flopped on so many issues, whether it's masks, or whether you have to mask if you've been vaccinated and so forth. There's just a lot of people out there who don't know who to trust right now.\par \par \uc2 And so, by having the government force it on -- you're not building the trust where we have the heart trust with those other vaccines. This is a process that's going to take time to bring people along, and that's why it should be a personal choice and not something mandated by the government buried\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But forgive me, sir. I'm old enough that I remember when the polio vaccine first came out, a lot of us and certainly our parents viewed it as a blessing. And immediately -- I lived in New York state at that time -- the state mandated that we all get the polio vaccine.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, we're in the middle of a pandemic. There is a new vaccine that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was largely responsible for. It's been approved, full approval by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Again, if polio vaccine is okay for parents and they have to comply with it to send their kids to school, why not for a lot of people, not just kids, the vaccine for -- for this disease?\par \par \uc2 RICKETTS: Yeah. I think this is very different from polio that has very devastating effects and certainly we know if you're older, 65 years and older, that's were 83 percent of our deaths in Nebraska came from, we know this is really devastating. But we also know that nearly 87 percent of our 65 years and older population has been vaccinated and if you're looking at young children, for example, here in Nebraska, we can look at the data and see that really children are at no more risk for the coronavirus than they are for the ordinary flu.\par \par \uc2 And so, it's all about balancing off these risks. And the risk for this is just such where this is something that we shouldn't be mandating it. Again, the whole goal for all we are doing at least in Nebraska how we're doing it, is around making sure we're preserving hospital capacity and we've successfully done that here, even without doing statewide mask mandates and without doing vaccine passports.\par \par \uc2 So let's keep the objective in mind, which is to be able to provide health care and which we have successfully done here in Nebraska by protecting our hospital capacity and not have the heavy hand of government come in and tell people what to do. They just want to hear it.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: President Biden was asked on Friday about leaders, governors like you who say they're going to challenge him on the vaccine mandates. Here's what his response was.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNTIED STATES: Have at it. Look, I am so disappointed that particularly some Republican governors have been so cavalier with the health of these kids, so cavalier with the health of their communities.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Governor, are you being cavalier with the health of your communities?\par \par \uc2 RICKETTS: What we're doing is focusing on preserving hospital capacity, which we have successfully done here Nebraska. We've got the third -- we're tied for the third lowest mortality rate of any state in the country for people who have contracted COVID-19, and so we protected that hospital capacity to provide that care.\par \par \uc2 And again, we can look at the data specifically around children and see the risks. At last year in Nebraska, if you were aged 10-19, you were 26 times more likely to die in a car accident than you were of COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 So, the president should look at the data and maybe the president should attend one of the weekly calls is an administration has with all the governors -- he's not been on one yet since he's been president -- and maybe talk to some of the governors and ask them about what's going on in their states, because he appears to be pretty ignorant of what's going on in places like Nebraska.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Let's talk about the COVID situation in your state, and again, it's not just about kids, it's about the entire population. Let me put the stats up on the screen. Back in June, Nebraska was averaging 23 new cases a day. Now with the delta surge, you're averaging 759 new cases a day.\par \par \uc2 Governor, that's the highest since last winter.\par \par \uc2 RICKETTS: Yeah, and again, what we focus on is preserving our hospital capacity and we have seen a steady increase in hospitalizations throughout the course of this summer. Here in the month of September, the number of hospitalizations has bounced around between suit 379 and then on Friday, it dropped down to 350. So it's been in that range through most of September. And that is in contrast to the 987 we had at our peak in November of 2020.\par \par \uc2 So you can see we're well below where we reached our peak last year. We did declare a staffing emergency for hospitals to be able to help them manage their folks, you know, to make it easier to bring in nurses and so forth.\par \par \uc2 But what we focus on is providing the hospital capacity. We set up a transfer center to be able to move people around between hospitals statewide and all that to make sure we're protecting the hospital capacity. And that's our guiding star here in Nebraska. And we've done a very successfully, as I indicated with our success in keeping people, you know, from dying from this disease.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I want -- I want to talk to about that, because I looked into the situation, the stress on hospital staffing and hospital beds and the fact is, on September 1st, you announced your state was opening a hospital transfer center that would allow patients to be moved around in case there wasn't enough staff or beds in one place.\par \par \uc2 You also have directed health measures to limit elective surgeries. And one of your top health officials says, the fact is there isn't enough room in your hospitals by the end of each day for high-level care.\par \par \uc2 RICKETTS: Actually what we've done is move the people around if we needed to, that's what the transfer center is for, to make it easier for hospitals who want to move a patient to get more acute care, to be able to officially move that person into hospital.\par \par \uc2 This primarily happens when you've got, say, a more rural hospital settings, that may not have all the facilities that maybe some of our urban centers do, and allow them to officially get that to the right hospital. And certainly, we are encouraging people to continue to get vaccinated as part of this, but we're managing our hospital capacity very successfully here in this state with the tools that we have provided the staffing emergency.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: So, I asked your question at the top, I ask you why it was so objectionable, you answer that. You didn't answer the other question. Let's finish with that, what are you going to do about President Biden's vaccine mandates?\par \par \uc2 RICKETTS: Yeah. So I've been talking to my attorney general. He's coordinating with the other attorneys general across the country who share similar views about the overreach. This is an egregious overreach of federal authority.\par \par \uc2 And as we see what these rules are, we will be able to know exactly how we will be able to challenge them in court. I'm also talking with my colleagues around the country as well, the other governors who feel the way I do, and we'll be working on other strategies.\par \par \uc2 But I've got to tell you, I have heard from so many workers, so many small businesses who say this is just not going to be something they can handle. So many people told me they're just going to be fired if they're forced to take the vaccine. This is really going to create huge problems for all small businesses and for our American workers.\par \par \uc2 And again, you shouldn't have to make the choice of keeping your job or getting a job in the arm.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Very briefly, you say you're going to court. Any idea how soon?\par \par \uc2 RICKETTS: Well, when we get an idea of what these rules exactly will be, we'll know how to be able to attack it in court.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Governor Ricketts, thank you. Thanks for your time today. Please come back, sir.\par \par \uc2 RICKETTS: Great. Thanks very much for having me on, Chris.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Up next, we'll bring in our Sunday group to discuss the Biden demonstration's battle with some governors over vaccine mandates as well as its fight with Texas over that state's new abortion law.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This is not about freedom or personal choice. It's about protecting yourself and those around you.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES (R), MISSISSIPPI: You would expect words like that from the president may be of communist China or North Korea.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): Scolding and condescending to Americans, dripping with scorn that somehow this is their fault that they've been infected with a virus.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Sharp pushback from Mississippi Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  and Senator Tom Cotton to President Biden's announcement the federal government will impose a vaccine mandate on businesses with more than 100 employees.\par \par \uc2 And it's time now for our Sunday group, GOP strategist Karl Rove, Susan Page of "\uc2 USA Today\uc2 ," and \uc2 Charles Lane\uc2  for "The Washington Post."\par \par \uc2 So, Karl, what do you think of the president's new vaccine mandates both as a matter of policy and as a matter of politics?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, first of all, the question is where does the authority exist to impose this? It's one thing to say, as the president of the United States, I'm going to require that all federal employees and members of the military and federal contractors get it, it's unclear to me where the authority comes to apply this to all employers with more than 100. He's doing this under a 50-year-old law that allows the OSHA at the Department of Labor to put in place an emergency temporary standard. This is been done nine times in 50 years. In three instances, it was relatively modest and wasn't challenged in court, but in six instances, the ETS was challenged in court and lost five times.\par \par \uc2 I think the president is doing this because he's afraid to go to Congress and ask for a law that gives him the authority to do this. Yes, we ought to get vaccinated. Yes, private companies have the ability to say to their employees, "you have to take the vaccine or test or you don't have a job".\par \par \uc2 But I don't think the federal government has the authority under law to do what the president is talking about.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Susan, there's no question that the president's standing in the polls have slipped as the delta variant has spiked and there's also no question if you look at the polls that Democrats are overwhelmingly in favor of vaccine mandates. So is it your sense that the White House feels one, this is the way to deal with the real problem that they are getting hit on, and in addition, whether they win or lose, it's a good fight to pick with Republican governors like \uc2 Pete Ricketts\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : Well, the White House insisted they have firm legal standing to take the steps, but the fact is the bigger threat to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his presidency is not accusations of government overreach, it's a failure, it would be a failure to get this pandemic under control. That is the issue that elected him president and it is job one for his administration.\par \par \uc2 And clearly, the administration has had more trouble than they expected in convincing the vaccine reluctant to go ahead and get a shot, so I think what they've decided to do is what David Axelrod says is getting caught trying. That you're better off trying to do something sweeping to get this pandemic under control even at the risk of court challenges from all these Republican governors.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Chuck, as our Supreme Court watcher, how strong do you think the president's case is that through OSHA, which says that it can establish emergency rules to make a safe workplace, that they have the authority to do this? I have to say in the last week, I've been reading up on the 1905 case of Jacobson v. Massachusetts where Cambridge's Massachusetts was able to order, according to the court, an individual to get a vaccination for smallpox.\par \par \uc2 Does the federal government and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , do they have the authority to do this?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES LANE\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, that precedent is very much on point and has influenced the courts recent limited rulings on various public health measures related to the pandemic. The difference in that case, of course, was that it was a state law that directly authorized or rather required this vaccine. What we're talking about here and Karl went through it a little bit before, is something a little more attenuated, working through OSHA to require employers to require.\par \par \uc2 I think in all of this, there hasn't been quite enough attention on the fact that there is an alternative for the employee to getting vaccinated, which is submitting to regular testing. And that may help the president's proposal stand up in court.\par \par \uc2 But you know, Chris, the one thing I remember from law school is, wait to read the text and we won't even see the text of this order it seems for several weeks. And it will only be then that we really have something to have a lawsuit about, as Governor Ricketts I think alluded to. So what we are going to have between now and then is a lot of posturing about the legal issues involved, and perhaps a lot more people rushing out to get vaccinated in anticipation of it, but as we sit here today, we still don't know exactly what the rule is going to be.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I want to turn in the time we have left in the segment to another story, and that is that the Justice Department announced this week that it is going to sue the state of Texas over its new abortion law that says that it can ban abortions after six weeks when a fetal heartbeat is detectable, and here was the attorney general of the United States, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , on that. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: This kind of scheme can nullify the Constitution of the United States is one that all Americans, whatever their politics or party, should fear.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Karl, they're real questions about whether or not the federal government has what they call in a technical stance standing, whether it's a legitimate party to sue the state of Texas at this point.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: I think that's right, but an abortion provider can, and a woman who finds herself in a situation that's affected by the law would have standing, but I agree the federal government is more like a political stunt to demonstrated administration is in favor of protecting a woman's right to abortion under Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 But I do think that an abortion provider, if they go to court, and there's one already on its way in Texas, is going to find a juicy target in this enforcement mechanism in which the government does not enforce the law, but it's instead enforced by private individuals suing in civil court. This is a clever effort to avoid judicial review of this law and it's why people like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , very strong pro-life governor of Florida, says we are going to have a fetal heartbeat law but we are knuckling to use the enforcement mechanism that they have in Texas.\par \par \uc2 And I talked to a number of leaders in the pro-life movement, they are concerned about this enforcement mechanism as well.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Susan, I want to ask you, why would the justice department announced it's going to sue even before there's a case, even before that there is a case that we know of where an abortion provider gave a woman and abortion after the six-week deadline, why would they decide to sue so preemptively?\par \par \uc2 PAGE: So legal ingenuity on both sides with this one. This peculiar and unique law tries to avoid court review by empowering citizens rather than officials to enforce it, and that's been a real challenge for those who might traditionally be bringing a suit against this law and I think that is why the Justice Department has stepped in. Legal ingenuity on the justice department's part as well on this.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of pressure from the president's political base, which is pro- choice, supports a woman's right to choose to get involved with this because it is going to be so tricky in the court system, especially in the wake of this Supreme Court decision not to enjoin this law when it was first -- when it first reached its docket.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: All right, panel. We have to take a break here, we will see a little later in the hour.\par \par \uc2 With the Supreme Court likely to get embroiled in disputes over vaccine mandates and abortion, up next, my exclusive interview with Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 . We ask him about adding seats to the court and why he ignored calls to retire.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Coming up, we ask Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  about his future.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: What you hope people say about your service on the court?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Our one-on-one exclusive on retirement and packing the court, and more, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: He's been a Supreme Court justice for 27 years and is now the target of a new push by liberals who want him to step down so he can be replaced by someone younger. That makes it all the more timely that \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  has just come out with a new book called "The Authority of the Court and the Peril of Politics," where he discusses the very real threat the court will get caught up in today's polarized debate.\par \par \uc2 I sat down recently with Justice Breyer for an exclusive one-on-one.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Justice Breyer, welcome back to "Fox News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN BREYER\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE OF THE SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I want to start with the premise of your book. You quote Alexander Hamilton as saying that the judiciary has neither purse, like Congress, nor sword, like the executive, and so it has to rely on public acceptance for its authority. Is the public acceptance - is the authority, the court, in jeopardy?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Hard to say. I mean, I don't think - I'm an optimist, you know, and so I think, well, people understand, to some degree, why it's a good idea what Hamilton thought. And he thought the court should be there because there should be somebody - somebody who says when the other two branches of the government have gone outside the confines of that document, who's that, the president? Well, he was a little worried the president might say whatever he did was right. Congress? Well, some countries do have that. And a member of Congress knows what's popular. If he didn't know what was popular, he won't be there - or she won't be there for a long time.\par \par \uc2 But this document is made for unpopular people, just as much as for popular people. So -\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I'm curious -- I'm curious, do you carry a copy of the Constitution with you, or just for TV?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: No, not just for TV. It's always in my pocket, and I hope I will put a jacket on that has one in my pocket, because you never know when somebody's going to ask a question.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Which brings us to the second half of the title of your book, "The Peril of Politics." You say that if people come to view judges, justices, as politicians in robes, that that undercuts the authority of the court.\par \par \uc2 BREYER: To a degree I think it would. I mean junior league politicians. Hey, if you want to have a politician, let's have a senior league politician.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: One of the most interesting parts of your book, you say at one point it's wrong to think of the court as a political institution. But then you quickly add, to say that there's a complete divorce between the court and politics isn't quite right either.\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: So which is it?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: When the court - in what I think was one of its, perhaps it's greatest decision, said the words of this Constitution, which say equal protection of the law, mean that you cannot have racial segregation by law. When they said that, a few years later a case comes up about marriage between a black man and a white woman. Ha, did they hear that and say it was illegal? Frankfurter said - I believe it was Frankfurter, don't take it now. Hear the case later.\par \par \uc2 Why? Because they were having a very, very, very hard time getting the south to accept their ruling. And so they're interested in that. And Earl Warren, of course, was a great political figure. And I've always thought - I have no evidence -- but I've always thought that his experience in politics there led him to think, let's not take it now.\par \par \uc2 Eventually they took it and they said, of course, people of different races can get married. Of course. But that timing - that timing is the kind of thing that, well, the law books don't teach you that. It's not there in the tredusis (ph). You see what I mean?\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: In other words, even the court can't go too far too fast.\par \par \uc2 BREYER: No.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: So let's pursue that.\par \par \uc2 You say judges should not play politics, they should not push ideology, but they do have different jurist prudential philosophies. And I want to quote you, specifically. You write, "some judges emphasize text and history, some emphasize purposes and consequences."\par \par \uc2 But, Justice Breyer, isn't that a bit of a copout because don't the judges, the textualist, almost always tilt conservative and don't the consequences justices -- you've been accused of being that -- generally lean liberal?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: I don't know. I mean it's so easy to say. And there is a recent case involving gay rights -- rights in the workplace, not to be fair because of sexual discrimination.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Right. Right, right.\par \par \uc2 BREYER: That was made up of a group of people, the majority there, who - they included people who emphasized consequences and they included people who emphasized absolutely pure text, you see.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: One of your arguments against seeing the court as political is the fact that it refused to ever hear the appeals from the Trump camp about the 2020 election, didn't even hear them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: No judge has had the courage, including the Supreme Court. I am so disappointed in them. No judge, including the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2 , has had the courage to allow it to be heard.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Why was that?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Why was it? Because they didn't bring a case, I guess, that met the normal criteria for being heard. When we decide to take a case, there have to be four votes to take it. So I can't go beyond that.\par \par \uc2 What we do know is that there were not four votes to take it because it wasn't taken. And there are criteria, and if we don't take a case it's, you know, I mean the reason was -- all likelihood is that the criteria weren't met.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: One of the problems that you discuss in your book is the growing partisanship of the confirmation process.\par \par \uc2 Back in 1994, you were confirmed by the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  87 to nine.\par \par \uc2 Question, do you think, in 2021, as a former staffer for Edward M. Kennedy --\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: There's any chance you would get 87 votes?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: I know what you're saying and the answer is, of course, no.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Look at what Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  did. In 2016, he refuses to give \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  even a hearing in the eight months before the election. And then, in 2020, he pushes through Amy Coney Barrett in one month before the election.\par \par \uc2 Doesn't that undercut the authority of the court?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: I was confirmed. And I was nominated. And the confirmation process and the nomination process, well, I say, usually when you ask me about that, and people do, I say it's like asking for the recipe for Chicken a la King from the point of view of the chicken.\par \par \uc2 And -- and I will say -\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I -- I -- that --\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Yes, that's a flip joke.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREYER: But the truth of the joke is, that's not -- that's the political environment. Now you may disapprove of it, I may disapprove of it, and if enough people in the public want it to change or be modified one way or the other, it will be.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I interviewed your colleague, the late Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2 , back in 2012. And I want to play a clip from that interview.\par \par \uc2 Take a -- take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Will you time your retirement so that a more conservative president can appoint a like-minded justice?\par \par \uc2 ANTONIN SCALIA\uc2 , FORMER ASSOCIATE JUSTICE ON \uc2 U.S. SUPREME COURT\uc2 : I would not like to be replaced by someone who immediately sets about undoing everything that I've tried to do for 25 years, 26 years, sure. But I mean, I shouldn't have to tell you that, unless you think I'm a fool.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Do you agree with Scalia that a justice who is unmindful of the politics of the president who replaces him, who's unmindful of that, is a fool?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: I intend to die on the court. I don't think I'll be there forever. But -- but I've -- I've said a few of the considerations previously and I - - I think --\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But do you think the consideration that Scalia mentioned, I don't want to be replaced by somebody who's going to undo everything that I've spent --\par \par \uc2 BREYER: That I've done? That I've -- undo everything I've done?\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREYER: I see the point. And probably, in your background, there's -- can be something there. There are many considerations. Many, many considerations.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: This brings us to the calls for you to step down while a Democratic president and a Democratic -- can appoint your successor and a Democratic Senate can confirm your successor. Here's one example of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR\uc2  (D-MN): He makes his own decision about if he's going to retire. But he's going -- if he's going to retire, it should be sooner rather than later if you are concerned about the court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: What do you think of those calls?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Well, I think that they're entitled to their opinion. I think they -- and not only they understand the political world much better than I, or they understand it pretty well. And there we are. What else to want me to say?\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: They would say you ignored those calls and increased the chances that a Republican Senate will be there to confirm your success.\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Well, I mean, there are factors. There are many factors. In fact, quite a few. And the role of the court and so forth is one of them and the situation, the institutional considerations are some and -- and I -- I believe -- I can't say I'd take anything perfectly into account, but in my own mind I -- I think about those things.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: So why didn't you retire?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: I didn't retire because I had decided on balance I wouldn't retire.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: President Biden has appointed a commission to come back to him in November and discuss -- weigh in on possible reforms to the court.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of the idea of increasing the number of justices on the court?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Well, if one party could do it, I guess another party could do it. And the more thing -- I mean it's fairly surface -- on the surface it seems to me you start changing all these things around, and people will lose trust in the court.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: What about term limits? People, even justices, live a lot longer now than they did back in the 18 century. Would it lower the political heat if, say, a justice served for 18 years instead of a life term?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Well, I think you could do that. It should be a very long term because you don't want the judge who's holding that term to start thinking about his next job. But it would make life easier for me.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: You just turned 83 years old. I've got to say, you look terrific. You're of sound mind and body.\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: What do you hope people say about your service on the court?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: The best thing they could say, I thought I liked very much, is it has an appeal to me, was what Thurgood Marshall said. And they said, well, what do you want on your tombstone? And he said, he did his best. That's it.\par \par \uc2 I'm there for everybody. I'm not just there for the Democrats. I'm not just there for the Republicans. And I'm not just there because a president was a Democrat who appointed me. It's a very great privilege to be in that job. And part of it is to remember that you're there for everyone. They won't like what you say half the time or more, but you're still there for them. And that's the privilege of the job in a way. You have to give your all. And you have to work as hard as you can.\par \par \uc2 So, you see, I think it's important we have trust.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: The name of the book is "The Authority of the Court and the Peril of Politics."\par \par \uc2 Justice Breyer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Thank you very much, Chris. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Up next, 20 years after 9/11, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is back in charge in Afghanistan. Where do we stand with the terror threat from overseas and security risks here in the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Peter Paul Apollo.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Faustino Apostal, Jr.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Joe, we love and miss you more than you could ever imagine. Our son is the spitting image of you. He lights up my world every day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They prayed, they voted on a course of action and then they struck.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Some of the sights and sounds from observant ceremonies at Ground Zero, Shanksville, and the Pentagon 20 years after the day that changed this country forever.\par \par \uc2 And we're back now with the panel.\par \par \uc2 Karl, I have to say, watching those ceremonies 20 years after the fact, it is still deeply emotional. I'm sure it was for all three of you as well. But, Karl, as somebody who was in the White House that terrible day, it still packs a punch, doesn't it?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FORMER BUSH WHITE HOUSE ADVISER AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It sure does. It packs a big punch.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Your thoughts, your remembrances of that day and -- and looking back on it now from the vantage point of 20 years?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, I must admit, I feel sorrow and loss every time this day rolls around. But this year I feel angry because for 20 years we kept America safe by projecting our power abroad and fighting them over there so we didn't have to fight them here. And now this September 11th was celebrated in Kabul by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , the people who gave \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  sanctuary in Afghanistan and allowed him to launch his attacks on our country.\par \par \uc2 The new prime minister of Afghanistan was the foreign minister in 2001. And when we said to him, surrender \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  or suffer the consequences, he told us to pound sand. The interior minister has a $10 million bounty by the United States on his head for having participated in the 2006 attack in Kabul that killed six Americans. His uncle, another member of the \uc2 Haqqani Network\uc2 , is a refugee minister, which allows him to provide cover for foreign terrorists to come into Afghanistan and receive documents, and he has a $5 million bounty on his head.\par \par \uc2 So we took Afghanistan, which was, you know, which was trying to be a modern nation, respected human lights, allowed women to be educated and work, and we surrendered it. And General Milley, the chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, was accurate the other day when he said that within 12 months, 24 months, 36 month, Afghanistan will be -- there will be, quote, a resurgence of terrorism because of what has happened in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Yes, I want to pick up on that in a moment, but one news development this week is that some Americans did get out of Afghanistan, either on charter flights to Qatar or over land, and the Biden administration is having to walk a tight rope in talking about how it is dealing with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , SECRETARY OF STATE: Our engagement with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and with a government, interim or long-term, will be for purposes of advancing the national interest.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Susan, this is a tricky issue for President Biden and his team. On the one hand, they do, as a matter of reality, have to do business with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , but they also have to be clear-eyed about who these guys really are.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": Yes, and they are being extremely cautious in the words they say now because we are continuing to try to get a handful of remaining American citizens who want to evacuate from Afghanistan, and other endangered Afghans who have been allies of ours during this long war.\par \par \uc2 But the fact is, this administration, while it's not likely to recognize the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  as a government, is going to have to deal with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  as the people in charge of Afghanistan, and that is going to become more and more difficult if we see video of women being beaten in the streets and reprisals against Afghans who helped us during that war. This is an issue that is not over for the Biden administration. It may just get more complicated.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: And then, Chuck, there is, as Karl mentioned, the terror issues, the Pentagon and General Milley, the chairman of the Joint Chiefs, they are now talking about having to reassess -- and reassess is a nice word -- to say upgrade the terror threat that comes from Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 And then there's also this issue of -- President Biden likes to talk about, oh, we can handle the terror threat from over the horizon, which means without anybody within hundreds or even thousands of miles of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 But in the last terror strike, drone strike, rather, of the U.S. war, the 20 year war in Afghanistan, I want to put this up on the screen. There's reporting now that our final drone strike in Afghanistan, that we were saying at the time supposedly took out a vehicle filled with explosives and armed by ISIS-K, may instead have killed 10 innocents, including seven children.\par \par \uc2 So -- so, Chuck, this idea that we're going to be able to effectively fight al Qaeda or ISIS-K or whomever from over the horizon, it's not going to be that simple.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES LANE\uc2 , "THE WASHINGTON POST" AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: That story couldn't be more tragic. "The Washington Post" also covered it.\par \par \uc2 It turns out that it's very likely that was not a car bomb in preparation but, if you can believe it, and aid mission being led by somebody who had an outstanding application to come to the United States. And, of course, it illustrates that the intelligence must have been, if those stories are true, must have been terribly flawed. And it goes to the point about what we have given up by leaving Afghanistan, namely that on the ground, in- country presence, including the support of a friendly government, indeed a dependent government, to make sure that our intelligence is more accurate.\par \par \uc2 On the subject, it leads you to the atmosphere that surrounded all those terribly moving moments yesterday. How different it might have felt if either we had not left Afghanistan yet or if the departure had gone more smoothly and more nobly. Of course, I think that's what President Biden had in mind when he embarked on the withdrawal of coming to this date in a much more celebratory mood. But, instead, we're left to contemplate a lot of these dilemmas that will -- that will linger. And that, I think, must have contributed -- certainly did in what I saw -- to the -- to the feeling of melancholy and frustration that did prevail all day yesterday.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Karl, I've got about a minute left. The Biden administration talks about hoping to resettle 95,000 Afghans in this country, but there's quite a debate, especially among Republicans about whether or not we have adequate vetting to bring 95,000 Afghan refugees into this country safely.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, it's going to be a difficult task. On a lot of people we do have good vetting. On some people we don't. You will see recently that I think it was 44 Afghans who -- who made it to the U.S. were returned abroad because the vetting showed that they were problematic. But we have a moral obligation to stand with those who stood with us.\par \par \uc2 I have a friend who was in and out of Afghanistan a lot. He had a number of interpreters He was attempting to get one of his interpreters out, not in Kabul, but he didn't get him out because the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  took him into custody, brought all of his family together, brought together witnesses and then killed him in front of his family before killing every member of his family except the 10-year-old daughter, who was given to a \uc2 Taliban\uc2  warrior as an arranged bride. That's what we need to stand -- why we need to stand with the people of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Pretty clear that our involvement and engagement with Afghanistan is not over after these 20 years.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, panel. See you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a look at the raid that killed the mastermind of 9/11, including the terrifying moment where a faltering helicopter forced the team to change its plans just as they reached the compound.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: As we've said, this weekend marks that moment 20 years ago when enemies carried out the worst terror attack in the history of our country. But it's also just over ten years since the U.S. brought the battlefield back to Pakistan and took out the architect of that terrible day.\par \par \uc2 That's the subject of my new book, "Count Bin Laden: The Untold Story of the 247 Day Hunt to Bring the Mastermind of 9/11 to Justice." It takes you behind the scenes of the special forces raid that killed \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  from the perspective of the intelligence, political, and military professionals who pulled it off.\par \par \uc2 And, tonight, you'll hear directly from many of them in a new documentary, "Countdown Bin Laden."\par \par \uc2 Here's a brief look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEON PANETA, FORMER CIA DIRECTOR: We were tracking the helicopters and realized that they were now over the compound.\par \par \uc2 I think everybody understood that this was the critical moment, because I - - once they hear those helicopters, you've got about 90 seconds to be able to rappel down and go in before bin Laden is truly alerted to what's happening.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (voice over): But the plan they had practiced so many times went south quickly.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I was looking on the big screens there as the helicopter came in and I could see it start to waffle.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: The lead Black Hawk fought to maintain control, then hit the wall of the compound.\par \par \uc2 PANETA: I immediately said to Bill McRaven, I said what the hell is happening? And Bill didn't miss a beat. He basically said, look, we've got a backup helicopter coming in. We're going ahead with the mission. They're going to rappel and go in through the walls. This mission is going on. And I remember saying, God bless you. Let's do it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our pilot realized in an instant, well, if he can't hover, I can't hover. So you're not going to the roof. And I remember getting out. I remember my first foot on the ground. And I just thought to myself, well, I guess we start the war from here then.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: You can watch "Countdown Bin Laden" tonight on 10:00 p.m. Eastern on Fox News Channel. And after it airs, you can stream it anytime on foxnation.com.\par \par \uc2 My book, which tells the full story, is out now, available online and in stores.\par \par \uc2 And that's it for today. Have a great week and we'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020210912eh9c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Taliban\uc2  Allows Evacuation Flight Out Of Kabul; Military Vet On Private Effort To Evac Americans; DOJ Announces Civil Suit Against Texas On Abortion; Taliban Beats Afghan Journalists Covering Protests; New Docs Shed Light On US-Funded COVID Research; GOP California Governor Candidate Attacked By Protester; Debate Over Requiring COVID Vaccine For Students; Reflecting On 9/11 Attacks 20 Years Later\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Trey Yingst, Jonathan Turley, Rich Edson,  Claudia Cowan, Marty Makary, Dan Henninger \par \uc2 8,071 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: Continuing coverage of 20 years after the 9/11 attacks here on the Fox News Channel throughout the weekend on Saturday, 9/11. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS HOST: All right, thank you, John and Sandra. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha McCallum in New York.\par \par \uc2 Breaking right now on THE STORY, "cooperative and business-like," that is how the White House is describing the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  at this moment after the terror group allowed, gave permission for 113 Americans and others included in that group to escape the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  rule in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 They're now on the ground in Doha have to be very happy when they come down those steps and hit that ground after a harrowing experience. It is stunning, but this is how the relationship is described 20 years hence, from September 11th.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Taliban\uc2 's acting Prime Minister, this man you see pictured here, who by the way, has a $10 million \uc2 FBI\uc2  bounty on his head for terrorist acts, and maybe holding an American hostage, just told Al Jazeera that all of those tens of thousands of Afghans who helped to start the Afghan government and who worked with the United States, he says, do not leave, come back if you have left already. He says, the bloodshed is over.\par \par \uc2 So tell that to the protesters who were just fired on, and women who were beaten with those short whip devices that you see them carrying around, because they came out and demonstrated and asked for freedom in the streets of Kabul.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now Foreign Correspondent Trey Yingst is in Doha where that happy flight just landed a short time ago and another one maybe on the way. Trey?\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon. Certainly some positive news here today in Doha, 113 foreigners landing here from Afghanistan, they were given permission by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . And we are learning more about how they got here.\par \par \uc2 According to a source familiar with the situation, the Qataris actually set up a safety corridor for these individuals to get to Afghanistan's biggest airport, the Hamid Karzai International Airport, and then they were able to get on that flight and fly here to Doha. Now that those individuals are here, they're going to take Corona test, they'll get some food and some rest.\par \par \uc2 Once they're processed they're free to return to their home countries. We do know something about the citizens on that flight, not only Americans but also Germans, British citizens and even Ukrainians. Also today, according to the Wall Street Journal, Americans who were stuck in Northern Afghanistan in the city of Mazari Sharif, were told to come to the airport for this flight, but it's unclear how many were able to actually make the journey.\par \par \uc2 That's why we may see future flights like the one earlier today, eight flights into Kabul are continuing with the UAE and Pakistan delivering aid to the country, an important development with over half of Afghanistan's population relying on international aid to survive. This does come as those protests erupted once again, in the Afghan capital of Kabul some disturbing images and reports out of Afghanistan, of journalists and women being beaten by \uc2 Taliban\uc2  fighters.\par \par \uc2 We talked about this earlier in the week, Martha, but really, it gives you a sense of what these demonstrators and civilians are facing on the ground now that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is in control. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Trey Yingst, thank you so much for your reporting from Doha this evening. Joining me now is Retired Navy SEAL Jason Redmond, 10 of his 21 years as a Navy SEAL were spent leading combat efforts in Iraq and Afghanistan? He and a group of Afghanistan war veterans have helped rescue hundreds of U.S. citizens and Afghan allies and their families in the mission that they call the Pineapple Express enormously successful for these individuals.\par \par \uc2 He's joined by retired Navy SEAL Commander Dave Sears, who served in Afghanistan and in Iraq. He is highly decorated for his missions in more than 40 countries around the globe as a U.S. Navy SEAL, he's also the author of "Smarter, Not Harder." Gentlemen, it's great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 So, it feels as though over the past week and a half, we've been reporting on the fact that there has been this sort of difficult relationship between the groups and the veterans who are trying to get people out. And the White House and the State Department, the State Department was charged by the president with the job of getting people out. Now, it feels like the gears are turning a little bit on this. This is General Jack Keane a short time ago, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE, FOX NEWS MILITARY CONTRIBUTOR: It looks like the White House. And DOD and the Department of State is going to put together a private public partnership with these veterans groups to access the list, put them in a central data system, which would be very comprehensive. They know the status. They know where these people are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Jason, you know, we pulled all of the United States military out, and groups like yours tried to fill that void. So, I think the Pineapple Express has been involved in a meeting at the White House is what we're learning. Can you tell us anything about where all this stands now?\par \par \uc2 JASON REDMOND, RETIRED U.S. NAVY SEAL: Yes, Martha, absolutely and thank you for the time. Dave, good to see you again.