rrying out their responsibilities under federal laws relating to abortion services. It also subjects federal employees and non-governmental partners to implement those laws to civil liability and penalties.\par \par \uc2 Among the federal agencies and programs whose operations the statute unconstitutionally restricts, are the Labor Department's Job Corps program, the Defense Department's TRICARE health program, the \uc2 Office of Refugee Resettlement\uc2 , the Bureau of Prisons, the \uc2 Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services\uc2 , and the Office of Personnel Management.\par \par \uc2 The complaint therefore seeks a declaratory judgment that SB-8 is invalid under the supremacy clause, and the 14th amendment is pre-empted by federal law and violates the doctrine of inter-governmental immunity. The United States also seeks a permanent and preliminary injunction prohibiting enforcement of the statute against the state of Texas including against the state's officers, employees and agents and private parties it has effectively deputized who would bring suit under SB-8.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice has a duty to defend the Constitution of the United States, and to uphold the rule of law. Today, we fulfill that duty by filing the lawsuit I have just described. Now, before I take some questions, I want to say a few words to the American people on the eve of the 20th anniversary of the attacks of September 11th, 2001.\par \par \uc2 That day, is seared into all of our memories. Nothing we can do or say, can replace the loss so many endured that day. Nothing can change the profound way the events of September 11th altered us individually and collectively, as a nation. And let there be no doubt the threat from terrorists, from foreign terrorists, like those involved in the September 11 attack is one we must constantly guard against.\par \par \uc2 But what we can do, and what we have done is learn from the past to better anticipate and prepare for the next threat 