luated the law and the facts, and this complaint expresses our view about the law and the facts.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thanks, everybody.\par \par \uc2 GARLAND: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, the Attorney General Merrick Garland, taking some questions there and laying out the suit that the Department of Justice is bringing against Texas for the bill known as SB-8, which limits abortion to prior to the period when a heartbeat is detected.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News Contributor Jonathan Turley, Constitutional Attorney, of course, and he's been watching this along with us. Jonathan, great to have you with us to respond to this. Essentially, he said, you know that this bill means that a woman can't exercise her constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 We can't stand by for anything that undermines the Constitution and also he touched on the empowerment of private individuals in this case to bring, to bring suits against individuals who are receiving abortions or involved in abortions. He said you cannot empower private individuals to impinge on the constitutional rights of others. How does this case look to you the way it's been laid out?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, it's hard to unpack all this because there's aspects of this that are quite breathtaking. I mean, the, among other things, he challenges the use of private attorney generals, but the type of citizen lawsuit that's involved in tend to take says abortion law, that is a common provision. It is used throughout federal law to allow citizens to enforce aspects of federal law.\par \par \uc2 I've brought a number of those lawsuits. So it'll be rather hard to see how you could challenge that as a concept. What really needs to be challenged is the bass violation. That is what he's really objecting to, is this pre- viability provision of the Texas law that sets it at roughly six weeks or eight weeks, depending on who you ask.\par \par \uc2 A Georgia law was just struck down last year. And most of us expect that the lower courts will strike down this law, it's already being challenged in the federal courts. But what is really odd about this is that the Department of Justice is entering as a party. Usually, the term justice is an ambiguous or a friend of the court.\par \par \uc2 Here, it's saying we're a party of interest. Anytime a state law threatens constitutional rights, we have skin in the game. That's not exactly how the Department Justice has operated in the past. And I think some courts are going to ask about that. You know, for example, Garland said, we're going to say that this field is partially or wholly preempted. The Supreme Court has always recognized that states have a right to legislate in the area of abortion, the question is where to draw that line?\par \par \uc2 So, this is a major assertion of federal authority and perhaps an unprecedented one. Now, the problem with that is he may have just created a new point of challenge for pro-life litigants. He could have made this the situation worse with his filing, because he's added a robust federalism issue that is quite novel in a lot of courts are likely to be somewhat uncomfortable with in this degree of federal jurisdictional authority.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's very interesting. I got to go. But I just want to ask you one quick question. You know, the interpretation was that it was the, the statute was written in a novel way that made it sort of understandable that the Supreme Court would uphold it. Can you explain that to everybody briefly?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: I'm sorry, you just broke up just slightly.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, that there was a sort of a novel writing of this bill. And that because it was sort of clever is the word that was used as well, the way it was written, you could understand how the Supreme Court would uphold it, a majority of justices.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, that's, that's right, because a lot of people said, including a member of Congress that the court had just overturned Roe and upheld the Texas law. That's untrue. And this press conference proves it. If the if the Supreme Court upheld the Texas law, they wouldn't be challenging it right now.\par \par \uc2 But what the court really said was you, you pick two people almost at random, a state judge and a clerk. They don't have any enforcement role here. They're not named in the law. And what the court said is you can't ask us to enjoin two people at random as a way of enjoying the law. And that was the basis. The court said it was not ruling on the merits.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. It can be very interesting to watch this. Jonathan, thank you, as always great to have you here to break down for us, Jonathan Turley. So, Senator Tom Cotton on the brutal crackdown on those daring to oppose the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  takeover in the streets of Kabul. He's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, this is going to be interesting. The \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2  is going to hold hearings in a week or so where the Defense Secretary General Austin, and the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs General Milley will be questioned before Congress about their handling of the Afghanistan exit. How many troops did they want? What was their idea of how it should all work?\par \par \uc2 Also the general in charge on the ground there a person that American really haven't heard too much from his general Scotty Miller, he will also testify and be asked these questions. And at the same time, this question today, this is life under the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , despite what they're saying their \uc2 Taliban\uc2  fighters opening fire on protesters in Kabul this week, and whipping women who were protesting.\par \par \uc2 They were shouting freedom. They were incredibly brave in the streets of Kabul, and they were beaten for it. These images of Afghan journalists and we warn you this is