avirus sequences published from network, NIH-supported, were not COVID, the strain, COVID 2 strain. So what he said was correct.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Now congressional researchers say they are still pushing for more information regarding this to figure out what exactly taxpayers were funding through these grants. Back to you to.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's a good question. Thank you very much, Rich Edson. Let's bring in David Asher. As I said he led the State Department investigation under Secretary Pompeo, worked with that team into the origins of the virus. And that probe was then halted and has never continued under this White House. David, good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 You said that there are revelations in here that are a big deal now that these documents have been exposed. What's a big deal that you see in here?\par \par \uc2 DAVID ASHER: Well, did you know that Dr. Shi Zhengli, the Bat Lady, that apparently was at the center of creating what we believe to be COVID-19 itself was on the payroll essentially of the NIH for years. (Inaudible) They didn't know that we were -- yes we knew we had used the funding from the NIH through this \uc2 EcoHealth Alliance\uc2  to contribute to their research but they even put salary for Dr. Shi herself and line items, rather senior scientists under that.\par \par \uc2 So we're basically, not only training them (inaudible) technology, but we're even paying them directly part of their salary. So the communist government, which was operating a top secret classified program from at least 2016, that Secretary Pompeo unveiled the world in January of this year, at that very lab, with those very people. With the way -- they were also being paid but at the same time they are being paid by the Chinese People's Liberation Army, by the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASHER: Pretty interesting, huh?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's very interesting. And I know that you revealed[, your group revealed that the Chinese military was also involved in this lab. So I think any, you know, you don't have to be a scientist or an investigator to say what the heck are US dollars doing in a Chinese lab that is involved with the Chinese military, and the transparency there is zero on what was going on with this.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, my other question is, we just did a 90, the White House did a 90 day investigation, which I know you said was just like an evaluation, it wasn't even close to an investigation. So why did it take the Intercept to find these documents and get them out there when supposedly 90 days of investigation was going on? Where were these then?\par \par \uc2 ASHER: They'd been in the government's possession the whole time. We told that the DNI that they should be asking for them, but they said they weren't intelligence. Oh, you know, it's like the intelligence that exists between your two ears is an intelligence. These are documents that the \uc2 US government\uc2  knew it had its possession, and there's far more data than this.\par \par \uc2 We knew about these documents when I was at the State Department but they don't class -- because it's not classified intelligence, it doesn't matter, even though it comes up to some very specific revelations very quickly. You remember in 2012, there was allegedly, according to Chinese, these miners that died of a predecessor to COVID-19 identified in a mine in Southern China, in Yunnan Province called Mojiang.\par \par \uc2 Six people got sick, three of them died. It's is the first case in history of bats killing human beings directly with coronavirus. If it's true, well, there's -- that effort was run by \uc2 EcoHealth Alliance\uc2  with NIH funding as well. There's no mention of the miners dying in the report, which is very suspicious because either it EcoHealth is covering up for the Chinese, which is sort of a cover up of the cover up, or the miners never