trict set to pass the measure less than two hours from now, which could have a snowball effect across parts of the country. Let's bring in Dr. Marty Makary, Professor at Johns Hopkins University School of Public Health, Editor in Chief of Medpage Today and a Fox News Medical Contributor. Doctor, good to see you as always. Do you think this is -- this makes good sense.\par \par \uc2 MARTY MAKARY, FOX NEWS MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Look, Martha, I believe in child vaccinations and I believe in the COVID vaccine for young, for adolescents. But the current dose is probably too high, and the interval is probably too short. And the reason I say that is that it never made sense that we give the same dose to a heavy 50 year old man that we give to a thin 12 year old girl. And we're seeing high rates of heart inflammation and they are clustered around this second dose, not the first dose.\par \par \uc2 So the first dose provides that immune protection, 100% effective in the Israeli study of kids 12 through 15. And that's the dose that I recommend now until we get better data. And also the FDA this watch this --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I just want -- everyone needs to pay attention to what you're saying here. The same dose is looked at being given to little kids and, you know, as you put it a large 50 year old men, right? And now, do we have any studies before we impose this on everyone, on the kids under 18? And you know, I think everyone should be vaccinated. You want to get all your vaccines to go to school, MMR polio, all of them, but we need to know what the right dose is. What are we doing to find out what the right dose is for these kids before we mandate them?\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: I think the FDA did rush the indication approval with the emergency use authorization for kids. They did not convene their technical experts, the VRBPAC, which is the external group of advisors, were never convened for the child indication that is the 12 to 15 indication.\par \par \uc2 And the first dose is so effective, that's the dose that makes sense to me and kids who don't have natural immunity. And that's the other problem with the mandate is it discounts those kids who already have immunity from circulating antibodies from prior COVID infection.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. So you think that people who have had COVID already, also may fall into and we haven't -- we don't have the studies done and we need to do them. We may also fall into that category that one shot is the best dosage for them.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: It appears that one shot confers pretty good immune protection and we know that with some immunization. You get a tenfold reduction in hospitalization. That's important, that's valuable. We've got outbreaks going on right now with the Delta virus in schools around the country.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: But when we tell those who already have immunity to get immunized, it makes no sense.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, doctor. These are all really important things for people to focus on. And we need to have CDC studies and FDA work so that we know what the right dosage is. That's why there's a children's medicine aisle in, you know, at the drugstore. Because the dosage is different depending on who you are and how old you are. Doctor, thank you very much helpful information.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So how Americans view the Biden exit from Afghanistan through the lens of the 20th commemoration of September 11th? How does all of this shakeout in the minds and hearts of Americans? That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: 20 years ago, Wall Street Journal Editorial Writer Dan Henninger watched it close range as the Twin Towers collapsed just steps away from his office building.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAN HENNINGER, WALL STREET JOURNAL EDITORIAL WRITER: I looked up at the sky just because it was so blue. And when I turned my head down, I said to myself, that plane is too low. And it look black back up, and I saw a gash in the side of the World Trade Center.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Unbelievable. Dan Henninger is Wall Street Journal Editorial Deputy Page Editor now and Fox News Contributor. Dan, good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 DAN HENNINGER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Good to be with you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, what an incredible story you and your colleagues tell you found a colleague's apartment in the middle of all of this, and managed to push the paper out the next morning and write about what you saw, right?\par \par \uc2 HENNINGER: Yes, that's exactly right. We hardly, of course, knew what to do with ourselves if we were there at the time. But everyone had to figure out some way to get through the day. And, Martha, we're and our journalists in the Wall Street Journal found a way to publish the newspaper. Everyone who could went down to our offices in Princeton, New Jersey. Our technicians wired up about 100 computers in ours, connected it to our publishing network. And writers who've been distributed all over New York City, some in Brooklyn, some on the Upper West Side.\par \par \uc2 I was in my colleague, Dorothy Rubina (ph). Rubina's apartment on the Lower East side, and I simply sat down and wrote a first hand account of what I had seen. And all the others at the Journal were doing the same thing, and we delivered a newspaper to our readers the next day. In hindsight, it was something of a miracle but the whole day seems like a fantasy in retrospect.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It sure was. And it was so potent and so immediate, obviously with your experience and all of those writings in that paper won a Pulitzer Prize for the covering of what happened that day. And I'm sure you also remember a day that we have been working on remembering on our team here at "The Story." And I want to play a piece of what happened three days later when President Bush went to ground zero. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The crushed fire truck and fading bullhorn would become the President's makeshift podium.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They go over and say they want to hear from the President. And he said, you're right, they want to hear from me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And I went over and asked the president, would you be willing to say something? And he said, do you think it's appropriate? I said, if you think it's appropriate, it's appropriate. Just be you, just be yourself. And he said, OK, and I went to the Secret Service agent and said, can the President stand on this thing?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The lead Secret Service guy is going nuts. He's like, we're not doing this. He's trying to make sure the President doesn't get visible. He's rather has (inaudible) on the ground. He doesn't want him highlighted up there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Not knowing what was about to happen or why he'd been asked to test the strength of the truck. Beckwith stayed put.\par \par \uc2 BOB BECKWITH, FORMER NEW YORK FIREMAN: I didn't know who called (inaudible). But says to me, somebody is coming over here. And when they -- when they come over, you help them up, and then you get down. I said, OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And I noticed Beckwith was sort of looking around at everything. He wasn't paying attention to the President's movements.\par \par \uc2 BECKWITH: I see the President coming around. He gets -- they will applaud him and yelling. He comes right in front of me, and he puts his arm up. I said my God, I pull him up. I turn around. I said, you OK, Mr. President? He said, yes. I started to get down. He said, where you going? I said, I was told to get down. He said, you stay right here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: When you felt him, pull his arm around you. Say, no, you're going to stay up here with me while I talk, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 BECKWITH: I looked up to sky and I said look at me, ma. I'm with the president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Dan, so many thoughts got through my head when I think about that day. But I also want people to understand. The country have been attacked in three different places, right, Pennsylvania, the Pentagon, downtown, how terrifying it was for those secret servicemen and women to put the President out there have him walking through that crowd. Everybody felt at that point that there was another attack coming and that it could be anywhere at any time, right?\par \par \uc2 HENNINGER: Absolutely. We all felt that way. The scale of this events were just outside human understanding. The death, simultaneously, the four airplanes, two into the World Trade Center towers, one into the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, what George Bush did then was what the country needs at a time like that, which is leadership. And he stepped in and did what you expect from the President of the United States. And he did it in a very comfortable and secure way. He seemed very comfortable in what he was doing, which had to be overwhelmingly difficult, Martha, standing down there at ground zero, amid the rubble, the death, the odor.\par \par \uc2 Many of us who are down there that day, did not return for weeks and months. But President Bush stood there and what did what the nation needed at that moment, was to comment and reassure it that we would be protected and that the government would do what was needed. And it was a very, very important thing that he did three days after the event.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It sure was. And the scars for all the people that were down there continue. The things that they saw what they worked through, very hard for everybody on this 20th anniversary. But look at this poll. ABC poll nearly half, 46% say the events of 9/11 changed the country for the worse. And right after 9/11, you know, there was a feeling or, you know, in the following years, that it had strengthened the country in a way as well as the tragedy. Why do you think that number is so different now, Dan?\par \par \uc2 HENNINGER: Well, you know, Martha, I look back on all this with a lot of sadness. There was such tremendous solidarity in the time after 9/11. The country really came together in a beautiful way. But we forget that just a year before that, you had the contested 2000 election between President Bush, then George W. Bush and \uc2 Al Gore\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It was a bitter political fight. And then the seven years after that, the George W. Bush presidency was attacked relentlessly and I think the political divisions of that time have simply widened even to our day. And that's what people recognize, and so disheartened some, that more better didn't come from that tragic event.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well said, Dan Henninger, thank you. I encourage everybody to watch the whole video with Mary O'Grady and Jason Riley. It's very, very moving the experience that all of you had down there. Thank you, Dan. Thanks for being with us today.\par \par \uc2 So that is "The Story" for this Thursday, but "The Story" goes on. And tomorrow, it goes on in this way. We will be down at ground zero, near ground zero tomorrow, doing the show live from there, near the 9/11 Memorial where the Twin Towers once stood. We hope you'll join us. Neil Cavuto is up next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020210910eh9900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Taliban Government Lacks Diversity According To State Department; Four Of Five Gitmo Detainees In Obama's Bergdahl Swap Are Now Leading The \uc2 Taliban\uc2 ; Senior Taliban Officials Have Ties To Al-Qaeda; Kamala Harris Supported Minnesota Freedom Fund; AOC Tries Really Hard To Erase "W" Word; Racist Attack On Larry Elder In LA; Joe Biden Uses Wild Weather To Justify Huge Spending\b0\par\par\uc2 Brian Kilmeade, Trace Gallagher, Tucker Carlson \par \uc2 7,946 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 RACHEL CAMPOS-DUFFY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Brian Kilmeade in for Tucker Carlson, up next.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, good evening, everybody and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT. I am not Tucker, I'm Brian Kilmeade and according to the schedule, I am in for Tucker for at least an hour.\par \par \uc2 It's hard to believe that it's been nearly a month since Joe Biden promised to evacuate every American from Afghanistan. "Any American who wants to come out, we will get you home," he said that. It's a talking point that many administration officials have continued to repeat.\par \par \uc2 The administration's allies, people like Senator Chuck Schumer saying it, too. For example, I'll put this in quotes because he said it, "Joe Biden rescued every American from Afghanistan." Isn't that interesting? "It was an extraordinary success," they continued to insist, but is it true? No, it's not true, at least according to Biden's own State Department.\par \par \uc2 Just today, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  muttered his way through a press conference saying that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is not allowing charter flights to depart Hamad Karzai International Airport in Kabul.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: As of now, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  are not permitting the charter flights to depart. They claim that some of the passengers do not have the required documentation.\par \par \uc2 While there are limits to what we can do without personnel on the ground, without an airport with normal security procedures in place, we are working to do everything in our power to support those flights and to get them off the ground.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: At another time, I'll go over why we don't have anyone on the ground and we don't have any control with anybody's airport, but that's for another show, what \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  didn't say is those flights include American citizens, though it's not clear how many.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, White House Chief of Staff Ron Klain said there were approximately 100 American citizens left in Afghanistan. About a week ago, Blinken said it was anywhere from 100 to 200. These numbers likely don't include green card holders, since even the administration doesn't have a clear number of how many of them are actually in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 What Blinken also didn't mention was that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  aren't the only ones blocking flights from leaving Afghanistan. FOX News has obtained an exclusive e-mail showing the State Department is blocking flights as well.\par \par \uc2 The e-mails are from a retired Marine named Eric Montalvo who is operating his own private evacuation flight from Afghanistan -- flights, I should say, it's plural. According to those e-mails, the State Department is refusing to give charter flights approval to land in other countries. The State Department also told Montalvo that private fights would not be allowed to land at the Department of Defense bases even though they were transporting American citizens and those are American bases, quote: "You need to find another destination country and it can't be the U.S. either." Well, isn't that nice.\par \par \uc2 None of this is stopping Afghan refugees from arriving in the U.S., however. According to General Glen VanHerck of U.S. Northern Command, less than half of the Afghan refugees coming to the U.S. are Special Immigrant Visa applicants who we kind of want here.\par \par \uc2 There are thousands of Afghans we know little to nothing about that are coming here, yet they are allowed to land on U.S. bases in our country.\par \par \uc2 According to a recent report, one male Afghan refugee was recently detained at Ramstein Air Base in Germany after explosive material and blasting caps were found in his carry-on luggage before he boarded his flight to Ramstein. U.S. officials reportedly believe the materials related to his work and not terrorism. Is his job to be a terrorist? Just a thought. But he was still placed on a restricted list.\par \par \uc2 Now normally, all of this would be a crisis for the State Department and they'd be focused on fixing it, right? But officials at the department are a little busy with apparently more pressing matters. For example, the number of women in the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 's new government.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, a statement to "The Hill," a State Department spokesperson expressed concern about the lack of female leadership in Afghanistan's new interim government. Could it be the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  doesn't really like women too much? Doesn't give them jobs, but I digress. Quote: "We have made clear our expectation that Afghan people deserve an inclusive government."\par \par \uc2 Wow, you talk about a strongly worded statement that'll get everybody's attention.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is already familiar with at least some of the new government's senior leaders. Why? Because we already caught them on the battlefield and then released them. Remember this? The new \uc2 Taliban\uc2  senior officials were Gitmo detainees that former President Obama traded for a traitor. A former U.S. Army Sergeant named Bowe Bergdahl back in 2014.\par \par \uc2 At that time, Susan Rice, by the way she is still playing in the White House said the most important thing was Bergdahl's well-being and not the fact that four terrorists had just been released back into the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  leadership.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SUSAN RICE, WHITE HOUSE CHIEF DOMESTIC POLICY ADVISER: He served the United States with honor and distinction and we'll have the opportunity eventually to learn what has transpired in the past years, but what's most important now is his health and well-being that he had the opportunity to recover in peace and security and be reunited with his family.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: And eventually be tried for treason, but I digress. President Obama also insisted there was nothing to worry about. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA, FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We will be keeping eyes on them. Is there the possibility of some of them trying to return to activities that are detrimental to us? Absolutely. That's been true of all the prisoners that were released from Guantanamo. There is a certain recidivism rate that takes place.\par \par \uc2 I wouldn't be doing it if I thought that it was contrary to American national security.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Well it is and you did it anyway. It turns out, it was contrary to American national security. The former Gitmo detainees now holding the following government positions in Afghanistan. Jot these down.\par \par \uc2 Acting Minister for Information and Culture, Acting Minister of Borders and Tribal Affairs, Acting Defense Minister, the Acting Director of Intelligence, of course all this pens on Senate confirmation.\par \par \uc2 Also Abdul Haq Wasiq returns to a position he held with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  before the 9/11 attacks.\par \par \uc2 Joe Kent is a retired Army Special Forces and former C.I.A. paramilitary officer. He joins us now to react to all of this latest chaos.\par \par \uc2 Hey Joe, can you make sense of this?\par \par \uc2 JOE KENT (R), WASHINGTON CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: No, there is no sense to be made. The only lesson that we can take here is that we cannot negotiate with terrorists from a position of weakness going back to the Obama administration. That's been the lesson, the disastrous Bergdahl debacle that put those terrorists back in power after they got a U.S. taxpayer- funded vacation to Gitmo essentially.\par \par \uc2 And then the deal that Biden failed to negotiate with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to get all of the Americans out. This just shows that the culture from the top matters.\par \par \uc2 When the Commander-in-Chief and the unelected bureaucrats don't care about America's standing in the world and they view us as just another nation on the decline, this is exactly what happens. Contrast that with four years of President Trump and negotiating from a position of power, it is a night and day difference.\par \par \uc2 We had the ability to get all of our people out under President Trump. He negotiated a deal that would have had us out of Afghanistan the summer of 2020, until the permanent ruling class, the unelected bureaucrats leaked made up Intelligence about Russian bounties that resulted in the uniparty leaving us in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 That's exactly how we got to this point right now.\par \par \uc2 They didn't want President Trump's plan to succeed and that put us in this position of weakness and got 13 great soldiers killed, and now has Americans being held hostage with our State Department absolutely helpless.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: You know, what is pretty amazing is, we told them to have a diverse inclusive government and they didn't. I am shocked they didn't listen. We said have an inclusive government, did they not understand it? Did we not have a translator?\par \par \uc2 Oh, we got rid of all of them. No, excuse me. The translators, we didn't get out.\par \par \uc2 Among the other people with important positions, is Siraj Haqqani. Who is he? A designated global terrorist by Washington who we have on a wanted list for $10 million. So we couldn't get that glossy headshot from him? So we were forced to use the wanted photo we had up.\par \par \uc2 How is that going for inclusive?\par \par \uc2 KENT: It's absolutely ridiculous and that just speaks to the relationship that we have had with Pakistan. Once again, when we negotiate from a position of weakness, that only lines the pockets of these corrupt governments and lobbyists in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 People like Siraj Haqqani can hide out on the Pakistani border, kidnap Americans, run terrorism operations, seek sanctuary with people that we are providing U.S. aid, too, even when they get caught red handed hosting bid Laden there. We still continue to pour U.S. aid in to them.\par \par \uc2 We need a massive amount of accountability. Our government and our permanent ruling class is an absolute disgrace to all the lives that have been lost over there. That's exactly why I'm running for Congress, to bring accountability to our permanent ruling class. That's why I was honored that President Trump provided me with his endorsement to bring the America First agenda back, to put an end to all this excessive waste and all of the lives being taken by the hubris and the -- the hubris and lies of our permanent ruling class.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Joe, the thing is, I really think we needed somewhat of a presence in the area. I did not want to give it right to the terrorists and already,