ese military bases.\par \par \uc2 I've worked heavily on this SIV process before when we are getting out of Iraq. It takes a good deal of time. You cannot work in the sheer volume that we're working in right now. And the bottom line is we shouldn't have brought all these people out of region. They should have been sent up to the Panjshir to fight against the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , to fight for their actual country. Just because you serve in an American funded unit overseas that does not buy you an automatic golden ticket to the U.S. heartland. We are over there to help those people and we did help those people. Lots of things went wrong. I could talk about that for days probably.\par \par \uc2 However, the people that we have a commitment to to get out, there is not that many. It's the interpreters. It's some of the intelligence sources. That's the bottom line. And we should have identified those people a long time ago. That shows that the military had no serious plans to actually evacuate Afghanistan. And that shows that really the Biden administration only cares about having this eyewash, this great statistic that they saved all these Afghans, but the end result will just be another 100,000 or so refugees that the Democrats can then translate into voters further down the road. That's the same plan they have down at the southern border.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Joe, you mentioned fighting for your country. Your of course fought for our country. Your beautiful wife, Shannon Kent, also fought for our country. She was killed in action in Syria in 2019. I just wanted to get your reaction to President Biden checking his watch at Dover Air Base last weekend?\par \par \uc2 KENT: It's an absolute disgrace. It really is. The commander-in-chief's job, he is the person ultimately at the end of the day that sent troops forward that tragically lose their lives. He has one small job on that day, and it is to represent a grateful nation, and he failed to do that.\par \par \uc2 What I think we really have to call attention to, though, is that Joe Biden is just not all there. He's clearly declining mentally. This is at the point of being senior abuse. However, Joe Biden has been doing this for so long, he's been sending young men and women off to die in these conflicts. He voted for the Iraq war. He voted for additional surges in Afghanistan. He was supposed to have us out of Afghanistan as the vice president. And it's on record that he knew things were going poorly in Afghanistan in 2010 and he wanted us out, and he didn't act.\par \par \uc2 So this is just another day at the office for our permanent ruling class and people like Joe Biden. So it is a complete disgrace. He needs to be impeached for this.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Yes, and he lied about it and asked the Afghan president to lie to the American people, too. Joe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The left's reaction to Texas's antiabortion law grows more hyperbolic and shrill by the hour. Horace Cooper and Allie Beth Stuckey fire back at the vitriol from the left when we return.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have been and continue to be a strong supporter of Roe v. Wade, number one. And the most pernicious thing about the Texas law, it sort of creates a vigilante system where people get rewards to go out to -- anyway. And it just seems -- I know this sounds ridiculous -- almost un-American.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Un-American? He went on to say after that that he respects the views of others. Really? Why did he characterize the Supreme Court's decision, which didn't rule on the merits of the law, and unprecedented assaults on women's rights? So much for his respect. Biden's response to the Texas law wasn't nearly the most overwrought. Listen to what they're cooking up over at CNN.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELIE MYSTAL, "THE NATION" JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: What Biden needs to do is, via executive order, create a federal force of doctors protected, clothed in the protection of the state, send them to Texas to counsel and protect women's privacy rights. And if they want to provide some medical procedures in the course of protecting people's constitutional rights, they can do that as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Joining me now is Horace Cooper, Project 21 co-chairman, and Allie Beth Stuckey, host of the "Relatable" podcast. Allie Beth, let's start with you. You recently had a baby, and you live in Texas. What's your reaction to this insane rambling from the left?\par \par \uc2 ALLIE BETH STUCKEY, "RELATABLE" PODCAST HOST: Yes, Joe Biden obviously, he doesn't really know what the law says. That's why he trailed off as he does when he's, for example, trying to close out the Declaration of Independence. The guy doesn't really know what he's talking about.\par \par \uc2 But as far as what CNN is trying to propose, what continually strikes me about all of this is just the rabid passion that some on the people showed toward the elimination of babies. We talk so much about the importance of women's rights. Another person's rights are at play here. And I guess Joe Biden, the, quote, devout Catholic has forgotten that, and I think a lot of people who claim to be fighters of social justice and claim to be advocates of compassion on the left have forgotten that too.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: So Horace, this week when the White House Press Secretary Jen Psaki was asked about this issue, she essentially made the argument that if you're a man who is pro-life, you have no say and you shouldn't have any opinion when it comes to abortion. But the left is totally fine with promoting men who are all for it.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ROY COOPER, (D) NORTH CAROLINA: I think it was just the FDA administrator who was explaining that we have to refer to birthing people instead of mothers. But let's put aside the hostage note reading that we just saw from President Biden. The key concept, the idea of allowing the citizen to be on the lookout to make sure that significant concerns of the community are being met, is a longtime legal concept. In fact, you can go back to the Lincoln administration, and you can see this concept where people were encouraged to go into court, and they got a bounty if they identified fraudulent behavior that was hurting our Civil War effort. We continue on today with the same concept. Far from being un-American, it is central to our operation for good federal government resources altogether.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: So Allie, whenever the topic of abortion and protecting the rights of unborn children comes up, the left portrays the right as extremists. But if you look at the data, the reality is that women are leading the pro-life movement. And the majority of women do degree with some restrictions on abortions. And yet the left makes it out to seem like all women agree with their perspective of unlimited abortion up until the day of birth, and in the case of the Virginia governor, maybe the day after birth as well.\par \par \uc2 STUCKEY: Right, and that's exactly what's extreme. It's not extreme to say that all innocent human beings have a right not to be murdered. What is a very extremist is saying that some human beings based on their size, their development, their location in the womb, for some reason don't have that right, that they don't have the most fundamental right to life. That is extremely extreme. That is radical. And I think pro-lifers have to do is always bring it back to life of the baby and the reality, the grotesque reality of what abortion actually is and does.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: So Horace, real quickly, Joe Biden ran as a moderate. He used to not believe in taxpayers funding abortion.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Absolutely. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: He now doesn't believe in the Hyde Amendment. He also changed his perspective this week and says he doesn't believe that life begins at conception, which is opposite of what we've been told by decades by Joe Biden. So it's not only just an issue of him not knowing the law in Texas, but it's an issue of him lying to the American people about what his perspective was.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: He doesn't know the law in Texas. He doesn't understand how the Supreme Court works and how these kinds of cases occur. But more fundamentally, he and others in his administration and on his team don't believe in the fundamental principle of self-government. You persuade people. There is a reason why so many places all across this land are fighting to protect unborn innocent life, because people are being persuaded.\par \par \uc2 It is not the case that this is being imposed by the state legislatures. It is the case that people care about this issue, and they ask their representatives. And the other side, instead of doing any pushback they have no arguments. They have nothing but condemnation. That doesn't work with self-government.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: All right, the debate continues. Horace and Allie Beth, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The ACLU is now backing COVID vaccine mandates. Did they forget what civil liberties are? Harmeet Dhillon reacts next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Californians are desperate for relief, but Governor Newsom is now considering imposing statewide vaccine mandates. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE's Jessica Curry spoke with small business owners who would be hurt the most. Here's what they told you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CURRY, INGRAHAM ANGLE PRODUCER: Small business owners in Los Angeles have taken hit after hit throughout this pandemic. Bar and restaurant owners that I talked to aren't sure that they can sustain another blow.\par \par \uc2 DAVEED MEIRON, OWNER OF NEW YORK BAGEL CAFE IN LOS ANGELES: Personally, I know 13 stores down the Ventura strip right here all the way to Encino that literally shut down.\par \par \uc2 CURRY: In the Sherman Oaks neighborhood of Los Angeles, Daveed Meiron's New York Bagel Cafe is a staple in the community. He supports local artists, local firefighters, and police officers. But after more than a year of tough COVID restrictions, he may be soon faced with eviction.\par \par \uc2 MEIRON: I just got a letter from our landlord that is a writ and a judgment which prompted us to start a \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  camping. We started making payments, we were back for about $60,000. I'd rather put the money into my employees. I have a single mom with two kids. I put most of the money, my personal money, into this place so they can keep feeding their children with the hopes that something is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 CURRY: Los Angeles bar owner Angela Marsden went last December calling out California's nonsensical COVID protocols.\par \par \uc2 ANGELA MARSDEN, OWNER OF PINEAPPLE HILL SALOON AND GRILL: They shut us down. We cannot survive. My staff can't survive.\par \par \uc2 CURRY: Eight months later, she faces a new problem on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 Right now Los Angeles does not have a vaccine mandate for bars and restaurants. What would happen to your business if they were to enforce that?\par \par \uc2 MARSDEN: It's going to devastate the community again. We're supposed to fire people now that think they have immunity because they have COVID, and we can't even find people to stay open full hours. I know restaurants that are closing and open less hours because they can't find staff. And then we are supposed to sheriff and police our community?\par \par \uc2 CURRY: What would it mean for your business if tomorrow L.A. implemented a vaccine mandate for employees and for patrons that come into bars and restaurants?\par \par \uc2 MEIRON: It would mean shutting down. It would mean being dead. It would mean being dead not by coronavirus, but being dead by our own government that is supposed to support us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: And as if vaccine mandates themselves weren't Orwellian enough, they're now being backed by the \uc2 America Civil Liberties Union\uc2 . The ACLU argues, quote, "Vaccine mandates actually further civil liberties. They protect the most vulnerable among us, people with disabilities and fragile immune systems, children too young to be vaccinated, and communities of color hit hard by the disease."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Harmeet Dhillon, CEO of the Center for American Liberty. Harmeet, the \uc2 American civil liberties union\uc2  doesn't seem to be protecting civil liberties anymore.\par \par \uc2 HARMEET DHILLON, CEO, CENTER FOR AMERICAN LIBERTY: Oh, yes, this has been a development that's been in the works for several years now. But when you read this op-ed in "The New York Times" by David Cole and his colleagues, their lawyers and head of religious liberties group there at the ACLU, it's indistinguishable from a statement that Dr. Fauci might put out or that the Chamber of Commerce might put out because it defends the rights to corporations to fire people if they decline to get vaccinated. It defends second-class citizenship for people who decline to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 And frankly, our country, you look at California, liberal state where I live, over 80 percent of the people have chosen to get vaccinated. So when you look at the history of the country, our civil rights, our Bill of Rights was really written to protect the minority against the tyranny of the majority. And the \uc2 America Civil Liberties Union\uc2  used to stand for that all these years until now they are demanding that for the benefit of a majority that already is vaccinated the rest of us, who decide that perhaps we as citizens in their individual choice do not wish to get vaccinated, that we should subvert our individual rights.\par \par \uc2 So this is upside down, Katie. This is, frankly, a very disturbing development. The column doesn't mention the First Amendment, the Equal Protection clause, the Due Process clause, or any of the scrutiny that the courts have built up over the generations here in the United States to protect the rights of minorities, religious minorities, people with medical issues and so forth.\par \par \uc2 So if this is the new ACLU, people better start shopping for a different organization to protect their individual civil rights, because that's not the business they're in anymore.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Yes, and just real quickly, in an emergency, as they say, people tend to jump into the mandates thinking they don't have effects down the road in terms of everything you just mentioned, the First Amendment, other civil liberties. What is the slippery slope here in terms of giving into this pandemic panic for other issues of civil liberties down the road?\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: It's terrifying. The ACLU a few years ago used to be against stopping and frisking people in neighborhoods and asking them for their papers. Now they're in favor of it. This is what China has been doing for many years. They have social credit scores attached to them. We are now being asked to show apps to be able to shop and eat. What next? Are climate change scores going to be on there, how much electricity I use is going to be an app and I'll be allowed in or not based on that? So this is endless, and it's scary.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Yes, people have to say stop. Harmeet, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, just when you thought the left couldn't get any more shrill, MSNBC's Joy Reid ups the ante. The Last Bite explains.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: I give you the most asinine reaction to this week's pro-life victory.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Between Republicans who don't respect the rules and the laws, and are willing to cheat and willing to do whatever it takes, and now the courts being on their side, and there now being a solid majority of them who want to enact hyper rightwing, basically a certain type of evangelical rule over us, which is \uc2 Taliban\uc2 -ism, right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: And you didn't you think the media could get any more deranged, \uc2 Taliban\uc2 -ism.\par \par \uc2 That's all we have for tonight. I'm Katie Pavlich in for Laura Ingraham. Thanks for watching this special edition of THE INGRAHAM ANGLE. I'll be on the big Sunday and Saturday show at 5:00. See you then. Greg Gutfeld takes it from here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020210904eh9300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Tours Destruction In Louisiana; Explosion And Fire At Banquet Hall And Nearby Homes; Concerns Over Stranded Americans As \uc2 Taliban\uc2  Seize Power; Democrats Renew Push To Pack Supreme Court; Rep. Markwayne Mullin (R-OK), Is Being Interviewed About His Effort Getting SIVs And AMCITS Out Of Afghanistan; Unending Battle To Keep Focus On Illegal Crossings; Mullin Mission Barred From Landing In Afghanistan; Mullin Disputes Claims About Rescue Effort\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Mike Emanuel, Peter Doocy, Jeff Paul, Bryan Llenas,  Griff Jenkins, Jonathan Hunt, Howard Kurtz \par \uc2 8,031 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, that is it for us. Thanks for watching. Have a great Labor Day weekend, everybody. We'll see you back here Monday for fan mail Monday.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Good evening, welcome to Washington I'm Mike Emanuel in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, we're following two big stories including the increasing concern over Americans and Afghans stranded in Afghanistan as the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  consolidate power. We will have a report from the State Department.\par \par \uc2 And Bret interviews an Oklahoma congressman who has returned from a highly criticized secret effort to get to Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Why should we not think that this was, you know, bravado or testosterone or an effort to get the spot? How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: His answer, still ahead, but we begin with devastation from Hurricane Ida and its remnants in the south and the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 We have Fox team coverage, Bryan Llenas is in Manville, New Jersey, where a banquet hall damaged in the storm exploded this morning, but we begin with Correspondent Jeff Paul in New Orleans with President Biden's visit to Louisiana. Good evening, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Yes, Mike, here in New Orleans, French Quarter, power is slowly flipping back on but throughout the state, hundreds of thousands of people are still without electricity, let alone water and gas and in some cases, without a place to call home.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice over): Nearly a week after it ripped through Louisiana, the grip of Hurricane Ida's impact continues to tighten. Power lines toppled, lines stretched long and some houses just pieces of their original form.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  got his first glimpse of the widespread destruction.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We also know there's a heck of a lot more work to do and that's why we're here today.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Welcomed to the State by Governor \uc2 John Bel Edwards\uc2 . President Biden toward one of the hardest hit communities of LaPlace, Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Folks, I know you're hurting -- I know you're hurting. And we're going to make sure we have someone coming through here going door to door, letting people know what's available to them right now.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: The area was not only walloped by the storm's strong gusts but feet of floodwater left folks trapped and their homes partially submerged.\par \par \uc2 The president also got to see the vast devastation from the sky flying above Lafayette, Port Fourchon and Grand Isle, a town where not a single home is still livable.\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN LASSERE, BAYOU BOEUF, LOUISIANA FIRE CHIEF: We just got to keep pushing forward. You know, the past behind us. We got to keep going forward. Oh, what happened happened, that's the way I look at it. We just got to rebuild.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: While crews work around the clock to restore power, refuel gas stations and distribute food and water, the state is now investigating several nursing homes.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say hundreds of elderly patients were moved to a warehouse amidst the hurricane. They received unconfirmed reports of patients being packed together, laying on the floor, water seeping inside and without power. At least four died and 14 were hospitalized.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This time, I didn't know if my mom was dead or alive. This is a nightmare. It's a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (on camera): Now, Biden at times joined by a Democratic governor and two Republican senators says this has nothing to do with party affiliation, this is about being an American, he says. He will be standing next to Louisiana as they rebuild but they need to Build Back Better, a nod to his massive infrastructure plan he hopes passes soon, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Jeff Paul starting us off from New Orleans tonight. Jeff, many thanks.\par \par \uc2 At least 49 people across five eastern states have died from the remnants of Ida. A building exploded this morning in New Jersey where some neighborhoods were inundated with as much as 14 feet of water.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bryan Llenas is in Manville tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAYESH MEHTA, OWNER, THE SAFFRON BANQUET HALL: Invested every penny that I'd earned in my life in this property.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): This smoldering rubble is all that's left of The Saffron banquet hall in Manville, New Jersey. The business exploded overnight, and three other homes caught fire after being inundated with up to eight feet of water.\par \par \uc2 Jayesh Mehta opened the obliterated banquet hall in 2018. Business was already tough because of COVID, now this.\par \par \uc2 MEHTA: I don't know what words to use. I bet everything in my life that I had for this property. I worked too hard. I don't know what to do. Totally helpless. And I just hope everybody pray for me.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: First responders in Manville were called to 500 homes rescuing 1,000 people from floodwaters spilled over from the Raritan River, which rose to a record shattering 28-feet.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS HERBST, POLICE CHIEF, MANVILLE, NEW JERSEY: It's not every year but I've been here 30 years and I think this is like I said in my eighth or 10th flood somewhere around there, and you know, this is the most catastrophic by far.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: These satellite photos show before and after the devastating flash floods in New Jersey, 10 inches of rain inundating homes and vehicles along the Delaware River.\par \par \uc2 More than two dozen people died in New Jersey, several are still missing. Most of the victims drowned in their vehicles.\par \par \uc2 In New York's Westchester County where floodwaters rose to 40 feet in some places, stunned residents are beginning to pick up the pieces.\par \par \uc2 ALI SHEBA, STORE MANAGER: It's a disaster. It's like a hurricane was in size. It's like Ida was actually in my store.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: More than a thousand buildings suffered damage in New York City where leaders admit they were surprised by the historic rainfall.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the mayor is promising to increase warnings and evacuation orders for those living in basement apartments that became death traps for 11 people including this two-year-old boy and his parents.\par \par \uc2 BILL DE BLASIO\uc2  (D), MAYOR OF NEW YORK: If we are seeing this kind of rain, we have to have an evacuation mechanism that can reach them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): The smoke is still billowing at the destroyed banquet hall behind me. Floodwaters made roadways impassable for firefighters. Flooding is still a concern for Jersey townships along the Passaic River which won't crest until tomorrow morning, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: So many heartbreaking stories. Bryan Llenas in the Garden State tonight. Bryan, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 There are growing questions tonight about what has happened to the Americans and Afghans who want to get out of Afghanistan but cannot.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Griff Jenkins looks for answers tonight at the State Department.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: We're in constant contact with Americans who remain in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): It's been four days since U.S. troops left Afghanistan and Secretary of State Blinken won't say if a single American left behind has gotten out.\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: Protect the viability of the -- of our tactics, I'm not going to go into any details.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Blinken says most of the remaining Americans are dual nationals who call Afghanistan home. And while he says the vast majority of the 124,000 evacuated were at risk Afghans, he acknowledges that a majority of SIV applicants did not make it out.\par \par \uc2 This as reports pour in of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  going door to door to find and punish Afghans who worked with the U.S.\par \par \uc2 FATIMA, AMERICAN TRAPPED IN KABUL: They are stranded, they are left behind. Their lives are in danger. And when I talk danger, it's extreme danger.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: At the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 , Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas charged with overseeing the vetting process says some of the evacuees flown out have been flagged on terror watch lists.\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, DEPUTY SECRETARY, HOMELAND SECURITY: We deny access to individuals whose derogatory information is not resolved and we do not feel confident in the safety and security of the American people.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Meanwhile, a group of Republican senators led by Tom Cotton, turning up the heat on the president.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): He broke his promise to Americans and he's continuing to break it because there are American citizens who are trying to get out of the country right now.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: But the fate of remaining Americans and Afghan allies depends on promises made by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  in their soon to be formed government, which Blinken says needs to live up to its commitments.\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: More important still is what any government does. And that's what we're -- that's what we're really looking at.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (on camera): And Mike, Secretary Blinken departs for Doha on Sunday. And while he is there, we are told he will not be meeting with any representatives of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . An official here at State Department saying we're not at that stage yet, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Interesting. Griff Jenkins live at the State Department. Griff, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has signed an executive order directing the declassification of certain documents related to the 9/11 terror attacks. Families of victims have long sought the records in the hopes of implicating the Saudi government. The order comes about a week before the 20th anniversary of the attacks.\par \par \uc2 President Biden says the Texas abortion law is an un-American vigilante system, the Supreme Court has refused to stop the new regulations and that's intensifying Democratic efforts to change the makeup of the High Court and get rid of the Senate filibuster.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy has the latest on that tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I have been continued to be a strong supporter of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): And before Roe v. Wade is challenged in front of the Supreme Court, President Biden is worried because they just upheld a Texas law restricting abortions.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The most pernicious thing about the Texas law, it sort of creates a vigilante system where people get rewards to go out to -- anyway, and it just seems -- I know this sounds ridiculous, almost un-American.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Progressives have had it. AOC tweets, Republicans promised to overturn Roe v. Wade, and they have. Democrats can either abolish the filibuster and expand the court or do nothing.\par \par \uc2 Some progressives have already outlined how they do it.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ED MARKEY\uc2  (D-MA): We undo the damage that Republicans have done by restoring balance. And we do it by passing the Judiciary Act of 2021, to add four seats to the court to create a 13-member Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The President describes himself as a --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Student of history.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And has historically been against expanding the court like when FDR tried.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He violated no law. He was legalistically absolutely correct. But it was a bonehead idea.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But as president, he has ordered a commission of experts to brief him on the pros and cons of court packing and his mind is open.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The president doesn't have any new thoughts on the structure of the court?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: He is waiting for the conclusion of this report, looks forward to reviewing it, seeing where they come out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): If the president decides he wants to add four spots to the court and fill them with progressives, he's going to have to do it while Democrats control Congress which could make the fight ahead of the upcoming midterms the equivalent of a quadruple Kavanaugh, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Peter Doocy in the White House Briefing Room. Peter, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Up next, feeling ignored at the border. One local mayor says he is fed up.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Bret Baier's exclusive interview with the congressman who took matters into his own hands in what he says an attempt to get people out of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're a congressman from Oklahoma, you're a businessman, mixed martial arts background but no military, right?\par \par \uc2 REP. MARKWAYNE MULLIN (R-OK): I wasn't trying to go over there and be a cowboy or anything like that. It was just, what else do you do, when you see a problem, how do you say no if you can be an asset?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you have any regrets about what you did?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: President Biden is pushing hard for his massive $3.5 trillion spending package to be approved by Congress. The president is also trying to explain a hugely disappointing August jobs report showing a gain of only 235,000 jobs, about a half million jobs short of expectations.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: There's no question that Delta variant is why today's job report isn't stronger. I know people were looking, and I was hoping, for a higher number. And now, we need Congress to finish the job to come through for the American people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHIEF CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): West Virginia Democrat Senator Joe Manchin says he will not support that $3-1/2 trillion dollar package.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed. The Dow lost 75, the S&P 500 was off two, \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  gained 32 for another record close.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow lost a quarter of a percentage point. The S&P 500 was up about a half-point. \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  gained 1-1/2.\par \par \uc2 The CDC does not want you to travel this Labor Day weekend if you've not been vaccinated. Some states are already dealing with hospitals at capacity. Idaho is reporting record numbers of emergency room visits, hospitalizations, and intensive care unit patients.\par \par \uc2 And in South Carolina, the National Guard is sending help to a hospital system where a surge in COVID patients is overwhelming emergency departments and doctors fear it will only get worse following Labor Day gatherings.\par \par \uc2 Some politicians who are not Republicans are turning on President Biden over the border crisis.\par \par \uc2 The mayor of Laredo, Texas is accusing the president of abandoning border communities.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, chief correspondent Jonathan Hunt shows us how the massive influx of immigrants continues to vex the people trying to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CHIEF CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Border Patrol agents are using every tool at their disposal. Airboats among them as they try to turn back the tide of illegal immigration that threatens to swamp our southern border.\par \par \uc2 In the Laredo sector alone, they have seen a more than 130 percent increase in the number of apprehensions of immigrants crossing the Rio Grande this year compared to last.\par \par \uc2 HUNT (on camera): On this side of the river, the Mexican side, those who want to cross watch and wait on that side, the U.S. side, Border Patrol agents do the same watching and waiting.\par \par \uc2 But there simply aren't enough of them to cover every inch of the 136 miles of this sector. And that's where these airboats can make a real difference.\par \par \uc2 HUNT (voice-over): And they do. But inevitably, they cannot stop everyone from making it to the north side of the river.\par \par \uc2 MATT HUDAK, LAREDO SECTOR CHIEF PATROL AGENT: When we encounter them, it's a foot chase on the ground, it's a vehicle pursuit. But, you know, from us or from our partner agencies, so the smuggling organizations are driving that.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: And so, it's dangerous.\par \par \uc2 HUDAK: It's dangerous. Right.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: The mayor of Laredo says he has had enough and accuses President Biden of breaking promises.\par \par \uc2 PETE SAENZ, MAYOR OF LAREDO, TEXAS: We want the cavalry to arrive. We've been waiting months now for that. And unfortunately, we haven't seen much of them.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: In a recent debate in the Texas State Senate. One Democrat summed it up this way.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JUAN HINOJOSA (D-TX): When a nation cannot define its border, it says to be a nation. And right now we do not control a border.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Trying to take back control, the hard-pressed agents on land and on water.\par \par \uc2 HUDAK: They do tremendous work, but there's no doubt that, that volume it does -- it does tire you out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HUNT (on camera): And the chief tells me Border Patrol could be even more effective here if they had more boats, more technology, and most of all more boots on the ground. But the money for all that, of course, has to come from Washington. Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Jonathan Hunt live in Laredo. Jonathan, many thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Bret talks with the Oklahoma republican congressman who set out on a mission to help Americans in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. WFXT in Boston, where former Cardinal Theodore McCarrick pleads not guilty to sexually assaulting a 16-year-old boy during a wedding reception in Massachusetts nearly 50 years ago.\par \par \uc2 McCarrick did not speak during the hearing. The court said bail at $5,000 and ordered him to stay away from the victim and have no contact with minors.\par \par \uc2 Fox2 in San Francisco with reports of price gouging as a massive wildfire forces evacuations in Lake Tahoe. A rideshare company quoted a fee of more than $1500 to reach the Reno-Tahoe International Airport, an eightfold increase.\par \par \uc2 Nevada Governor \uc2 Steve Sisolak\uc2  signed a price gouging prohibition in June but it does not take effect until October.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Atlanta from Fox5. One of the big stories there tonight, after going virtual in 2020, Dragon Con is soaring in person again. The convention welcomes back sci-fi fantasy and pop culture fans for five days of events and celebrity appearances.\par \par \uc2 Attendance might show a proof of vaccination or a proof of a negative COVID test taken within 72 hours prior to arriving.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Tonight, we get a firsthand account of how difficult it is to get in and out of Afghanistan. My colleague Bret Baier talked with Republican Congressman \uc2 Markwayne Mullin\uc2  earlier today.\par \par \uc2 Mullin is back from his trip to the region, which he says he paid for himself and with the help of private contributions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Republican Congressman \uc2 Markwayne Mullin\uc2  from Oklahoma. Just had an interesting trip overseas, got a lot of attention. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 REP. MARKWAYNE MULLIN (R-OK): Yes. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's start at the beginning.\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How does this start?\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Well, I want to make it very clear. I wasn't looking to try to get on Afghanistan. That wasn't what this thing started out to be. What started was people started calling me want help. And we'd been getting friends of mine that was in the service that said, can you help our interpreter get out, which we had a lot of conversations about that.\par \par \uc2 Then, we started getting phone calls from people that had family members or friends that were AMCITS or Americans -- American Citizens, AMCITS for short, to get -- to get them out. And I said, let me make the phone call.\par \par \uc2 And it came out that they needed a state department or a government entity to sponsor their flight going in. That's the only way they could get in. And no one would sponsor the fight, even though that it was designated to get these 20 Americans out.\par \par \uc2 And so, they called me back and said would you be interested in sponsoring the flight? And I said, I mean, what do you mean financially? And they said, no, a congressman can sponsor a flight going in. And it gives one of these other departments cover because it's kind of no one wants to touch it because I personally believe ready it was because they knew this was a hot mess. And so, that's we -- that's how this thing -- whole thing started.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, you don't actually get into Afghanistan to get people out?\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: So, as this thing move forward, is they wanted -- everybody kept denying them, and then they asked, would you go with us? And these are Tier 1 operators. These are Delta guys, Special Forces. These guys are legit. I'm nobody. I'm just a congressman.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're a congressman from Oklahoma, you're a businessman.\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mixed martial arts background, but no military, right?\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: No, that doesn't know -- that that doesn't play well in this. I wasn't trying to go over there and be a cowboy or anything like that. It was just what else you do? When you see a problem, how do you say no, if you can be an asset? And they believe I could be an asset.\par \par \uc2 We got who a humanitarian -- a flight plan from \uc2 FAA\uc2 . And everything was good to go. We left -- we left the United States, we had a time of arrival at HKIA -- in the Kabul, with HKIA's airport there in Kabul.\par \par \uc2 And we got there and we're gone approach and it canceled our PPR. And so, we circled the airport, probably for an hour, and we had five PPRs canceled. Every time they'd cancel, the agency that would -- that was trying to get us in, would give us a new one. They actually went into the tower to say, let them land.\par \par \uc2 We were on the phone with them texting, we couldn't talk, we're texting back and forth. And they kept issuing us new ones. And they would never let us in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 And I'll tell you this, too. Unfortunately, one of our American citizens, her two-year-old son, and I believe it was her father to which I all have passports on, we lost contact of, because they were at the Abbey Gate.\par \par \uc2 And a few hours later, the Abbey Gate blew up. And we haven't had -- we haven't had any contact with them since. And I believe it's because they were caught up in that.\par \par \uc2 And so, what they did by playing politics on this? For whatever reason?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, you don't know who shuts you down from landing?\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: I don't know what -- I don't know who shut us down. But I think it was 100 percent directed from the State Department.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: At some point, you go on to Tajikistan.\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: No, I never went to Tajikistan.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You didn't go in.\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: No, that is absolutely, absolutely false. We did work around the region, but Tajikistan was -- unfortunately, the Ambassador Pommersheim was not helpful at all. And a lot of misinformation was -- is going out about that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And for the storyline that said that you possibly interrupted some covert thing that was going on or some operation Tajikistan.\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: No, I don't -- I don't have a clue what they're talking about. This is what Ambassador Pommersheim told me. He says it's very sensitive right now Mark (INAUDIBLE) sensitive because Putin and Chinese -- and the Chinese governments come to take me here September 5th, it's very sensitive.\par \par \uc2 I said I get it very sensitive. I don't need any of your assets. I don't need anything. I, first of all, is making you aware that we're coming in. But I also -- and I also made the point I said, but we are coming in with a large sum of cash. Why are you coming out at a large sum of cash?\par \par \uc2 Sir, you do know that we got to go through 16 checkpoints, just to get from mess, each one of those charge. Someone to come through.\par \par \uc2 I don't know what their requirements are. But what are the requirements to bring in this cash? And he says, I can't assist you. I can't help you. Why? I was told not to. You were told not to help an American citizen. You were told by the federal government in Washington, D.C., not to help when we're not trying to smuggle anybody over. We're trying to do a job that President Biden wouldn't do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, a lot of people look at what you're trying to do as admirable. You had House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy saying he really didn't want members doing this wrong.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Sorry.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Because, one, they could become hostages, two, they could become problems that the military would have to deal with.\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think about that? Whether that was worth it?\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: I did. But how do you say no? I mean, how do you say no, when you have the opportunity to do something? Believe me, my wife wasn't happy about me going.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, I'm sure.\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: My kids wasn't happy about me going in, and be quite frank with you, all of us that was on that plane, we felt like we probably had a 50-50 chance of coming back.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Why should we not think that this was, you know, bravado --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Testosterone or an effort to get the spotlight?\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: I understand that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Like how do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Well, I'm not Rambo.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: I never pretend to be Rambo. We were surrounded by great people. Of all the guys that I was working with, I'm the low man on the totem pole. And I understood that. But they felt like I could be the assistance to them.\par \par \uc2 How? Logistically maybe. Open the door for them, making phone calls, and being able to take in the SIVs and the AMCITS as they came on to the plane. And that was the plan. That plan changed, and it changed when we wasn't allowed to get into Afghanistan. We quickly realized that the president and the State Department, General Milley, General McKenzie were lying to the American people by saying everybody who wanted out could get out. That is a lie. That a boldfaced, 100 percent lie, because we were working with these individuals.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you know today that there are just a bunch of them still stuck there?\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Right now as we speak, right before we started talking, I called the guys that are still over there, and they said there was five planes setting at Meza Sharif with Amcits, SIVs, and refugees on it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: American citizens, Special Immigration Visas.\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Special Immigration Visas, and refugees. And there may be P-1, P-2s which are levels of refugees that we can get out. But they can't get clearance to get out because no country is willing to help them. We called the State Department about this. The State Department said we are not in control of the airspace. We're not handling any of that. Well, it's our mess. I don't care which way you think about it, it's our mess. The United States created this vacuum to allow the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to come in. President Biden and his horrific withdrawal has caused this to happen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you have any regrets about what did you?\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Absolutely not. I would do it again tomorrow. We were saving people's lives.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What if 535 members decide to get on a plane and --\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Everybody is helping right now. I don't care if they are Republican or Democrat. Everybody is helping. I have heard from a lot of different members that are trying to do their thing. Everybody has a different way of helping.\par \par \uc2 Do you know why all those other governments were going out and picking up their individuals and bringing them back in? Because there was three \uc2 Taliban\uc2  checkpoints before you could get to HKIA, and all three of them could charge you a tax. And the tax was anywhere between $500 to $4,000. This one young lady and her four kids to which I have recordings of, we were recording as we were walking through because I was on the phone with the State Department for 12 hours trying to get someone from the State Department to come out of the gate to get her, for 12 hours, 12 hours. I have it recorded. Couldn't get anybody to come out. She had to come through there. It cost her $2,000.\par \par \uc2 The State Department kept saying, hey, go up and talk to them. Tell them they're waiting on us. Give them your name. They know you are coming. Five times she approached this guard with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . The last time they stuck a pistol to her head with her kids there. I have it recorded with her kids screaming and crying. She goes back and she -- she is crying so hard that she begins to puke, the mother did. And the State Department sent her up there five times saying we've got clearance for her.\par \par \uc2 And here we are, the \uc2 United States government\uc2 , and we're negotiating with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to get American citizens out, and then the president of the United States is going to go to the mic and say that every American that wanted out got out? I have a list of 50 that want out that they haven't got out. I have a list of 50 that we have ran out of Kabul and put in safe houses around Afghanistan that I promise you they wanted out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think we're going to get these people out?\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: We're going to get some, but there is going to be some that's going to die because of the failure from President Biden. I promise you that. At some point they are going to lose patience and aren't going to keep letting us drive past checkpoints and paying them off. Sorry, taxes. That's what they call it, tax.\par \par \uc2 And when I say that blood is on his hands, that means President Biden's hands, I mean that with everything in my heart. It's his fault. I wouldn't have been over there. I wouldn't have even thought about going if it wasn't for his failure. I wouldn't have put my life in danger. I wouldn't have put my kids and my wife through that. I wouldn't have put anybody through it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you'd do it again?\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Without even hesitation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman, we appreciate your time. I know you just traveled back. And the story is compelling.\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Bret, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Up next, why many in the mainstream media are moving on from Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The mainstream media are concentrating on the Texas law and hurricane Ida as many try to push Afghanistan into the background. FOX News media analyst and host of FOX's "Media Buzz" Howard Kurtz takes a look tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST: With new polls showing public disapproval of President Biden's handling of Afghanistan, some liberal commentators are focusing on his decision to pull out U.S. forces.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is a fact that ending this war required a level of resolve that no one had prior to President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: But a "Washington Post"-ABC poll today shows a crucial distinction. While 77 percent back Biden on getting out of Afghanistan, a striking 60 percent say they disapprove of Biden's handling of what proved to be a chaotic and violent evacuation. Only seven percent of Republicans approve of that. The obvious political damage to the president may help explain why some liberal outlets are quickly moving on from the war. One hour after Biden defended the exit from Afghanistan --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Extraordinary success of this mission.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Several MSNBC hosts started leading their programs instead with comments by freshman Republican Congressman Madison Cawthorn, who said the failure to change what he