t to turn the page from the issue that is fueling accusations of broken promises from the president. We`ll take a closer look at that in a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, dozens of Republicans are now calling for the president`s impeachment over his actions and this phone call with the Afghan president before the fall of Kabul. But impeachment is something the top GOP senator says will not happen.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich starts us off tonight, live in the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. On the third day since the troop withdrawal, no answer on whether any more Americans have made it out of Afghanistan or how many Green Card holders or SIV applicants remain. The White House is directing those questions to other departments. And there`s a distinct feeling President Biden is trying to move on from Afghanistan to his domestic agenda.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There`s a lot of damage.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): President Biden addressed the nation about storm recovery but gave no update on Afghanistan and took no questions from reporters as more than a hundred Americans and countless at-risk Afghans who helped the U.S. in the 20-year war remain behind enemy lines.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: There are people who are eligible, we may not even know they`re eligible yet, right? It is very hard to define those numbers.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Meanwhile, accounts of reprisals those Afghans face are getting more dire.\par \par \uc2 This person said they killed 12 members of special forces in Kandahar, three soldiers in Jalalabad as well. The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  took them out of their homes and shot them.\par \par \uc2 Given this kind of reporting or hearing, is it possible that our reliance on the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to keep their word in terms of our evacuation is misplaced, we`re going back on things like the amnesty promise?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: No one is saying from the federal government, no one. The president, Secretary of Defense, no one from the Intelligence Community that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  are good actors. That is one of the reasons we are being so clear that we are not rushing to recognition.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Politico reports shame among anonymous sources inside the White House. One person telling Politico, I am absolutely appalled and literally horrified we left Americans there. Kabul`s airport remains inoperable and the White House says there are risks associated with charter flights seeking to extract Americans to U.S. military basis.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: We do not have reliable means to confirm the basic details of charter flights including who may be organizing them. We know ISIS-K has a keen interest in attacks against aviation targets and our personnel on the ground and our Air Force in our military bases.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Among the House GOP, growing calls for the president to resign but the top Senate Republican \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  says President Biden will not be impeached and the ballot box is a better place to hold him accountable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Still, other Republican senators want accountability now. 25 members wrote to President Biden saying while they disagree on whether troops should have remained in Afghanistan, they`re all in agreement President Biden`s chaotic withdrawal caused this crisis, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui, obviously, the administration, the president want to focus on domestic agenda items, but you have this human infrastructure bill that`s kind of sitting out there, the 3.5 trillion, a key Senate Democrat is pretty much blocking that path.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: That`s right. West Virginia Senator Joe Manchin said there is no way he will support that $3.5 trillion social infrastructure package that Democrats want to pass along party lines.\par \par \uc2 He dragged his colleagues for what he called a strange belief, there`s an infinite amount of money to go around. He wrote, Democratic congressional leaders proposed to pass the largest single spending bill in history with no regard to rising inflation, crippling debt, or the inevitability of future crises. Ignoring the fiscal consequences of our policy choices will create a disastrous future for the next generation of Americans.\par \par \uc2 Without Manchin`s vote, Biden`s agenda really seems to go nowhere and it appears that Manchin is dug in, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, we`ll follow that. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`re hearing tonight from an Afghan interpreter who helped save then- Senator Biden 13 years ago and is now pleading for rescue himself. That man and his family are joined by as we mentioned, Americans and thousands of Afghans who helped the U.S. through the 20 years of war who today still cannot leave that country.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Griff Jenkins has the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NED PRICE\uc2 , STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESMAN: We will provide them a tailored guidance but that is not something we will provide to all of you. And we won`t do that for their safety.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): For those stuck behind enemy lines, the plan to get them out is unclear. With the Kabul airport shut down, dangerous ground routes appear to be the only option. The State Department also won`t say if any American citizens have gotten out since the last flight out of Kabul on August 30th. Leaving behind 100 to 200 Americans, tens of thousands of Afghan allies, including the majority of SIV applicants, and even the State Department`s own journalists.\par \par \uc2 Also, among those stranded, an interpreter known as Mohammed, who helped rescue then-Senator \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2008 when his helicopter made an emergency landing in the snow-covered mountains of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If you want to know where Al-Qaeda lives, you want to know where bin Laden is, come back to Afghanistan with me. Come back to the area my helicopter was forced down.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Unable to get out before the deadline, Mohammed is now pleading for President Biden to save him and his family.\par \par \uc2 MOHAMMED, AFGHAN INTERPRETER: Maybe die at home. There is no way I can`t escape from my house. All borders in my country are sealed, but only I and my -- and my son have a U.S. passport. The rest of the family do not have.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: The State Department`s other focus is resettling and vetting the tens of thousands of refugees arriving on American soil amid growing concerns that individuals were extremists could slip through the cracks.\par \par \uc2 State Department spokesman \uc2 Ned Price\uc2  says there`s a plan for those who get flagged.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: In cases where that vetting may take longer, we do have options available to us. But I`m just not in a position to detail.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: This as a new diplomatic mission for Afghanistan, headquartered in Doha must decide how they will work with as soon to be formed \uc2 Taliban\uc2  government and if they can trust them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And Bret, any moment now, we expect Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  to arrive here to the Dulles Expo Center behind me. It`s one of the major processing centers for arriving refugees as State Department says that more than 31,000 of the total 124,000 people evacuated have made it to the U.S. soil with about 77 percent of those being at risk Afghans, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Griff Jenkins in Chantilly, Virginia. Griff, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The shockwaves continued tonight over the admission by the nation`s top military officer that the U.S. may actually have to cooperate with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  in counterterrorism operations.\par \par \uc2 The terror group is solidifying its control over Afghanistan tonight. National security correspondent Jennifer Griffin is at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): \uc2 Taliban\uc2  fighters took on the role of the police in Kabul today helping with traffic at checkpoints and issuing a statement like this one urging calm.\par \par \uc2 AMANULLAH, \uc2 TALIBAN\uc2  BARDI SPECIAL FORCES: Everyone should go to work and live. Offices, shops and companies should start operating and have no fear of anyone.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Pentagon leaders raised eyebrows when asked Wednesday about working with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  in the future.\par \par \uc2 GEN. MARK MILLEY, CHAIRMAN, JOINT CHIEFS OF STAFF: In war, you do what you must in order to reduce risk to mission and force, not what you necessarily want to do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Any possibility of coordination against ISIS-K with them?\par \par \uc2 Milley: It`s possible.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Dan Hoffman, a former CIA Chief of Station spent years hunting Al Qaeda and says such an intelligence relationship would be complicated.\par \par \uc2 DAN HOFFMAN, FORMER CHIEF OF STATION, CIA: We would need to corroborate everything the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is telling us on our own before we would launch any strikes.\par \par \uc2 And whatever information we provided to them, we`d have to be very careful that they are not going to take it and then go out and conduct operations which violate human rights of innocent civilians.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Former Defense Secretary \uc2 William Cohen\uc2  explained how the U.S. could leverage the Taliban`s need for U.S. Intelligence.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM COHEN\uc2 , FORMER DEFENSE SECRETARY: They may come under severe attack from Al-Qaeda and from ISIS-K or ISIS-Z. They`ll need intelligence that they don`t have that we may be able to provide them under certain conditions.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: A difficult reality for the 800,000 veterans of the past 20 years and Gold Star families like this one, the last American soldier to die in Afghanistan, Staff Sergeant Ryan Knauss. He died of his wounds after the suicide bombing at Abbey Gate. His wife Elena was tiling her shower floor when she received the knock at the door. She told NPR she began screaming, collapsed and then stayed up all night through her tears to put their initials and date in the shower tile floor.\par \par \uc2 ELENA KNAUSS, WIDOW OF STAFF SERGEANT RYAN KNAUSS: I`m in shambles and I`m hurting but that no mother, father, wife, brother, sister ever has to feel such emptiness, I would be grateful to know he was the last.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): Of the 22 wounded in the Abby Gate attack, we`ve learned that 15 Marines are currently at Walter Reed. One is in critical condition, three are serious and 11 are stable, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin live at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 At least 43 people have died in the East after massive flooding from record rain fall produced by the remnants of Hurricane Ida.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bryan Llenas shows us the devastation tonight from Queens, New York. Good evening, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening. The rainfall in New York City was extraordinary and unprecedented. At least 13 people have died in New York City, 11 of them drowning in their basement apartments like this one behind me.\par \par \uc2 The flash flooding, stunning residents and state officials.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Tonight, New York City`s in a state of emergency after a cascading waterfall shut down the city subway trapping train cars and flooding stations Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 The historic rainfall turned the city`s highways and streets into rivers, forcing first responders to save drivers from trapped vehicles.\par \par \uc2 At least 11 people in the city died in their basement apartments in Queens, New York, including a 2-year-old boy, a mother and her 22-year-old son drowned in this apartment when four feet of water rushed into a gaping hole.\par \par \uc2 AMIT SHIVPRASAD, HOMEOWNER: It was devastating. I can tell you that my body`s still trembling as we talk about this right now.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: The remnants of Hurricane Ida dumped a historic 10 inches of rain over just several hours in New York City. Central Park saw 3.15 inches of rain in just one hour. Shattering an old-time record set just two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Look at that tornado.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: In New Jersey, a tornado ripped through the community of Mullica Hills, destroying an estimated two dozen homes.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY THOMAS, TORNADO VICTIM: It was like a warzone.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Ashley Thomas is eight months pregnant.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: I just couldn`t believe that everywhere the ambulance went, they was just slamming on his brakes because there was a wire down or a tree and I`m like our community has just destroyed in five seconds. That`s all it took. Heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Nearly a foot of rain caused historic deadly flash flooding in New Jersey, submerging a ballpark, homes and businesses.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, first responders have conducted thousands of water rescues saving people trapped on their roofs. New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  today said state officials were caught off guard by the flooding.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KATHY HOCHUL (D-NY): We did not know that between 8:50 and 9:50 p.m. last night that the heavens would literally open up and bring Niagara Falls level water to the streets of New York. Could that have been anticipated? I want to find out.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: President Biden today blamed climate change.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That these extreme storms and the climate crisis are here. We need to do -- we best be better prepared. We need to act.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): The death toll has shockingly risen to at least 43. Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  says at least 23 people died in New Jersey, the majority people who were trapped in their vehicles as the floodwaters rose and swept them away.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is here though that the flood danger continues tonight into tomorrow as the rivers continue to rise and have yet to crest, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, we`ll follow it here on Fox. Bryan Llenas live in Queens. Bryan, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were up today, the Dow gained 131, the S&P 500 Rose 13 to a new record close. The \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  finished ahead 22, also a new record close tonight.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we take you to the southern border for a Fox News exclusive. A look at the organization and sophistication of human smuggling opera (AUDIO GAP).\par \par \uc2 First, here`s what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 40 in Sacramento as firefighters throw all their resources into boxing a wildfire, the Caldor Fire after winds finally turn in their favor.\par \par \uc2 The blaze which began August 14th has scorched more than 210,000 acres in that area.\par \par \uc2 Fox 26 in Houston were veterans suffering from post-traumatic stress disorder can now legally get medical marijuana to ease those symptoms.\par \par \uc2 Cancer patients can also obtain cannabis to lessen suffering and side effects particularly from chemotherapy.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Cleveland, Ohio from one of our affiliates Fox 8. One of the big stories there tonight, officials determined a foul odor smelled in several lakeshore communities resulted from an inversion of Lake Erie`s waters.\par \par \uc2 Strong winds and large waves churned up sludge along the bottom of the lake, blowing the smell along the shore. One official