erican president again, but so far on foreign policy, his word and the way things have gone have been different. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy in the White House briefing room. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the program, we showed you the horrific damage in the northeast from the remnants of Hurricane Ida.\par \par \uc2 Well, Louisiana is trying to recover from even more devastation. Senior correspondent Casey Stegall is in Louisiana tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CASEY STEGALL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Parts of Louisiana, including metro New Orleans, are slowly coming back to life. For some, the lights are on.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR \uc2 LATOYA CANTRELL\uc2 , NEW ORLEANS: We saw some critical success on yesterday as it relates to power restoration. We expect more to come online and gradually throughout the day and, of course, every day making and pushing for significant progress.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: FOX News got an exclusive ride with the \uc2 U.S. Army Corps of Engineers\uc2  as the agency`s top brass flew along with state leaders to the hardest hit areas of southeast Louisiana. The fact-finding mission was to assess the individual needs of each community, like how to best remove standing water that hasn`t yet drained outside the federal protection system.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. SCOTT SPELLMON, \uc2 U.S. ARMY CORPS OF ENGINEERS\uc2 : There are other places where we need to go south of New Orleans where we`ll actually go in and we`ll cut a levee to help that water get back into the river, and then we`re going to patch it right back up again.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: In other places like Lafitte, they`ve determined pumps will work best on draining the judge along with mother nature.\par \par \uc2 SPELLMON: Gravity is going to take that water over the course of the next two or three days and put that water back in Lake Pontchartrain.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: More than 948,000 are still in the dark across the region, four days after Ida`s devastating landfall. Thousands more don`t have clean drinking water.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My wife needs it. She needs a lot of fluid and stuff because she takes a lot of medicine.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Thomas Washington and his neighbors have had to rely on distribution sites like this one in Gramercy, Louisiana, run by the National Guard.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is a blessing right here, this water.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Fuel is badly needed, too. Lines stretched for miles that only a handful of gas stations with supply and power to run pumps. There is also a sense of urgency with this recovery as we`re only halfway through the Atlantic hurricane season.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEGALL (on camera): Today we also learned of at least three new deaths here in the state of Louisiana, all residents of a nursing home that had been evacuated to a parish north of New Orleans. The state department of health is investigating that. It brings the total now to 11 people who have lost their lives in the gulf coast region. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Casey Stegall in Louisiana tonight. Casey, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the politics of Afghanistan, also the California recall.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. India imposes a near total lockdown in the disputed areas of Kashmir following the death of a revered separatist leader there -- 92-year-old \uc2 Syed Ali Geelani\uc2  died at his home late Wednesday. He was buried quickly amid fears of anti-India protests. The family claims it was blocked from attending his burial.\par \par \uc2 Ambulance drivers in Athens, Greece, joins hundreds of health care workers to protest new mandatory COVID vaccinations. The government is threatening to suspend without pay hospital personnel who refuse the vaccine. Workers warn the requirement could lead to staff shortages.\par \par \uc2 And climate envoy \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  is in China calling for action to cut global carbon emissions. Kerry says the effort needs Beijing`s full engagement. China`s foreign minister warns the deteriorating relations between the two countries could undermine cooperation.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You called for his impeachment over Afghanistan. Do you still feel he should be impeached over this?\par \par \uc2 SEN. LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): I think he has been derelict in his duties as commander-in-chief. I don`t think he got bad advice and took it. I think he ignored sound advice, and this is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  being \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He has been this way for 40 years. But now he is the commander-in-chief.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: The president is not going to be removed from office. It`s a Democratic House, a narrowly Democratic Senate. That`s not going to happen. There isn`t going to be any impeachment. But I think they have a good chance of an a very bad election next year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senate minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  saying there is not going to be an impeachment, the president is not going to resign. But there`s a number of Republicans calling for that. If you look at Republicans calling for removal, resignation, 46 Republicans, impeachment 11, raising the 25th Amendment because of the decisions over Afghanistan, there you see three.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, "The Wall Street Journal" about the Afghanistan impact on elections. At home Bidenites hope Afghanistan will be in rearview mirror long before the midterm elections. Swing district Democrats can immunize themselves by criticizing the administration over the execution of the withdrawal. All Democrats will benefit from their association with a president who, however inelegantly, is ending unpopular wars rather than starting new ones. These hopes are not completely unfounded, and Republicans who want to ride the Afghanistan issue back into power may be disappointed. History, however, always punishing failure."\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in our panel, former Education Secretary Bill Bennett, \uc2 Mara Liasson\uc2 , national political correspondent of National Public Radio, and Jonah Goldberg, editor in chief of "The Dispatch." Jonah, what about that analysis from "Wall Street Journal" and the lasting impact of the decisions in Afghanistan?\par \par \uc2 JONAH GOLDBERG, "THE DISPATCH": Yes, I certainly think that history is going to be brutal on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for what he has done here. And I think that if the story goes away the way it did after the fall of Saigon to a certain extent, the "Journal" might be right on that, that Americans don`t generally vote on foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 The problem with this vanishing into the rearview window theory is that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and Al-Qaeda and the facts on the ground in Afghanistan in effect get a vote. And if we see ongoing executions, brutalities, if there are terrorist attacks that can be linked back to \uc2 Al-Qaeda in Afghanistan\uc2 , it keeps it a live issue in the way that the North Vietnamese taking over South Vietnam wasn`t.\par \par \uc2 I should add one cultural point -- the very thought that there could still be POWs and MIAs in Vietnam had a profound cultural half-life in this country for a half century. And we are going to be probably hearing stories about Americans and American allies left in Afghanistan for a long time, and I think that is going to have not only just a shameful impact on our reputation around the world, but it is also going to be part of our culture and part of the conversation for a long time to come.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I think you are right. The shelf life of that, especially with Americans on the ground, plus the thousands of Afghans who helped America, and the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  perhaps reverting to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  we know so well, at least the atrocities that we`re hearing already from the \uc2 BBC\uc2  and others, Mara. The tough part is that the administration really can`t get us a firm number on any of this. It seems like we are out of State, out of the Pentagon, out of the White House, we cannot get a number.\par \par \uc2 MARA LIASSON\uc2 , NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, NATIONAL PUBLIC RADIO: Yes, a number of how many Afghan allies are SIV candidates and want to leave, how many Americans are still there, green card holders, legal residents who are there and want to leave. Yes, that number is pretty important, because the bigger it is, the more problem it is for the administration, because they`ve got to get those people out. They`ve said they would. They said that somehow there is a way to get them out even without American boots on the ground there. So we`ll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 But I think in terms of what impact had has on the next election, that depends on all those things Jonah just listed. Maybe Afghanistan will fade from the headlines, but if there are stories of atrocities, stories of people being trapped, and Republicans will call them hostages, not being allowed to leave by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , that`s going to be a real problem. But I think in general, we have learned that Americans don`t really care that much about Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill?\par \par \uc2 BILL BENNETT, FORMER EDUCATION SECRETARY: Well, but they do care about those stories and about those people. The numbers matter, the data matters, those individual stories have a resonance. Here is this man who helped \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and others when his helicopter was in trouble or went down in the snow. And now he is writing him directly saying help me get out. And the president seems powerless to do it.\par \par \uc2 Look, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is right. Impeachment here is deserved, but the currency of impeachment I think has been cheapened over the last few 