e TSA will now allow customers to use the new feature at select airport security checkpoints in participating airports.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Two \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  senior officials are reportedly stepping down from their posts over a disagreement with the White House on the administration of booster shots.\par \par \uc2 Source telling Fox Business, the director of the agency's Office of Vaccine Research and Review, and her deputy director resigned after the Biden administration went ahead with the booster program starting the week of September 20th without \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval.\par \par \uc2 Idaho's Republican governor is calling in 220 medical workers available through federal programs and mobilizing 150 Idaho National Guard soldiers to deal with overwhelmed state hospitals.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Brad Little\uc2  says the moves are a last-ditch effort at avoiding -- to avoid activating for the first time statewide crisis standards of care that could force medical professionals to decide who lives and who dies.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, Governor Little said there were only four adult ICU beds still available in the state of Idaho.\par \par \uc2 State officials in Georgia say more residents are being diagnosed with COVID than ever before. Georgia's rolling seven-day average for positive tests rose more than 9,600 per day Tuesday. That tops the previous high from January.\par \par \uc2 Meantime in Texas, the Texas law banning most abortions in the state is now in effect. Opponents have asked the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  to intervene.\par \par \uc2 Fox News chief legal correspondent, anchor of "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT" Shannon Bream joins us from New York with the latest. Good evening, Shannon. Where does this request with the Supreme Court stand right now?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Well, Brett, it's a bit of a wait and see at this point. It is true that inaction by the Supreme Court meeting it didn't issue anything in response to a request to block the law before it went into effect today means just that, it's now in effect.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): The initial request to block it went to Justice Alito. Now, that's solely based on geography. He can decide this alone or refer to the full court. He did ask the opposing side to file a response to the emergency request. They did that yesterday, along with a final reply by the abortion clinics and groups seeking to block the law.\par \par \uc2 So, those of us who watch the court thought, hey, there's a good chance we get an order or decision before midnight last night. Both sides of the abortion debate are now standing by.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What we're seeing in Texas is frankly, chaos. Patients are scared.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We've already seen an uptick in the number of pregnant women seeking assistance going to pregnancy resource centers because abortion clinics in Texas have announced that they can no longer offer abortions after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): So, just because it didn't happen -- I was getting something for the Supreme Court, doesn't mean we might not still get something from them, Bret. I'm glued to my phone.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. You know, obviously, it's controversial, but what are the critic's main objects -- objections to this bill? And really, what are the odds it survives legal scrutiny?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, well, it bans most abortions in Texas after six weeks. But it's this enforcement mechanism that is getting the real attention because it allows average private citizens to sue abortion providers or those who assist in an illegal abortion, they can recover a minimum of $10,000 in damages plus their attorney's fees.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Private citizens enforce the law. The government has no role in enforcing it. So basically, every Texas citizen is now a private attorney general.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden now says that the law he thinks is blatantly violating the constitution. And he calls this private citizen component of it, outrageous. It's definitely unique. So, we'll see how it stands up under scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, a lot of pro-life groups have been planning different aspects of this. How does this fit in with that strategy, number of red states making a run at overturning Roe v. Wade?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, over the last couple of years, a number of states have passed laws that many elected officials are openly saying or specifically about trying to overturn Roe v. Wade. Nearly all of them immediately entangled in legal proceedings.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): And now everyone is watching and waiting for this case over Mississippi's law to be argued at the Supreme Court this fall. That law bans nearly all abortions in the state after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 And no matter how the case is decided, it's likely that the underlying issue for the most part is going to revert to the states.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Many of them already passed laws saying that the right to abortion is secured. Now, others have these so-called, trigger laws. Meaning, if the actual underpinning of Roe v. Wade is overturned, there will be no right to an abortion in those states.\par \par \uc2 This court, as we know, under the guidance of Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  has been reticent to issue, big sweeping decisions. I'd expect most likely whatever comes down from that Mississippi case could be very narrow. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will see if you get a ruling before "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT". See you then.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll let you know.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "BREAKING TONIGHT", a manhunt has concluded in North Carolina after a student was killed in a shooting at a high school in Winston Salem. The Forsyth County Sheriff's Office just tweeting the shooting occurred around noon at Mount Tabor High School. The sheriff says the suspect is now in custody.\par \par \uc2 Up next we go live to Louisiana to check on the devastation from Hurricane Ida.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Absolutely the worst storm I've ever seen. I hope I don't see another one. Not like this. South Louisiana in a whole is destroyed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The remnants of Hurricane Ida are causing problems in the East tonight. 20 homes knocked off their foundations in Western Virginia. Heavy rain pouring down from the central Appalachians to New England.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the devastation is keeping many people in Louisiana from returning to their homes and huge numbers are still without power tonight. President Biden will travel to the region on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Senior correspondent Mike Tobin is in Metairie, Louisiana tonight. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. That food distribution point is one of 17 operated by the National Guard in eight different parishes across Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 At last tally, the guard had given out almost a quarter-million meals ready to eat, 300,000 boxes of water, and almost 20,000 Tarbes (PH). This, as the Gulf Coast is on the long hot miserable road back to normal.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice-over): Countless communities along the Gulf Coast and further inland face days, weeks, and months of rebuilding.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Coming to a third world country, no water, no gas, no lights.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: On the barrier island of Grand Isle, around half the homes were destroyed by Hurricane Ida.\par \par \uc2 SCOOTER RESWEBER, POLICE CHIEF, GRAND ISLE, LOUISIANA: In my neighborhood, I lost my home. My grandson lost his home. I have a deputy had lost his home.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: In New Orleans, officials know they are in a race to restore services to the half of the population that decided not to evacuate.\par \par \uc2 LATOYA CANTRELL\uc2 , MAYOR OF NEW ORLEANS, LOUISIANA: Primary objectives today are getting our people what they need. We know they need food, water, cool air.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: With paramedics, firefighters, and EMT responding to call volumes 185 percent above normal, the dusk to dawn curfew began yesterday will remain in place until further notice.\par \par \uc2 SHAUN FERGUSON, POLICE CHIEF, NEW ORLEANS: So, it is totally unsafe. And I would like to encourage each and everyone to please remain calm and vigilant.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: But progress is happening. A small number of residents finally getting power turned back on.\par \par \uc2 DEANNA RODRIGUEZ, PRESIDENT, ENTERGY, NEW ORLEANS: Lights came on around 1:02 a.m. And this is the first step, again, in returning to normal.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Shortages continued throughout the regions. Even for blood, this donation center in Baton Rouge has less than a day supply in the shelves and some transfusions have already been delayed.\par \par \uc2 ALEX COHEN, PLASMA DONOR: Well, everybody has got to do their part. I mean, these tough times right now.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: And help from across America is also ramping up fast. Donations are piling up in places like Atlantic Beach, North Carolina where locals know exactly what folks further south are going through.\par \par \uc2 CASEY, ARTHUR, DEPUTY CHIEF, ATLANTIC BEACH FIRE DEPARTMENT: I know how I felt when we started receiving items after Hurricane Isabel. It really meant a lot to me to just see that people cared and that we were not alone in the situation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): Sheriff Marlin Gusman of Orleans Parish tells me, 55 people have been arrested since the storm, most of them for looting or vandalism. But there was a taskforce of guardsmen, sheriff's deputies, and metro police out in the street. And they learn from the mistakes that lead to chaos after Katrina.\par \par \uc2 Bret, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike Tobin, live in Louisiana. Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 We're getting a new look tonight at the damage left behind from Hurricane Ida. This look from the sky. Senior correspondent Casey Stegall is in Baton Rouge tonight. Good evening, Casey.\par \par \uc2 CASEY STEGALL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. We spent the better part of the day up in a Blackhawk helicopter with the \uc2 U.S. Army Corps of Engineers\uc2 , and the purpose of the mission was really twofold, to give the top brass of the Corps of Engineers an up close and personal look at the damage, but also to survey the levee system, for example, and get better sense of what specific communities need to help speed up their recovery.\par \par \uc2 Like in LaPlace, they now plan on cutting some of the levees to allow standing water to drain back into Lake Pontchartrain. South of New Orleans in Lafitte they have decided to bring in additional pumps and let gravity pull the water back into the gulf. But one thing is clear -- when you see the damage by the air, it is sobering, because you see stretches of hundreds and hundreds of miles that look like they have been wiped off the map along the southeast Louisiana coast. Row after row of home and businesses blown apart, boats in streets, nearly every power or communication line down and crumbled. It really puts it into perspective and the scope of this disaster, Bret, and that the rover will take weeks, if not months or even years for some people. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Quite a view. Casey Stegall in Baton Rouge. Casey, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Another disaster is growing worse out west. A massive wildfire is threatening thousands of structures in South Lake Tahoe. And that's where we find national correspondent William La Jeunesse tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, yes. We don't want to go any further.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: High winds, a record drought. The Caldor fire turning this California playground into a ghost town.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So I promised my kid I would be out of here as soon as I saw any flames anywhere.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm hoping at some point I can just nab that cat and be out of here like a shot.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: And 53,000 forced to evacuate, some now sheltering in tents, others fill California and Nevada hotels.\par \par \uc2 JIM DUDLEY, METEOROLOGIST: We are in red flag conditions. And those red flag conditions are for low relative humidity and strong gusty winds, especially up over the ridges.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Over the past 18 days, the Caldor fire burned more than 300 square miles and 544 homes. Moving west to east, firefighters struggle to keep it south of the homes and businesses around Lake Tahoe.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm getting out as soon as I secure this area, which is my business. As soon as I secure it, I'm getting out of here.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Tahoe is America's largest alpine lake, pristine waters surrounded by pines, clean mountain air now a billowing blanket of ash stretching hundreds of miles.\par \par \uc2 DAVE LAUCHNER, SACRAMENTO FIRE DEPARTMENT: Basically, a flame or an ember lands on the dry brush, and it ignites. It's not stopping it.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Torching pine needles in an overgrown forest that hasn't burned in decades, add in 40 mile-per-hour winds, you get this.\par \par \uc2 STEVE VOLLMER, FIRE BEHAVIOR ANALYST: They create what's called an active crown fire run where the fire actually goes from treetop to treetop. When it does that, those ember casts that it throws 