ccepts full responsibility for what he calls his misinterpreted actions, but blamed politics for his persecution.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE" tonight. David Lee Miller in New York City tells us how Cuomo may still have legal problems despite his resignation. And correspondent Bryan Llenas is in Albany tonight with the blockbuster details of this day. Good evening, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening. Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 's reputation in Albany is infamous. A bully and a fighter who gets his way no matter what.\par \par \uc2 But tonight, facing the biggest political fight of his life. Cuomo did what few believed possible, he gave up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. ANDREW CUOMO\uc2  (D-NY): The best way I can help now is if I step aside and let government get back to governing.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice-over): New York Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  resigned today amid sexual harassment allegations, criminal investigations, and a looming impeachment. In a surprise speech, the governor apologize for offending women, but at the same time denied that he ever groped anyone, while also saying he's the victim of a political witch hunt.\par \par \uc2 CUOMO: The most serious allegations made against me had no credible factual basis in the report. I am a fighter. And my instinct is to fight through this controversy. Because I truly believe it is politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Just minutes before Cuomo resigned, his attorney, Rita Glavin leveled a blistering attack via PowerPoint against last week's state attorney general report which found the governor sexually harassed 11 women, including groping.\par \par \uc2 RITA GLAVIN, ATTORNEY TO GOVERNOR \uc2 ANDREW CUOMO\uc2 : It failed to include witnesses whose testimony did not support the narrative.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Glavin discredited each accuser one by one including \uc2 Lindsey Boylan\uc2 , 