ntrol half of Afghanistan's 421 districts, and are putting pressure on half the country's provincial capitals.\par \par \uc2 General Milley warned momentum is now with the side of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  as the U.S. military withdraws, which is now more than 95 percent complete, Martha.\par \par \uc2 We also learned the first Afghan interpreters would arrive soon to Fort Lee, Virginia outside Richmond. There are currently 300,000 Afghan forces battling 75,000 \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . Militia, the Afghan army has the capacity to defend their country but admitted the numbers don't tell the whole story in warfare.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the recent books on former President Trump, General Milley would not confirm or deny if he played any role in stopping a potential coup, Milley did offer the following.\par \par \uc2 MILLEY: I always personally provided the best military professional advice to President Trump previously. We take an oath, an oath to a document an oath to the Constitution of the United States, and not one time do we violate that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: General Milley also said the U.S. military will remain apolitical. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Lucas, thank you very much. Lucas Tomlinson.\par \par \uc2 Here now, General Jack Keane, former vice chief of staff of the Army, chairman of the Institute of the -- for the Study of war, I should say, and a Fox News, senior strategic analyst. General, always good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 This is interesting, this press conference, news conference that we saw by the defense secretary and the chairman of the Joint Chiefs. It appears that they obviously they knew they were going to get some questions on this issue of woke training to the extent that it does or does not exist in our military.\par \par \uc2 What did you think of their answers and their willingness to engage on that issue today?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Well, I think the secretary of defense has been pretty clear on this, during testimony and also at the press conference today. You know, it's not going to be a part of the United States military. We're not going to teach you, we're not going to embrace you, we're not going to support it.\par \par \uc2 There's no policy advocating that. And that also implies that if it's going on someplace, he expects his commanders to knock it off. It's that simple. And not anybody that's been around in the military for any bit of time knows full well that for the military to be effective on the battlefield, to advance into enemy fire, despite casualties, in spite your personal fear, the only way we can do that successfully is we must have cohesion. We have to be together.\par \par \uc2 Anything that divides us and separates us, we have always been against, and will always continue to be against that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: That's my sense of it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And I think that you know, everybody in every institution has to keep their mission first. And the mission is very clear for the United States military to protect the United States of America, and to do it in a unified way, not in a way that, you know, leads us to sort of be in different factions within the military and have them, you know, commenting to any extent on how they feel about that is a question, I think that's come up a lot lately.\par \par \uc2 And we certainly hope that, that mission is clear and carried out for the benefit of the whole country, as I know you do.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you also about \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2 , and this pipeline issue, because it has surfaced today that the German Chancellor Angela Merkel spoke on the phone with President Putin of Russia. And all of this happens as they -- as the United States expresses his desire on behalf of President Biden to make sure that Ukraine is protected in this arrangement that allows Russia to become so dominant in the energy supply for Europe.\par \par \uc2 What do you think about where this is right now? Tell us?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes. Well, these responses, I think are really quite feckless that what we're trying to dress this thing up. And the fact is this is just really bad public policy that we have here.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the idea that Putin during a crisis or a conflict, would not use the pipeline and the gas distribution into Europe, as economic and strategic leverage is, is an absurdity. And the fact that somehow we're going to constrain him from doing that, having given up any leverage already, it's just not reality.\par \par \uc2 Listen, this is a pattern we have seen among the Europeans in the past. Time and time again, not all the time, but far too often, they will put economic and commercial gain ahead of national security.\par \par \uc2 We've seen it in the nuclear deal, and an insistence on continuing to do business with Iran, ahead of all the dangers that Iran poses. That's what this is about for Merkel. She saw the commercial benefit and she wrote off the opposition to it within her own party, within her own country, in terms of what the implications were for national security.\par \par \uc2 Not just for Germany, but for all of Europe, particularly Eastern Europe, and certainly for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. We know that the Ukraine President Zelensky is going to visit with President Biden on August 30th. And we can just imagine that he's going to have a lot to say to him about the lifting of sanctions that allowed this deal to go through, because it's tough to put that genie back in the bottle at this point.\par \par \uc2 General, thank you very much. It's always good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, great talking to you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 How about these alleged shoplifters? I'd say it's pretty clear they're shoplifters, but we don't know their identity. Just casually helping themselves to pile on (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 This used to happen during looting, now, it just happens on a normal day. Brazen crime caught on camera, Greg Gutfeld joins us with a special appearance here on THE STORY taking on this. Oh my, here he comes.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is not another brazing crime caught on camera. Police say that a man hit a New York City police officer over the head with a bottle in an unprovoked attack.\par \par \uc2 It happened just before 5:00 a.m. on Sunday in Brooklyn. Police zoomed in on the body cam video to show it this is a bottle in the guy's hand. The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officer says that -- the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  says that the officer needed stitches and staples in his head neck, but he's going to be OK. So, I guess that's all on the line of duty these days for these New York City police officers.\par \par \uc2 And in California, police say that shoplifters didn't even bother to run. They just gathered up all this stuff, and then they walked out of the store carrying piles of clothes.\par \par \uc2 This video was posted three days ago. It shows the incident in Granada Hills, outside Los Angeles. One officer blames Prop 47 to measure California voters passed that lowers the jail sentences for shoplifting under $950 worth of items. So, you can bet that those guys probably got about $949.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Greg Gutfeld, our great special guest today. Host of "GUTFELD!" and co-host of "THE FIVE" Good to see you again, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I mean, this is the -- it looks like looting video, but it's not. It's just like a no -- there's people inside the store and there's no looting. It's just brazing.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And it's -- as if it's in slow motion. First, you have to understand, it's like almost impossible to steal $950 worth of merchandise from T.J. Maxx when you can buy a suit for $10.\par \par \uc2 So, that -- I mean, are you do see how much they are carrying them, and they're not even like worried. And everybody's here like, yes, what the hell? This is -- this is insane. And so, you have that coupled with the other crime, which is this brazen attack on a police officer, these things, they point to a great -- a greater problem, and that is it is known you can do it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It is absolutely known, there is an acceptance. There's an -- there's an abdication of civil society. No one is interested anymore in this. It's frightening. You know, we're talking in the break about how does an empire fall? It doesn't fall at once.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It just -- it's like the frog in the -- in the -- what do you call?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's right.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Pan of water.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Slowly boiling. And every day, if you live in a city, you can feel that boil. You can just feel it changing and changing. And everybody you talk to, Democrat or Republican are against crime. They really are. Everybody honestly believes that crime is wrong. So how is this happening?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the crazy thing is you have these DAs across the country. You know, we did guest gone a lot in Los Angeles. Now, you've got probably the likely new D.A. in New York City, Alvin Bragg, who is joining this sort of cadre of prosecutors across the country who are lowering the offences.\par \par \uc2 So, for instance, Alvin Bragg says that, you know, he would decline to prosecute any of the following: turnstile jumping, trespassing, driving with a suspended license, violations like disorderly conduct, consensual trades -- sex trade, and outdated offences, or resisting arrest. So, he says, we're not going to prosecute that.\par \par \uc2 So, the poor police officers on the street, they watch the guys jump the turnstile, they go, you know, we can't, yes, we really -- we can't really pursue.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And you know what the problem with that -- the idea of low-level crime, there is a range in there that is low level, turnstile jumping, and there were some other pointless ones, even sex -- consensual sex trade. That's not like attacking the police officer, right?\par \par \uc2 They are putting it together and then, so, that allows the argument, it's like camouflage. They're camouflaging and the actual really destructive legal -- the offenses by putting them in with less kind of inflammatory law-breaking, and it's all just a game.\par \par \uc2 This is the elite activist class at work. They're in our legal system, they're in human resources, they are everywhere, they are deciding now that you can completely remake a country without ever firing a gun. You just handicap -- you handicap law and order, you handicap politicians by marrying the idea of racism to law and order.\par \par \uc2 So, that if you are interested in being safe or having a safe community, there's somehow some racist part behind that. And ironically, it's hurting minority neighborhoods worse.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And the ideas that if you don't press offenses against any of these things, right, that somehow you're going to make that person's life better.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Because they're not going to go to jail. Now, you know, I think about the pharmacy that we had, that video a while back, the guy was just like clearing the shelves and putting it all in the bag. And so, he walked out the door, he rode his bike down the aisle, and he got away with it.\par \par \uc2 So, these guys look at that and they go, well, you can do that. So, why wouldn't you do that?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's not going to stop until something ugly happens, and then whoever defends themselves is the one who goes to jail.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, you're right. All right, good to see you