partment of Justice. I spoke with the senator just moments ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Senator, good to have you with us once again. Thank you. So you basically accused him of lying it before Congress. Now, you've sent a letter to the Department of Justice asking for them to do an inquiry. So the basis, as I understand it, is that he claimed quite clearly that there was never any US funded gain of function research that was happening at the Wuhan lab. And you say that, that they're being sort of cute with the definition of what gain of function is. Can you explain that in basic terms to our audience?\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Well, I think it's important to know that Dr. Fauci has a self interest in obscuring any relationship or responsibility for anything done in the Wuhan lab. Without question, the evidence we presented in committee yesterday shows that the NIH was funding research in Wuhan. We showed the exact numerical series of numbers that goes with the grant number. So yes, they were funding the Wuhan lab.\par \par \uc2 The second question is, whether or not the research was gain of function. The NIH actually defines gain of function, and I read that definition to him, but he didn't listen. The definition is that any kind of animal virus that occurs in nature that affects animals only, or infects animals only, if you recombine it or mutated or adapted in the lab with other viruses, so it has characteristics that change it from being an animal only virus to being a virus that now can infect humans that you've gained in function. You've gained pathogenesis or you've gained virulence. You've made it more dangerous.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Without question, this is what happened in the Wuhan lab. We have a number of professors who have said so. We put the quote up from a professor of 30 years at Rutgers, a tenured professor who said that what they were doing in the lab in Wuhan was the epitome of gain of function. But you have to realize that Dr. Fauci is not disinterested.\par \par \uc2 If this virus came from the lab and it becomes clear that he was funding dangerous research, moral culpability, responsibility attaches to him, and he does not want that. So he's shirking that. But in so doing, he's not being honest with the American people or Congress.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So he claims that you lied about him. He says that there was no gain of function research that was funded by US dollars. And of course, you know, the scariest aspect of this would be if there was any link between the dollars that went from the United States to potentially the actual virus that leaked. And he at one point said to you, is that what you're suggesting that I, you know, was connected to? Is that what you're suggesting?\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Well, he once again set something up that we weren't suggesting. He was saying that the viruses discussed in the paper that we presented from Dr. Shi, the bat scientist, that those viruses were not COVID-19. Well, we are not alleging that. We're not alleging that Dr. Shi wrote a paper talking about how she created COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: In 2017.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: We're saying that she was doing gain of function research, the paper proves it. And you know, the one way to get to the bottom of this, Fox could do this. You could bring in 10 disinterested scientists in this field and ask them is this gain of function research. There are a bunch of them that will speak on this, but there are many of them who are frightened because their livelihood depends on NIH dollars and Dr. Fauci controls that money.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: But there are a number of them that have been discussing this all the way back to 2002. The professor that we brought the quote from, he says he's been interested in gain of function research and limitations that we should have on it since 2002.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So let me ask you this, 