e USSR.\par \par \uc2 People today haven't updated. They haven't gotten the memo.\par \par \uc2 You know, we were nice to China in the 80s. We wanted them on side versus the Soviets, and that made sense then.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: But then, as soon as the wall falls, as soon as the Soviet empire is defeated, we don't course correct. And you have politicians in the 90s, Republicans and Democrats by the way, who just continue to enable the rise of China, right?\par \par \uc2 This is Joe Biden saying China is never going to be a threat, man. Come on? You know, meanwhile we outsource millions of jobs to China. We have completely lopsided trade deals with them and for 20 years, we just enabled their rise, and a lot of Republican politicians still today don't even understand that, which is why they just kind of repeat mindlessly, you know, we need free trade. You couldn't possibly have a tariff against China.\par \par \uc2 You know, think about all the higher prices people would pay for toasters in this country or something as if that were the core problem.\par \par \uc2 So, I think politicians, they're just not in the business of thinking for themselves.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: No, they're like a ghost ship that just, you know, the crew is gone, but the rigging is set and they just kind of sail until they hit the rocks, but I think we have hit the rocks at this point.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: We've hit the rocks and you know, it's going to be a brand new decade, I think. We actually take back this country this decade or it's gone forever.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Blake Masters, a very smart guy. More than an hour long conversation, streaming now on foxnation.com.\par \par \uc2 Well, the White House is demanding mandatory vaccinations, but one man plans to stop it. We'll tell you if he can do that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, the D.N.C. has decided it's going to control what you get over private text messages. Repeat after us, "This is not East Germany. This is not East Germany." But it's getting close.\par \par \uc2 Chip Roy is a Republican Member of Congress from Texas, he has thought a lot about this. He joins us to respond.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thanks so much for coming on. Where are you on this question of the D.N.C. controlling what texts you can receive?\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY (R-TX): Yes, well, that's obviously patently absurd, Tucker, and this whole issue of the vaccines hits very close to home for me.\par \par \uc2 My father in 1949, was seven years old, he was diagnosed with polio, and my grandmother was nursing my grandfather who was sick of cancer. He died that fall and then raised my dad as a single mom. Nursed him to health and he lived his whole life ravaged with polio.\par \par \uc2 I'm grateful to have a vaccine for me, vaccine for my son, and my daughter. I believe in vaccines and I'll be damned if I am going to allow Democrats like you're describing come in and start calling you an anti-vaxx hack, or call me an anti-vaxxer because I dare to believe that it is our duty as Americans, our patriotic duty, to make decisions for ourselves, not have Democrats looking at our text messages, not having them try to shame the American people into taking a vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Now, we've got the H.H.S. Secretary, as you know, Becerra saying, quote, "The Federal government has spent trillions of dollars to try and keep Americans live during this pandemic, so it is absolutely the government's business to know who is vaccinated. It's taxpayer business." That is what is -- this is the danger we face as Americans.\par \par \uc2 The forgotten men and women out here who are getting rolled over by the power of government, but also the oligopoly of these corporate cronies who are trying to use all of these to fatten their own wallets.\par \par \uc2 Heck, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  said they expect to get $15 billion out of these vaccines in their announcement in February. Our job is to stand up and say no, and I'm proud right now, one of my staffers just texted me, there's a Sheriff in one of the counties I represent in Texas who just told a crowd, many of these vaccine Feds come up to your property, you kick them off and I'll be there to defend you and back you up.\par \par \uc2 And we need to stand up strong for the liberties that protected us as Americans and stop all of this ridiculousness where the Federal government is telling us our children have to be masked, our small businesses have to be closed, while Amazon makes another hundred billion dollars. We have to have --\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Chip Roy, we're almost out of time. I never say this to politicians, but God bless you for saying that. It is really inspiring to hear an office holder make those points because they are all true and it's brave of you. Thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 We are out of time, sadly and unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 ROY: Thanks, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Sean Hannity takes over right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020210713eh7c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  And \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  Point Fingers On Human Rights Issues; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  And \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  Say Progress Made But Divisions Remain; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Confronts \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  About Recent Ransomware Attacks; Texas Governor Commits To Wall Expansion; Migrant Groups Cross Into U.S.; \uc2 Taliban\uc2  Seizing Territory In Afghanistan As U.S. Troop Withdrawal Continues; Israel Launches New Airstrikes Against Militant Sites In Gaza Strip As Palestinians Send Fire Carrying Balloons Across Border; Catholic Bishops In America To Debate Providing Sacrament Of Holy Communion To U.S. Politicians Who Support Abortion Rights; Reporting Indicates Possible Bipartisan Agreement On Infrastructure Bill\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Juan Williams, Trey Gowdy, Bill Melugin,  David Spunt, Jennifer Griffin, Ben Domenech, Trey Yingst, Lauren Green \par \uc2 7,464 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 June 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Living 10 to 15 minutes of your entire family, that sounds tough. I don't know about you, I'd move. No offense mom and dad, I love you guys.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: We love all of you. That's it for us. "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Hi, Dana. Thank you. Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, President Biden is on his way back to the White House following his meeting with Russian leader \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in Geneva. We'll have complete coverage of that one-on-one summit in the mean -- in a moment.\par \par \uc2 But first, we're looking at some other big stories. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  signals interest rates may go up amid concerns over inflation. The governor of Texas is talking about what he wants to do with the border wall.\par \par \uc2 And overseas, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is taking over parts of Afghanistan even before U.S. troops leave.\par \par \uc2 But back to our top story, President Biden says he did what he came to do, describing his summit with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  as positive and without any strident action. Putin says it was constructive. President Biden says the last thing Putin wants is a Cold War.\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  does insist his country is not responsible for cyberattacks against American institutions.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Peter Doocy traveling with the president starts us off tonight from Switzerland. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. The world's two biggest nuclear powers got together and agreed to send ambassadors back to each other's countries. And that's about the only tangible thing that came out of this much hyped summit that only wound up lasting about three hours and 15 minutes, even the White House officials had us bracing for a meeting that could go four or five hours or longer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It was kind of after two hours there, we looked at each other like OK, what next?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Next comes a wait.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We'll find out within the next six months to a year whether or not we actually have a strategic dialogue that matters.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: On cybersecurity, President Biden says he gave Putin a 16 item do not hack list.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I talked about the proposition that certain critical infrastructure should be off limits to attack, period, by cyber or any other means.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Putin alleges that's misplaced.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , RUSSIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): But as for American sources, they've said that most of the cyberattacks in the world are carried out from the cyber realm of the United States.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The President says he did not threaten retribution for ransomware but posed an interesting question.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, how would you feel if ransomware took on the pipelines from your oil fields? He said it would matter.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Biden also brought up President Putin's political rival \uc2 Alexei Navalny\uc2 , who remains in prison.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I made it clear to President Putin, I will continue to raise issues of fundamental human rights.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Putin then says he accused the U.S. of prosecuting people for political reasons.\par \par \uc2 PUTIN (through translator): As for who is killing whom and throwing whom in jail, people came to the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2  with political demands. 400 people, over 400 people had criminal charges placed on them. They faced prison sentences.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Apples and oranges according to Biden.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think that's a -- that's ridiculous comparison.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Apparently, the two got along well enough.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The tone of the entire meetings, I guess it was total of four hours was good, positive.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And Putin complimented Biden as a statesman.\par \par \uc2 PUTIN: I'd say he's very constructive. He's very balanced just the way that I expected. He's very experienced, you can tell that at first glance.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But tensions will remain.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This is not a kumbaya moment as you used to say back in the 60s United States, like, let's hug and love each other.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Biden's next big diplomatic challenge getting China to cooperate with the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You've spoken many times about how you've spent perhaps more time with President Xi than any other world leaders. So, is there going to become a time where you might call him, old friend to old friend and ask him to open up China to the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  investigators who are trying to get to the bottom of COVID-19?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Let's get something straight, we know each other well, we're not old friends. It's just pure business.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That might be news to President Xi, because when Biden visited China as vice president a few years ago, he spoke fondly in formal remarks about his friendship with Xi and Xi reciprocated, telling Biden, let me again, warmly welcome you to China, my old friend, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy in Geneva. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel early Ben Domenech, publisher of the Federalist. Juan Williams, Fox News analyst, contributor and \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , former congressman from South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Ben, your thoughts about what we heard today and what has come out of this?\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, PUBLISHER AND CO-FOUNDER, THE FEDERALIST: Well, unfortunately, I think that what we heard today is an indication that, once again, the Biden administration is going to give up the store when it comes to all of these different aspects of American policy.\par \par \uc2 And my question about their foreign policy in this early going, really is what are we getting back in return? When we give them the Nord Stream pipeline, when we eliminate the sanctions against the people involved in that, when we set these arbitrary red lines that will obviously not be honored when it comes to Russian cyberattacks, when we engage in restarting the Iran deal or any other number of foreign policy decisions, what is it that America is getting in return?\par \par \uc2 I realized that the criticism of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's administration was that his approach to foreign policy was far too transactional. There's some validity to that. I think that American interests include an ideological interest in favor of freedom.\par \par \uc2 But there's also a transactional element to foreign policy, and one that we ought to respect. This is not in any way a situation that I think redounds to the American benefit. And if the outcome of this is that we have a stern talking to about the potential death of \uc2 Alexei Navalny\uc2 , we have some warnings that are given to the media and the like from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and we have them oing and eyeing over his gift of American made aviators. We're not really emerging from this having achieved anything in the American interest\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Juan, you know, they had the dueling press conferences of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's going for a lot longer and then, President Biden's, they were not together in answering questions.\par \par \uc2 You know, the build up to this was big. Foreign policy experts were waiting for this, like, you know, get around the T.V., get a bowl of Cheez-its and watch this thing. But what happened, did it -- did it deliver?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think from the Biden administration perspective, Bret, it did deliver because all they wanted to do was hammer in guardrails to set what they want, which is a more stable and predictable relationship here, I'm using their bare words on this, after the first very tense six months of their ties with Russia being strained.\par \par \uc2 And as you know, overall, I think the Biden administration has made it very clear, they view all world affairs at this point as a matter of democracies versus autocracies, China would be number one on that list.\par \par \uc2 But you know, Russia has the power, you know, in terms of distracting and disrupting everything from the hacking to involvement in Middle East peace deals, you know, aid to Iran, to Syria. Even in terms of Eastern Europe, Europe, with Belarus. And what's been going on in the Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So, I think they wanted to just kind of say, here is what our American interest are. Here are lines that we are setting now in this conversation, especially on cyber security. I think that was probably the number one was in Biden's words at the press conference, he said he let Putin know that the U.S. has tremendous cyber capability if that's the game he wants to play.\par \par \uc2 And I think again, it's the cybersecurity, it's human rights, no revoking the Magnitsky Act (PH) on Russian oligarchs. And then finally, I think you have to think about the global stability, you know, stay out of our business.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right, Trey, what about giving a list of entities, facilities that are off limits to cyberattacks? I mean, are there others that are OK to attack?\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, apparently, that would include our 2016 election. You remember, Bret, our country has been torn apart for five years because of an allegation that a Republican candidate colluded with Putin. We don't have to wonder whether Putin colluded with Putin. You have the guy in front of you who tried to sow the seeds of discord in our democracy. Did you bring it up? How about the indicted? How about the people Mueller indicted, the Russians under indicted -- under indictment? I wonder if that came up.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, if you got a list of things that are off limits, then you necessarily have to have a list of things that are within limits. And I'm just wondering where our election structure falls? In limits or out of limits?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You mentioned, Ben, the kind of back and forth with the press. Here's a little clip of that with President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PUTIN (through translator): We know that there were cyberattacks on a pipeline company in the United States. What are the state organs of Russia have to do with that? We are encountering the same threats.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: What happens if that ransomware outfit were sitting in Florida or Maine, and took action as I said, on their single lifeline to their economy, oil? That'd be devastating. And they're like, you could see them kind of go, oh, we do that, but like, whoa. So, it's in -- it's in -- and everybody's interested these things be acted on.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He denied any involvement in cyberattack, he downplayed human rights abuses, he refused to say \uc2 Alexei Navalny\uc2 's name. So, how does that account to a constructive meeting as a president? --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If you don't understand that, you're in the wrong business.\par \par \uc2 To be a good reporter, you got to be negative, you got to have a negative view of life, OK? It seems to me, the way you all -- you never ask a positive question. Why, in fact, having agreement -- we'll find out. We have an agreement to work on a major arms control agreement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A lot of back and forth there got testy at times, Ben?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: You know, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has a long history both in the Senate and during his tenure as vice president of getting testy when he is challenged in some of these areas. But it is an aspect of his character that only seems to come out more in this day and age.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, all these questions are very valid, and ones that ought to be answered responsibly by the president. I think they would be directed at the president, any party engaged in this kind of interaction.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, I don't think that we got any real answers from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I don't think we're going to get any real answers from this administration. Because unfortunately, this meeting only redounded to the benefit of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . It did not benefit us in any clear way. I have no idea what we got from this.\par \par \uc2 Putin got ambassador's back, he got to laugh at the press leaving the room, he got to assert that there were certain areas that were off limits and on limits when it came to the ransomware attacks and the likes as Trey said.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately, I think that that is really an example of weakness when it comes to our approach to this. We never should have had this meeting in the first place given everything that's happened in recent months.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Juan, I mean, just respond to that.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think it's important we had that meeting because of the tensions that have existed for the past six months. Because I think you want to avoid, you know, this is an old sort of archaic term, but Cold War. We're not interested in having any battles with Russia.\par \par \uc2 Look, we hold the aces in this, let's just -- I mean, I'm not trying to be, you know, overly boastful about the United States. But our economy is 15 times the size of Russia, Canada has a bigger economy than Russia. Their economy is basically a gas station, you know, based on oil and gas and that's a declining asset in the modern world.\par \par \uc2 So, when he does something, he is just basically a disrupter. And what you have, the President of the United States holding all the aces in the conversation saying, is here -- here's the deal, Mr. Putin, we'll see where this goes. But we're telling you, we don't want to fight with you, we want a stable relationship, don't antagonize.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Juan, that's all the more reason that we didn't have to meet with him. That's all the more reason to say that this wasn't something that was necessary.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Last word is a country within an economy the size of Italy ripped apart the greatest super power on the face of the earth for five years. And there needs to be a consequence for that.\par \par \uc2 For Biden, it was a brave bipartisan moment, both Republican and Democrat presidents don't like CNN, so I'm marveled at the bipartisanship there for just a second.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, we'll leave it there. Panel, we'll see in a bit.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we go live to the southern border to hear what the governor of Texas is going to do to get the wall built.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): Texas taxpayers are having to step up so that we as a state can protect our citizens.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Republican governor of Texas says he will lead the effort to secure a wall along his state's southern border with Mexico. Governor Abbott talking about how he's going to do that a short time ago.\par \par \uc2 This comes as the blazing heatwave in the southwest is doing little to curb the influx of illegal immigrants into the U.S.\par \par \uc2 We have new video tonight of dozens of Ecuadorian migrants crossing over. Correspondent Bill Melugin reports tonight from La Joya. Good evening, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening to you. Just a short time ago, Texas Governor Greg Abbott made a pretty major announcement, telling the public that Texas is going to begin the process of building its own border wall down here.\par \par \uc2 He says the state has to do it because, in his words, the federal government has completely abandoned its responsibility here at the border. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): We know that temporary barriers and fences won't be enough to slow the flow of the record amount of illegal immigration that's taking place. That's why today, we are announcing that Texas will build a border wall in our state to help secure our border.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And the governor says the state has identified hundreds of miles of both private and public land they believe will work for this new border wall. On top of that, we have been witnessing tons of activity down here. This announcement coming as this border surge is not slowing down, and there are new bipartisan calls for V.P. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to come down here and see this surge herself.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice-over): La Joya, Texas, early this morning. A scene we have witnessed play out here every single day. Like clockwork, large groups of migrants crossed into the United States when the Texas heat is tamer. Many of them, women with children, and infants.\par \par \uc2 This group of about 50 seeks out Border Patrol, walking down local roads until they're eventually apprehended.\par \par \uc2 Late Tuesday afternoon, we witnessed this group of about 30 migrants emerge from the brush behind us, also in La Joya. Many of them appearing to be exhausted in the blistering sun. They sat down, waiting for an overstretched border patrol to find them. There were no agents in the immediate area as the agency continues to be overwhelmed here in the Rio Grande Valley.\par \par \uc2 Some of the migrants told me, they never heard \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' message to them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TEXT\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: You didn't hear the message from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to not come here to the border?\par \par \uc2 TEXT\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, we didn't hear. We didn't hear anything.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: This, as Texas Democratic Congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  penned this later to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , urging her to visit the border with him to see the impacts of the current surge. The crisis, he says, is only getting worse.\par \par \uc2 And today, Florida's Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  announcing he is going to be sending Florida law enforcement to Texas and Arizona to help supplement their border security.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS\uc2  (R-FL): The state of Florida is answering the call. We believe that securing the southern border is important for our country.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Meanwhile, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  announced he will visit the border on June 30th with Governor Greg Abbott.\par \par \uc2 The former president saying \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's handling of the border has turned it into an "unmitigated disaster zone". And new disturbing information from Border Patrol. The agency reporting this week, right here in the Rio Grande Valley, they arrested a convicted child rapist from Peru and a Mexican national with the conviction for sexual assault of a child. Both men taken into custody after illegally crossing into the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And Texas Governor Greg Abbott went on to mention that the state legislature here in Texas has allocated about a billion dollars for border security. He says that is not fair whatsoever to taxpayers here in the state of Texas having to pay for the job of what he says the federal government should be doing.\par \par \uc2 He says the first step to correcting this is building their own border wall, and he says he wants his local troopers, local deputies down here to start making more arrests of people as soon as they come across the border. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin live in La Joya, Texas. Bill, thanks.\par \par \uc2 A bipartisan group of 20 senators today saying it will support a framework for infrastructure investment. The five-year plan calls for $953 billion in total spending. Of that, $580 billion in new spending.\par \par \uc2 Those senators agreeing to pay for the plan without raising taxes, and it will repurpose unused COVID relief money, again, it's 20, it's not 60, but a bipartisan group agreeing with that.\par \par \uc2 The federal government is investigating how the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  manages its grant program. That probe comes amid the controversy of the Wuhan Institute of Virology in China and its possible role in igniting the COVID-19 virus.\par \par \uc2 Fox News has confirmed an inspector general spokesperson's concerns regarding oversight of \uc2 NIH\uc2  funds, and that the agency is monitoring the issue and considers it a high priority. A report is expected sometime in 2022