nd your take on where we are right now.\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Well, certainly, this is a complex typical situation, you know, to be sure. Just a couple of points to understand, what \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is doing? They're doing Iran's bidding here.\par \par \uc2 Iran is the chief sponsor of what is taking place here. They use the wind fall money of the 2015 nuclear deal, which was $140 billion to fund their proxy wars and their -- fund their proxy terrorists in the region. It is likely that -- if not all, but most of the rockets and missiles that are raining down on Israel from the three terrorist organizations who are firing them, all came from that wind fill deal.\par \par \uc2 This is Iran calling the shots here. Why was there nothing like this during the Trump administration? Why didn't we see rockets raining down on Israel during that period? Because Iran knew that the Trump administration would hold them accountable. And likely sanctions would increase on them and the pressure would increase on them.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: What I fault the Biden administration for -- I do support them in making the strong statement that Israel has a right to defend itself. I agree with that. This bipartisan support in the Congress for that as well despite the loud voices from the Democrat minority here.\par \par \uc2 But the fact -- the facts are really that Iran is causing this mischief.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: And they see advantage here. They believe that what is taking place in Vienna is key to them. And President Biden is not even talking about Iran. Iran is the sponsor of everything that is taking place, but there is no discussion of it. And that's by design, Martha because they want the Vienna talks to be separate.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: From anything that's taking place here. And that's -- that is absolutely irresponsible behavior. What's on the table in Vienna, I know for a fact, that discussions that are taking place there, that are being reported that the United States is negotiating, eliminating the sanctions that Trump imposed on terrorism that Iran sponsors. And also eliminating the sanctions that Trump imposed on missile development.\par \par \uc2 Now, can you imagine that? Based on what has taken place now that we're going to give Iran more money as a result of this potential deal to further destabilize and undermine the Middle East, which is fundamentally what is taking place here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we're certainly seeing a clear demonstration of what that would -- what havoc that would wreak, to lift all of that. We're seeing it play out in live time right now on the Israel-Gaza border. And the other question is whether or not it makes any sense at all to lift sanctions, to lift things that have been put in place to affect behavior before you have some sort of agreement. You would think that those things would be something that would come after we saw behavior change.\par \par \uc2 General Keane, thank you. Very good to see you as always. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, Great --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, a Biden-backed panel is studying whether or not court packing is ok. They just wrapped up their first meeting. They've got 36 academics on that panel including his campaign attorney, Bob Bauer, is part of that team.\par \par \uc2 So, this happens as we learn that there's going to be a key abortion case heard at the Supreme Court. So, that has thrown gasoline on the push to add justices and to do it quickly. Shannon Bream is next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JERROLD NADLER (D-NY): As some people say we're packing the court. We're not packing it, we're unpacking it. Senator McConnell and the Republicans packed the court over the last couple of years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, President Biden once called packing the court a bone-headed idea. But the first meeting of his commission to decide if maybe it's not such a dumb idea after all has just wrapped up for the day moments ago.\par \par \uc2 It is comprised of 36 academics, one of whom led his -- was involved in his campaign as his attorney, Bob Bauer. And you can bet that with perhaps the most significant abortion case since Roe v. Wade now on the docket for the court this fall, this idea is picking up a huge head of steam.\par \par \uc2 Shannon Bream is here. But first, correspondent David Spunt takes us inside that meeting today. Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon. Bob Bauer is actually the co-chair of that commission. It's made up, as you said, 36 people, Republican and Democratic scholars that are going to be meeting for several times over the next few months to specifically talk about many different issues.\par \par \uc2 The one getting the most attention though, Martha is specifically potentially adding more justices on the Supreme Court. Should there continue to be nine, should there be more, perhaps less.\par \par \uc2 The Constitution does not establish a set number of justices. Numbers have changed but it's been nine justices since 1869.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOB BAUER, CO-CHAIR, SUPREME COURT COMMISSION: We really do not believe that we can do this work without the benefit of the broadest diversity of perspectives on the issues that we're going to be considering.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice-over): In 1937, Franklin D. Roosevelt wanted 15 justices on the court. The plan was called court-packing. He wanted to push progressive policies, believed this was the way to do it, FDR's plan failed.\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned, Martha, in 1983 that senator from Delaware even called it -- well, here, listen again.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It was a bonehead idea. It was a terrible, terrible mistake to make.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Look, at potentially changing the life tenure aspect of the court, I spoke to a constitutional law professor who himself has argued in front of the court 19 times about this commission.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SMITH, DISTINGUISHED VISITOR FROM PRACTICE, GEORGETOWN UNIVERSITY LAW CENTER: There is a long tradition in Washington of appointing a commission when you try to do something that it's not actually doing anything. And, you know, this looks like it may be that --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): The commission will put out a final report by November. Important to point out they're not going to be actually putting in recommendations to make a change on the court, it takes both Houses of Congress. Also, the president and House Speaker Nancy Pelosi has said that she really has no appetite to make any changes to the number of justices as far as legislation is concerned. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: David, thank you. David Spunt in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Shannon Bream, anchor of "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT" and Fox chief legal correspondent joining me now.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, great to have you with us and it's interesting to hear the end of David's report there on the reasons why this might look unlikely based on what an enormous move it would be. But there is no doubt that there will be an enormous amount of pressure headed in this direction coming from a lot of different sides.\par \par \uc2 Here is -- here is Joy Reid talking about the significance of this moment. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT, MSNBC: The conservative-led Supreme Court has agreed to take up Mississippi's restrictive abortion law, and women's reproductive rights appear to be in serious, serious danger.\par \par \uc2 It's straight out of the handmaid's tale with a Gilead-style government right here in America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This case is a big deal, Shannon. Explain to us how you see this moment.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, it is. I think that might have been a little overstatement of the case. It's not as if this case is decided in favor of what? Pro-life advocates would like to see that abortion would go away in this country. That is not what this is going to be, it's not a direct run at row.\par \par \uc2 Although we know a number of governors out there have said over the last couple of years, they're actually letting their legislatures pass laws that would be a direct run at row, and they're signing them specifically knowing they're going to be legally challenged and go to the court.\par \par \uc2 So, now, we have this case out of Mississippi which bans most abortions, there are exceptions after 15 weeks. So, that is the very narrow question now before the court. This is what the court has said they'll to look at, whether all pre-viability bans on a -- on elective abortions violate the constitution -- so, it's a narrow question and we know the science and progress and technology and medical advancements.\par \par \uc2 The viability has gotten earlier and earlier. It's considered now to be around 23-24 weeks of pregnancy for many unborn babies are able to be saved at that point. And as the science changes, a lot since Roe v. Wade 1973, there are new questions the court's got to consider.\par \par \uc2 So, what we know is that at least four of them had to vote to get this on the docket. What we now have to think about is where the final votes were come down for those nine that currently sit on the court.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's going to be a fascinating case to watch. Just also to mention some news out of Texas today, Governor Abbott signed a new law that would ban some abortions as early as six weeks. Tell us a little bit about that development out of Texas.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, not surprising. As I said, a number of these governors in red states who are conservative, very unabashedly pro-life have said, we're going to pass these measures, so, they go to the courts and they get vetted. And they think they have a better chance at chipping away at what Roe did back in 1973, because of all of the advances and changes in science and medicine and technology.\par \par \uc2 So, it seems that, that is Governor Abbott's aim and his strategy as well. But again, this court case that they're probably going to hear in the fall maybe early next year is one that will shape the contours of where this conversation goes. And pro-choice advocates are very worried because states across the country over the last couple of years have passed a number of similar measures. They almost immediately end up in court many of them are tied up there right now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, Shannon, what's the likelihood, real quick, of there being another justice on the court before that case is heard?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You know, we always do this trying to bring the tea leaves. Justice Breyer, there's a lot of pressure on him. But we see when it comes from the left, look how that turned out with the late Justice Ginsburg, she dug in her heels and said there's no way I'm leaving.\par \par \uc2 I tend to get the same feeling from Justice Breyer he's giving us no signals he would leave this year.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Nor is any other justice.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, I was suggesting that, you know, that they could add more justices to the court. I mean, that would be a very tight time frame.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's -- well -- listen. Yes, it would. But this commission has six months to make recommendations.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And they're going to look at term limits and all kinds of things.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Shannon, thank you. Great to see you. Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You too.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, mask hysteria hits the House. Several defiant Republicans now fighting Pelosi-backed rules that don't line up with the president's own CDC. The breaking details ahead of a key vote. We'll show you what's going on in there on THE STORY, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRANHOLM, UNITED STATES ENERGY SECRETARY: We have um doubled down on ensuring that there's an ability to truck oil in, gas in, but it's the pipe is the best way to go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, the pipe's the best way to go. Energy Secretary Jennifer Granholm now clarifying the comment that she made last week during the \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2  shutdown because Republicans were asking why President Biden canceled the Keystone XL pipeline if as his energy secretary says the pipe is the best way to go if you want to get oil from point A to point B. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. BILL JOHNSON (R-OH): Politics, because it was clearly not for climate benefits, because it's going to increase climate, it's going to increase carbon emissions.\par \par \uc2 GRANHOLM: No, it canceled them, and the president made this commitment because he believed that there are other ways to go, especially in promoting clean energy and cheap energy.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: OK, but you said that pipe -- but you said that pipe is the cleanest way -- the safest way to move fuel.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Granholm now says that pipe is sometimes the best way to go, depending on the location and the product pumping through it. OK.\par \par \uc2 So, live look now at Capitol Hill, where House Republicans are fighting for the right to ditch their masks. They say the policy should follow CDC guidance that fully vaccinated people do not need masks indoors in most cases.\par \par \uc2 But the Capitol doctor says the mass mandate stands until all members are fully vaccinated. Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram live on Capitol Hill, where they're always looking for something to argue about and that's the subject today. Hi, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. There is a rebellion brewing over the mask policy here at the Capitol. Many House Republicans want to see their colleagues the faces of their colleagues if they're vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 In a resolution up today in the House, minority leader Kevin McCarthy says the mask requirement on the House side of the Capitol, "is not based on the best available science, and is contrary to the CDC's own guidance." This leads to mass confusion at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN KENNEDY (R-LA): If I walk over to the House of Representatives, do I have to wear a mask?\par \par \uc2 DR. ROCHELLE WALENSKY, DIRECTOR, CENTERS FOR DISEASE CONTROL: Those are locally driven policies, but we felt that it was important for the science to -- for us to convey the science of what is safe for individuals.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: But --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Under the Constitution, the House can make its own rules. Capitol attending physician Brian Monahan just reiterated the mask requirement for the House floor. He says that's the only place where all members gather during the day, and Monahan says it's consistent with CDC policy. Democrats defend the mask rule.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PETE AGUILAR (D-CA): We're following the exact CDC guidelines which says workplaces can be unique and different based on their conditions. We would encourage our colleagues on the other side of the aisle to encourage their members to become vaccinated and --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Now, members are docked 500 bucks if they don't wear masks on the floor. The House fined four members yesterday: Beth Van Duyne, Brian Mast, Ralph Norman, and Marianette Miller-Meeks. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Chad, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Dr. Brett Giroir, former assistant health and human services secretary. Served on the Trump White House coronavirus task force. Admiral, always good to see you. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 So, I guess the first question is a simple straightforward one. Is it safe for the members of the House to go in there to do business without masks?\par \par \uc2 ADM. BRETT GIROIR, FORMER ASSISTANT SECRETARY, HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES: Absolutely 100 percent yes if you've been fully vaccinated. It's safe for you to be unmasked, and very importantly, it's safe for other people even if they haven't been vaccinated to be around you unmasked.\par \par \uc2 You are protected and you are safe around others. The rule of Speaker Pelosi is pure political theater, it is not based in science.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, if you've been vaccinated, you can't get the virus unless you have a breakthrough case, I guess. Right?\par \par \uc2 GIROIR: Very, very rare, but you're correct. You're highly, highly protected.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And can you have it and transmit it to someone else unknowingly if you have been vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 GIROIR: Incredibly small risk. And that's why the CDC came about last week and said that if you're vaccinated, it's OK to not wear a mask even in indoor spaces, even if there are other people who are unvaccinated because the risk of you transmitting is so small. So this gets back to it. Congress, you know who's vaccinated. If you've been vaccinated, you're at no risk to yourself or others. This really is just politics. It's not based on the science. And it's actually sending the wrong message to Americans.\par \par \uc2 Again, we should be inspiring confidence in the vaccine. And what Americans are seeing is that Biden administration is talking the talk, but the Speaker of the House is not walking the walk. I think this is dangerous to public health, it needs to be fixed.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, ANCHOR: I don't get it. I mean, they're trying to encourage everyone to get vaccinated, they should be really proud and happy to stand up and say, we as a body, we got vaccinated. And so, we want to show you how great life can be if you can take off the mask. It makes absolutely zero sense based on your first two answers to those questions, and the fact that they have been vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Here's Dr. Fauci on the misinterpretation of the mask mandate. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI, CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: I think people are misinterpreting thinking that this is a removal of a mask mandate for everyone. It's not. It's an assurance to those who are vaccinated, that they can feel safe, be they outdoors or indoors.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So who should, based on that, who should still be wearing a mask, doctor?\par \par \uc2 GIROIR: People who are unvaccinated if they're in public places, especially indoors, should wear a mask to protect themselves. People who have conditions like cancer or organ transplants should do it. And people who have just not been able to get the vaccine should do it. But anyone who's vaccinated, fully vaccinated, really does not need a mask and as cases go down further, and they will continue to plummet.\par \par \uc2 Then, no matter whether you're vaccinated or not, you're going to be masked free. We're not there yet, but we will get there as cases continue to go down.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, which is terrific. Great, great news. Thank you so much, Admiral Giroir. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GIROIR: Of course.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Coming up, we're going to talk to Andrew Giuliani on his quest to become the governor of New York. That is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: An 80-year-old veteran walked down the street at 4:45 in the afternoon, and he was hit by a stray bullet. His son tells the New York Post that violence in their Bronx neighborhood feels out of control. Surveillance video shows Andres Rodriguez, at the bottom left of the screen, as the bullet hits him.\par \par \uc2 His son says that his father is out of the hospital. He's recovering, but that they could use more police in their neighborhood to keep them safe. The city is sending more police into the subway system after a string of slashings and stabbings that seem to happen every day here in New York City. The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  says total crime is actually down compared to last year, but assaults and homicides are up dramatically. So were shootings, they're up 79% compared to May of last year.\par \par \uc2 My next guest says that he has a plan to crack down on crime. New York Gubernatorial candidate Andrew Giuliani joins me now. Andrew, great integrate to have you with us. Congratulations on your decision to run for governor of New York State. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW GIULIANI, NEW YORK GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Martha, thank you for having me. You know, I'm seeing those numbers right there, I knew before you ask a question, you know, it just shows me. I mean, thinking about that, the fact that they're up that much. In 2020, there were more shootings in 2020 than there were in 2018 and 2019 combined. So to see them spike even from 2020 now, I mean, it just tells you the path that we're on. And it's really, really scary.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It is really scary. And I have been in this area long enough to remember when your dad became mayor and cleaned up Times Square, cleaned up corruption, cleaned up the mafia presence in the city. He accomplished quite a bit and then became mayor of New York. One of the things that's being asked about you, is if you have the experience to take on the job of governor. You worked at the Trump White House for four years and a professional golfer before that.\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI: Well, I ran 