drew Giuliani, joining us today. So here's a question that coming up for you, is woke culture silencing single white men one of the most popular podcast hosts Joe Rogan? And America says yes, that's exactly what's happening. Will Cain weighs in, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: One of the most popular podcast hosts in America with a prediction about cancel culture's next victim. Speaking on his show, "The Joe Rogan Experience," Joe Rogan discussed the difficulty of making a movie today for the fear of getting canceled, and then he goes on to suggest who's next to be silenced. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE ROGAN, PODCAST HOST: You can never be woken up. That's the problem. It keeps going. It keeps going further and further, and further down the line. And if you get to the point where you capitulate what you agree to all these demands, it will eventually get to straight white men are not allowed to talk.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROGAN: Because it's your privilege to express yourself when other people of color have been silenced throughout history. It will be, you're not allowed to go outside because so many people were imprisoned for so many years. I mean, I'm not joking.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, I know.\par \par \uc2 ROGAN: It really we'll get there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joining me now for "Wednesday's with Will," Will Cain, Co-Host of "Fox and Friends Weekend." Will, great to see.\par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, "FOX AND FRIENDS WEEKEND" HOST: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to see you in the studio here. So, what do you think about that? You think his right?\par \par \uc2 CAIN: I think we have to start with the irony that a straight white male is speaking on national television right now, so not exactly silenced. But here's the thing, it's not hyperbolic either. Cancel culture, which is directly linked to woke-ism, is in the words of Alec Baldwin actually, and I think accurately, like a forest fire in constant need of fuel. And it always plays from the position of oppressive oppression and victimhood.\par \par \uc2 And I don't think Joe Rogan is wrong, that eventually it will encompass everyone and everything. I think I'd go further than Joe Rogan. It's not going to silence just single white men, it will silence anyone who dares walk outside of whatever is deemed popular and acceptable at the moment. Because I mean, look around. I mean, people of various minorities who do not control -- Senator Tim Scott has he not been essentially attempted to be canceled or slandered throughout time. So it's everyone.\par \par \uc2 It's everyone who holds a point of view this currently not acceptable to popular culture.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: There is pushback to this beginning. And one other example that I would just point out that just came out today, Mayor Lori Lightfoot of Chicago said that she only wants to do interviews with black and brown reporters. She said that they're underrepresented in the press pool in Chicago. I mean, I've never heard of such a thing.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the idea that we have a free press that everyone can ask questions, she's saying I only want to do interviews with black and brown reporters. But I think that there is some pushback. I think people are waking up to how twisted some of this really is.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Well, you know I've discussed that in the past, and I do think there is some awakening to what's happening. But at this point, it's so integrated into so many aspects of our culture. We've talked about integrating to the school system's popular culture, movies. It's so integrated into academia that it's going to take more than a little bit of an awakening from parents or anyone else out there.\par \par \uc2 You're going to have to seriously fight back because this type of indoctrination, that forces us to see each other through the prism of our skin color, that forces us into our tribes. It is interwoven at this point. It's going to have to be weeded out.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I wanted to ask you about Randi Weingarten because we've talked about that a lot too. And here's what happened when she was interviewed by \uc2 C-SPAN\uc2  and they opened the phone to some colors. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Public Schools have failed these children before on education basis.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The parochial schools and the private schools could navigate their way through this scenario. But the public schools didn't seem to be able to manage that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is pathetic how the teachers union and the government has failed our children. I don't see how you can sit up there and defend that.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Blistering. Wow.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Something she needs to hear, accurate, parents who have lived this experience. And if you played that clip for about 30 seconds longer, Randi Weingarten answer to that caller who was asking her about the way they betrayed children has been. Well, I think the union has done well for communities and families across this country.\par \par \uc2 And the first thing she pointed to as evidence of that was higher wages for teachers. The mask is off. The teachers union is not looking out for our children. I'm not talking about the mask wearing for COVID, but the mask that they put out in front to pretend that our children's interest was at the forefront of their priorities. It clearly is not, the last year's exposed that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Will, she got higher wages, that's for sure. Because you got $126 billion for teachers, for education across the country as part of the COVID Rescue Bill, and they didn't even have to open up to get it. I mean, it's absolutely extraordinary. They didn't even have to, you know, everything that they got vaccines. You know, they asked for rent money, and they asked to defund the police in California.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Right, because that's connected education.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Incredible, incredible. Will, always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll watch you at night and watch you this weekend.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Yes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All the time.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: 7:00 tonight.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll watch.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much, Will. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So day one of the trial of a Mexican national accused of murdering Iowa college student Mollie Tibbetts, left her body in a cornfield. We're going to take you there live, it's getting underway.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Day one of the trial of Mollie Tibbetts accused killer has just wrapped for the day. They'll be back at it 9:30 tomorrow morning. The 20- year-old went missing after going for a run in Iowa almost three years ago. Her murder so brutal prosecutors warn potential jurors that the evidence will be very graphic. Correspondent Matt Finn reporting live from the courthouse in Davenport, Iowa today. Hi, Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, CORRESPONDENT: Martha, 24-year-old Mexican national Cristhian Rivera in the country illegally is charged with first degree murder in the brutal stabbing death of Mollie Tibbetts. He has pleaded not guilty. Today in court, the state moving rather efficiently through some of its most critical witnesses including Tibbetts' boyfriend and the woman last believed to see Mollie Tibbetts alive.\par \par \uc2 Dalton Jack and Mollie Tibbetts were together for about three years. They were living together at the time of her death. Jack testified today that Mollie was the love of his life. They talked about marriage. And after Dalton jack and Mollie's sudden death, he wanted to get away so he joined the army now based at Fort Bragg.\par \par \uc2 The prosecution trying to prove Tibbetts' boyfriend had an airtight alibi, working construction more than 100 miles away. The defense of Cristhian Rivera trying to poke holes in the boyfriend story and getting him to testify, he previously had a temper and cheated on Mollie Tibbetts. Dalton Jack said he would not have taken a stand today unless subpoenaed because he did not want to see Mollie's alleged killer in court today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why do you not want to be sitting in this room with that guy? With this man?\par \par \uc2 DALTON JACK, MOLLIE TIBBETTS' FORMER BOYFRIEND: I wholeheartedly believe he's guilty.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FINN: Also emotional testimony from Molly's occasional hairstylist believed to be the last person to see Tibbetts alive when she drove by her the night that she went missing. The hairstylist told the court today that what she did when she found out about Mollies' disappearance.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA STEWART, LAST WOMAN TO SEE TIBBETTS ALIVE: My sister had sent me a text message, asking if I heard about Mollie. My heart sank because I knew that I passed her. I jumped in my car and I said we're going -- I'm going to go out on blacktop and drive around to see if I can see anything.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FINN: And that woman can testify that she never saw anything related to Tibbetts. And in court, Cristhian Rivera, the man charged with Tibbetts' death has been relatively emotionless. He has headphones on the entire time because he has a translator, and he has just been looking quietly and steadily at the witnesses. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Finn, thank you very much. Let's bring in Geraldo Rivera, once again, Fox News Correspondent-at-Large and Author of "The Geraldo Show" on that and one other topic that we want to get to. Geraldo, your thoughts on this case as we watch this get started here.\par \par \uc2 GERALDO RIVERA, CORRESPONDENT-AT-LARGE: It's heart-wrenching what the perpetrator did to that poor girl is horrific. What, you know, the knife wounds, 7, 8, 9, 10, knife stabbing wounds to her chest. It's just, I don't know if this person did it. The evidence seems pretty powerful. The circumstances are pretty powerful. They found her blood in his trunk and so forth. It is sad that the case is becoming such a referendum on illegal immigration. I don't think that's relevant to this to this case.\par \par \uc2 The family profoundly asks that it not be brought up. The father, Rob, has been very, very strong about that. He wants the case tried on the evidence and the murderer, if convicted, sent to jail for life.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So brutal murder, and we will continue to cover it. There's a lot of angles here that needs to be needs to be presented. Before let you go, I wanted to remember Charles Grodin, who has made me laugh so many times, who passed yesterday, just a brilliant actor and writer. Here just a little moment of him with Johnny Carson. Quick thought on the other side. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES GRODIN, ACTOR: Look at you. I mean, you don't really care what I have to say, do you honestly?\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY CARSON, HOST: I got to do an hour a night. I'm looking for a warm body.\par \par \uc2 GRODIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CARSON: I can get seven minutes out of you. I take a bundle I go home to Malibu.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Quick thought, Geraldo.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: He's so great. The "Heartbreak Kid," "Midnight Run," "Beethoven," "King Kong." We were colleagues at CNBC. We've shared an agent, the great Jim Griffin. You know, Charles and I would get together after his show. You could never tell him he was being serious. He had that Rye deadpanned as Johnny Carson just experienced. He was a wonderful icon and he had a great run.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He'll be missed. Geraldo, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 That's THE STORY of this Wednesday. We'll see you see you back here tomorrow, have a great day.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020210520eh5j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 New Airstrikes Amid Calls For Cease-Fire; Infighting Among Lawmakers And Biden Administration; Biden Attempts To Ease Mask Confusion; Scientists Call For Investigation Into COVID-19 Origin; Supreme Court To Hear Major Abortion Case; Gas Stations Back To Normal Following Pipeline Hack; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Comparison Over Crisis Management; \uc2 IRS\uc2  Announces Monthly Payments To Low And Moderate-Income Families Under Newly Expanded Child Tax Credit; Pentagon Under Deadline To Submit Report To Congress On Unidentified Aerial Phenomena; Clashes Continue Between \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  And \uc2 Hamas\uc2  In Gaza; CDC Guidance On Mask Wearing For Fully Vaccinated Americans Causes Confusion\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Greg Palkot, Chad Pergram, Peter Doocy, Jennifer  Griffin, Brit Hume, Steve Harrigan, David Spunt, Brit Hume, Trey Gowdy,  Harold Ford Jr., Gillian Turner \par \uc2 7,627 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS HOST: No. Nicely done.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS HOST (on camera): I think -- I think that's a Dana's corny joke right there.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Nicely done.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: Gutfeld, who do you have on tonight?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Gee whiz, Robby Soave, Susan Lee, Tyrus, and Kat.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Excellent. All right, that's "GUTFELD!" with an exclamation point.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: 11:00 p.m. "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next. Hey, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Hey, Dana. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier. Breaking tonight, calls for a ceasefire but there does not appear to be an end in sight, at least now for the conflict between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists. Today, the heaviest fighting yet, which entered its second week.\par \par \uc2 What remains to be seen is when and how the U.S. will become involved diplomatically. A phone call late this afternoon between President Biden and Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the top U.S. diplomat is sidestepping a claim the U.S. had intelligence about a media building attacked by Israel. Senior foreign affairs correspondent Greg Palkot begins our coverage tonight live from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Bret. Yes, we are getting that late word that President Biden has spoken once again with Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu.\par \par \uc2 In a White House statement, the president reiterated his firm support for Israel's right to defend itself against indiscriminate rocket attacks. Those rocket attacks have been coming from the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militant group, neither side in this conflict appears to be backing down.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice-over): Israeli airstrikes pummeled Gaza again today, targeting militants, their homes, and a network of underground passages. That triggered more rockets from Gaza into Israel, and Israeli defenses.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): You're looking behind us. If our cameraman, Ilan (PH), can just show what we're looking at and that's the batteries of the Iron Dome defense system. And it is going up in the sky. A second one is going up the sky looking the target. Like that a third, like that a fourth.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice-over): Four rockets from Gaza were intercepted. One got through, hitting a building and injuring three people. The system has knocked out 1,000 rockets in the past week.\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. JONATHAN CONRICUS\uc2 , ISRAELI DEFENSE FORCES: For Israel, for the region, it is absolutely a strategic game-changer. That's how important the Iron Dome is. Most importantly is a success rate of more than 90 percent.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: All told, some 10 Israelis have been killed since fighting began. 200 Palestinians have died, included in the casualties many women and children. Increasing pressure for a halt to the hostilities. Israel today did not sound ready.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , PRIME MINISTER, ISRAEL (THROUGH TRANSLATOR): Battle against the terror organization continues in full force. We will continue to act as long as it takes to bring quiet and security.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: While U.S. diplomats were also in the region pressing for peace, America's top diplomat didn't sound ready to demand a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATE: Ultimately, it is up to the parties to make clear that they want to pursue a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Blinken was also not ready to pick a side in the controversial Israeli bombing Saturday of the building in Gaza where media organizations had offices. Netanyahu asserts \uc2 Hamas\uc2  had an office there as well, and U.S. intelligence was informed.\par \par \uc2 The secretary of state said he had not seen the information and was asking more questions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): \uc2 Hamas\uc2  had once again tonight threatened this big metropolis of Tel Aviv with more rockets. So far, we haven't seen any. Analysts here are trying to figure out if they're pausing to reflect or reload. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Greg Palkot, early Tuesday morning in Tel Aviv. Greg, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let's examine how this conflict is leading to some infighting among Democratic lawmakers on Capitol Hill. Here is congressional correspondent Chad Pergram.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): There is tension between Democrats about the Middle East. Members of The Squad support the Palestinians and criticized how the Biden administration is handling the crisis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. RASHIDA TLAIB\uc2  (D-MI): As long as the message from Washington is that our military support for Israel is unconditional, Netanyahu's extremism right-wing government will continue to expand settlements.\par \par \uc2 REP. AYANNA PRESSLEY\uc2  (D-MA): We cannot stand idly and complicitly by and allow the occupation and oppression of the Palestinian people to continue.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): We're scared to stand up to the incarceration of children in Palestine is because maybe it'll force us to confront the incarceration of children here on our border.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: It's not uncommon for Republicans to call out The Squad.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): Let me just say, you know, shame on the progressives. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is a terrorist organization.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But the Middle East fighting exposes rifts among Democrats over support for Israel or the Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELAINE LURIA\uc2  (D-VA): Throughout history, the Jew has never been safe. We have a duty as Americans to stand by the side of Israel in the face of attacks from terrorists, and suicide bombers, and malign regimes, who again have the same goal in mind, to kill Jews.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The White House is trying to calm the internal party battle.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We support their ability to have different points of view, but we are approaching it through the prism of how we feel we can come to most effective outcome.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Some Republicans criticize the president's approach, accusing the administration of tilting away from Israel.\par \par \uc2 SEN. LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): The Biden administration is openly talking about giving the Palestinians more money while rockets rain down on Israel. There is no game plan from the United States, in my view, to make it end on terms favorable for Israel.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Some Democrats and Republicans may be more closely aligned on this issue than Democrats themselves.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Connecticut Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  and Republican \uc2 Todd Young\uc2  demand a ceasefire. They say Israel has the right to defend itself from \uc2 Hamas\uc2  rockets.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): The administration just approved $735 million in guided missiles for Israel. The real test for Democrats could come if there's a vote to oppose the sale. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: More on this with the panel. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", there's increasing confusion among state and local governments over the wearing of masks to guard against the coronavirus infection. With the pandemic easing across the country and an about-face from the CDC announced by President Biden, some officials simply do not know what to do about face coverings.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Peter Doocy has details.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Spotted in a mask outdoors Sunday, the fully vaccinated president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Some people may want to continue to wear a mask even if they are fully vaccinated. That's a decision they can make.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And if people do want to mask up despite updated CDC mask guidance, the White House says, let them.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: We need to treat people with kindness.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Let's all be kind and respectful to one another.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Now, the CDC director is trying to clear up confusion she caused.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROCHELLE WALENSKY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, CENTERS FOR DISEASE CONTROL: This is not permission for widespread removal of masks for those who are vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But that's not what President Biden said.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The rule is very simple. Get vaccinated or wear a mask until you do.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That was Thursday afternoon. And according to The Washington Post, the CDC mask guidance was updated so abruptly, "Biden himself was not briefed until Thursday morning, officials said."\par \par \uc2 JEROME ADAMS, FORMER SURGEON GENERAL OF THE UNITED STATES: You don't blindside the White House with this kind of information.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: States were blindsided too, including Nebraska.\par \par \uc2 GOV. PETE RICKETTS\uc2  (R-NE): We really need to see better communication from the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And New Hampshire where the governor says, "We were as surprised as anyone by the CDC's announcement -- learning about it through the press."\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: The CDC made the right play call but they fumbled the ball at the one-yard line. The communication was just quite frankly abysmal on this.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Adding to the confusion, the commander-in-chief.\par \par \uc2 DR. NICOLE SAPHIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: One week, you have President Biden doing zoom calls with international leaders wearing a mask despite being fully vaccinated too.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SAPHIER: Now, it's a free-for-all. Take off your mask anytime you want. So, you know, obviously, it did leave a lot of people confused.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Critics charged these changes were made as a distraction; gas shortages and violence in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELISE STEFANIK\uc2  (R-NY): It does look political Steve. You know, again, last week was the worst week of Joe Biden's presidency.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2  insists the mask updates were made on the up and up.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISOR TO PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : The underlying reason for the CDC doing this was just based on the evolution of the science.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The update that it's OK to go maskless indoors applies only to the vaccinated. Everyone else is getting a new grim warning.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Ultimately, those who are not vaccinated will end up paying the price.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): So, Biden administration officials say that science has evolved, but some Democratic leaders across the country are worried that the science changes once you cross state lines. Like in California where officials are saying now they think people there need a few more weeks to figure out how to live without masks, they're going to keep their mandate in place until June 15th.\par \par \uc2 In New York, it's different. They're going to lift their mandate on Wednesday. Here in Washington, D.C. where there is a democratic mayor, most restrictions were lifted this afternoon, effective immediately. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Interesting to watch. We'll talk about with the panel.\par \par \uc2 Peter, there's also news about the vaccine. The U.S. sending vaccine overseas. What's President Biden saying about that?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: He's sending 80 million doses of vaccine, Bret. Some of it is \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved could be used here if there was a need from \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , Moderna, and \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 . The rest of it is the \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  vaccine that was made here, but it is not approved for use on American patients.\par \par \uc2 The president says he wants the rest of the world that's in need to get more vaccine from the U.S. than they are getting from Russia or China. And he sees parallels now with World War II when the U.S. was called the arsenal of democracy, he thinks instead now we are the arsenal of vaccines. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live on the North Lawn. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there are new demands from some of the nation's top scientists for a new investigation into how the coronavirus was born. They focus on these allegations the pandemic started in a Chinese laboratory. Here is national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice- over): 18 top scientists are now calling for an investigation into the pandemic's origins that does not discount the possibility of a lab leak from the Wuhan Institute of Virology.\par \par \uc2 The new letter printed in the magazine Science suggests the investigation into the Wuhan lab is not closed, despite a WHO report that ruled out a lab leak. "Theories of accidental release from a lab and zoonotic spillover both remain viable."\par \par \uc2 The letter is signed by Dr. Ralph Baric, a top coronavirus expert at the University of North Carolina, whose gain of function research was cited by the Wuhan lab's Dr. Shi Zhengli, also known as the batwoman because she had been collecting samples of bat droppings and mapping coronaviruses for years. Gain a function researches designed to enhance the transmission of potential pandemic pathogens by speeding up evolution.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Every piece of evidence that we saw throughout the entire time I was there suggested that this originated in that laboratory at the Wuhan Institute of Virology. I haven't seen a shred of evidence that suggests anything to the contrary.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  says he supports the ongoing investigation into the origins of the pandemic, and an interagency group of experts are looking back over the evidence.\par \par \uc2 DAVID ASHER, SENIOR FELLOW, \uc2 HUDSON INSTITUTE\uc2 : If this wasn't deliberate attack, I can guarantee you the next one will be. And we don't have a system of biosensors out there in the world to detect these things.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: David Asher spearheaded the state department team that revealed in January the Chinese military was conducting classified research at the Wuhan lab, and several Chinese scientists became sick with mysterious flu- like symptoms in November 2019.\par \par \uc2 ASHER: This was the People's Republic of China doing something that could have been prevented had they given us early warning. So, whether this actually was a weapon in development, I contend that it likely was. But the way that they allowed it to spread, essentially weaponized the virus.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): A new letter sent by Republicans on the House Intelligence Committee to President Biden accuses the intelligence community of relying on outside experts with compromised financial ties to the Wuhan lab to conclude that COVID-19 spread naturally from a bat. That is now under review. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin, live at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  agrees to take a case that could change a