ughts on all of this tonight from senior political analyst Brit Hume. Good evening, Brit.\par \par \uc2 You know, the president is pretty confident about where he sees things but some Democrats on the Hill are not as confident.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, they're facing -- you know, they're facing an election in less than two years now. The president is not up for another couple of years beyond that.\par \par \uc2 But I think the reality is this, Bret, the huge expectations were created in a sense of momentum that produced all this talk about, you know, comparisons of Biden to FDR and so on, which is, you know, I think not only premature considering, you know, he had no coattails.\par \par \uc2 You know, he's got a 50-50 Senate and the thinnest of majorities in the House which is not exactly a mandate to do all sorts of the things that he is proposed to do. So, some of that I think was bound to come a cropper.\par \par \uc2 And of course, he's now confronted with the kind of problems that every president faces that come seemingly out of nowhere. You know, people have been worried about inflation for a while, and whether technically, in a macro economic sense, there's inflation, an underlying inflation going on, people are seeing prices rise and as far as they're concerned, there's inflation.\par \par \uc2 And of course, this fiasco at the gas pump for the last week or more, gas pumps for the last week or more, does nothing to help the administration's image in terms of people feeling good about how things are going. So, he says things are going well, in some ways they are, but in some ways they aren't. And I think his colleagues in Congress are worried and probably have good reason to be.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And the question is whether that effects the bipartisan effort to get the next bill through, the big infrastructure bill. Just tonight the Treasury Department, the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , announced they will begin sending monthly payments, $250 and $300 to low and moderate income families under the newly expanded child tax credit come July. That's separate and apart from everything that is already stacked up ready to go out the door.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, I don't know if this will to the problem that employers are having around the country of people having a lot of money in their pockets, more than they would normally expect to have when they're not working, and, therefore, not being willing to work. But that job situation is something that Democrats and the president need to be worried about.\par \par \uc2 When you have an economy that is supposedly in trouble and beginning to grow now, and jobs are going begging, that is an undesirable situation. And that is where we are. Places I have been, there are "help wanted" signs out everywhere, and they can't find people to work. And work isn't getting done, and the economy cannot really fully grow unless people can find workers to do the jobs that they need done to produce the products and the goods and services. So that is of genuine concern. And all the money the federal government has been shoveling out to people because of the pandemic seems to have caused or at least helped to cause that problem, serious.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One last thing quickly. You mentioned the glowing pieces early on that tried to compare President Biden with FDR even before everything is out the door. But what about the critics who are comparing President Biden to President Carter? A little too early for that?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, that's the danger he faces, that if you have an accumulation of problems on a particular president's watch, a president can be overwhelmed. Carter got overwhelmed by circumstances some thought were within his control, some thought were not. But, anyway, that's why he was a one-term president. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ought to be worried about that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit Hume, as always, thanks.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Thank you, Bret.\par