t intelligence, and here's the intelligence we had. It's about Palestinian terrorists, an intelligence office for the Palestinian terrorist organization housed in that building that plots and organizes the terror attacks against Israeli civilians. So it's a perfectly legitimate target.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The secretary of state and the prime minister of Israel talking about this targeting of this building that, as the Israelis said, housed \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , but also housed the Associated Press, and there is concern in the administration, as you heard there from the secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 Late this afternoon President Biden spoke with Prime Minister Netanyahu about the now two weeks of fighting in between \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and the Israelis, saying that he firmly supports Israel's right to defend itself against indiscriminate rocket attacks, but at the same time encourages Israel to make every effort to protect innocent civilians, and is expressing support for a ceasefire, and that the U.S. is going to engage with Egypt to try to help that along. It does not seem like that's heading that way any time soon. We'll see.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Bill McGurn, columnist for "The Wall Street Journal," Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, CEO of Empowerment and Inclusion Capital, and \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , former Congressman from South Carolina. Trey, first to you. Your thoughts on where we are with this. Obviously, we have seen this many years before, but it is intensifying, it seems, and how the administration is handling it.\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA REPRESENTATIVE: Well, I don't know the administration's policy. The right to defend yourself? I don't think Israel needs Biden to tell them that they have the right to defend themselves. What has changed, Bret, is there are House Democrats that are having a really difficult time telling our allies from terrorist organizations. Last summer they couldn't tell the cops from the robbers, or the arsonists from the peaceful protesters. I tell you what, Bret, the Squad is on the verge of doing something no other group of Democrats can do by themselves, which is guarantee that Kevin McCarthy is the next speaker of the House. If they keep equivocating between Israel and a terrorist organization, they will guarantee that Kevin is the next speaker of the House.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, to that point, that political back and forth up on the Hill, take a listen to some of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Palestinians aren't going anywhere no matter how much money you send to Israel's apartheid government.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Israel is a democracy, the only Jewish state in the world, and our friend and ally.\par \par \uc2 REP. ILHAN OMAR\uc2 , (D-MN): Many members of Congress have instead fallen back on a blanketed statement defending Israel's airstrikes against civilians under the guise of self-defense.\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE WASSERMAN SCHULTZ\uc2 , (D-FL): These terrorist organizations do not care about people or peace. They seek only to destroy the Jewish state. Israel has every right to defend herself.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: We're already seeing some push, push the false narrative that this conflict is a tragic dispute between two legitimate combatants where both sides share blame that is roughly equal. What nonsense.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold, what do you make of all of this?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: Well, Israel certainly has the right to defend herself, and I support that. I think the measure that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the tone that President Biden has struck this afternoon and this early evening is exactly the right one.\par \par \uc2 Some of the tragic parts of this are that the Palestinian, the PLO is no longer, or I should say is weak and corrupt, and the Palestinians are having to rely on \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , which I think is going to make it difficult to find peace here at the end. I think some of the commentators and congressional voices that you did not play today calling for containment, calling for ceasefire, calling for a longer conversation. We can't have peace talks right now. We have to have a ceasefire. But we have to think about all that we have done in the past.\par \par \uc2 President Trump worked hard to move the embassy to Jerusalem, the U.S. embassy, and he worked hard for the Abraham Accords, and they clearly did not advance the kind of are arrangement we would like to see between the Palestinians and the Israelis. Does this call for a more sustained engagement by the U.S.? We will have to see. But I hope it does not come at the expense of a pivot to Asia because that's where our focus has to be as a nation over the next five, 10, 20 to 30 to 40 years.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill, Harold mentions Trump administration policy. When they moved the embassy to Jerusalem, there was a lot of pushback, including former secretary of state \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 , saying it was going to ignite the Arab street. Then with the Israel -- the Abraham Accords, that that was never going to happen or hold strong. And even through this, it is holding strong.\par \par \uc2 BILL MCGURN, COLUMNIST, "THE WALL STREET JOURNAL": Yes, the conventional wisdom in the Middle East is almost always wrong, underestimates things. It's extraordinary to me in terms of equivalence that people are talking about Israel has done this, Israel has done that. And no one really talks about, well, what about \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ? Is there a call on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to stop firing rockets into civilian areas? And to equate striking a building, which I believe the Israelis warned the people that are in the building to get out first. Until we come to grips with this fundamental thing, that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  shouldn't be allowed to launch rockets. I believe if someone were launching rockets into Manhattan from Long Island, I don't think we would have any mercy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey, last thing on this, the Iran equation and Iran part of this definitely has it tied to policy and the ability to funnel money or support to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: There's no question. And I'm sure Israel is wondering why the Biden administration is so heck-bent on restarting talks with Iran. There is progress being made in the Middle East with Jordan and with Qatar and United Arab Emirates. But Israel has the right to exist. And when you are surrounded by people who want to push you in the sea, including Iran, they are not surrounded by them, but Iran is funding it, yes, I would say they have the right to not only defend themselves -- here's a novel thought. If you don't want rockets going into Gaza, quit firing rockets into Israel. There, I fixed it. I did it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: After all these years, that's all we needed to do.\par \par \uc2 It seems small, but I'm going to turn to this other topic, and that is the mask mandates and what's happening across the country, a bit of a mixed message as we talked about here before. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ROCHELLE WALENSKY\uc2 , CDC DIRECTOR: We're not a homogeneous country, right. There are some places that have more disease than others and less vaccination rates than others. And what I would say is in those communities, they should still be looking within those communities before removing mask policies.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEROME ADAMS, FORMER SURGEON GENERAL: I understand why people are confused. I want everyone to know the science behind this is sound. We know that cases are going down, vaccinations are going up. The CDC made the right play call, but they fumbled the ball at the one-yard line. The communication was just, quite frankly, abysmal on this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former surgeon general there. These are the mask policies, and in fact during this show, after we made this graphic, it changed. Mask mandates still in place while reviewing CDC guidance two states plus D.C. But D.C. actually changed its mask mandate just before this show. And 23 states say it will be soon. There are still a number of them in the red there.\par \par \uc2 Harold, thoughts on this, and is it too much about too little?\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : I think it's a little too much about nothing. The numbers are going in the right direction in everything, everyone. Vaccinations are going up finally. I hope we can get above the 36, 37 percent to 50 percent before midsummer. That would be a positive thing. Infection rates are going down, hospitalization rates, death rates, all have been going in the right way.\par \par \uc2 Reasonable people should cut her some slack. Think about this, every year - - I will make this up -- we read some scientific data that says something I like, like red meat might be unhealthy. They I read another scientific study a year later that says moderate amounts of red meat are OK. The science changes, evolves, and we have to be willing to adjust.\par \par \uc2 How does the CDC been a little clumsy at times? Sure. But I don't think there is science in the foundation has been. I think perhaps Mr. Adams had it right. Maybe they fumbled a little bit around the communications. But I have got to tell you, if we were going with the wrong direction with the health matters, I would be worried. The fact that we are not, I will take the good news, and we all should.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Bill, final word?\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: Yes, I agree here. The problem with the CDC is not the science. It's the way that they present. They fumbled this long before this decision. It's the way it's been presented as sacred and absolute and beyond question. Nothing wrong with not knowing something in the beginning of a crisis and learning more later. But there has been absolutely no modesty in pushing forward these dictates, and so people are very skeptical today.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Panel, stand by. When we come back, tomorrow's headlines tonight.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally tonight, a look at tomorrow's headlines tonight with the panel. Bill?\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: My headline is Bernie Sanders rubs the SALT deduction in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's wounds. I know this is the headline because I just wrote it for my column, and it's about the class between Bernie Sanders and the progressives and the other Democrats over this tax break for the rich which is the SALT deduction.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: SALT in the wounds, there you go. Harold?\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : Harold Ford, my son, had a great sixth birthday with his parents, his older sister Georgia, and a few friends. Reports say his mom and dad are exhausted. Harold Ford, happy birthday, young fella. I love you.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Happy birthday, my man. You read good papers. Trey?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Happy birthday to Harold's son. My headline is Snoop Dogg and Justin Bieber break ties with \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  claiming he is out of control and they are worried about him.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I knew we could count on to you end the show. All right, guys, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. FOX NEWS PRIMETIME, have you seen this show? This week it's hosted by Will Cain. It starts right now. Hey, Will.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020210518eh5h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Joe Biden Scrambles To Clarify CDC Mask Guidance; Applebee's Offering Interviewees Free Appetizers; 19 States Ending Extra Fed Unemployment Benefits; Trial Set For Ex-Officer In Daunte Wright Shooting; Joe Biden Pushes Big Spending Amid Inflation Fears. Economic Impact Of Biden's Spending Plans; Cuomo To Make $5.1M From Pandemic Book Deal; Supreme Court To Heat Abortion Law Case; Prince Harry: First Amendment Is Bonders\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Marty Makary, Joe Concha, Lydia Hu, Karl Rove,  Matt Finn, Blake Burman, Jonathan Turley, Nigel Farage, William La  Jeunesse \par \uc2 8,016 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: I went to 7/11 to get a Slurpee, and we had to put it on. We go get the Slurpee and then take a ride off soon as we get right back out.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: Slurpees are awesome. All right, great to be with you as we begin a new week. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith in New York.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm John Roberts. We will see you again tomorrow. THE STORY with Martha and her Slurpee starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS HOST: I wish I had one. Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 All right, so here is THE STORY that is breaking right now more mask guidance updates as the White House struggles to stay on a united message. President Biden now saying that fully vaccinated Americans actually can't ditch the masks, which seemed like what he was saying a couple of days ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Some businesses may want to continue to require wearing masks. Let's all be kind and respectful to one another as we come out of this pandemic and respect those who want to continue to wear a mask even if they've been vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And the CDC director Dr. Walensky under pressure too, because she's now saying it may take some time for vaccinated people -- vaccinated people to feel comfortable removing their masks. And it seems like the president is one of them.\par \par \uc2 Here he is coming out of church outside, puts the mask on as he goes from church to the car. And then, there's the late holdout states like California, New York, Maine, Massachusetts, they're starting to play catch- up.\par \par \uc2 Over the last couple of hours, headlines crossing every hour it seemed about things that they're dropping and letting people do now in those states. New Jersey though, still likes the indoor mask mandate for now.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Marty Makary has been ahead of the curve all along. He gets credit for that. He is the professor of health and policy and management at Johns Hopkins School of Public Health, also a Fox News medical contributor, which is lucky for us. And he's joined by Joe Concha, media columnist for The Hill, and a Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 So, you guys represent the two sides of this equation, you've got sort of the public message, what it looks like, you know, what the media is saying and doing about all of this from Joe. And the medical side from Dr. Marty Makary.\par \par \uc2 So, I actually want to start with you, Doctor, you know, what do you make of this confusion? And why isn't anybody just being very specific about the specific areas that might still have some concerns?\par \par \uc2 DR. MARTY MAKARY, FOX NEWS MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think it was a reaction to a lot of public frustration out there that we've exceeded the goal that we outlined, which was less than 10 cases per 100,000 as a goal to get back to normal.\par \par \uc2 And many of us were sort of sounding the alarm on this saying, why aren't we back to normal? Look at the data and then the CDC came out out of the blue with no heads up and expected people to change the entire way of life overnight. And they argued it was because of the emerging science.\par \par \uc2 Well, the science they cited was from February and March. So, I don't think people can go from zero to a hundred overnight like that sometimes, and they should have been prepared.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, well, the president was outside in Delaware. I think that, you know, that doesn't -- the threshold that you just mentioned would not be in play in that situation, right? He would be fine without his mask.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: That's right, outdoors there's no threshold. There's no case numbers.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, let's take a look at -- this is a tweet from Robby Soave on Friday at Reason. And he's replying to David Hogg, who is an anti-gun activist, as we all remember from the Parkland situation, but he's weighing in on a lot of things.\par \par \uc2 David Hogg says, I feel the need to continue wearing my mask outside even though I'm fully vaccinated because the inconvenience of having to wear a mask is more than worth it to have people not think that I'm a conservative, to which Robby Soave says. Like I said, the mask is the MAGA hat for Team Blue, Joe.\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I like Robby Soave. I haven't liked the Soave this much since Rico Suave, which obviously when we were clubbing back in the day, Martha, which was very big.\par \par \uc2 Hey, look, the CDC director really needs to stop talking in general in public, maybe hire a spokesperson because the messaging like we've talked about is all over the place.\par \par \uc2 And she talked about six weeks ago about impending doom for COVID, based on the fact that the governors of Texas and Mississippi lifted their masks mandates, and that was her sharing her feelings and lining up with the president politically who called those governors or at least accused them of having Neanderthal thinking, right?\par \par \uc2 Now, let's look at Texas. From March 29th when they lifted that masks mandate to now on March 29th, the seven-day rolling average of deaths in the state of Texas was 99 deaths per day. You know how many deaths Texas had yesterday? 0.0.\par \par \uc2 So, those governors had been vindicated and the CDC director was very wrong. But again, she's talked about just this month how kids two years old should be wearing a mask at outdoor summer camp. She later revised that statement, but again, she had to walk it back.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Fauci talked about in March how we had to be below 10,000 cases in order to lift restrictions and the three-day -- the seven-day rolling average is still above 30,000.\par \par \uc2 So, they keep contradicting themselves and as a result, while the CDC last year, Martha, in May, according to 538, was polling in the 80's in terms of favorability, trust approval, it's now down to only 52 percent of Americans have a great deal of confidence in the CDC, that is a precipitous drop.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: When you've lost Susan Collins, you've probably lost some other people, too. She said in the hearing that she felt that they didn't have credibility anymore. Too many mixed messages.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Makary, do you believe that we have reached herd immunity? Because, you know, when you put that out, when you wrote that piece several months back, saying that it would probably come toward the end of April, beginning of May, that was branded by \uc2 Facebook\uc2  as missing context and you got slammed by a lot of people. What do you say about it now?\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: Well, here we are back to normal, you know, we've achieved the thresholds that we wanted to achieve. And I think where people debated herd immunity is the definition because it is poorly defined.\par \par \uc2 But if you go to the Johns Hopkins COVID information website that has been up the entire pandemic, it says very clearly that herd immunity is when a majority of the population is immune and we're there.\par \par \uc2 And our medical leaders have been ignoring natural immunity. That's why case has plummeted so fast and that's why we are where we are today. That's one of the reasons why this announcement last Friday was so rushed. And I think people are looking at this saying, are they responding politically or was this planned?\par \par \uc2 And I think this has been the most political CDC in history. They parade around science, but most of this is discretion. It's not science.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes and you have said all along, that more people out there had COVID than realized it. And there were tests, there were blood tests in California that were showing this early on.\par \par \uc2 So, what we're seeing now is exactly what you said we would be seeing, and that is that eventually, with a certain number of people vaccinated and way more people than we realize who'd had it, that your -- the virus starts running into walls, it can't find people to go into because there's more people who have immunity than anyone thought. And I just want to point out that you were absolutely right on that.\par \par \uc2 Now, with regard to schools, should kids still be masked? Parents are outraged about this in a lot of places. And you say, first thing they should do even now as they're finishing up those who are in -- who are in school, lose the plexiglass and lose the social distancing. Tell me.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: Yes, the distancing does not make sense in kids. So, loose those restrictions first, the mask can come off last. And we've got to have criteria for the mask to come off. You can't just shut things down without reopening criteria.\par \par \uc2 That criteria should be less than five cases per 100,000 in the community. Most of the country is there, Martha, and that's why I think many kids should get back to normal shortly with summer camps. There should be no restrictions with summer camps.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Joe, final thought to you. Politically, what is going on here? Because we had this big message. Everyone was all elated about it at the end of last week. And then, all this backpedaling on the Sunday shows over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Yes, and it's been confusing. At a carwash in a little town called Franklin Lakes, New Jersey, which isn't exactly the home for fight club. It's very peaceful. There was an altercation at a carwash when a guy walked in. And the manager came out to him and said, hey, sir, you have to put on your mask. He says no, the president of the United States says I don't have to wear my mask anymore. I'm fully vaccinated. And then the police had to be called in because it was getting so heated.\par \par \uc2 So, there's all this confusion now. You see these happening in stores all over the place where people want to go in without masks. And then these poor kids 18, 20 years old, (INAUDIBLE) say at Walgreens have to be the masks police. It's horrible.\par \par \uc2 But to your point around the political point, I'll make it quick. When you have a gas crisis going on right now in those southern states of South Carolina, Georgia, North Carolina, when you see the Middle East on fire in Israel and Gaza, when you have a border catastrophe going on where we have a record number of people coming across the border in April when inflation is a very real fear for Americans right now.\par \par \uc2 This announcement last week, it's hard not to argue that there weren't politics in play when the data wasn't supporting the move to the doctor's point earlier.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Thanks, you guys. You were the guy at the carwash, right, Joe?\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Yes, no, no, no.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Just admit it.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: I'm a peaceful person. Peace and love, Martha. Peace and love.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Joe. Thank you, Doctor. Great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, how about a job with the side of mozzarella sticks? So, now you can get free beer with your vaccine and free appetizers, if you will just please apply for a job.\par \par \uc2 Karl Rove got his whiteboard wisdom on how to entice America back to work with mozzarella sticks, when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We've got a whole new situation going on here because millions of families are about to get more money in the form of checks in the mail or direct deposit. Some might call this a form of universal basic income, child tax credit payments which have been around for a long time.\par \par \uc2 But now, instead of being a line that is changed on your tax return, these are going to come in the form of actual checks in the mail and it's going to start on July 15th to about 39 million households covering 88 percent of children in the United States. It's not a new credit. But for the first time, you can get an advance on part of next year's payment coming to you in monthly installments and the number is bigger than ever.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's American Rescue Plan also increased the total -- tax credit she said to $3,600 for children under six and 3,000 for 6-17-year olds, it used to be 2,000.\par \par \uc2 And only families who owed taxes would be filling out that form and be able to take that amount out of there -- out of the bigger number. So, this year all couples earning up to $150,000 do qualify.\par \par \uc2 On the Republican side, Senator Marco Rubio and Mike Lee have a proposal to increase the child tax credit even more for working families.\par \par \uc2 Missouri Senator Josh Hawley has his own plan for tax credit for parents who work and Utah Senator Mitt Romney proposing equal benefits for stay at home and for working parents.\par \par \uc2 So, lots of plans to send lots of benefits across the country. We've been telling you about the additional 53 weeks of unemployment benefits under President Biden, which was extended, it was supposed to end next month. Now it goes all the way through Labor Day. And we've talked a lot about what the impact on businesses is because they're starving to get people back to work.\par \par \uc2 So, now, \uc2 Applebee's\uc2  is cooking something up to fix that. In moments, Karl Rove is going to join us. But first, Fox Business correspondent Lydia Hu at \uc2 Applebee's\uc2  in Clark, New Jersey. Hi, Lydia.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Martha. It is called Apps for Apps. That's the program as \uc2 Applebee's\uc2  is trying to hire 10,000 workers to join them across the country.\par \par \uc2 Today, you can go into any \uc2 Applebee's\uc2  in the country, apply for a job and get a voucher for a free appetizer that you can use today or any point in the future.\par \par \uc2 The company is hoping that that will entice some people to come out and get a job, check out what's available because they're hiring everything from entry level, waitstaff positions, all the way up to management, full time and part time.\par \par \uc2 Now, this comes as restaurants across the country are really struggling to find workers to help them meet a new demand from people who are vaccinated and want to get back out to eating out at restaurants.\par \par \uc2 The Applebee's CEO says they are busier than ever, but they need help. And now is a great time to join their team, he says, listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN CYWINSKI\uc2 , PRESIDENT, APPLEBEE'S GRILL AND BAR: As a matter of fact, the months of March and April are two of the highest sales volume months in our 40-year history, which is phenomenal. Now, that creates challenges from a labor and supply standpoint.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: Now, \uc2 Applebee's\uc2  is not the only chain to make a hiring blitz and join some other major restaurants that are turning to nationwide recruitment efforts. Places we all know like \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  and Taco Bell, KFC and Firehouse Subs.\par \par \uc2 Now, just this morning, \uc2 Applebee's\uc2  told me that so far, they have received 40,000 job applications for these 10,000 open positions. So, it seems like they are generating some interest in the past few days leading up to today.\par \par \uc2 But whether those applications turn into actually jobs, people in uniforms here to serve people at the tables, that remains to be seen. But so far, they say it is generating some interest, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I might be heading over there with some applications shortly. Lydia, thank you. All that looks really good.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk to our whiteboard wisdom man, Karl Rove, former deputy chief of staff to President George W. Bush and a Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Karl, have you ever seen anything like this where it's so hard to get Americans to want to go back to work?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, we haven't seen this nationwide. But there have been examples.\par \par \uc2 I was talking over the weekend to former North Carolina Governor Pat McCrory, who had he came into office in 2013. The North Carolina unemployment rate was nearly 10 percent. And he and his staff took a tough decision. They thought that some of this had to be the economy was recovering everywhere around them.\par \par \uc2 ut North Carolina had a higher unemployment benefit than most of the states in the region. So, they cut that back. And within two months, they had the beginnings of a dramatic drop in unemployment as people went back to work. In fact, the New York Times called it the strongest drop in unemployment in the country at the time.\par \par \uc2 So, look, people are logical, if you say to them, if you want to stay home and not work, we will give you money that is maybe more than you made or close to what you made, or maybe even less than you made. But you don't have to work in order to get it. They're logical.\par \par \uc2 Some of them will say, you know what, I'm happy between now and September to stay at home and get most of what I made, or maybe even many instances more than I made. I'm happy to stay at home and not worry about going to go back to work in September with this extra benefit cuts off.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: How do you think it plays politically? Because you've got 19 states, I think we have a map to show it that have eliminated these extra benefits because they are under this job struggle to get people to come back.\par \par \uc2 And so, I wonder what's going to happen politically when those unemployment numbers start dropping in those states, how is that going to play? And is the White House playing this right to not make the same decision across the board for the country?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes, look, I think -- I think it's going to be beneficial in those states because remember, you've got a lot of small business people who are looking for workers. And all those small businesses, there are workers who are hoping that they've got some other relief so that they're -- they don't have to stay work overtime or work the extra days to beat the demands that that business is facing.\par \par \uc2 I had -- I had dinner a week ago with a -- with about eight or nine business CEOs and presidents. Privately held -- privately held clo -- or closely held companies. California to Florida, all the way up into the Dakotas, lots of them in the Midwest, Illinois and Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Every one of them said their number one problem was getting enough work to meet the demand that they were receiving now in their markets.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I believe it. I hear it everywhere I go. But before I let you go, I want to ask you about a different topic here. There was a piece that came out in the New York Times. Deep dive piece about what it's like to work in the White House right now. What the president is like to work for.\par \par \uc2 And one of the really interesting -- and it goes into his -- you know, his demeanor, his mood, he gets mad at people. He is hungry for more details.\par \par \uc2 You know, you could read the piece in a couple of different ways and I saw your comment on it. But I want to point this out, because obviously, as you rightly point out, Karl, you don't get to do a piece like this unless you get some agreement from the White House communications team. Yes, you can come in and talk to a bunch of our folks.\par \par \uc2 So, the first headline on the New York Times was this. Beneath Joe Biden's folksy demeanor, a short fuse and an obsession with details. That's the May 14th Times headline.\par \par \uc2 Fast forward to May 15th, this is what they changed it to, Karl. Beneath Joe Biden's folksy demeanor, a deliberative Biden. What's that work there?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, one or two things. Either it is a headline writer whose eager to please or more likely, the White House communication shop went nuts --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's what I'm guessing.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: -- in essence that threatened -- yes, me too. Threatened the New York Times unless they didn't change the headline that they might be less forthcoming and cooperative in the future.\par \par \uc2 But I thought that the story spoke for itself. The first headline writer got it right, the second the headliner -- headline writer, well, he was just trying to make peace I suspect.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Karl Rove, always good to see you, Sir. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Piers Morgan is leveling a blistering attack against Prince Harry today, calling it entitled arrogance to mock the American Constitution. We'll talk about that after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIERS MORGAN, EDITOR-AT-LARGE, DAILYMAIL.COM: The one thing needs to happen is silent. They never stop yapping. Another yapping on a global T.V. interview stage. It's hypocrisy after hypocrisy, and I'm afraid this might play (AUDIO GAP) in America. They might fall for this gaff, it's not playing well over here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking now, a judge has just ruled that Kimberly Potter the former Minneapolis police officer -- Minnesota police officer, I should say, charged in the fatal shooting of 20-year-old Daunte Wright will stand trial this coming December.\par \par \uc2 Potter faces second degree manslaughter after the April 11th shooting that sparked nationwide protests in the midst of the trial of ex-officer Derek Chauvin in the death of George Floyd.\par \par \uc2 National correspondent Matt Finn live in Minneapolis with this breaking news for us. Hi, Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Moments ago, Kim Potter appeared in court via Zoom with her attorney. She did not enter a plea and our understanding is she has not entered any plea to date. December 6th was floated as that trial date.\par \par \uc2 Kim Potter right now is free on $100,000 bond. She's charged with second degree manslaughter for shooting and killing 20-year-old Daunte Wright last month during a traffic stop.\par \par \uc2 In body camera video, Potter is heard warning right that she was going to use her taser but claim she then accidentally grabbed her gun and shot him.\par \par \uc2 It happened in a Minneapolis suburb of Brooklyn Center as the George Floyd Derek Chauvin trial was ongoing here in Minneapolis, there was rioting and looting afterwards.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, the Brooklyn Center City Council voted to form a new unarmed department that will now respond to traffic stops, mental health and low-level violations.\par \par \uc2 Daunte Wright's mother says her son would still be alive if this new department was around last month during that shooting.\par \par \uc2 And here in downtown Minneapolis, Martha, you were here. There was a lot of razor wire, a lot of fencing to protect this courthouse, lots of protesting today. Much more peaceful and a very different scene here in downtown Minneapolis, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Matt, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, nobody at the White House appears to be listening to the alarm bells that are coming from former Clinton Treasury Secretary Larry Summers.\par \par \uc2 Back in February, he said that his assessment of President Biden's economic policy was that it was about to cause the highest inflation that America has seen in half a century. But nobody in Pennsylvania Avenue took that advice.\par \par \uc2 He is now speaking out even more telling Axios, "Data are pointing to higher inflation than I expected, and sooner. If spending is coming up front, and the taxes are coming down the road, then that's going to add fuel to the fire."\par \par \uc2 So, by some estimates, prices on goods are anticipated to rise more than four percent. That is quite a bit.\par \par \uc2 In moments, Dr. Ben Carson who wrote an op-ed for FoxNews.com called Biden's inflation crisis.\par \par \uc2 But first, we got to Fox Business correspondent Blake Burman at the White House. Hi, Blake.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE BURMAN, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Martha, good afternoon. There's no doubt about it. When you look all across the country right now that prices are rising. You can see that in top line inflation numbers and gas prices as well.\par \par \uc2 Let me walk you through both of those real quick. For example, right now, AAA says the average gallon of gas across the country right now just north of $3.00 a gallon. But a year ago, it was a buck $1.87.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, inflation rose by 4.2 percent in April 2021 compared to the year before with food prices rising nearly 2-1/2 percent.\par \par \uc2 Now, over here at the White House earlier today, the Press Secretary Jen Psaki was asked that if inflation persists -- if it persists, whether or not the White House would reconsider its spending proposals and she suggested that would not be the case.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Turning back on, we take inflation very seriously. The view of economists I would say around the country that there's more that needs to be done to put 8.5 million Americans back to work to ensure that we are getting working families, working mothers the assistance they need and to ensure we're competitive over the long term. So, it has not changed our overarching objectives and approach and our proposals.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURMAN: But many Republicans here in Washington are worried about rising prices. For example, Senator John Barrasso says he sees it in his home state.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN BARRASSO (R-WY): What I'm hearing all around Wyoming is we can't hire people and costs are going up. Gasoline, groceries, because so much money has already been sent out by this administration and we're paying people more to not work than to work. These are important key issues for our economy into the future.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURMAN: Now, over the last few days in to weeks, officials over at the Federal Reserve, Martha, have said that they believe that inflation is temporary, but they also say that this is something that they are going to be keeping an eye on here in the near future, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Blake, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, Dr. Ben Carson is urging a return to fiscal discipline. Before "The monster of inflation becomes too large to tame and we're left holding the tail of the proverbial tiger." He's the founder and chairman of the American Cornerstone Institute, and former HUD Secretary.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Carson, always good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 So, this is the question, have we had too much stimulus? We had, obviously a terrible year, more than a year, with COVID. Then, we have this attempt to fix it by throwing tons of money at the problem, $6.5 trillion if President Biden gets all he wants, how do you see it?\par \par \uc2 BEN CARSON, FORMER SECRETARY, HOUSING AND URBAN DEVELOPMENT: Well, if you look at our national debt in 2008, we were looking at $5 trillion. By the end of 2020, we're looking at 22 trillion and we just added more trillions of dollars to that. To think that that can be done without consequences is foolish. And as Milton Friedman, the well-known, economist, said when you have more money than spend, then you have goods and services to spend it on, you're going to get inflation. That's exactly what's going on.\par \par \uc2 And I find it hard to believe that many of the people making policies these days don't know that. And it could be that there's another reason for all the spending. And that is, if you increase the national debt to an astronomical level, you can justify massive taxation and redistribution of wealth, and gain more control.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm not a conspiracy theorist but I think that's really one of the reasons that that Khrushchev said many years ago that your grandchildren's children will live under communism, and will never have to fire a shot. Because he understood that all you had to do is gain control of the educational system, gain control of the media, so you could feed the people only what you want them to hear, massively raise taxes justifying it with huge national debt, and replace God with government. And it seems to me that those are the things that are going on right now.\par \par \uc2 There is no question that the average man is going to suffer significantly from these massive infusions of money. There's no free lunch that you think, oh, the government's going to give me another check, oh, this is great. Who's going to pay for that, your children and your grandchildren. Their lifestyles are going to be negatively impacted in a very, very major way. All of our lifestyles are going to be impacted. You can't just spend money that you don't have with no consequence.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's amazing to me when you look at the amount of money that is going into this. And simultaneously, you have an economy that seems to be champing at the bit to get rolling, right. Businesses that are really wanting to open and they can't get people to work because a lot of, in many cases, a lot of people are OK, collecting that check until September, when it was originally supposed to run out in June, which is starting to feel like it would have been perfect timing, Dr. Carson, as we head into summer. So why is there no acknowledgment of that?\par \par \uc2 CARSON: And, again, I think there may be some ulterior motive sets of other things that are going on here. But the American people are the ones that we have to have confidence and they have to wake up. They have to understand that this candy is poison that's being pumped into them. And it's going to have a very negative impact on everything that they do.\par \par \uc2 And just remember back in the days of Jimmy Carter, with massive inflation and joblessness that was going on, and think about the policies that happen. Think about when we lowered the regulations, and we lowered the taxes, and we created the environment that caused a tremendous explosion in jobs and a tremendous explosion in income. There is a way to do this. These things are not coincidental.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it's a very interesting perspective and I thank you for sharing it with us. It's very clear that we are not getting the message of can do spirit, and get back to work, and start a new business, and we're going to make that easier for you. That is not the message that's being sent right now, and there's a lot of questions about why. Dr. Carson, thank you. Very good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 CARSON: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we've just gotten our hands on New York Governor Andrew Cuomo's tax returns, and it reveals how many millions were made off of the book that he released during his COVID era leadership, patting himself on the back while so many people died in nursing homes on his watch. We're going to show you exactly how much he made on the book, coming up.\par \par \uc2 And for the first time, the newly six to three conservative majority Supreme Court is about to hear a pivotal case on abortion. This is a huge story. It's going to be a test to Mississippi's ban after 15 weeks, significant. We'll tell you all about it with constitutional attorney Jonathan Turley only on "The Story", next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Is this about Roe v Wade? Is this about people who initially, right off the bat, said they wanted to see you never take the spot on the Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So this is breaking, Governor Andrew Cuomo will make more than $5 million from his book lessons in leadership from the COVID-19 pandemic. That is according to the financial disclosures to a state's ethics agency. A spokesman says that after taxes and other expenses, Governor Cuomo will end up with 1.5 million and he donated $0.5 million to \uc2 the United Way\uc2  of New York State. The governor is under investigation for how the state reported COVID deaths in nursing homes for possibly using state workers to help with the book and for sexual harassment accusations.\par \par \uc2 He says he's done nothing wrong. And any aides who worked on the book were volunteering their own time. So Andrew Cuomo, the governor of New York, continues under heavy pressure and a number of friends. Remember, he and Dr. Fauci talked about who would play each of them in the movie of their success story of handling COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 And for nearly 50 years, Roe v. Wade has been the law of the land. But there is a case that has now been taken up by the Supreme Court. They will hear it in October. They've just agreed to hear it. And it could present the biggest showdown over abortion in half a century. The case deals with a two 2018 Mississippi law that banned abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy. It will be the first dispute on the issue where all three of former President Trump's justice appointees will participate, as part of a six- three conservative majority.\par \par \uc2 Constitutional Attorney and Fox News Contributor Jonathan Turley joins me now. Jonathan, always good to have you with us. Thanks for being here to weigh in on this today. What are your thoughts on the fact that they've decided to take this case?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It's a momentous moment for those that have been waiting a long time for the showdown that you just mentioned. The Dobbs case is now coming with a six-three conservative majority. Just last year, we had a decision where the left side of the court was able to eke out a margin thin victory, it was a plurality decision, in a case called June medical. And they won because Ginsburg was on the court, and Roberts gave a concurrence.\par \par \uc2 But when you read Roberts' concurrence, it sort of damned by faint praise, it did not really give the left of the court much assurance. He said he was voting, because the record really wouldn't allow him in his view to overturn an earlier similar case out of Mississippi -- out of Texas, I'm sorry. So Roberts is still in play here, but we also have these three newly minted justices put on the court by President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Now, this law itself would have this earlier date in terms of the ban, at 15 weeks. That's seven weeks earlier than laws that have passed constitutional muster. So this is a significant moment, as you pointed out, after the court was reframed. A lot of legislators put these torpedoes in the water. And this one just hit me, I mean, because this was opposed by pro-choice groups tried to get the court not to accept this case. Well, it just did.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I just want to remind everyone of this exchange between Dianne Feinstein because, as you say, you now have Gorsuch, Kavanaugh and Coney Barrett on this court, and that is significant, it's a six to three majority. Watch Dianne Feinstein asking Amy Coney Barrett this question.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DIANNE FEINSTEIN (D-CA): Let me try again. Do you agree with Justice Scalia's view that Roe was wrongly decided?\par \par \uc2 AMY CONEY BARRETT, SUPREME COURT JUSTICE: Senator, I completely understand why you're asking the question. But again, I can't pre-commit or say, yes, I'm going in with some agenda, because I'm not. I don't have any agenda. I have no agenda to try to overrule Casey. I have an agenda to stick to the rule of law and decide cases as they come.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Obviously, that was a major focus of that hearing. I just have 30 seconds, but give us just a very quick window into how they specifically tailored what they're going to decide here.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, this is going to be looking at how much of a burden this may plays, but also the look at Casey. In his concurrence and the decision last year, Chief Justice Roberts said nobody asked us to look at Casey or the burden under Casey. He said I'm not making any decision on that. So he reserved his vote.\par \par \uc2 As for Barrett, as an academic, she wrote a rather lengthy and comprehensive, in my view, profound pieces on her view of why these earlier cases were wrongly decided.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's going to be fascinating. Thank you very much, Jonathan Turley. Good to see you today.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So here's something completely different, Prince Harry says that our Constitution, one part of it anyway, is bonkers. He says he doesn't get the First Amendment. Some warn that he is wearing out his welcome on this side of the pond now. Nigel Farage joins me, next\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, for somebody who said that out of the spotlight, Prince Harry seems to keep seeking it. So he did a podcast with Actor Dax Shepard where he slammed his father and grandparents parenting skills. Then he said that his treatment by the paparazzi here leads him to say that the First Amendment is "bonkers."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PRINCE HARRY, DUKE OF SUSSEX: I don't want to start sort of going down the First Amendment route because that's a huge subject, and one of which I don't understand because I've only been in for a short period of time. But you can find a loophole in anything. And you can capitalize or exploit what's not said rather than uphold what is said. I've got so much to say about the First Amendment, as I sort of understand it, but it is bonkers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bonkers with emphasis. Joining me now Nigel Farage, former Leader of the UK Independence Party and a Fox News Contributor. Nigel, what do you think about what Prince Harry had to say there and what advice do you have for him?\par \par \uc2 NIGEL FARAGE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I was quite disgusted at the attack upon his father, the Queen and, of course, his recently deceased grandfather. I mean, that was appalling enough. And in response to this now, Buckingham Palace are talking about taking away the title of the Duke of Sussex.\par \par \uc2 So maybe given that interview, where he attacks America's First Amendment rights, maybe he should become the prince of canceled culture because clearly, what Harry and Meghan believe is only subjects they approve of should be freely debated, and he ought to learn his history because the last senior royal that told people in North America what to do was called George III, and it didn't end very well for him.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It didn't end very well. Here's Piers Morgan who holds back nothing when it comes to the topic of these two. He said like so much of the absurd Meghan-inspired psychobabble that spews out of his mouth, none of this makes any sense, and he clearly doesn't understand what he's saying either. It takes an extraordinary amount of entitled arrogance to mock the most revered building block of your adopted country's history. And it does raise questions about how long the welcome well we're out on this side of the pond, Nigel?\par \par \uc2 FARAGE: Well, I agree with that because the Oprah Winfrey interview, I did notice quite a lot of Americans thought, oh, isn't it awful? These two have been treated terribly. But when we drill down into the interview, virtually everything Meghan Markle said was simply untrue.\par \par \uc2 So I hope that Americans are beginning to see through these two. They frankly, they're out for themselves. They don't care what damage they do to the monarchy, to the United Kingdom. And ultimately, they won't care about America, right? They're purely out for themselves, and they're actually becoming quite a dangerous young couple, because they're spreading to a younger generation a message of freedom of speech doesn't matter. That freedom of speech is bonkers. So I hope people here reject them, I really do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I think to be fair, the context of what they were talking about is the paparazzi. Obviously, a very sensitive topic for Prince Harry, given what happened to his mother. And I think Dax Shepard was talking about infringement on his privacy and his wife's privacy as celebrities. So that's the context for it. Given that, does that change anything about what he said in your mind?\par \par \uc2 FARAGE: The paparazzi here are is nothing compared to the way they are back in London, in the United Kingdom (inaudible). Our media in Britain is much more vicious than it is in the USA. And if you're a public figure out there pronouncing on what's going on in the world, you have to expect a degree of people looking and scrutinizing what you're doing.\par \par \uc2 They can't have it both ways. If they wanted to go and live on Vancouver Island, and live in privacy, that perhaps that could be respected as it is. They're out there as full time political campaigners, and they must accept what comes with it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think it's that hypocrisy that is turning people off, frankly. Here's Candace Owens. She says, Sunday riddle how many more Hollywood style interviews will Prince Harry and Meghan Markle give before they finally achieve the privacy they claimed to be so desperate for?\par \par \uc2 And as you so rightly point out, if he was overwhelmed and sick and tired of his lifestyle over there, being watched all the time, there's a really easy answer for that. Buy a home, not in the middle of Los Angeles, in the ritziest neighborhood, but somewhere private and raise your son the way you claim to say that you want to.\par \par \uc2 FARAGE: Yes. They are hypocrites, their fingertips on everything, from issues of privacy, to lecturing us all about carbon emissions, and then flying around the world in private jets. These two are terrible hypocrites, dishonest people. And I think at last, they're beginning to be seen through by American people.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And they may not be duke and duchess anymore, at some point if this keeps going. They might just be Harry and Meghan, so we'll see. Nigel, thank you very much. Like the rest of us, we do fine without those titles. So maybe they'll be OK too. Thank you, Nigel, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So, this one is pretty, urgh, did \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  solicit marriage advice from sexual predator Jeffrey Epstein? This is one of the issues that is being raised around this divorce. We'll tell you the details when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, new details in the divorce saga between Bill and Melinda Gates. The Wall Street Journal reports that \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  resigned from \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's board last year after the board hired a law firm to investigate an affair he had with an employee 20 years ago. And a separate report further links \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  to convicted sex offender Jeffrey Epstein. For more on all of this, let's go to National Correspondent William La Jeunesse in Los Angeles. Hi, William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well imagine, Martha, you're on the board of \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , the MeToo movement just dropped kicked a few executives and blasted management for protecting them after allegations were known.\par \par \uc2 You're also America's second most valuable company in the claims against founder \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 . Well, that's where the board was in 2019 after a female engineer wrote a letter demanding changes to her job, sharing details that that romantic relationship with Gates, and demanding that Melinda Gates, the wife of two decades, be given the letter. That's according to the Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Well, the board hires this law firm to investigate. Some board members see the relationship as inappropriate and want Gates to resign immediately. Should it leak out? Well, Gates agrees, a spokesman admitting. And I'm quoting, "There was an affair almost 20 years ago which ended amicably. The decision to transition off the board was in no way related to this matter."\par \par \uc2 Well, details of this affair come as the New York Times said Sunday, Gates asked two women on dates while married, one a \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  employee he approached for a date in 2006 after watching her presentation, and in pursuit, he says an email, "if this makes you uncomfortable, pretend it never happened." The paper also said years later he pursued another employee who declined his advance.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned the Daily Beast, the claim that Gates sought marriage advice with convicted pedophile, Jeffrey Epstein, and called his marriage to Melinda toxic. The Gates divorce was filed earlier this month. Melinda called their marriage irretrievably broken. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, probably not a great idea to send that email that says, you know, if you're uncomfortable that I asked you out, then they just did like, we're done like it never happened, right?\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Attack of the nerds.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, exactly. William, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: You bet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: William La Jeunesse in Los Angeles. That saga is going to continue for some time, I think.\par \par \uc2 And is "The Story" of this Monday, May 17, 2021. "THE STORY" goes on and we look forward to seeing you back here tomorrow at 3:00. Thanks for being with us today, everybody. Your World with Sandra Smith, in today for Neil Cavuto, is about to get underway as we watch the markets this afternoon and get ready for the big close. Have a great day, everybody. I'll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020210518eh5h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Some Businesses Begging for Workers; Texas Bill would Ban Gender Reassignment Surgery for Children; Disney Employees Forced into Woke Trainings; More Trouble for Hunter Biden\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson, Matt Finn \par \uc2 7,453 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHT