emptation, like a song clouded his mind. It demanded that he slide his arms around her, that he trail his hand on long her spine to sink into the silken curls, and her nape.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: "Heat, like an inferno, temptation like a song." Now we know what you're thinking, grammarians, hitting the simile is a bit hard. At first we thought so, but then like a half empty train at Penn Station in the middle of the workweek. We thought we could just pull away, oh, but we were wrong. We had to continue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL (voice over): Their tongues tangled, danced, and she reveled in the movement. She splayed eager palms against his chest and tormented the flesh he found there. Too far away, she gripped his waist to pull him closer, until she stood cradled between his hard thighs.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: "She tormented his flesh with her palms." We were tormented as we read it. As we read it more, we noticed our thighs were also getting harder, much harder.\par \par \uc2 But Stacey Abrams wasn't done with us yet. We hadn't encountered any of her dialogue yet. It was all exposition, and then it hit us, a full line of her characters actually speaking to one another, not just grunting, but speaking in the language. It sent us over the top. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL (voice over): Hunger raged inside her, demanding to be sated. Wicked thumbs teased her too sensitive flesh and stole her breath, but it would mean nothing if he didn't understand, "You're all I've ever wanted."\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Wicked thumbs. Will we ever, going forward, ever be able to call Stacey Abrams, anything but wicked thumbs? We'll see, probably not.\par \par \uc2 The book is called "Hidden Sins" and frankly, we feel like we've just sinned in public. You can have that feeling, too, for the low, low price of just $7.00.\par \par \uc2 Get it. But go easy on your thighs. It'll wear them out.\par \par \uc2 Peter McCullough is an eminent physician. He's an internist and a cardiologist. We had an amazing conversation with him that lasted for more than an hour on "Tucker Carlson Today." We covered a lot of ground.\par \par \uc2 It was mostly about the coronavirus and how to respond to it. But one part of the discussion stuck out and we want to share it with you tonight.\par \par \uc2 It turns out there is still no treatment protocol for physicians trying to treat patients with coronavirus. What? It's true. Here is part of our conversation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. PETER MCCULLOUGH, INTERNIST AND CARDIOLOGIST: I'm a doctor, I treat patients one by one, and I can tell you and I testify to this, I have treated all my high risk patients. I think it would be immoral, unethical, and from a civil perspective, illegal not to do that.\par \par \uc2 So that question is best posed to all the doctors and medical centers and groups that have been treating COVID-19. We are a year into this. Where's the Mayo Clinic protocol? Where's the Harvard protocol? Where's Johns Hopkins?\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: There isn't one?\par \par \uc2 MCCULLOUGH: Do they have a COVID treatment?\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I don't know.\par \par \uc2 MCCULLOUGH: Have they helped people avoid hospitalization and death? Or have they just sat back and just received the cases as they've come in?\par \par \uc2 I'm telling you, something is up. The entire world has been on defense. Maybe it's all driven out of fear, but we are not treating something that is a treatable problem early. We are making this so much harder than it should be.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: And people have died as a result of that, obviously.\par \par \uc2 MCCULLOUGH: I testified in the in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , I thought -- at November 19th -- I thought 50 percent of the deaths could have been avoided because there was a learning curve of how we put this together.\par \par \uc2 Remember, there are no large randomized trials of multidrug therapy. None are even forthcoming.\par \par \uc2 And our naysayers have said listen, Dr. McCullough, you don't have enough evidence.\par \par \uc2 It's like, of course, I don't have enough evidence. That's five years away. And the guidelines say well, there's not enough evidence to treat patients. But what am I going to do? Let them die? Of course not.\par \par \uc2 I'm telling you, I have no agenda, but I am deeply concerned that something has gone off the rails in the world. It involves science. It involves a medical literature. It involves a regulatory response. It involves populations kept in fear and in isolation and despair.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Okay, so you -- this is upsetting, but it's also fascinating, I think. You've alluded a couple times to something being up, I think is the phrase that you used.\par \par \uc2 Can you put a slightly finer point on that? Do you believe that NGOs, the enormous nonprofits that have a lot of sway, it seems like in the public health arena are exercising influence over COVID policy and the direction that you are describing. Is it that? Is it some international regulatory body? Is it W.H.O.? I mean, like, what is this, do you think?\par \par \uc2 MCCULLOUGH: That is really going to be the goal of investigative reporters to figure this out. There must be stakeholders, or there must be some fundamental drivers for groupthink -- now, this is a group think. It is in the minds of people.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Is anyone profiting from it?\par \par \uc2 MCCULLOUGH: I have no idea. I'm just focusing on the sick patient right in front of me, Tucker. I can't tell you. But I have seen things in the last year that I can't explain as a doctor.\par \par \uc2 Why are other doctors not helping with a simple illness to help these patients avoid hospitalization and death? Why are they not doing this?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So the question Dr. McCullough asked and that we spoke about for an hour is that why isn't the medical establishment making a greater effort to treat people who have COVID until they get to the hospital, where some of them die?\par \par \uc2 All the emphasis is on getting the vaccine, but not on the treatment. As we said, there is still no treatment protocol produced by Mayo or \uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2 . Why is that?\par \par \uc2 The conversation was fascinating. It is on "Tucker Carlson Today" on FOX Nation. Highly recommended.\par \par \uc2 Well, "The Daily Mail," one of the last trusted news sources in the world, has uncovered some amazing text messages between Hunter Biden and his secretary, who has direct ties to China. We will tell you what that means, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Frank Luntz is a pollster in Washington, D.C. who advises the Republican Party. His real job is working for \uc2 Google\uc2  and promoting OxyContin in the middle of an opioid epidemic.\par \par \uc2 Whatever you think of Frank Luntz, he is great to live with, to share a toothbrush with. We know that because Kevin McCarthy does it. How much does he pay for that? That's a question we had asked because there are of course rules in the House about accepting gifts at under market prices.\par \par \uc2 Well, we learned that Kevin McCarthy paid $1,500.00 a month to live in Frank Luntz's apartment. That sounds fine until you learn the apartment is 7,000 square feet and it's a penthouse.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy's spokesman defended that saying that that's fair market rate because McCarthy is renting only one of 12 bedrooms in the apartment and it's only 400 square feet.\par \par \uc2 So I guess that means that he doesn't use the kitchen or the living room. It was just a 400-square foot bathroom. It gives the term friend and family discount a whole new meaning. Wish you had a friend like Frank Luntz.\par \par \uc2 Well, we have learned a lot more