're following the math there? Thanks to BLM says this researcher, police shot about 300 fewer suspects. And in exchange for that, up to 6,000 new murders took place, many of innocents and children.\par \par \uc2 So that's the bargain that we have made and we just doubled down on that.\par \par \uc2 Victor Davis Hanson is a senior fellow at the \uc2 Hoover Institution\uc2 . We're happy to have him on tonight. Professor, thanks so much for coming on. How do you read --\par \par \uc2 VICTOR DAVIS HANSON, SENIOR FELLOW, HOOVER INSTITUTION: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Not the verdict itself, all of us got to watch over a month of this trial unfold, but the response of the people who demanded a guilty verdict to the verdict we received. What does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 HANSON: Well, I think it is part of a wider pattern. I think there's a lot of people who get up every morning and they scan the news cycle and whether it's a Katrina or \uc2 Michael Brown\uc2  or the tragedy of George Floyd, they see this as useful for a larger agenda that does not have public support.\par \par \uc2 And so whether it's open borders or reparations or redistribution or the end of the Second or First Amendment or alternate constitutional customs and traditions, it doesn't really matter. They want to use this crisis to indict all of America and the majority of population. And then the majority population of America says continually, what can we do? What medieval indulgence or reparation can we do?\par \par \uc2 And then they're given some things they can do. You can change the voting laws. You can have a greater proportion of this particular group admitted or hired. You can make race base reparations in the Department of Agriculture, but it's never going to be enough.\par \par \uc2 And there's certain rules of this process, Tucker. One is that you mentioned data. Data doesn't matter. You start deductively. African- Americans are being slaughtered by police. End of discussion.\par \p