unity.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Of course, I think we -- I think all of us know that that our families are the keys to our success. All of us, I think, feel that way. We've lived it.\par \par \uc2 Victor Davis Hanson, appreciate you coming on tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So maximum aggression -- that is always the guiding principle on the left. And with that in mind, the verdict comes down and the White House immediately tells us that systemic racism and implicit bias are America's biggest problem, in fact, may have motivated Tuesday's police shooting in Columbus, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Now the officer in case you haven't followed this story, attacked someone who was -- shot someone who was attacking another person with a knife. Several media outlets promptly used the shooting to inflame racial tensions.\par \par \uc2 For example, NPR reported this quote, "Columbus police shoot and kill black teenage girl." Meanwhile, Valerie Jarrett, who was consigliere to former President Barack Obama argued that police should not interrupt knife fights, quote, "A black teenage girl was killed because a police officer immediately decided to shoot her multiple times in order to break up a knife fight. Demand accountability." What would that accountability look like exactly? And who would pay the price for it?\par \par \uc2 Horace Cooper is the co-chairman of the Project 21 National Advisory Board, and he joins us tonight. Horace, thanks so much for coming.\par \par \uc2 You didn't imagine even a year ago, someone like Valerie Jarrett, one of most powerful people in the world, in public saying police have no right to stop knife fights, and we've moved a long way in a short time it feels like.\par \par \uc2 HORACE COOPER, CO-CHAIRMAN, PROJECT 21: The left is throwing out all of the rules, all of the principles to pursue their objective. And as your discussion with Victor David Hanson demonstrated, they are not interested in the data that show