e, I like that -- federalism. Go Chris Bedford, thank you.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So the Chauvin trial is over, as you well know, but the people who benefited from George Floyd's death are demanding still more power. What will happen to the rest of us if they get more power? Senator John Kennedy of Louisiana joins us after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So it looks like Derek Chauvin could spend the rest of his life in prison, a bunch of older people who trespass the Capitol are still behind bars in a D.C. jail, and at the same time, BLM and Antifa rioters who torched courthouses are getting non-prosecution agreements, but it's still not enough.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, Congresswoman Sandy Cortez said the verdict in the Chauvin case was not justice.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: And I want to just let you all know and create the affirmation that this isn't justice. It's not justice.\par \par \uc2 Justice is a municipality and a government that does not -- because it trickles down. Right? That does not value military and armaments, more than it values healthcare and education, and housing.\par \par \uc2 So no, this verdict is not justice. Frankly, I don't even think we call it full accountability.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Sandy Cortez of \uc2 Boston University\uc2 , ladies and gentlemen, defining justice for America. Senator John Kennedy of Louisiana has thought a lot about what justice is. He joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thanks so much for coming on. I'm not a lawyer, as I know that you are. But my understanding was you assessed the justice rendered in the case on the terms of that case, so if a guilty person is convicted, that is justice. Why is this case a referendum on the whole country?\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN KENNEDY (R-LA): I'm listening to the Congresswoman and I'm thinking no disrespect, but how does anyone get to be 