can't wait. Great week with you, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Good. Good to be with you too, as well. I'm John Roberts, thanks so much for joining us on AMERICA REPORTS.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And I'm Sandra Smith. "THE STORY" starts now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you very much, John and Sandra. Good afternoon, everybody on a busy Friday, Super Bowl almost here, as you just heard. Great to have you with us here on THE STORY today. I'm Martha McCallum, President Biden saying just a short time ago, he's taking a swipe at Republicans for voting no, on his big, nearly $2 trillion COVID relief bill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: What Republicans have proposed is either to do nothing, or not enough. All of a sudden, many of them have rediscovered fiscal restraint and the concern for the deficits.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But hold, on how much is enough? Because you've got over a trillion dollars of the last bill that has been allocated but hasn't actually gone out to anybody yet. So, this bill is going to pass with basically Democrats support, it's reconciliation measure which allows them to squeak it by only 51 votes. And it makes it clear that compromise and unity, which we heard so much about, are not the priority at this White House so far.\par \par \uc2 Republicans say this is a liberal wish list, but President Biden says that he will be the most progressive president in history. He told us that on the campaign trail. And that's clearly a promise that he's making good on so far, and progressives are thrilled with it. Here's one progressive senator who said Biden has been: "Very good at tending the garden so far." So, are these packages about COVID?\par \par \uc2 Or are they about expanding the government here in the United States over the longer term? As this plays out, though, in the background of all of this about the money, there's a shift going on in the country. Take a look at this: cases have dropped 47 percent since the daily average peak and January 8th, that's very good news about where this virus is headed. And think about this, the governors who have really embraced the most-strict lockdowns, and that has not led to better outcomes necessarily, they're starting to scramble politically.\par \par \uc2 You've got Gavin Newsom, who's facing a recall in California. And Andrew Cuomo, who is getting a serious reality check on his nursing home policy from his own attorney general. These same leaders and others like them are starting to sing a very different post-election tune on the subject of science, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. ANDREW CUOMO (D-NY): I don't think it's realistic to say don't open the economy until COVID is solid.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Time is running out. We need our kids back in school.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): We can safely reopen schools as we process a prioritization.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the wise man, Mike Rowe, is going to join us in just moments on the impact on workers across the country on all of this. You are wise Mike. We'll be with you in a second. And Republican Congressman Darrell Issa from California is going to join us as well on that really interesting effort that's gaining a lot of momentum now in California. This is a big story to watch. We'll talk about that with him.\par \par \uc2 Also, Leo Terrell joins us to comment on this viral video of a New York City High School senior who says that he believes that the leaders in New York has have given up on them on these students as the fight to reopen their schools rages on. We're going to show you what that young man had to say. But we begin now with Former Trade Adviser to President Trump on the economy and reaction to this new Biden economic deal for COVID, Peter Navarro. Peter, good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 Today, you spent a lot of time looking at the economy and looking at COVID issue, I'm doing well, thank you very much. So, what's your reaction to this $1.9 trillion bill which as we said, went through with looks like no Republican support?\par \par \uc2 PETER NAVARRO, FORMER TRADE ADVISER TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: The most important word we heard today was from Janet Yellen, and it was scarring, permanent scarring. And I'd like to take you back in a time machine, Martha, because we go to the Oval Office in July of 2020. I'm sitting there with the president and other the advisors, and we were we were very much wanting to pass a $2-trillion bill. Because we understood at that time, that if we didn't do that, we were likely to lose the heart and soul of our economy, which is small business.\par \par \uc2 And Nancy Pelosi made essentially a devil's bargain, which was to deny the president of victory on the stimulus and relief package on the bet that it would lose him the election. She got that wish, but the problem we face now and you can clearly see it in the data is that she saddled the Biden administration with an economy which I believe in 2021 is going to significantly underperform and likely lead to a stagflation scenario.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you think, the timing of this. I mean, you go back to the political nature of the debate and we, really, I think people across the country and let's put up this poll 68 percent of Americans support this stimulus plan by the Biden administration. Everybody is, you know, looking forward to the money that will come along with it. Of course, there's a price to be paid for that as well. So, you, you think that the timing is essential, and you think that ultimately, we're going to look back and say that if it had happened last summer, it would have worked, but that this money will not?\par \par \uc2 NAVARRO: Yes, with this proviso, if we had done it six months ago, we could have saved literally hundreds of thousands of small businesses. The problem, Martha, is that there's no turn on, turn off switch for small businesses. Once they go away, they're gone. So, what we're facing is, is this twin scarring, we've got, on the one hand that this massive loss of small businesses, on the other hand, we have this structural problem in our major metropolitan areas where the CCP virus has hit kind of the three main pillars of every major metropolitan areas, the mass transit, it's the commercial office buildings, and it's the sports and entertainment districts.\par \par \uc2 And so, if you look at the labor stats that came with today's numbers, it's alarming in the sense that we still got something like 10 million people unemployed, and yet there's labor shortages in selected sectors. And what that means is that there is what's called structural unemployment, the people who are working in restaurants and service sectors can't necessarily make that bridge to these other sectors. So, what we're going to see is slower growth, with higher wage inflation, which is a stagflationary scenario and whatever.\par \par \uc2 I noted that that Biden was pimping \uc2 Moody's\uc2  as one of their rosy forecasters in support of their bill. The problem with \uc2 Moody's\uc2  is that they're there, they've been joined at the hip as a Democrat pollster, and forecaster, so you can't really take that. My bottom line, Martha, is 2021 is likely to be the year the big short in the financial markets and the underperformance. I think it's important that we get this bill passed. But we, we have scarring that we're going to have to deal with.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Now, we've heard a lot about go big, and we did waste a lot of time over the summer on this, this kind of program. There's also a lot of money that's not spent yet. Peter Navarro, thanks. Good to have you here today. Thank you, Peter.\par \par \uc2 So, joining me now is Mike Rowe, who has his finger on the pulse of America's workers. He's the former host of "Dirty Jobs" and now hosts "Six Degrees of Mike Rowe" on Discovery Plus, which we want to ask him about as well. So, Mike, how do you think American workers across the country, look at this whole thing right now?\par \par \uc2 You've got an improving COVID picture. Thank God. It looks like it's starting to decrease in terms of its prevalence in the country. And then you've got these massive packages that are being sent out pretty much to, to, to everybody across the board.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ROWE, TELEVISION HOST: I think in a general way, we're starting to understand the importance of balance again. Six, seven months ago, I remember when Andrew Cuomo said to your earlier point, no measure, no matter how draconian could be deemed unwise if it saves but a single life. And I got a lot of flak when he said that because I did jump in and I and I said, wait a second, that's a, that's a safety-first way of thinking.\par \par \uc2 And deep down, we're not a safety-first society, we can be for short periods, but eventually, people get bored of being terrified. And we're now starting to see that the price of safety is devastating. And what's happening right now, in my view, in the energy industry, is really the thing that I think we ought to be focused on because they're, there feels to me and there feels to a lot of people I talk to on a day to day basis, a concerted effort to wage a kind of war against energy.\par \par \uc2 And it's not a war, we can win, especially with regard to fossil fuels and all of the jobs that are wrapped up in that industry. I don't mean to sound like an apologist. However, I know of no greater investor in alternative energy than the fossil fuel industry. And if we don't get people who wear yoga pants and rely on plastic keyboards to understand the relationship that we all have, and the way that everything is connected with all elements of our workforce, I'm afraid we're just going to keep pushing that boulder up the hill.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, you make a lot of great points. And one that I want to circle back to is this issue of safety. You know, one of the phrases that we hear a lot is, you know, well, when it's safe, we can go back to doing that. When it's safe, I hope we can all you know, get together again. And you know, without sounding too cavalier, we're a country that was built on, on risk taking.\par \par \uc2 And we want to be we want to take wise risks; we don't want to be reckless as I just said. But sort of that element of being strong and fighting through is something that I think is such an American value. And I think people in many parts of the country have approached this that way and have done so pretty successfully. But there's a great divide on that.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: It's something that most sensible people know intuitively. Risk is the only four-letter word that truly matters. It's the thing that impacts and informs every decision we make from driving a car to walking around without a mask, or with one mask, or maybe two masks. But why stop there, right? Why not three or four? We're starting to see that if you elevate the business of staying alive to the very, very top of all things, then the only thing you'll ever do is stay alive.\par \par \uc2 You won't go anywhere. You won't try anything. You won't build anything, right. And so, you're absolutely right. Anything less than safety first feels glib and irresponsible. But the truth is, most people on the front lines of work, most people who raise a family, most people who get in their car and drive anywhere, understand that. There's a bargain and all things and all things are connected.\par \par \uc2 And it's funny as you were talking, I was thinking a garbage man pulled me aside about six months ago and said, you wait, if we get a vaccine, what do you see what happens to the glass vials? And I said, what are you talking about? And he said, the recycling factories have been shut down. We're not going to have the glass to make the vials. The bottleneck is not going to be where you think, it never is. The garbage man was right. The garbage men are always, right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The garbage men are always right. Mike, your new show is "Six Degrees with Mike Rowe," can you give me a real quick thumbnail on it?\par \par \uc2 ROWE: Every episode starts with a preposterous question like Kendall horseshoe help you find your soul mate. I get an hour to answer that question doing whatever I want with puppets and animation and recreations and shoddy costumes, and ill-fitting wigs and actors that may or may not be very good. When the dust settles, you wind up learning things about stuff you didn't know you cared about, and you realize everything is connected.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's usually the case when we tune into you. Mike Rowe, thank you very much. Great to see you. Thanks for coming in.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: You too, and thank you for calling me wise, it doesn't happen often.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think you are. Thanks, Mike. Good to see you too.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: You too.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez taking to the House floor to deliver another lecture to her colleagues questioning her dramatic telling of the Capitol Hill riot.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): Bang, bang, bang, bang! Like, someone was trying to break the door down. This was the moment where I thought everything was over. I mean, I thought I was going to die.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, on the floor of the of a special house session, lawmakers were given a safe platform to speak about their experiences during the Capitol Hill siege. Congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez once again slammed critics for questioning her version of events.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ: Sadly, less than 29 days later, with little to no accountability for the bloodshed and trauma of the sixth, some are already demanding that we move on or worse, attempting to minimize, discredit, or belittle the accounts of survivors. In doing so, they not only further harm those who were there that day, and provide cover for those responsible, but they also send a tremendously damaging message to survivors of trauma all across this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, let's bring in Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace from South Carolina. Congresswoman Mace was the first woman to graduate from the Citadel, she's brand new member of Congress, but she has already gotten into it over this issue and tweets with AOC and other members of the squad. Representative Mace, good to have you with us today. I just want to ask you, good, good afternoon to you too.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about that whole event yesterday, where people could sort of step to the podium and talk about their experiences, you know, it felt a little bit like a therapy session. Nobody belittles the how frightening this was for so many of you, but was that? What was that exercise all about, really, do you think?\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): Well, in many cases yesterday, what we saw on social media, you know, in some cases was that, that this was politicizing a lot of what happened. I mean, if you politicize a traumatic event like this, and I've been very vocal about the events of that day, and I have not belittled or minimized anybody's experiences whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 I was, I was fearful that day, I talked about being barricaded in my office, I've talked about the trauma and trauma affects everyone differently. And if, and if folks needed to have time on the floor to express their feelings, their emotions, the trauma that they experienced, and they have every single right to do so. But what we don't have the right to do is either allow the media to exaggerate what actually happened, or to peddle in, in false rumors or false facts that happen.\par \par \uc2 I, I live in facts and not fiction, and there were no writers in the hallways coming to our offices that day. We were fearful that that might happen, but that never happened. And we were seven to 10-minute walk away from the Capitol dome where the violence was transpiring. This was a terrible day in our nation's history and one that I never want to witness again.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So you and, and the others, including AOC, were, as you say, about a seven to 10-minute walk away through the tunnels that connect the Capitol building and the Cannon House office building where your offices are.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here's AOC, mentioning the Capitol Police and what we'll talk about why she may have done that. Let's watch this.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ: To our Capitol Police who are willing to defend us, we thank you. And again, to our staffers, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, she said that the Capitol Hill police officer, it turned out to be one of them who was banging on her door and saying where she is, where is she? No doubt looking for her to make sure that she was safe because she was, you know, probably high on the list of people that they might have wanted to encounter.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, she did a bit of cleanup on that topic with the Capitol Hill police officers with this comment.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Right. Well, earlier this week, she was disparaging our Capitol Hill police, she did it again last month. I mean, this is a week where we saw in the Capitol rotunda, we paid homage to officer capital bluffs police officer Brian Sicknick. This is not the time and the place to disparage Capitol Police this week and, and we need to support them. The only thing standing between members of Congress and their offices, and the violent rioters on the Capitol Hill at the dome, were our Capitol Hill Police. They were there to protect us.\par \par \uc2 And the reason they evacuated us out of our offices that day was because there were threats, there was a pipe bomb delivered to the Democrat and the Republican national headquarters. And so, these were, this was a traumatic event. It was a violent event. Five people died that day, something we should take very seriously. And we need to defend and protect those who were there to protect us.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It feels like this could have been an event that would have brought a lot of members closer together, having been through this together. And yet, it feels like with this experience that everybody came out and you know discussed about their feelings and all of that last night. It seems like it just is being used in some ways to drive a deeper wedge and to broad sweep all of you, all of the members of the of the GOP, when you know you're sort of a little bit like them too.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Right. Now, absolutely, and the divisions that we see, and I said this yesterday, I'm tired of the division and the American people are tired of it. At some point, we have to look each other in the eye we have to look ourselves now look in the mirror and say this, this needs to stop, the divisiveness, politicizing every single decision, every single vote to the highest extent possible is never going to help our country. It's never going to hurt the American people who are out of work. Their businesses are shut down and kids aren't back in school. We have work to do, and it's time to get back to do our job.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Nancy Mace, thank you very much, Representative from South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to have you back on the show.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Thank you so much, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, coming up, you got Republican Congresswoman Marjorie Taylor Greene digging in on the issues of abortion, the \uc2 Black Lives Matter movement\uc2  and border security. She was very upfront with her thoughts on all of this one day after she was "freed from her committee assignments." We're going to bring you that story coming up after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This story breaking right now, we're just getting some new details on a major security breach. A man broke into a plane that is used by senior \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  leaders that is kept in Joint Base Andrews. This is according to a senior defense official telling Fox News.\par \par \uc2 Air Force security has apprehended this individual. They've handed him over to local law enforcement. A source says that there were warrants, arrest warrants for him on death charges in this man's history. So, we're going to keep digging and we'll get to more on this information. They're going to have to do a very big review at Joint Base Andrews following this breach of security.\par \par \uc2 Also, today, Congresswoman Marjorie Taylor green says that the Republican Party belongs to former President Trump. She says, the 11 Republicans who voted to kick her off of two of her House committees, she believes could keep the GOP from winning the majority next year. Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram live you've been following all of this drama on Capitol Hill over the recent days as usual. Hi, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Martha. Well, Greene sought to prevent the House from exiling her from committees. Now, such panels are important in Congress. That's where members write legislation and lawmakers seek to serve on particular committees which handle issues which are important back home. Now, Greene said she has "a lot of free time on my hands," and she called congressional committee work a waste of time.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE (R-GA): So, going forward, I've been freed. I do, I feel free. Because you know what's happening on these committees. You see, we have a basically a tyrannically controlled government right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Greene summoned reporters to a press conference on Capitol Hill after last night's vote. But when reporters asked her about past remarks that Georgia freshmen ripped the press corps\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GREENE: I think he heard my speech yesterday. You owe the people an apology. You lied about President Trump. You owe the people and apology. I've done mine yesterday. OK, next question. Did any of you hear my speech yesterday?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Greene defended her 2019 confrontation with Parkland massacre survivor David Hogg. Today, Greene said hug was an adult and she compared herself to Hogg. Greene told a story of when a classmate brought a gun to her school in the 1990s. Now, the full house has never voted to strip a member from committees. The party leaders have removed members for ethics issues, racist comments and for not being team players, Martha\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting. Chad, thank you very much. Chad Pergram on the hill. Coming up, California Governor Gavin Newsom is really in a political crisis right now in California. There's a recall effort underway. They are roughly about 100,000 signatures away of triggering an election in California. We're going to talk to California Congressman Darrell Issa, who knows this date very well, he'll weigh in coming up next\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Campaigns recall governor of California Gavin Newsome is well on its way to collecting the 1.5 million signatures that they need to force a vote in that state. They've got 1.4 million already. They just passed that mark, and now some big Trump donors are reportedly getting involved in the mix here in moments. California congressman Darrell Issa joins us with his take on what's going on in his state. But first, Chief Correspondent Jonathan Hunt live on this story for us from Los Angeles. Hi, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. It seems increasingly likely that Governor Newsom's critics will be able to get enough signatures to trigger a recall vote. But that's the easier part, frankly, winning the vote and removing Newsome from office would be much tougher.\par \par \uc2 Now, Newsome has faced criticism over his handling of the COVID-19 pandemic, in particular over restrictions on businesses and the continued closure of many schools in the state. And as a result, his job approval numbers have dived. A new poll from the Berkeley Institute of Governmental Studies gives him an approval rating of 46 percent, down from 64 percent last September, while 48 percent disapprove of the job he's doing, up from 36 percent in September. But on the critical question of whether they'd actually vote to recall the governor, only 36 percent of registered voters' question for the poll said yes, while 45 percent said no, albeit with a significant 19 percent undecided.\par \par \uc2 Now, the recall petition needs just under 1.5 million signatures by mid- March to trigger the recall vote. It's getting very close according to the efforts leaders and those who'd likely run against Newsome including former San Diego Mayor Kevin Faulconer are bullish about the prospects.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FAULCONER (R), FORMER SAN DIEGO MAYOR: This is across the board in California. I believe it's going to qualify, and I believe we're going to be into a recall situation here very soon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: And money is suddenly flowing into the effort including large donations from some California-based backers a former President Donald Trump. The group supporting the recall claimed they will, within days, hit $3.5 raised. Now, Newsome, publicly at least, seems unconcerned about the effort and his backers point out that a former mayor of San Diego, a Republican businessman with little name recognition and other likely candidates hardly match up to the celebrity and cross party appeal of Arnold Schwarzenegger who ousted Governor Gray Davis in the last California governor recall vote back in 2003. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. We all remember that, Jonathan. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, California Republican Congressman Darrell Issa, former Chairman of the House Oversight Committee. Sir, good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 REP. DARRELL ISSA (R), CALIFORNIA: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you think about this? What do you think about the likelihood of a recall vote? And do you think that Newsome would likely survive it?\par \par \uc2 ISSA: Well, having led the 2003 effort, I'm aware that this is not a vote about who might replace him. It's a vote of right direction, wrong direction. And it's clear now that wrong direction is taking the larger share in a very strong way as to his handling of the government. Not just the vaccine issue, but issues about the $11 billion lost to unemployment, canceling of those benefits to those who are entitled to them, and a myriad of other activities, including his decision to lock down people well, in fact, violating his own rules when it comes to facemask protection. All of these create a no confidence likelihood once this becomes validated.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But do you think that in reality, there's any likelihood that he would lose his job as governor of California based on all of that?\par \par \uc2 ISSA: Well, there is a likelihood because once it qualifies, he isn't running against a candidate. He's running against the question of right direction, wrong direction. And the Democratic Party will either have no major candidate on his side, or as they did in 2003, where they ran the lieutenant governor who had no option to the wrong direction. And as a result, Arnold Schwarzenegger beat him.\par \par \uc2 Again, it's not a question of who runs to replace him. It's a question of right direction, wrong direction. Some candidate will run who believes in the right direction, and he will be a referendum on whether his current direction is wrong for California, where people and companies are fleeing the state.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. This is a young senior in high school who very much wanted to play the football season this year, talking about why that is so important to him and to his family as well. None of the kids have gotten to play in most places in California this year. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH NAVARRO, CALIFORNIA HIGH SCHOOL FOOTBALL PLAYER: Give us a chance to play. This is what a lot of students is their only chance to help family out. And we want to support our family and relatives.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, it's clear that students want to be back in school. It's also clear that Governor Newsome has started to say, I think under a ton of pressure, that he wants things to go finally in that direction as well. But there was an op-ed in the LA Times today that is asking him to put more behind it than just wishes and hopes. It says, Newsom's reassurance about safety accomplished only so much against powerful unions. The state needs to back that up with requirements for schools to reopen when COVID-19 rates have fallen reasonably to safe levels, rather than trying to sweet talk them into it. That's an interesting point.\par \par \uc2 ISSA: It is, Martha, and it's exactly one of the critical issues. Either Governor Newsome will take on the powerful teachers union and make them allow and encourage, and in fact required teachers if they can safely return to return to the classroom. You know, I, yesterday, the day before yesterday, I congratulated a young West Point appointee from my district who had to apply for the academy without his senior year even being in school. And it put him at a great disadvantage to other applicants from Florida and other states.\par \par \uc2 So, we are working at a disadvantage for education in California and the governor so far has not been willing to take on his own teachers union. And that's just one of the many wrong directions that's causing a lack of confidence in our current governor.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Before I let you go, very quick comment on this, if you could, the likelihood for Adam Schiff to become the Attorney General of California?\par \par \uc2 ISSA: I think it's extremely likely. The governor has already filled a lot of those roles for minorities and set asides, if you will. He now has to choose someone he believes will have his back. Adam Schiff is a consummate fundraiser and a democrat who is reliable to his base. So, I suspect he will be the logical choice. He wouldn't be my first choice. I don't think he's been honest in a number of things he's done, but I would not be surprised at all that he does get the nod.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much, Congressman Issa. Nice to see you today, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ISSA: Nice to see you. Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So coming up right here on "The Story," thank you. The New York Post slapping a failing grade on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for failing America's students, constitutional attorney and former teacher, Leo Terrell, on the importance of children being in school and what he says is being robbed of their future right now. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PATRICK AMOYAW, FREDERICK DOUGLASS ACADEMY STUDENT: I feel like they just kind of looked at the public school kids and was like, you know what, it's not -- (inaudible) point.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The deadlock continues today in Chicago where Mayor Lori Lightfoot has issued a "best and final offer" in the long showdown shut down with the teachers unions against the city there. They say they're not living up to their safe reopening demands, that's what the unions are saying, to the city. In other cities, students now sharing personal accounts of futures at stake as the resistance to open their schools drags on. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 AMOYAW: I felt like they've given up on us. I felt like they gave up on us. I feel like they just kind of looked at the public school kids and was like, you know what, it's not -- (inaudible) point. And I just feel like it's really unfair because you work so hard for your senior year, and it just gets taken away from you. You wake up in the morning. You just look at the screen from the morning till 3:00, and not visit the school. And I feel like that's not enough. The virtual school is our school.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's incredible. I mean, that is heartbreaking. That young man from Frederick Douglass Academy is speaking out saying he feels like everybody forgot about him and forgot about his future. Let's bring in Leo Terrell, constitutional attorney and also former teacher and Fox News Contributor. Sir, great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 What goes through your mind when you watch that young man speaks honestly, to the leaders of his city about school?\par \par \uc2 LEO TERRELL, CONTRIBUTOR: Well, first of all, thanks for having me on your program, Martha, the first time I've been on your program glad to be on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Great to have you.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: As a former schoolteacher, it hurts. Let me tell you right now, here's a kid in a public school, basically saying that the teachers and the politicians do not care about them. He's absolutely right. 12th grade is the most important year for college choices taking the SAT. And I'll tell you, Martha, the problem is very simple. You got the teacher union and the Democratic leadership embed with each other. And bottom line is, they care about power and control.\par \par \uc2 This is the same teacher union that do not want school choice. School choice is a key to break the poverty cycle. Yesterday, I saw your program with the mayor, Lori Lightfoot, in Chicago and she was crying. Those are crocodile tears, because for six months, if she really cared about those kids, you look at what happened in Chicago and all these democratic cities. And isn't it ironic, Martha, and these democratic cities, they have kept the public school closed but yet the private schools are open. That is the problem in LA where I live, in Chicago, and in these big democratic cities. Teacher union, Democratic leadership embed with each other. They care about power and control.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, you really -- that is the greatest example of all, right? You look side by side at these private schools and Catholic schools that, you know, parents who have the ability to have pulled their kids out of the public school and they're sending them to these schools. But that leaves so many other children who don't have that option, without an option.\par \par \uc2 Here's Lori Lightfoot, who you just referred to at her press conference yesterday. Let's watch this. I want to get your thoughts on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR LORI LIGHTFOOT (D-IL), CHICAGO: In this difficult time, black and brown kids who look like me coming from circumstances like the one that I grew up in, who are struggling and are failing. We are failing those children by not giving them the option to return to school. Failing grades, depression, isolation and so much more.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And she's right about that. Depression and isolation, they say they don't have enough time, don't have enough money to get this figured out. They had the whole summer, Leo, to work on this and many schools did it.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Crocodile tears. Martha, all summer Chicago, black on black crime, the rioting, the protesting. She's sitting here now with nine months later trying to gain sympathy. She was in charge all year. And I know the Democrats don't want to mention it, there's a guy named President Trump who said open the schools up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's right.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Even \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's own CDC director says, open the schools up. The Democrats play the card of we follow the science. The science, they open the schools up. You know what this says, Martha, the teacher unions are more powerful than the care and the welfare of the kids. And that's what has been happening in these democratic cities. And I hope the voters wake up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And the whole professional is supposed to be about putting the children first and the students first, but what we see is that not that at all. You know, before I let you go really quickly, Jen Psaki, the press secretary, sort of stepped in on that CDC Director Walensky because she dared to say that it was OK for teachers to go back without vaccines. And she said, oh, no, no, she's acting in her private capacity when she says that.\par \par \uc2 How could that be possible? I mean, how many hats can a person put on? She's the CDC director.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Let me think about that. It's impossible. She threw her under the bus on national TV, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, she did.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: I mean, and it's embarrassing. And she insults our intelligence to say, oh, there's a different opinion because it's her personal opinion. I don't think so, Martha. It's insulting.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And it got no attention every time Redfield and President Trump disagreed. It was absolutely everywhere. So, I kept looking for this story in other places than here, and I really couldn't find it. Leo, I so appreciate you coming on, I hope you'll come back soon. It's nice to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: My pleasure, will do. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet. So, coming up, former Super Bowl champ Ben Watson as America prepares to watch the big game a little bit differently this year. Ben is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The Bible tells us we may endure for a night but joy cometh in the morning.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joe Biden's presidency marks only the second time in history that a Catholic has been in the White House. This week, he told People Magazine that religion is his safe place and that prayer is what centered him. But some of his actions, especially on the hot button issue of abortion do not line up with the church's teaching.\par \par \uc2 On the anniversary of Roe v. Wade, the administration said this, "In the past four years reproductive health, including the right to choose, has been under relentless and extreme attack. The Biden-Harris administration is committed to codifying Roe v. Wade and appointing judges that respect foundational precedents like Roe."\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is pro-life advocate Ben Watson. He is also a Super Bowl champ, former New England Patriots tight end and co-host of "Football Sunday," a Sports Spectrum Production. Ben, always good to see you, thank you so much for being here today.\par \par \uc2 BEN WATSON, HOST, "FOOTBALL SUNDAY". He is also a Super Bowl champ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, as someone who is a leader in your community, how do you respond to that from the President? He says that he doesn't support abortion privately, but as a as a politician and a president he upholds it.\par \par \uc2 WATSON: Well, like so many other people, we've been disappointed. We're not exactly disappointed, this is what we expected. But we understand that we must push back. Look, there are several things that many of us may agree with the President on, President Biden are right now. But where he falls short, we need to take him to task and on the issue of abortion, on the issue of protecting life from conception until natural death, this is an area where we must push him.\par \par \uc2 Super Bowl Sunday, as you mentioned before, is coming up. And the great thing about it, it's a time of unity. The Bible says in Psalm 16:8 that I've continually set my eyes upon the Lord because he is at my right hand. We will not be shaking.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we're in the time where America is shaken. We're shaking economically. We've been shaken by political turnover. We've been shaken by a health pandemic that is continuing. And so, right now specifically, even from the White House all the way down to the courthouse and the poorhouse, it is time for America to refocus on that scripture. And understand that in order for us not to be shaken, there are biblical truths about justice and righteousness that we must adhere to, again, from conception until natural death.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Wise words. Ben Watson, you wrote a piece in the Wall Street Journal. You co-wrote it with Tony Dungy, calling for 2021 to be a year of revival. Tell us about that.\par \par \uc2 WATSON: We wrote a piece because Dungey is also co-hosting with me on "Football Sunday," footballsunday.org, which is the programming for pastors and groups to watch on "Football Sunday" that is gospel centered. We wrote a piece about where America stands. And the issue is complex, but we believe the remedies are simple.\par \par \uc2 It's about repentance. It's about reading scripture. It's about getting those truths in our hearts so that we know how properly to live our lives. It's about community. It is about doing the things that we know God has required of us and understanding where we have all fallen short. And again, it is about a time of returning to those things.\par \par \uc2 But maybe if you've never been there, and there was a time for you to turn through repentance and faith, and put your trust in Christ, maybe now is the time. There's a sense of urgency. And what we're understanding is that, right now is our time to affect the next generations. But we cannot do it alone, we cannot do it as that verse in Psalm 16:8 says, without putting the Lord continually before us.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You are always an inspiration, Ben Watson. Thank you. I know you'll be cheering on your former teammate, Tom Brady, and all the players on both sides on Sunday. We look forward to seeing you then. Thank you so much, Ben, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 WATSON: Of course. Good to be with you. You too.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, before we go, we will say our feet are saying goodbye to Christopher Plummer, who died at the age of 91, was known to many of us as Captain von Trapp.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): Christopher Plummer, the star of the "Sound of Music" and so many other movies, award winner on many fronts and entertainment, he passed away today. His manager says the actress wife was by his side at their home in Connecticut. Plummer spent more than 50 years in Hollywood. He became the oldest Oscar winning actor at 82 for the "Beginners." Christopher Plummer was 91.\par \par \uc2 Have a good weekend, everybody, I'll see you coming up on "The Five." Stick around for that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020210206eh2500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Sen. John Barrasso (R-WY) Is Interviewed About The Impact Of Biden's Climate Executive Orders; Biden Begins To Deliver On Progressive Demands; Two \uc2 FBI\uc2  Agents Killed, Three Wounded In South Florida; \uc2 Chicago Teachers Union\uc2  Slammed For Dancing Video; Frustrated Parents Plead For In-Person Learning; Mothers Of Murder Victims On Defunding Police; Canadian Rapper Calls Out "Woke" America\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Grady Trimble, Phil Keating, Mike Huckabee, Marc  Thiessen, Carely Shimkus \par \uc2 7,648 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 February 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: This is -- this is what's helping keep me warm today, my big Baffin boots. Yes.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: Well, you are prepared.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Well, I grew up in a place that's like the Arctic, so why not?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Good to be with you, John, "THE STORY" starts now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you, John and Sandra. Good to see you both today. Today, a short time ago, though, in Florida, we all witnessed this awful scene. The body of an FBI agent draped in the American flag, one of two agents who were killed in a bloody gun battle that was part of a raid on a home where they were investigating violent crimes against children.\par \par \uc2 It was the first time in 12 years that agents, \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents were killed in their line of duty. And with that, good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha McCallum and this is THE STORY. We're going to take you to the scene there in Florida shortly as we learn more about that unfolding situation today.\par \par \uc2 But first, we want to turn our attention to a major change that is underway in America. Today, workers in our energy industries are wondering where the Biden White House is leading them. We've spent decades building energy independence in this country. And that has been great news for workers, and not such great news for other big energy providers like Venezuela, or Russia, or the Middle East, we're no longer reliant on them. And we were building our own enormous energy supply. That shifted the global power dynamic in a way that was not seen, really, since the advent of the automobile. But now, that is changing, as executive orders are shutting down new leases for fracking on federal lands. And as you know, the XL Pipeline is also a casualty here. So, here's what some of those workers are saying as they react to all of this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You prepare for these -- for these large projects. And when one gets the rug yanked out from under it, I mean, you have nothing for that year, or you're going to have nothing for the next two years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I just can't believe that all this in one day. That a man's stroke of a pen that can sit there, it's just un-American.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How's that guy that's had 30, 40 years of welding (INAUDIBLE) pipelines in? How does he, you know, how does he transfer that to solar? Flyover country, that's what they call us. They don't -- it's like we don't even exist.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we've been down this road before. The Obama administration promised a plethora of green jobs. They said they would be easily transferred from canceled projects over the wind and solar work.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA, FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Workers in 22 states are manufacturing the supplies for this project. Workers in a dozen states are building the advanced manufacturing equipment that will power this new facility. \uc2 Solyndra\uc2  expects to hire 1,000 workers to manufacture solar panels and sell them across America and around the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But that is just one example. But after receiving two years, two years after receiving their half-billion-dollar loan from the Obama White House, \uc2 Solyndra\uc2  filed for bankruptcy, as you probably well remember. So, fast forward today to where House Republicans went to the Houston Ship Channel, an enormously busy energy port in the United States. And they began to push back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In a time of pandemic when jobs have already been lost, you thought you would take action to help them, not cut them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It weakens America and empowers our enemies.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We have to support American energy. That's what the science actually says.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, another hard hit state will be New Mexico because more than half of their oil production takes place on those federal lands. And that's where we find our Fox Business Network reporter Grady Trimble there today. Hi, Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK REPORTER: Hi, Martha. This oil pump and just about every oil pump, as far as the eye can see out here, is on federal land. And as you mentioned, New Mexico is disproportionately impacted by this executive action, pausing new leases on drilling for oil on federal land. That's because about half of U.S. oil production or rather a quarter of U.S. oil production takes place on federal land. But in New Mexico, that number is closer to 56 percent. Claire Chase knows this industry well. It's a family business, Mack Energy Corporation. And tell me how this is going to impact you guys and this entire state.\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE CHASE, DIRECTOR OF COMMUNITY RELATIONS, MACK ENERGY CORPORATION: It's going to be really tough for us, for our company in particular. We do about 60 percent of our production on federal lands. And at the state level, it's about 56 percent of all the oil that we produce. That represents about $1.4 billion in revenue to the State of New Mexico and north of 9 billion to the federal government. So, it's a -- it's a really huge hit.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: A lot of people in the industry see this as a first step to banning drilling entirely on federal lands, and that would be devastating for your industry.\par \par \uc2 CHASE: Yes, it's absolutely devastating because as we look at our future and how we're going to spend money, we want to -- you know, we obviously need to be able to drill to produce this commodity that people need. And if we're not allowed to do that, we're going to have to find other areas where we can drill, places like Texas that don't have a lot of federal lands. So, I think New Mexico is looking -- it's looking really bad for us. And hopefully, our governor is able to take a stand for it.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: And Claire mentioned this, Martha, but 33 percent of the state revenue here in New Mexico is from the oil and gas industry, about $1.4 billion from this industry goes to public schools. So, it's not just these workers that are directly impacted by it. It's all of that trickle down all the way to school teachers and students. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very interesting. Our thanks to Claire for speaking with you today about her family's business and the larger implications for New Mexico. Grady Trimble down there today. Senator John Barrasso was a key player in passing climate legislation last year, and he is now poised to be perhaps one of President Biden's biggest opponents when it comes to this issue. He's also the ranking member of the Energy and National Resources Committee. And he's from the great state of Wyoming. Senator, great to see you today. Thank you so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN BARRASSO (R-WY): Great to be with you, Martha. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, how do you see this? What's the impact on Wyoming?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, it's a dramatic impact on Wyoming. And I stand with the workers. And these are the workers I was with this weekend in Wyoming, the people who work in oil and gas and coal and who provide affordable energy for America. You know, you can protect the environment without punishing the economy, and punishing the people who work to provide energy. But Joe Biden hasn't taken this approach.\par \par \uc2 He's taken a sledgehammer to the energy workers of the country. The energy needs are going to continue to be there. But the jobs are going to go other places, whether it's to Canada, whether it's to Saudi Arabia, because we need the energy. And the people that are going to suffer aren't just the workers in this country; they're also the people that purchase energy, people that fill their cars, people that heat their homes, because we're going to see an increase in the cost of energy for consumers, because of these executive orders.\par \par \uc2 You know, President Biden said he wanted to work together, but when he got to the White House, and he drew a big target on the back of American energy, and he pulled the trigger, killing the Keystone XL pipeline, you know, killing oil and gas leases, and then putting us back in the -- in the Paris Accord.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I mean, when you look at the huge gains that were made, in terms of energy independence. I mean, I remember gas lines when I was a little kid. And you know, you look at all of the impact of OPEC and the power that they had over the world and over the United States through that entire period, gaining energy independence has put us in a completely different position against these countries when we go to negotiate with them and talk with them. We know that John Kerry, the former Secretary of State has now been put in charge of climate change. That's his area for the Biden administration. Watch this little snippet of an interview that he did with Fareed Zakaria.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAREED ZAKARIA, HOST, CNN: Canceling the Keystone Pipeline, the decision on no new fracking on new federal lands, those governmental decisions are going to put people out of work, in fact, already are doing so. What do you say to those people?\par \par \uc2 JOHN KERRY, U.S. SPECIAL PRESIDENTIAL ENVOY FOR CLIMATE: We're a great nation at creating jobs, so they're going to be a massive, literally millions of jobs created over these next years. And there will be a transition in our economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, are there going to be million -- I just noticed on the graphic in Grady Trimble's piece, a million jobs likely lost in up to 2022. That's a very big number. But Kerry is --John Kerry is saying that there's going to be millions of jobs created in these new industries. Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: No, I do not. John Kerry is wrong. If he were standing here, I'll look him straight in the eye, and I'd say, John Kerry, you should be straight with the American people. Because even the fact checkers, The Washington Post, who endorsed President Biden for president has said of what John Kerry said, they said it was false hope and it was misleading information. At the most, you're talking about 10,000 new jobs over the next 10 years in solar and in wind.\par \par \uc2 You know, the nominee to be Secretary of Energy said jobs may need to be sacrificed. That's the approach of this administration. These are climate alarmists, and they want to do things dramatically, unilaterally, immediately to no benefit to the -- to the climate, but to devastation of the economy. Energy is called the master resource for a reason. It powers our economy, jobs, the military. We are a world energy superpower, and we have been acting like it in the last four years. President Biden has now turned off the switch.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, is it legal for the President of the United States to ban energy production on federal lands because there's a question about that. Right?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: I agree with you, Martha. When we take a look at it, there's an executive orders that have come out, there will be lawsuits, I can assure you. And with the fact that over the last four years, we've put over 200 conservative judges on the courts, I think there's a very good chance that what President Biden is trying to do will be overturned, will not be found to be legal. And there are a number of us in Congress actually on both sides of the aisle that are trying to stop Joe Biden and his climate alarmists from moving in that direction.\par \par \uc2 There are a number of members of Congress who are Democrats from Texas, who are opposing what President Biden is trying to do. And I'm going to work closely with Joe Manchin, Democrat from West Virginia, who is chair of the Energy Committee, I'm the ranking member of the Energy Committee. And we both believe that we need all of the energy. We need oil and gas and coal. We need uranium for nuclear power. We do need wind and solar. But we need to make sure that American energy, and we make energy as clean as we can as fast as we can, and do it in ways that don't raise costs for American consumers.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it's a very big story. And it's a big political story, as well. A lot of these energy producing states are red states, but there are swing states on that list, as well. So, it's going to be interesting to see where this goes. I had the pleasure of being in your beautiful state of Wyoming all last week, Senator, and it's an amazing place that you come from. So, thank you very much. It's great to have you here today.\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Great people, hardworking, and we produce a lot of energy. Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's for sure. Thank you, Senator. So, less than two weeks in now, how Republicans who vowed to give President Biden a fair shot are now feeling about what Rich Lowry is calling that this perhaps could be the most radical left-wing presidency in history. We break down the numbers and the actions just so far, but it is disguised, as Lowry puts it, under the mood music of moderation and unity. Marc Thiessen and Governor Huckabee, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And I pledge this to you, I will be a president for all Americans, all Americans. And I promise you, I will fight as hard for those who did not support me as for those who did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, that was 13 days ago. President Joe Biden calling for unity. And hours later, he signed 17 executive orders and actions, rolling back some of President Trump's policies and he has signed dozens more executive orders, a record number actually, in the early days of his presidency. Now, Republicans who said that they would give Biden a chance appear to be at kind of a crossroads with all of this. Rich Lowry writing this in the New York Post, quote, "The dulcet tones of Biden's inaugural address already seem an artifact of a bygone era. The fact is that Biden is governing as he promised, further to the left of his own record, further to the left of his ex-boss, former President Barack Obama, and further to the left of any Democrat who made his career prior to the ascendancy of the cultural left.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in American Enterprise Institute Scholar Marc Thiessen and former Arkansas Governor Mike Huckabee. Both are Fox News Contributors. Gentlemen, great to have both of you with us today. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Good to be with you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I'm struck by a couple of things. When I look at the moves so far from President Biden. One is that he won, and that the country seems to be open to a lot of these ideas. There was a poll that showed that people generally support 13 out of the 14 of those executive orders that he did on the first day, Marc, and let me start with you. Is this becoming a country that is shifting center left, or is that a misread on President Biden's part?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: I think all of us want to give President Biden a chance. I think most of us want to say, you know, he promised to unite the country, that what they -- what people remember as he campaign, I'm the only guy who can bring Republicans and Democrats together. I did it for years on Capitol Hill, I've spent 40 years in the Senate. I know how to do this. Nobody else can do this. So, we're all waiting for him to do that. You know, and what I -- what we need -- I think he needs to realize, and the Democrats need to realize is that unity is about more than not insulting each other. You know, it's great. I mean, I hope we can cross that bar. If we can't, God help us. But it's about compromise. It's about letting the other side get some things they want and moderating your own demands and being willing to give up some things in order to bring the other side along and reaching a consensus.\par \par \uc2 And that's what Biden promised to do, and he's not doing that. He's not doing it with the executive orders. And he is not doing with legislation either. He has no major bipartisan initiative, his one first big initiative, this COVID relief bill, he's -- he, just after meeting with Republicans, said we're proceeding with budget reconciliation, I can pass this with just Republic -- with just democratic votes. So, there's no -- they're not interested in real unity.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's interesting, because, you know, he did talk about it a lot on the campaign trail, governor. And you know, he is seen as somebody who has worked with both sides. So now, he does seem to be pitching much further to the left, I think, than a lot of people ever thought he would. This is a soundbite here from Pramila Jayapal, congresswoman. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-WA): 1.9 trillion is an excellent start. It should be the floor. If anything, we should strengthen it, not weaken it. I think we could probably argue that we -- really, economists have argued that we need between 3 and 4 trillion. And so, we're continuing to work on that. But 1.9 trillion should be the floor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We've already done 4.5 trillion, governor. And I -- there's at least a trillion, I think, that's still on the table, hasn't been spent yet, but she's going for 3 or 4 trillion. So, this is what he's dealing with.\par \par \uc2 MIKE HUCKABEE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Why stop there? Let's make it 25, 50 trillion. Let's just give everybody everything they've ever wanted. Let government be Santa Claus. She said, that's the floor. Heck, I'm on the floor thinking about how crazy this is. Joe Biden said he wanted to fight for me. My gosh, I'd hate to think if this is what he's doing for me what he would be doing to me, if he was fighting against me. A lot of these executive orders are killing people's jobs all over the country, especially in the energy field.\par \par \uc2 And despite the fact that 58 percent of Americans don't want taxpayer funded abortions, he's pushing ahead. 77 percent of Americans, including Democrats, this isn't a bipartisan issue, don't want America to be paying for foreign abortions. He just suspended the Mexico City policy, which does exactly that. This is not a unity president, this is not a, gee, let's all come together. Joe Biden is making Barack Obama look like Ronald Reagan in terms of his leftist policies. And I think a lot of people who voted for him. Goodness, I hope they're as disappointed as they need to be over this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, it just raises a basic question about how the president, you know, none of us can get inside his mind and figure out why he's looking at it this way. But he seems to feel an obligation to check a ton of these boxes off for the further left part of his party, Marc. And before he knows it, he's going to be dealing with a midterm election. And he may wish when that rolls around, that he listened to some of the workers that we heard from in the first segment here from the energy sector in places that are potentially swing states.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: No, no doubt. I mean, look, we shouldn't be surprised that a democratic president and we're representing a far-left liberal Democratic Party is doing some things by executive order that we as conservatives don't like. And you know, and if he goes too far, it's going to create a backlash, and it's going to come back -- it's going to come back to haunt him. You know, keep in mind, it was Barack Obama going and saying elections have consequences, and I won. That led to the backlash that started the Tea Party movement, and eventually over time, created Donald Trump if Joe Biden doesn't tack to the center, and to the moderate path and try working with Republicans.\par \par \uc2 He just had 10 Republican Senators come to him in the Oval Office yesterday, and offer their hand outstretched and saying, let's work on a scale down COVID relief bill that we can find consensus. $618 billion, that's on top of the 900 billion we just passed a few weeks ago. So, $1.5 trillion since Biden was elected. That's real money, that's a lot of money. And he seems to be uninterested in compromising with them. And that means his whole promise of -- his whole promise of his inaugural address is a lie.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I got to believe there's a lot of suburban voters across the country who if you walked out of that meeting with them and said, You know what, we're going to meet in the middle on this, and we're going to get this done right away, and we're not going to use reconciliation, and I think it would be a very interesting outcome. I do think it's what Americans want. Gentlemen, thank you very much. Great to see you both today.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's a very sad story coming out of Florida today. And we have breaking news on this killing of two \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents. We're going to bring you some new information on this, as they were serving a federal search warrant. It was violence against children that was being committed in that home. That was the investigation. We're going to tell you exactly what happened as we learn more about these details of this gunfight, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Law enforcement officers saluting one of two \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents who lost their lives in action today in South Florida. Three other agents were wounded. In this operation, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says that they were serving a search warrant in a case involving violent crimes against children. They are the first \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents to die in the line of duty in more than 12 years. Correspondent Phil Keating joins us with the breaking news in this developing story in sunrise Florida this afternoon. Hi, Phil.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEATING, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. An absolutely devastating, dramatic, and brutal early morning here in sunrise just west of Fort Lauderdale as a suspect, who was being served a federal court ordered search warrant for child pornography and violent crimes against children, instead of opening the door to his apartment down the street. Instead, he grabbed his gun and started shooting very unusual for \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents to be shot and killed in the line of duty, and yet today, two of them were, both of them, and three other agents were shot and wounded. Two of them remain in stable condition. It was a dramatic and chaotic scene inside the gated apartment complex. The road here remains closed to traffic. The FBI director has named the two fallen agents as Daniel Alfin and Laura Schwartzenberger.\par \par \uc2 And director William (PH) Wray did release a short statement today, and I quote, "Tragically, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  lost two of our own today. Special Agent Daniel Alfin and Special Agent Laura Schwartzenberger were both shot and killed in the line of duty while executing a federal court ordered search warrant in crimes against children investigation. The last time agents, as I mentioned were -- an agent was shot and killed in the line of duty was 2008. The suspect inside his apartment barricaded himself in the minutes after the shootout. That person is now dead, as well. still unclear is whether the subject of the search warrant ended up shooting and killing himself or whether crossfire from \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents and local law enforcement on the scene took his life. We will find out more this afternoon as the \uc2 FBI\uc2  in South Florida is going to have a news conference at 5:30. Sad day up here on Sunrise, Florida. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Absolutely. We will look ahead to that at 5:30 tonight for more information on those fallen officers. Thank you very much, Phil Keating, in sunrise, Florida today. So, coming up right here, Republicans see an opening to winning back suburban voters by helping parents and fighting the teachers' unions to get their students back into the classroom. Here's union president Randi Weingarten blaming the prior administration.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RANDI WEINGARTEN, PRESIDENT, AMERICAN FEDERATION OF TEACHERS: We had an administration that completely failed, that distorted fact from reality. The same kind of distortions of fact versus untruths have happened when it comes to school reopening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Dr. Scott Atlas was part of that White House team, and he responds with his look at the science behind all of this, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: President Biden said that he hopes to have most kids back in the classroom within his first 100 days, but the clock is ticking. And, you know, by that time, you're going to be close to summer for a lot of these students. Meanwhile, you've got Catholic schools, which are celebrating National Catholic Schools Week this week and it's been quite a year for them. Because across the country they're seeing surging enrollment, they have really set the standard for safe open schools, as the unions continue to blame others for the fact that they are not going back into the classroom.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: We had an administration that completely failed, that distorted fact from reality as everyone has seen because of the big lie and the effects on Washington, DC before January 6th, and then in the aftermath of January 6th. The same kind of distortions of fact versus untruths have happened when it comes to school reopening, and that people of goodwill don't actually even know what the science is, and how to have a roadmap in terms of safely opening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Let's bring in Dr. Scott Atlas, former Special Adviser to President Trump and a Stanford University Hoover Institute Senior Fellow. Dr. Atlas, good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 You heard Randi Weingarten, the Head of the American Federation for Teachers. She says that there's no pathway, there's no guidance, there's no science involved in the urge across the country to get these teachers back in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT ATLAS, FORMER SPECIAL ADVISER TO TRUMP: Yes. Hi, Martha, thanks for having me. Well, you know, this is sort of surreal at this point. It's been known for months and months. I'm talking about eight months that the science the data has said school should be open.\par \par \uc2 And there's no new information that contradicts that, the only new information is confirmatory. From \uc2 Brown University\uc2  from \uc2 Duke University\uc2 , from all over the world, we know children have very, very little risk of a significant illness. We know that children are not significant spreaders to adults. We know that teachers do not get significant cases when in schools that are open.\par \par \uc2 And we know, overwhelmingly, the harms to children, not the teachers, to children of lack of in-person schools. These people are, I don't know what they're talking about with lie. These are just bizarre statements that have no fact whatsoever. There's been a complete perversion of the term "the science." There is no reason not to open schools. There is nothing special about schools except that they're low risk environments.\par \par \uc2 If I had a job where I wanted to be safe from COVID, I would become a teacher. I mean, these people are just, I don't understand that. In fact, there's no reason, by the way, to prioritize teachers in vaccination programs, teachers are not at high risk. High risk teachers should be prioritized. But, you know, 82% of teachers in the United States are under 55, half of them are under 41. They're a low risk young profession. They should be back at work. I said this months ago, but aren't schools and social business --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, you did. You said it on our program and, you know, people give you a hard time.\par \par \uc2 I just want to play for anybody who may have missed it. This is a dance montage that was put together by Chicago teachers. They are in a cooling down period right now because they're about to strike because they don't want to go back to the classroom. But here they are dancing and talking about safety. Let's listen to this for just 20 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Make it make sense. Safety is essential. Keep our students and our teachers safe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. You know that -- I know you couldn't see that, Dr. Atlas, but it's teachers dancing around in their apartments, in their homes, doing interpretive dance and talking about making teaching safe and making children safe, and listening to science. I mean, you know, as you point out, it's like we're living in parallel universes here. Quick thought.\par \par \uc2 ATLAS: Well, you know, I think that at some point, if teachers don't want to work, they should get out of the way and let people do their jobs. There's plenty of people who are unemployed in the country. They are not fit to be teachers if they cannot be critical thinkers and look at the data themselves. I will say that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. You know, I think everyone understands if someone has a specific health concern, then they should maybe be in a different category, but most of these people, as you point out, are not in those categories and children are suffering across the country. Dr. Atlas, thank you. Good to have you back on the show. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 ATLAS: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet. So, my next guests have been personally impacted by the shutdown of schools in Northern Virginia, Denise Warburton and her son Max, a senior at TC Williams High School join me now. Great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 I know you're at home because where else would you be, right, at this point given the circumstance that's going on. Max, let me start with you. What has life been like for you and do you wish that you could go back to school?\par \par \uc2 MAX CONSTANDY, SENIOR, TC WILLIAMS HIGH SCHOOL: Life at home has been terrible. I have to do Zoom calls along with my barking dogs, and I would really want to go back to school so I can meet someone in-person, and have fun lunches.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, you want to have lunch and you want to be with your friends, and be in a room. Denise, you know, when you listen to that Chicago teachers' video and, you know, what goes through your mind as you listen to this debate? What do you want to say to these teachers?\par \par \uc2 DENISE WARBURTON, VIRGINIA MOTHER: I think what's hard to see as I have one in the public school and one is in the private school, I've seen that the private schools have gone back successfully in a Hybrid model. They brought them back. None of these kids are falling ill. They're all able to have -- there's a complete change in their demeanor when the days my daughter does actually go to school versus virtual at home.\par \par \uc2 I see my son isolated on home with no social interactions all day. He is on a spectrum. And for a kid with Asperger's to be home alone, with absolutely not a single social interaction all day, is completely detrimental. And to do virtually Zoom social skills groups or occupational therapy groups, they're completely ineffective and not serving these children whatsoever. It's been heartbreaking to see the fact that he's home alone all day and suffering.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I know you said that he's gone and looked in the window at school just sort of dreaming about being there, right?\par \par \uc2 WARBURTON: Yes. He -- the Catholic school that he used to attend for 9th and 10th grade is only a block from us. And he knows they're back, and we were pretty much pushed out of the private Catholic school because they said they couldn't serve his needs. They couldn't do enough for social skills. They couldn't do enough for executive functioning. And they kept encouraging us to go to the public school, saying they have more resources.\par \par \uc2 But right now, they don't. They don't and it's been so sad that he's actually walked over to the school. And he's been found peering in their windows and inside their campus trying to interact with the kids. And when I asked him what he's doing. He says, I just want to see my friends.\par \par \uc2 And I just wanted to cry. I just thought that was really poignant. And it just shows how desperate they are to be around other people.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. And, Max, you have a beautiful smile and it's so nice to meet you. And I'm sure that your friends miss you so much, too. And I hope that you can all be back together in the classroom very, very soon. I want to thank both of you for being with us today. Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 CONSTANDY: Yes. My friends really missed me so much and desperate to see me go back to school.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know they are. And we hope everybody can go back soon. And we thank you very much for being here today, both of you. It's great to meet you.\par \par \uc2 WARBURTON: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 CONSTANDY: I would also like to thank the people who came and have the suffer studying at home. And I would like for them to go back to school.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I hear you. I think everybody in the country hears you, Max. Thank you so much, great to have you with us. Thank you, Denise. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 So coming up, we're going talk to a group of mothers who band together to honor the lives of children lost to violence, by standing up against calls to defund the police in their communities. That is not what they want. They want safer cities. The mothers leading that charge are up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A mother says that if a police officer had been at the gas station, the night a stray bullet hit her 19-year-old daughter, her daughter might still be alive today. Now 17 years later, Sylvia Bennett- Stone is leading the Voices of Black Mothers United Initiative.\par \par \uc2 She says, they're coming together to honor the lives of their fallen children by spreading the message, positive police reform not defunding local law enforcement could help stop violence on America's streets. A new study discovered homicides jumped 30% last year compared to 2019, a jump that has no modern precedent in history.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Sylvia Bennett-Stone and former civil rights activist, Robert Woodson, Founder and President of the Woodson Center and 1776 Unites. Thank you so much for being here, both of you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOODSON, FOUNDER, WOODSON CENTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SYLVIA BENNETT-STONE, DIRECTOR, VOICES OF BLACK MOTHERS UNITED: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Sylvia, I want to start with you. And tell me why you think it's so important for these mothers who have lost children the way you lost your daughter, to bond together. Because these stories don't get the kind of attention that we see other shootings in this country getting?\par \par \uc2 BENNETT-STONE: Absolutely. We have a network of over 2,500 mothers thus far, and we are assured to be quiet. You bury your child, the fool goes away, and then they want you to go away as well with no resolve. So, we band together to say enough is enough.\par \par \uc2 We're no longer going to be silent. We're going to take a stand, and we are going to help do whatever we can to make our communities safer. We're going to do whatever we can to help save the life of the next child that goes to the gas station.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Your daughter is beautiful. We have a picture of her up on the screen right now and we are with you, and your grief that I'm sure it doesn't get any easier 17 years down the road. But you hear these calls to defund the police, and you see what happens in the aftermath of some of these shootings. What's your message on that?\par \par \uc2 BENNETT-STONE: We are saying defunding the police is not even an option at all. It's not at all. However, we're coming forward with some solutions. We're saying we want police accountability, of course. No one should have to bury their child as a result of shooting. Either way, whether it's community of police, it hurts, but defunding and taking police out the community is not even an option.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Robert Woodson, your thoughts on this today?\par \par \uc2 WOODSON: Well, for the past four decades, the Woodson Center has worked with local leaders like Sylvia in successfully preventing youth violence in the cities. The answers are found there, and the monies that are being spent to groups who are calling for the reduction of police or defunding, it is really exacerbating the very prominent, saying that they're trying to solve.\par \par \uc2 So, the Woodson Center is supporting these mothers and as a consequence of the relationship they have with law enforcement, they have increased the closure rates in homicides in cities, and their other crime prevention efforts.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We thank you both so much. I hope you'll come back when we have more time. Oh, Robert Woodson, thank you so much, sir. We'll see you next time. Sylvia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT-STONE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you both. You might not consider yourself someone who cares about a rap song that's climbing the charts. But it is a pretty interesting cultural moment when the tune is called "Fake Woke." It attacks cancel culture, and it questions the Black Lives Matter Movement. The exclusive interview you don't want to miss, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A song exploding on the music charts this week with more than 2.5 million views on \uc2 YouTube\uc2  in just a couple of days. The viral hit is called "Fake Woke." Canadian rapper Tom McDonald serves up a controversial take on cancel culture and much more.\par \par \uc2 Fox News Headlines 24/7 Reporter Carely Shimkus has just spoken with Tom MacDonald exclusively Hi, Carely,\par \par \uc2 CARELY SHIMKUS, FOX NEWS REPORTER: Hey, Martha. Yes, that's right. It was a very interesting interview. And, you know, it's common for musicians to release politically charged songs these days. But you've probably never heard one quite like this before. Rapper Tom MacDonald rails against cancel culture and censorship in his new song titled "Fake Woke" and his message has been heard by millions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOM MACDONALD, RAPPER: We're ashamed to be American, you should probably love it, because you have the right to hate it and not get stoned to death in public. As children we were taught how to walk and talk, but the system wants adults to sit down and shut up. Cancel culture runs the world now, the planet went crazy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: Now, \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  anti-censorship anthem has racked up over 2.5 million views on \uc2 YouTube\uc2  since it was released just four days ago. More impressively, it's currently the number one most downloaded song on iTunes, beating out this year Super Bowl performer, The Weekend and Grammy winner Ed Sheeran. MacDonald believes his music is popular because he is willing to scream what others would never say out loud.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACDONALD: This song was just about screaming my truth at the top of my lungs and sort of there's this performative wokeness that's going on in society today. And I think it was just important to point some fingers at the hypocrisy and the way that the world is changing not for the better.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: Now, the 32-year-old is no stranger to tackling hot button issues in a track released last year titled "People So Stupid." He made his feelings on abortion clear, wondering why some don't consider a heartbeat a sign of life.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACDONALD: Let's talk about abortion, sorry, tell me how this works. Bacteria is life on Mars but a heartbeat isn't life on Earth? Weird.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: In another song, he discussed Black Lives Matter calling it a valuable movement, but adding all lives matter isn't racist or stupid. Supporters call his stance refreshing but critics have accused him of racism and homophobia. One \uc2 Twitter\uc2  user writing, if someone listens to Tom MacDonald cut them off completely. Another tweeting, if you like Tom MacDonald, you got to be racist.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACDONALD: I know who I am. My friends and my family know who I am. My fans know who I am. If I see something on TV, I change the channel. If I'm reading something I don't like I close the book. Like I don't try to call the radio station and call the people who were on TV names and try to cancel them or call the publishing company that published the book that I don't like, and try and get the author cancelled.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: And while MacDonald has the most popular song on iTunes right now and 1.6 million followers on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , he is doing it only with the help of his girlfriend who shoots all of his music videos. He does not have a manager, a record label, a publicist or PR team. He also doesn't get a lot of media attention. He attributes this to not fitting in with today's accepted narrative.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACDONALD: People have become allergic to opinions in North America and these last five years, and it's pretty soon like they're going to cancel everything. And all we're going to have is tall gray buildings and quiet car rides. And then, we're all going to be kicking ourselves in the butt for this craziness.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: And while you may think he's conservative, MacDonald says he doesn't support either political party. He thinks the most important thing is to have moderate voices instead of the political polarization facing America today, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Carely, thank you, very interesting interview, a controversial rapper with a pretty strong message, interesting to hear from him.\par \par \uc2 I also just want to do a shout out to my friend, Charles Gubish. She was 102 years old today. He is one of the featured veterans of Iwo Jima in my book "Unknown Valor." So that's Tuesday, February 2nd, happy birthday, Charlie. THE STORY goes on. We'll see you back here at 3:00 tomorrow. "Your World with Neil Cavuto" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020210203eh2200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Media baselessly ties GOP reps to extremist groups; Biden admin signals robust domestic spying apparatus; The Left's persecution of conservatives continues\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Ashley Strohmier, Raymond Arroyo \par \uc2 7,480 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 January 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY, FOX NEWS HOST: We're not the media mob. We'll give you the news that they will never give you. We'll tell you the truth. Anyway, thanks for being with us. Have a great weekend. We'll see you back here on Monday.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Hello, everyone, I'm Laura Ingraham, and this is The Ingraham Angle from Washington tonight. With a big lineup for you. Senator Rand Paul and Congresswoman Tulsi Gabbard are here on the destructive political intelligence and media apparatus that is being constructed in the first weeks of the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, we're going to expose the real anti-vaxxers and ask why Dr. Fauci still continues to downplay an early and effective treatment for COVID. Our medicine cabinet digs in. And Biden bumbles why Kamala's family is covered in fluff. Raymond Arroyo has that in Friday Follies.\par \par \uc2 But first, the Left's persecution of Americans who don't echo their party line is getting more alarming by the day. And it's not just DHS that's targeting conservatives and those opposed to lockdowns and worried about election fraud. This sinister effort is now being aided and abetted actively by the media.\par \par \uc2 Now, today, The New York Times published a lengthy article detailing the affiliations between certain GOP lawmakers, including Arizona Rep's Paul Gosar and Andy Biggs, and supposedly racist Right-wing militias.\par \par \uc2 According to the Times, Mr. Gosar, and a handful of other Republican members of the House had deeper ties to extremist groups who pushed violent ideas and conspiracy theories and whose members were prominent among those who stormed the halls of Congress.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, the Times evidence of these deeper ties doesn't go beyond giving speeches, attending similar events, maybe even posing for photos. And the evidence that the groups in question are white supremacists' militias. Well, that comes from the smear group, the \uc2 Southern Poverty Law Center\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In fact, the leader of that group that the paper claims have deep ties to Congressman Gosar was itself horrified when he heard about the January 6th Capitol riots. You have to read quite a way into the Times hit piece before coming across this line. it is not clear whether any elected officials played a role in directly facilitating the attack on the Capitol. So far, no evidence has surfaced publicly to back up those claims.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Times isn't alone in hurling these baseless and poisonous accusations at the GOP. Last night, you recalled that we showed you how Pelosi is labeling the entire GOP now as the enemy within, and she's accusing them of aiding and abetting murderous mobs. And President Biden, he's apparently fine with all that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Speaker Pelosi said, I believe yesterday that she feels that threats are coming from inside the House with other members being allowed to carry guns. Of course, conspiracy theorist among the ranks of the GOP. Now, do you and does the White House agree with that assessment?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Well, he speaks with Speaker Pelosi on a regular basis and certainly has reiterated his support for her and members of the caucus. I don't think I have anything more for you to read out beyond that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Unity? Is that what they meant about unity whatever Nancy alleges, no matter how defamatory, it's AOK. Well, the vitriol that she's spewing from the podium is seeping down to the rest of her caucus. Politico reports that multiple Democrats said they're privately mulling whether to sever ties completely with those Republicans as their caucus weighs potential forms of punishment.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if they understand that Donald Trump, he opened Pandora's box, California Rep. Barbara Lee said, adding that the behavior can't go unpunished and she believes more violence could be ahead. We need to do something.\par \par \uc2 This hate, this lust for revenge and punishment, this slander and yes, it is defamation is taking this country down a very dangerous path. And we've warned you about this for years. We warned you that if the Democrats got into power, they were going to try to turn the entire American national security apparatus into its own domestic spy ring that tracks and punishes Joe Biden's political opponents.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Tulsi Gabbard, former Hawaii congresswoman and former presidential candidate. Congresswoman, great to see you tonight. Now, January 6th was obviously a sad and horrific day, but my fear in all of this is that this talk of this forever threat, the enemy within, it's just going to mean the government has more power and we have fewer rights and freedoms. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 TULSI GABBARD (D), FORMER HAWAII CONGRESSWOMAN: It is an incredibly dangerous situation we are in with our country on edge and this kind of broad, inflammatory rhetoric is like throwing a match into the tinder box. Now, what Speaker Pelosi is talking about here is potentially a very serious thing, if there is evidence to back what she is saying and her quote was, we have members of Congress who want to bring guns on the floor and have threatened violence on other members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 Now we're a country of laws. So, if what she is saying is true and there is evidence to back it up, then this is a legal issue for law enforcement. When I was a member of Congress a few years ago, there was someone in the public who is making threats on my life, repeatedly saying that he would track me down and behead me.\par \par \uc2 I reported it to law enforcement. They investigated the threats, found them to be credible, tracked the guy down, charged him, prosecuted him. He was found guilty and held accountable for that crime. What Nancy Pelosi is talking about here is a serious crime of terroristic threatening and if true, should be reported to law enforcement because members of Congress are not above the law either.\par \par \uc2 However, if there is--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congresswoman--\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: Let me just finish--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: My point is also inciting, potentially inciting. I mean, if you think there's someone among the Republican caucus who is actively plotting the murder of Nancy Pelosi or other members of Congress, that could then bring violence upon those individuals, some of them we named in the Angle. So that in and of itself is inflammatory and perhaps inciting.\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: Well, exactly, that's really the issue here. If this is a criminal threat, let law enforcement deal with it. If there is no evidence of what she is talking about and if it's not true, then it is exactly that. It is inciting further division, further harm potentially and further destroying the possibility of our country coming together. And this is why it's so important for Nancy Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 Again, if these accusations are baseless, she needs to apologize. Not really so much to her colleagues, but really to the American people who right now more than ever need leadership coming from the Speaker of the House, who represents all members of Congress and the American people to bring us together for her and President Biden to deescalate these tensions, to turn down the temperature, to denounce those who are seeking to undermine our civil liberties and our constitutional rights and who are inflaming these tensions to say, hey, we're all Americans, we're all Americans and we need to come together.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, that would certainly be a nice thought, but it certainly looks like this is stage setting going on, Congresswoman. I mean, they're setting the stage for, OK, because of this threat, we need to either have aggressive gun control. We need to put a wall around the Capitol. We need to have more metal detectors in Washington. Washington has to be a state. I mean, whatever it is, it seems like this is a perfect way for them to set that narrative and set it early to justify future encroachment on civil liberties. And even - that's what I'm worried about.\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: Yes, I share your same concern, because if that is the motivation behind this, then it is not a stretch of the imagination by any means to say that, well, if the party in-charge can extend this kind of government overreach and using the military and intelligence agencies against people who are of the opposing party or who hold different views, then what's to say the next party who comes in and takes charge can't do the same. And that that really leaves us with a banana republic, not a democracy, and that that's the dangerous path that we see before us.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This cynicism is even infecting some Republican lawmakers too, take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, CNN HOST: Tell me what it's like being in a Capitol building surrounded by fencing a war zone, not the battle part of the war zone, but like a Green Zone type area.\par \par \uc2 REP. PETER MEIJER (R-MI): It was supposed to be inviting. It was supposed to be the people's house and to have it be walled off because we can't trust that, frankly, the people won't try to overrun it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congresswoman, what happens when government officials, representatives stop trusting their own constituents? Maybe they need to go into a different line of work.\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: Yes, it's remembering who all of us who have served or who serve in elected positions were there to serve the people, to represent them, represent their voices, not be in a position where our government is now viewing the people, the public as. Who, the opponent, people who may come and attack them as the threat, this is such an incredibly dangerous thing? And again, it just undermines the foundation that our country was built upon and why I continue to call for leadership. We need voices of strong leadership, not for one party or the other right now, but for America.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: For America. Congresswoman, wonderful to see you. Thanks for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And just this week, Customs and Border Patrol confirmed that construction of the southern border wall has been halted, which makes what we're about to tell you all the more jarring. Now, first, let's take you back to January 7th.\par \par \uc2 A non-scalable wall was erected around the Capitol, and this show has long questioned the need and message of walling off the people's house. Now, the only comfort came in the realization that this was temporary, but that was apparently giving the Democrats in D.C. way too much credit. I think I warned you at the time, it probably wouldn't be.\par \par \uc2 Well, not only are officials now calling on the fence to be permanent, they want a backup force of officers to be ever present as well. And while most Americans are aghast at seeing D.C. becoming a Baghdad inspired Green Zone, the Biden team hasn't really given it much thought.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I was wondering if the Biden White House or President Biden himself has any thoughts on this current fencing is the right answer, or does it project the wrong picture to the American public?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I'm not sure we're going to have any comment on that specifically, but I'm happy to talk to our national security homeland security team if we have anything further to add.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Apparently, they're just not going to comment much at all. Joining me now, Kentucky Senator Rand Paul, a member of the Foreign Relations Committee. Senator, is this all part of something bigger, as Congresswoman Gabbard and I just said, setting the stage for future encroachment on civil liberties, smearing of the American people?\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAND PAUL (R-KY): Well, it could well be. I mean, when you look at the Army encampment that we have now, it really looks like a major army base there. There are still 5000 troops this huge perimeter with razor wire. This isn't really a great image for a free country or for our country. So, I say the sooner it's torn down, the better. Who's making these decisions, I guess, they're unilaterally being made by Nancy Pelosi, maybe Chuck Schumer.\par \par \uc2 But I think also Democrats need to look in the mirror a little bit. They very quickly assumed that the enemy within is Republicans and they're potentially violent. But nobody really looks in the mirror for the Democrats. They're not really very self-aware.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris said contribute to the Minnesota Freedom Fund to bail out some of these protesters. Well, one of the protesters that they bailed out soon as he got out, cracked someone's skull, fractured someone's skull, and caused a brain injury. And that was because Kamala Harris, not specifically her on that issue, but she was encouraging the bailing out of these violent protesters. But no one's called for her impeachment. No one's saying that she should be held responsible for her speech.\par \par \uc2 Maxine Waters, Cory Booker, they say get up in your face. They say mob them. So, it seems to be sort of a double standard that the enemy within only these Republicans apparently, and none of the things that Democrats say seem to be of concern to them.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Senator Paul, you and I would always for years, say bring the troops home. And now we're saying bring the troops home from Washington. Kabul, Iraq, but now it's bring them home to back to Indiana from Washington, D.C. We have to get into what else is happening with Biden. We mentioned the border wall.\par \par \uc2 So, it looks like we have massive amounts of people coming up through Central America, other places. We don't know where they're all coming. How are we concerned about COVID, if we have no walls? If they had their druthers in our southern border, walls around the Capitol to protect Nancy and company, but no walls at the southern border and thousands of people coming across unchecked, untested.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Well, I guess you didn't hear, Laura. They're going to give them double masks. And all of the new immigrants coming across the border are going to be given double masks. But you know what they're going to do, in addition, is they're going to have everyone flying domestically is going to have to get tested before you get on a plane before you get back on a plane.\par \par \uc2 You talk about devastating the airline industry. They've been through so much in the last year. Does the Biden administration, and these nuts he has over there on his virus group, they really want to kill America, try to make us test twice or twice a week or three times a week or four times a week, realizing also that the science isn't there? If you have no symptoms and you get a rapid test, you know what the error rate is, 30 to 40 percent error rate, if you have no symptoms on the rapid test, it's a worthless test. But they're going to say the science, the science, but then they're not going to let you fly without a test domestically that will kill the economy.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, that has to be challenged at every level in court administratively beyond. Now, another issue with the Biden doctrine of multilateralism. He was bragging going through the election that he was going to work with the allies to be tough on China. And I'm thinking, didn't Obama try that? And it didn't work.\par \par \uc2 But even Politico's European outlet, Senator Paul, can't ignore what's really going on. Today, that given the opportunity to show the new U.S. administration it was serious about collaboration, Europe has done no such thing, basically giving the middle finger to the Biden administration, and cutting side deals with China. That's a pretty big loss in two weeks on foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: I have a feeling there's going to be a lot of Biden words and that you have to watch closely as to what action we actually get. We've had this discussion with his new appointments. Tony Blinken, he appoints, and I asked Tony Blinken, are you going to be a change or are you going to be more of the same? Because he advocated for the Iraq war. He advocated for the Libyan war.\par \par \uc2 And then even after that, he said, we learned from the Libyan war that there were not good replacements. And I said, well, why did you then go into Syria? This is where our main regret was Syria, is that we didn't go far enough in. That we've had a regime change, and I told him, look, if you've had regime change in Syria, you know who you would have gotten, you would have gotten jihadist because the fiercest fighters on the other side of Assad were jihadists and al Qaeda and al Nusra. And it's like, when are they going to learn the lesson?\par \par \uc2 So, I'm not expecting big things that I am really disappointed that this is an administration that is putting people in place who are very much in favor, they say, of internationalism, but they're really in favor of military intervention.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Here we go again, the war machine. Senator, great to see you tonight. Thank you so much for joining us. And what's behind Dr. Fauci's obsession? Well, against hydroxychloroquine. We're going to show you his odd moves. He goes out of his way to mention the drug and reveal a new study. Once again, actually, we show you proving its efficacy. Our medicine cabinet is here in moments.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: As Chinese President Xi calls for unity and multilateralism at Davos, the CCP is engaging in an online misinformation campaign against vaccines that we developed to stop COVID.\par \par \uc2 Politico is reporting that the Chinese Communist Party's new brand of aggressively online diplomatic defenders, known as Wolf Warriors, have added vaccine conspiracy theories to their trolling repertoire.\par \par \uc2 It claims that the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and Moderna shots are risky or even deadly have been boosted by headlines from nationalist Chinese media and Chinese officials. Now, Politico goes on to note that the effort is already seeding doubts about the safety shots and giving countries rhetorical cover to purchase Chinese made vaccines that appear to be less effective than the two leading Western ones.\par \par \uc2 So, my question tonight, where the heck is Dr. Fauci on this? He was always so quick to correct former President Trump's supposed misinformation, so will he now stand up at the next \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  meeting and call out China's malfeasance? More importantly, what's President Biden going to do? Why isn't he speaking out?\par \par \uc2 Well, I think we know the answer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN (D), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The rising China is a positive development not only for the people of China, but for the United States and the world as a whole.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Get ready for four years of the CCP walking all over us. All right, speaking of Dr. Fauci, Biden's COVID point man is using his latest media blitz to continue dismissing the now well-documented benefits of a drug we first told you about nearly a year ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI, NIAID DIRECTOR: There were things that were said, be it regarding things like hydroxychloroquine, and they were not based on scientific fact. If you look at the pushback that I got, but hydroxychloroquine was one of the reasons why I felt it was essential for me to not in a confrontative way. I took no great pleasure out of contradicting the president, but I had to get up just to maintain my own integrity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But a new volume of the American Journal of Medicine includes a study showing the clear benefits of the early use of hydroxychloroquine to treat COVID. Joining us now is one of the study's co-authors, Dr. Harvey Risch of the Yale School of Public Health, and Dr. Scott Jensen, a physician and former Minnesota state senator. Dr. Risch, tell us about this study and your response to the almost obsessive effort on the part of Anthony Fauci and others in the medical establishment to discredit the drug?\par \par \uc2 DR. HARVEY RISCH, YALE SCHOOL OF PUBLIC HEALTH: Good to talk to you. I really wish Dr. Fauci would actually say something in terms of evidence, but since he never quotes any actual studies, it's really hard to know that he has any evidence to actually talk about. He's very dismissive, but he never actually says anything.\par \par \uc2 The study that we published, Dr. McCullough and 30 plus co-authors published is a review of all the evidence for early treatment as a whole. It's not just one drug, it's not just hydroxychloroquine. There's many drugs that can be used as we've seen this week, colchicine (ph) is another drug that can be used for the ivermectin (ph). There's a whole no repertoire armamentarium of drugs that work in early treatment. And we combine them in order to get maximal benefit. And that's how we treat early, and it works very well.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Dr Jensen, whether it's hydroxychloroquine or ivermectin, the drug Nacke (ph) is a whole series of these therapeutics that Dr. Risch is talking about. What's going on here, like what happened to medicine being an accumulation of data and an analysis of that. I'm not a doctor, but I certainly I've gotten to know Dr. Rich and another many practicing physicians. And they're like, Laura, we don't. This is the most disturbing development we've seen is the seeming politicization of medicine.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT JENSEN, PHYSICIAN: Laura, you're spot on. This is going to be a dark chapter for medicine going forward, because if you go back to 1976, when Legionnaires disease took place, we didn't have to wait to treat our patients and save their lives. When it became evident that the normal antibiotics we might try weren't working, people were still dying. But when we stumbled across erythromycin as being the drug, we didn't have to have a Dr. Fauci say something or anything else. We went ahead and used it.\par \par \uc2 And I would remind everybody that Dr. Fauci is not clinically seeing patients. And he didn't just contradict President Trump. He contradicted U.S. Senator Amy Klobuchar because she'd been quite clear that when her husband received hydroxychloroquine, when he had COVID that she was grateful that he was going to be able to benefit from that. And she thought he did. And I think that's important.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And when you also talk about this, Dr. Risch, ivermectin has received a huge amount of publicity in recent weeks because of various studies that have come out about its benefit as well. An early use of patients, friend of mine just took it, I guess, last week. She had COVID for four or five days and feeling really lousy. I think maybe 20 hours, 18 hours. She was gardening out in a house, you know, and she's I feel almost back to normal. But she said one major pharmacy chain would not fill it. They said, no, we're not filling this prescription. What?\par \par \uc2 RISCH: It's astonishing that everything has become politicized and it's like Big Brother is watching over every pharmacologic move that we make with some script behind the scenes that nobody actually understands. These drugs are relatively inexpensive. They're generic. Nobody's making a huge profit over them. And maybe that's the real bottom line here.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dr. Risch, Biden's COVID team, by the way, they can't seem to agree because first here's doctors Fauci and Osterholm on vaccine effectiveness as it relates to all the new strains. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: We've seen announcements that actually the vaccines that we're using are still effective.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL OSTERHOLM\uc2 , BIDEN COVID ADVISORY BOARD MEMBER: It's actually more than one variant that may actually duck the vaccine or our natural protection that we get from having had infection.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, here's the same contradiction on the two-mask deal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: If you have a physical covering with one layer, you put another layer on, it just makes common sense that it likely would be more effective.\par \par \uc2 OSTERHOLM: Using the face cloth coverings, doubling them, actually, we need to be very careful about that. That may actually be counterproductive. Not helpful.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, Dr. Risch, I'm confused, I've gotten pretty up on the medicine, medical literature, but now they're confusing me, and they criticized Trump's response is confusing.\par \par \uc2 RISCH: Well, two plus two equals six or two plus two equals four, so maybe it's somewhere in the middle. There are a lot of gray zones and a lot of lack of specific knowledge, and nobody quite knows the answer. One thing about those new viral strains, the mutant strains, is that the vaccines and natural immunity seem to have some degree of immunity against them, but not as much as the normal strains. Whether that will be enough or not and we need backup for the vaccines is only something we'll know empirically when it gets here. So, we have to wait and find out.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Dr. Jensen, that means, again, bringing us full circle here that those early interventions with relatively safe antivirals, we should not be discouraging, correct?\par \par \uc2 JENSEN: Absolutely not. Honestly, the idea of telling someone that when they get sick with COVID, they should either get better on their own or wait till they get so sick they have to go to the hospital and then once they go to the hospital, they'll be told hydroxychloroquine and ivermectin are no longer appropriate to use. It makes no sense. Patients are saying we don't want that binary choice. We'd like to do something early on an outpatient basis. And that's reasonable.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Gentlemen, as always, elucidating. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 RISCH: Pleasure.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: With President Trump out of the White House, so the media, they have a brand-new obsession and no, it's not Joe or Kamala. Raymond Arroyo has it all in Friday Follies. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY STROHMIER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Live from America's news headquarters, I'm Ashley Strohmier.\par \par \uc2 Traveling lawmakers getting stepped up security. The move follows increased threats against representatives and senators and follow the January 6th insurgency at the U.S. Capitol. The acting House Sergeant at Arms says Capitol Hill police officers will be stationed at Washington area transportation hubs during busy travel days. A travel portal has been set up so lawmakers can alert law enforcement about their travel plans.\par \par \uc2 And COVID-19 vaccinations for kids can begin by late spring or this summer, that's according to Dr. Anthony Fauci, the government's top infectious disease expert. The vaccine still has to be approved for children, but testing is under way. Children make up one-fourth of the population, and to reach herd immunity, 70 to 85 percent of Americans have to be vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 I'm Ashley Strohmier. Now back to THE INGRAHAM ANGLE. For all of your headlines, log on to FOXNews.com\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's Friday, and that means it's time for "Friday Follies." For that we turn to FOX News contributor Raymond Arroyo. Raymond, Biden signed some more executive order and actions this week. That can't be possible.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, 40 so far, Laura.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: God.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Though his introduction to some of them, you're not quite sure if Biden fully understands that he's president, and what his administration is actually doing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Like the previous administration, we'll start to properly manage -- unlike it, we're going to start to properly manage lands and waterways.\par \par \uc2 The second order I'm going to be signing also changes what the president has done -- the president -- what the former president has done, and a memorandum reversed my predecessor's attack on women's health -- excuse me -- health access as we continue to battle COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: No wonder he is choking on that, Laura. Let me translate what he's saying there. Biden actually signed an action to overturn the Trump policy, which banned federal funding of abortions abroad. That's why he's coughing. And look, it has nothing to do with healthcare. But the taxpayer picking up abortions abroad, that's what this is really about, even though 77 percent of Americans in a new Marist poll, including Democrats, opposed that policy.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But remember, as Nancy Pelosi has said, they're all devout Catholics, Raymond.\par \par \uc2 Now, Jen Psaki's spin on this was quite compelling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: In terms of executive actions, that includes overturning some of the detrimental, harmful, and at time immoral policies and action of the prior administration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: So the Trump policies stopping the funding of abortions abroad, that was immoral, Laura. It's a wonder there wasn't a flash of lightning in the press room.\par \par \uc2 The executive action she is talking about is truly about killing children abroad. And for an administration that has been so focused, worried about separating foreign children from their parents, we are now going to pay as taxpayers to permanently separate foreign children from their parents. I hardly see this as an advance in the agenda, raising the dignity of people.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, the timing of this is -- tell me about the timing, why did they do it now?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It comes of the eve of the prolife march, the March for Life, that's when this happened. But very hard for a Catholic like Biden to talk about dignity, respect for the human person, and then enact these policies.\par \par \uc2 In addition to this, there was a Planned Parenthood bailout he also signed. Really bad policies top to bottom.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, so Planned Parenthood gets big money, they get big money. Small businesses that actually produce jobs and help life are struggling. And we're paying for abortions abroad. And they're the moral people.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But you shouldn't worry about any of that, Laura. Close your eyes to how the money is being spent or the lousy policies. The media is serving up marquee malarkey like this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Bunch of malarkey.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There is now an official dictionary approved term for the role, the official role filled by Doug Emhoff, Vice President Kamala Harris' husband. Here it is, "second gentleman."\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Emhoff, the new second gentleman, is just like Jill Biden, continuing to work in his own capacity.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So many Americans are in families that look very different than what may be was leave it to beaver in the 1950s. It's really cathartic and refreshing to see that reflected in the highest office.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Or at least the second highest office in the land, Laura. It's amazing how these reports cover Kamala Harris and her family as if she were already president.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They cover the diversity aspect of things, what her pantsuit looks like. Her daughter gets a modeling contract. That's great, good for them.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Right, her shoes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But there's no coverage of what's coming -- tax hikes, our allies embracing China. But the A.P. just dropped this story, Raymond. Hold your horses, or dogs. Biden's two dogs settle in at the White House.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Oh, my gosh, they are still covering the dogs. Why is this news? Forty executive actions and orders. Do these reporters have something else they might cover, anything? I'm bracing myself for the first kitten spreads coming any day now.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No, Raymond, I think we really have to cover this in depth. If we're going to do dog coverage, I want to know their rabies vaccination. I want to know when they're getting dewormed.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, when are they getting their nails clipped. We want it all.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Isn't it enough that we have to watch Kamala and Joe getting their shots and dewormed. We don't need to see the dogs, I'm sorry. I draw the line here, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, \uc2 Disney\uc2 , your favorite, has announced that it's altering their classic jungle cruise attraction to omit what the "L.A. Times" described as racist stereotypes. What is that about?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: This is like Splash Mountain, Laura, Splash Mountain 2.0. \uc2 Disney\uc2  says it will remove negative depictions of some cultures in that jungle cruise attractions, essentially the natives and the headhunter who appears as a gag at the end of the ride, they're out. But the new edition seemed to my eye more insensitive than what's there now. They will replace the native people with chimps attacking a sunken boat. So, is \uc2 Disney\uc2  saying there were now violent tribes in Africa?\par \par \uc2 And my biggest problem here is this is an attraction that was actually overseen and created by \uc2 Walt Disney\uc2  himself in 1955. Here's how he explained it on TV.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALT DISNEY\uc2 : It all started with an idea that sprang from our true-life adventure film. Jungle resident guaranteed to furnish chills even in the warmest weather is this cannibal chieftain.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, they should have had the founding fathers attack the boats, OK. If it was Thomas Jefferson, that would have been OK.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: This is my problem. If there is a sensitivity to something in these attractions, interpret them, put them in context, but don't rip them apart. \uc2 Walt Disney\uc2  is a 20th century creative vision that has captured the world like Norman Rockwell or Louis Armstrong. You leave it as it is. Let people interpret it through time.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Raymond, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 And Biden inc. is alive and well. We'll explain, the latest ways the family is cashing in on the big Biden name. Fleischer, Bruce, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN PIERRE, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I think Donald Trump and his family, they are a just walking, talking conflict of interest. And they take any opportunity that comes their way to profit off of the presidency.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): I think there are serious grounds and violations of the Emoluments Clause from day one.\par \par \uc2 DON LEMON, CNN HOST: It's OK for nepotism in the Trump family. But it can't happen with the Biden family, even though Biden it was not nepotism. The whole \uc2 Burisma\uc2  thing was not Joe Biden's company. It wasn't Joe Biden's administration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Of course, we know that the Trump family business ended losing money while President Trump was in the White House. However, on the other end of the spectrum, it looks like business is booming for Biden inc. We have long known that Joe's brother James has profited big time off the family name, and last month we learned criminal investigators are looking into James's role in the now bankrupt company Americore Health. And more recently, Joe's other brother, Frank, appeared in an Inauguration Day ad touting his brother's ascendence to the White House. This is despite being warned by his big brother Joe during the campaign to watch yourself. Apparently, frank didn't listen.\par \par \uc2 And then there's Joe's sister Valerie Owens. "The New York Times" reporting that her relationship with him reveals the powerful and integral role that the families played in Mr. Biden's ascent to power. And it's been especially lucrative for Biden's sister.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Ari Fleischer, former White House press secretary and FOX News contributor, and Tammy Bruce, FOX News contributor and host of "Get Tammy Bruce" on FOX Nations. Great to see both of you. Ari, of course, the media is barely paying attention to any of this after years of putting the Trump family in the guillotine.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ARI FLEISCHER, FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: And after how long of ignoring all the evidence about Hunter Biden to the point where they refused to print it. Even a Senate committee that reported that he was getting money from the ex-mayor of Moscow's wife, and they had documents. But the press wouldn't go with it.\par \par \uc2 Look, Laura, the problem here is the Bidens can get away with this. They will get away with it, because the ethical press that used to hold people to account is really one and done. They touch on these stories and then they go off of them. It's not a feeding frenzy. It's not immoral to them. It doesn't bother them. They don't dig in because it's Joe Biden. The fact is, if you are a Biden, keep it up because you can.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tammy, this is what Biden said last year. It was on the campaign trail, asking questions about his family's business ties as president. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No one in my family will have an office in the White House, will sit in on meetings as if they are a cabinet member, will in fact have any business relationship with anyone that relates to a foreign corporation or a foreign country, period. Period. End of story.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Period. Well, except we just learned that Hunter still owns 10 percent of a Chinese equity firm that is also co-owned by the state-run bank ever China. Tammy, are there going to be any follow-ups on this from "The Times" or any of these media people? This is outrageous.\par \par \uc2 TAMMY BRUCE, INDEPENDENT WOMEN'S VOICE PRESIDENT: No. It's up to you. It's up to other responsible journalists to bring it up. And this is why they hate the Internet world where there is a free flow of information, where they can't keep information from the American people at this point.\par \par \uc2 We saw them try during, of course, the election with social media. And in fact, a poll afterwards noted that a good -- I think it was over six percent of Biden voters said they would have changed their vote had they known about the Hunter Biden story. So, it does matter to the American people.\par \par \uc2 And of course, there are ways now for this information to get out. But when it comes to Joe Biden, look, this is a guy who has a plagiarism problem. He has a problem with telling the truth in a certain fashion. And it's only been a week and already the American people are agape at what it is that's been going on. I think it's only a matter of time. I think, though, at the same time, Republicans have to step up. We can't just shrug at this. This is serious. This is influence-peddling. Action has to be taken. And if they don't think the American people care, I think we are very much like the rearview mirror where the objects in the mirror are closer than they appear. And everyone who thinks they are going to get away with this for the next two or four years has another think coming.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Ari, I also want to move on to this. House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy made a trip down to Palm Beach to chat with former President Trump, and the press just went insane. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: McCarthy even made a deeply humiliating, almost too awful to watch pilgrimage down to Mar-a-Lago to kiss the ring of the twice- impeached, possibly soon to be indicted ex-president.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Kevin McCarthy going to see and pay fealty to President Trump.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think its white slavery what I just witnessed from Kevin McCarthy. He looks like he's owned by his master. His master is Donald Trump. It is pathetic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Isn't this the same thing, Ari, they said about Trump and Putin? Poodle, owned by. It's the same lingo, different scenario. This is kind of all they've got.\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: The lingo is Hillary Clinton's lingo about the deplorables. What they're saying is the 74 million Americans who voted for Donald Trump, the siege of the Capitol notwithstanding, but the 74 million Americans who voted for Donald Trump, it doesn't matter. All those people are terrible people, so why would Congressman McCarthy do anything that could appeal to half the country? This is what they're saying.\par \par \uc2 And this, Laura, is why politics is so lousy and so raw right now, lack of respect for other people's opinions. Yes, there are 74 million good people, intelligent people, smart people who intelligently voted for Donald Trump. And there's nothing wrong with that. And there's nothing wrong with talking to President Trump. So Republican success is through addition, not subtraction, and I am glad that Congressman McCarthy is doing whatever he can to keep our party together.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Tammy, really quick, AOC said she doesn't want to work with Ted Cruz. She said she'll work with other Republicans, but he just should resign. So, do they really care about bipartisan, or is this just brand building at this point?\par \par \uc2 BRUCE: Again, it's unity within their own party. That's what they're building on. Everyone else can go to hell as far as they're concerned. And that was of course after she accused him of attempted murder of her. And that is what Ari has mentioned. Of course, the smearing of the 74 million Americans. This is the boiling down of the destruction of the individual in this country, and the smearing of everyone who is the opposition. It may work in Venezuela, in the former Soviet Union, and in Cuba. It is not going to work in the United States. It is a very dangerous dismissal of the American sensibility.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's what China does as well, erase history and then demonize all opponents, then ultimately jail them. Tammy and Ari, have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 And a couple of bittersweet goodbyes, I'm going to cry, in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I've told you many times that, although my name is on this show, this show would not be possible without the great team that supports it every single night. And we have two departures from THE INGRAHAM ANGLE family. We're going to miss them both very much. Our great producer Allie (ph) Holston (ph), she will be going on to of course bigger and better things. And Molly Ringel who has been with us for two years and very close with the Washington staff, THE INGRAHAM ANGLE, both great gals. We're going to miss you both. Thanks for everything you have done for us.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020210130eh1t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 White House Press Briefing Underway; Biden Signs New Exec Orders In Unprecedented Blitz; McConnell Slams Biden Executive Orders\b0\par\par\uc2 Trace Gallagher, Raymond Arroyo, Joe Concha, Byron York, Jeff  Paul, Jacqui Heinrich, Lara Logan \par \uc2 9,449 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 January 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Well, the president talked about his commitment to putting together a commission for reunifying families. He remains committed to that, and we'll have more to share on that soon. Go ahead, all the way in back.\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL KRAHENBUHL, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, GLOBO NEWS T.V., BRAZIL: Thank you so much, Jen. Raquel Krahenbuhl from T.V. Global Brazil. I have two questions. Going back to the climate crisis, during the campaign, President Biden warned of economic consequences if Brazil did not reduce deforestation on the Amazon. I'm wondering what kind of consequences he's considering, if it could hurt, for example, the trading negotiations with Brazil or the \uc2 OECD\uc2  support?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, we just announced yesterday that we're going to be doing a climate summit coming up in the next couple of months.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, this is a big priority for President Biden. And that's why he asked his good friend former Secretary Kerry to lead our climate international effort. And, certainly, Brazil will be a key partner in that.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of what those negotiations and discussions look like, it's a little too early for that at this point. And I'd certainly send you to the State Department and Secretary Kerry's office for more details.\par \par \uc2 KRAHENBUHL: OK, so just a follow-up. Did he invite the Brazilian president, will he invite for the summit? And is also President Biden still planning to label countries as climate outlaws? And if so, would Brazil be part of this list?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, we're not quite there yet, given we just announced it. There haven't been invites or anything that went out. But we're happy to keep everybody updated. And, certainly, as the United States is a large emitter, so is Brazil, so is China, so are many large countries and large economies, and we all have a role to play in addressing this crisis.\par \par \uc2 KRAHENBUHL: One more question, Jen. When does President Biden think he will be able to start doing -- or he will do his first international trip? And where it would be the first place he would go?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I don't have anything to preview or predict for you at this point. As you know, we haven't done any domestic travel yet, given COVID. So, I certainly don't have any predictions about a foreign trip.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead, Sabrina.\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SIDDIQUI, WHITE HOUSE REPORTER, THE WALL STREET JOURNAL: Sure, I had a question. At her confirmation hearing yesterday, U.N. ambassador- designate Linda Thomas-Greenfield said she believed that BDS, the \uc2 Boycott, Divestment, Sanctions movement\uc2  to pressure Israel, is unacceptable and borders on -- or verges on anti-Semitism. Is that a characterization that the White House shares when it comes to the \uc2 BDS movement\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I don't have anything more for you on kind of our position. It's, I think, been -- the president has spoken to this as a candidate and prior as vice president, but I don't have anything new. And I'm happy to get back to you if there's anything I can get to you from the national security team. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 ROB CRILLY, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: Thank you. Rob Crilly with The Washington Examiner. Just to actually follow up on that, Linda Thomas-Greenfield also said she would be standing against the unfair targeting of Israel, the (INAUDIBLE) resolutions that are proposed against Israel (INAUDIBLE). Is that the position of the White House?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: You know, I didn't see her full hearing yesterday. Actually, I didn't see any of her hearing. I'm happy to review it and take a look and see if there's more we can speak to from our end.\par \par \uc2 CRILLY: (INAUDIBLE) back to the executive orders, the questions we were discussing at the start. So, I'm still trying to sort of reconcile this daily -- what are we at? I think we're at 26 orders now and reconcile that with the campaign rhetoric, because, in October, Joe Biden said there were limitations on the use of executive actions, things he can't do by executive order, unless you're a dictator. We're a democracy. We need consensus.\par \par \uc2 I think you said earlier that some of these executive orders were being used to roll back some of the immoral things that the previous administration had done. If you're calling these things immoral, is that seeking consensus and unity? And then also, does it suggest that President Biden sees himself as perhaps a benevolent dictator?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, Rob, I'm sure this wasn't your intention, but I think you took the president's comments a bit out of context.\par \par \uc2 He was asked about tax reform, and whether that could be done via executive order during the interview with George Stephanopoulos, which you're referring to.\par \par \uc2 CRILLY: (INAUDIBLE) question, but I think the answer --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I think -- I think the question is pretty important context for everybody. And he said no.\par \par \uc2 And the president also said during an interview with columnists back in December that he didn't think executive action should be used for everything. And that certainly is his point of view.\par \par \uc2 But there are steps, including overturning some of the harmful, detrimental and, yes, immoral actions of the prior administration that he felt he could not wait to overturn. And that's exactly what he did.\par \par \uc2 Now, any historian will tell you that he walked into the presidency at one of the most difficult moments in history. That required additional executive action in order to take -- get immediate relief to the American people.\par \par \uc2 But he believes, as is law -- as everybody knows how a bill becomes a law, is that, in order to make action and policy permanent, you need to work with Congress. That's why he's also proposed a COVID relief package, a big, bold package. Some say very big -- I agree with that -- and also an immigration bill.\par \par \uc2 He has not -- he has not held back or delayed putting forth legislation either. So, he is going to use the levers that every president in history has used, executive actions. But he also feels it is important to work Congress, and not just one party, but both parties to get things done.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead in the back.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you, Jen. A couple of questions. One technical question on the E.O. signed today. Does the --does the E.O. on ACA open the marketplace to noncitizens, to legal residents or undocumented immigrants that may be able to benefit from the extra care?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It doesn't change the requirements for qualifying for the Affordable Care Act. It opens it up for three months for people who would be already eligible for the Affordable Care Act. So -- but, obviously, there are -- you know, the Health and Human Services team will continue to look for ways to expand access.\par \par \uc2 I will say -- you didn't ask this, but this is a common question, so I'm just going to proactively answer it -- that we do feel, as an administration, that ensuring that all people in the United States, undocumented immigrants as well, of course, should receive access to a vaccine, because that one is morally right, but also ensures that people in the country are also safe.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And that was my second question.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Oh, all right, good.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How are you guiding the states --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Actually, yes. OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How are you guiding the states?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It's a common one.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes. How are you guiding the states, if any -- in any way to get those undocumented communities to come forward to get vaccinated? Many of them in the states just don't have any way to prove that they're residents of those states in order to go to their local clinics and prove residency to get the vaccine. So, how are you working with the states to get that done?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It's challenging. We have talked about this a little bit in here. I mean, there's obviously vaccine hesitancy, which certainly exists in communities of undocumented immigrants and the concern of what it will mean for their status.\par \par \uc2 And -- but we are focused on ensuring we get the vaccine out broadly into communities across the country, because that will make the American people safer. And it's something we have to be both creative about, aggressive about, ambitious about. It's one of the reasons we need additional funding through the American Rescue Plan, but one -- also one of the reasons why we plan to have an aggressive public education campaign to help embolden and empower local doctors and authorities.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Let me ask one more on Cuba. Do you guys plan on starting -- walking back all of those restrictions that were put into place under the Trump administration, after the Obama administration had opened things up with Cuba?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, our Cuba policy is governed by two principles. First, support for democracy and human rights. That will be at the core of our efforts. Second is Americans, especially Cuban Americans, are the best ambassadors for freedom in Cuba. So, we'll review the Trump administration policies, as we are in a number of other areas of national security, with an eye to assure -- ensuring that our approach is aligned with that. But, you know, we will take our own path. I don't -- I don't have anything to predict for you at this point in time.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead, (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I wanted to clarify something. You talked a little bit about the administration's stance on school re-openings yesterday.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Sure.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I wasn't -- I wasn't really clear on that. So, I got a little better information.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (INAUDIBLE) indicated that he believes, that the administration believes that schools need more funding for them to be safe enough to reopen for students to go back.\par \par \uc2 But there's been a number of studies, including the recent one out of the CDC, that have shown evidence that schools are perfectly safe for students to go back. And there were recommendations that they should go back.\par \par \uc2 So, this seems like a bit of a dispute between teachers' unions and sort of data and the science. So, where does the administration stand in that dispute?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, first, let me be absolutely clear. President Biden wants schools to open and he wants them to stay open, because it's obviously very disruptive for families -- I'm a mother -- you know, for anyone has kids, for kids to come in and out of school.\par \par \uc2 But that means making sure, as you said, that every school is able to have the equipment and resources to open safely, not just rural schools or private schools, which often is where a lot of the school re-openings and schools staying open is happening. It's more of a challenge in public schools, where they don't have that funding from tuition or smaller populations, where it's easier to put in place the actions needed to keep the schools open.\par \par \uc2 But the CDC study, which I know has gotten a lot -- received a lot of attention, was based on kind of an area that was more rural in Wisconsin. It was not -- and I think what Dr. Walensky has said is that -- I think she said this on CNN last night -- is that for areas where they are more populated, where schools where there is a lot more foot traffic, that there are going to need to be a lot of steps put in place in order to make the schools reopening safe.\par \par \uc2 But we are committed to doing that. That's why the President signed an executive order last week supporting the safe reopening of schools. It requires the Departments of Education and CDC to provide evidence- based guidance. So, that will include things like testing, smaller class sizes, more ventilation, better cleaning, PPE, strong state and local public health guidance.\par \par \uc2 But we want to provide that clarity from the federal government, because there's a lot of confusion. And public schools especially don't know what steps they need to take, how to ensure they're safe. Those are all decisions that are going to be made locally. But we want to give them the guidance. That's in process. Our team is working on that, Dr. Walensky at the CDC and our health experts. And we hope to have more specifics soon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Maybe two more questions.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, Americans' savings rate is -- has been up throughout the pandemic. And so, I'm wondering, why give all Americans a $1,400 check? And why not means test for the relief that your administration is talking about?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, first, the proposed checks, I mean, the -- have the same criteria as the package that received broad bipartisan support in December, right? So, approximately, the $1,400 checks would go to families making approximately $150,000 a year. There's a scaling up, as I think you're referencing, up to about $300,000. I think you all are aware of this, but just for everybody else.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, there has been some questions about that. President Biden, our economic team, we're open to discussing that. But we want to ensure that the families that need relief, that need assistance get it. And I would say, at a time where we saw the economic data today -- and this was reflected in Brian Deese's statement -- it was worse than any week -- I don't want to misquote myself here -- that we saw during the Great Recession.\par \par \uc2 I mean, people are suffering. Thousands -- millions of people are out of work. People don't know how to put food on the table. These challenges are real. We -- the data shows it, but we also see long lines for food pantries. We see, you know, people who are out of work and don't know what they're going to do to make ends meet. And so that's tangible. That's real. And we want the package -- the package is going to need to be big and bold to address that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And then since we have been talking about women's health and access to abortion today, does the administration plan to suspend the requirement that the medication abortion pill be picked up in person? I know that that's something that advocates have been pushing for after the reversal under the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes, I have certainly seen some reporting and questions about that. I will have to talk to our health team about that specifically.\par \par \uc2 KRAHENBUHL: Can I ask one more about the travel restrictions?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Sure. Go ahead. And, Kaitlan, then I will go to you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KRAHENBUHL: A lot of our viewers are asking if there will be any kind of consideration for waivers for students or unmarried couple like fiances, since there is now the requirement for testing and quarantine?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I know there's been a lot of coverage of this and questions, because this is -- these are people's lives and human beings who are missing their loved ones and their family members. And just to restate what the requirements are now, so spouses of American citizens and lawful permanent residents are able to travel under these restrictions we have put in place on international travel, although they have to take part in the mandatory pre-departure testing that applies to everyone.\par \par \uc2 And we are reviewing and assessing, I should say, how students and unmarried couples -- and how we can proceed with that moving forward. I don't have anything to preview for you. But we're certainly -- our team is looking into that.\par \par \uc2 KRAHENBUHL: (INAUDIBLE) criteria. Why countries with high numbers like Russia or India or Mexico are not on the list?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, our health and medical team takes a close look at what restrictions we need to put in place in order to keep the American people safe. And that's how we review it. It's not about geopolitics. It was at times in the prior administration. That's not how we approach things. It's about where we need to put restrictions in place to keep the American people safe.\par \par \uc2 I promised Kaitlan she would get the next one.\par \par \uc2 KAITLAN COLLINS, CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, CNN: So, just to get some clarification on the CDC study about schools being good to reopen if precautions are taking, is the White House position that it needs to be further studied, since you're citing just the one school in Wisconsin, or does the White House agree with the recommendations from the CDC about schools reopening?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, the CDC hasn't issued the formal recommendations or requirements on how all schools across the country can open.\par \par \uc2 They did a report, as they do reports frequently, based on an area in Wisconsin, important -- interesting data, no doubt. But that is not reflective of every school district and community in the country.\par \par \uc2 And so, they will be coming out with more specific requirements -- or more specific, I should say, guidelines for the public, because there are schools that are in much more highly populated areas. There are schools that have far more kids in classrooms, far more kids walking into the school. And they want to ensure that there's clarity on testing needs, class size needs, you know, public health guidance that people can abide by.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, their recommendation is that K through 12 schools should be the last settings to close after all of these mitigation measures have been employed and the first to reopen.\par \par \uc2 So, we all want schools to open and be open for good. What I'm reflecting on -- or what I'm trying to clarify, I guess, is that, you know, schools in cities are dealing with different challenges than schools in rural communities, and schools in Wisconsin may be dealing in different challenges than schools in, you know -- you know, downtown Atlanta, right?\par \par \uc2 So, there needs to be kind of -- the CDC will issue more specific guidelines soon. They're focused on it now.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: OK. And last question on this. So, President Biden has said he wants all schools to reopen in his first 100 days. Is that still the goal if Congress does not pass this proposal, which has the money for schools, which Ron Klain and others have said is what they believe schools need to reopen?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I sense skepticism about the bill getting passed. We don't feel that. Look, I think the reason --\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: We have to go through everything.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I know. That's your job. Look, I think, Kaitlan, one of the reasons why we're so focused on the American Rescue Plan and getting it urgently passed through Congress is because we need this funding in order for schools to reopen and for them to have the support to ensure that they have proper ventilation, better cleaning, testing, PPE.\par \par \uc2 That's a key component of the package. And that's one of the reasons we're not going to negotiate out that versus vaccine funding or, you know, unemployment insurance, because that's a key component. And we need that to ensure that schools will have the resources across the country to be able to reopen. And that's exactly what the President wants to happen.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Thank you, Jen.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Thank you. Oh, OK, we're good. She was very patient. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you. I just wonder if there is a general sense of frustration just about the pace of things, you know, given the vaccine distribution, you know, some of what you inherited from the Trump administration, trying to roll out executive orders, you know, working with Congress, and sort of having some lawmakers raise concerns.\par \par \uc2 Is it harder to change things than you all thought it would be?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I'm only laughing because we're on our eighth day here. So, you know -- and in that period of time, the President has signed multiple executive orders. He has proposed several comprehensive bills. He's had many conversations with members of Congress about the urgency of getting things done.\par \par \uc2 And I will say, in those conversations, there is an understanding and agreement about the urgency of addressing these issues. So, no, we're actually pretty hopeful and optimistic about the opportunity and the ability to work with Democrats and Republicans to get packages through to help bring relief to the American public.\par \par \uc2 But we also recognize that members of Congress have -- they're not wallflowers. They have different points of view. They have lots of ideas. They're going to bring those forward. We're hearing them. And we're just eager to move things forward as quickly as we can. But we're on day eight. So, we are confident we're still on a pretty rapid pace here. Thanks, everyone.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I appreciate all of you. I'll be in my office. Thanks for your time, and we'll do this again tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, there you have Press Secretary Jen Psaki wrapping up the White House press briefing. Good afternoon, everyone. I'm Trace Gallagher, in for Martha MacCallum, and this is THE STORY.\par \par \uc2 The Press Secretary question today about President Biden's ever-growing list of executive orders and actions. Now remember, just three months ago, Biden railed against using the presidential pen without Congressional approval.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have this strange notion. We are a democracy. Some of my Republican friends and some of my Democratic friends even occasionally say, well, if you can't get the votes by executive order, you're going to do something. Things you can't do by executive order unless you're a dictator. We're a democracy. We need consensus.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: But Biden has been acting quickly to reverse the Trump agenda while the progressive wing of his party makes its own push.\par \par \uc2 Just this week, reports indicate he is beginning of commission on Supreme Court reform. Progressives have been pushing to pack the court, and today, a group of House Democrats including the squad urged the President to get on board with recurring COVID stimulus cash payments.\par \par \uc2 It all comes as just hours ago, the President signed executive orders to rollback President Trump's policies on abortion and Obamacare. Let's get reaction to all this from Joe Concha, media opinion columnist from The Hill, Raymond Arroyo, he is a EWTN News managing editor, and Byron York, chief political correspondent for The Washington Examiner, all are Fox News Contributors. Gentlemen, welcome to you all.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if you heard -- Byron York, let's start with you -- Jen Psaki saying earlier that, you know, the President is looking for bipartisan support on various measures, but if he doesn't get them, they're more than happy to push things through with reconciliation. Karl Rove wrote in The Wall Street Journal, quoting here, "It looks increasingly as if the new president offers soothing words about bipartisanship and then delivers policy prescriptions favored by the left. Let's hope Mr. Biden changes direction by finding the actions to match his comforting words. If not, Republicans must find ways to rein in his leftward movement."\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, actually, it's going to be very hard. I don't think anything that Biden has done so far, using the executive authority has been a surprise because he said he was going to do it. And there has been taking a longer view to kind of ratcheting up effect using executive authority. Barack Obama did it a lot, especially after he lost the House in 2010 and then the Senate in 2014. And then, Donald Trump, who faced just absolute stonewalling Congress for lots of things, found ways to use his executive authority to do other things like build the border wall. And now, it's no surprise that Joe Biden is doing exactly the same thing. There's -- look, you heard Jen Psaki say a number of times, hey, we've only been here eight days. There's no way he could have gotten any of that done without just using sign it and there it is executive authority.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. And you know, Joe Concha, your Op Ed in The Hill just dropped a little bit ago, and you write in this thing, and we heard President Biden just kind of refer to this a couple of months ago. "You can't legislate by executive order unless you're a dictator." You say the 2020 version of Joe Biden said three months ago. That guy should say hello to 2021 Biden sometime and ask what exactly has changed since his pre- election remarks, Joe?\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, and White House Press Secretary Jen Psaki really may need a chiropractor after this particular answer when she was asked about it by a Washington Examiner reporter or correspondent there, quote, "President Biden is going to use the levers of every president in history has used to get things done." Without any degree or context around those comments, obviously, Trace, because the 40 executive orders that President Biden has issued in just one week is 36 more than the guy he was referring to as a dictator in then-President Trump. It is 35 more than that of his former boss, Barack Obama in his first week. It is 40 more than George W. Bush issued in his first week.\par \par \uc2 And even the New York Times now is saying that the president needs to ease up and work through Congress because it seems now that the administration is not weighing the consequences of their actions, particularly when it comes to the Keystone pipeline where 11,000 full and temporary jobs are going to be gone during a pandemic when jobs are at a premium. The backlash is already coming in. He controls Congress, his party, Joe Biden does, and the Senate work with them. Executive fiat is never a good look, particularly at a degree we have never seen before. This is executive orders on five-hour energy meet steroids, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, Raymond Arroyo, you know, I kind of read your talking points. I was fascinated when you said, you know, it -- Raymond, you say, Biden's call for unity is like Darth Vader urging Luke to join him to rule the universe together. You care to expand on that a bit?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Wow.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: I do stand by that. Because, you know, you can have unity, but you got to be careful who's asking for that unity. And you can -- you can give away a lot of good in the transfer. Look, Jen Psaki during this presser made it seem as if Joe Biden is some saint delivering us from, you know, this demonic reign of his predecessor. She said he was rolling -- he's rolling back immoral actions.\par \par \uc2 Now, bear in mind, Joe Biden just moments ago, signed away the Mexico City policy, which now allows the United States taxpayer to pay for abortions abroad. So, just to clarify this for the viewer, Joe Biden and his administration were very concerned about separating foreign children from their parents at the border. But now, the U.S. taxpayer is going to fund the permanent separation of these children from their parents. Let that sink in for a moment. This is a -- this is a terrifying moment in some ways. And there should have been debate, particularly over something truly moral and grave like this.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I'm getting low on time. I want to get all three of you back in again. To you first, Raymond, since we've got you riled up here, what do you make of the President's staffing commission to look at reforming the Supreme Court among other courts?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, look, he -- there was a lot of talk about this during the campaign. This commission is now going to come together. I don't think it's a -- it's a foregone conclusion, what Bill they'll come up with. I assume they're going to say, let's expand these appellate courts. Let's expand the Supreme Court, and that will take away some of the raft of appointments that Mitch McConnell and Donald Trump were able to repopulate that bench with over the last few years. They need that to keep some of these policies in place.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Byron, Mitch McConnell said this about Biden's executive orders targeting fossil fuel. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): One welder from Pipeliners Local 798 who've been working in Nebraska, says, he's already had to lay off his whole team before losing his job himself. He said he sat down in his truck and simply cried. One million lost jobs by next year alone. It's a heck of a way to kick off a presidency.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And John Kerry says, come on, if you lose your job, you just get another career by, you know, building solar power panels. Byron?\par \par \uc2 YORK: Yes. Well, listen, Joe Biden, again, said he was going to do this sort of thing. And this is going to be, for him, an absolute disaster in terms of job creation. Now, one more thing on the -- on the court thing, packing the courts is something that will have to be done through legislation. And it is absolutely impossible without killing the filibuster. Never ever, ever happen unless they could kill the filibuster first.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, I want to play this for you, Joe Concha. This is the media and Democrats and their anti-unity montage, and then I'll get your final thoughts.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: All right.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think a lot of people, particularly the Democratic Party are out for blood after the Trump years. They want to have some payback on this.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We've done away with horse whipping in this country. But what I think should happen to him is that Josh Hawley needs to be expelled from the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-CA): There are legitimate white supremacist sympathizers that sit at the heart and at the core of the Republican caucus in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: The heart and the core of the Republican caucus. Your final thoughts, Joe.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: When you speak in such hyperbole, Trace, it will have a boomerang effect, because it's just too far out there. And Mark Penn did a great poll right after President Trump was elected in 2017. Mark Penn, of course, was a senior adviser to Bill Clinton later, Hillary Clinton, and he found that 91 percent of Americans said they want the two parties to compromise. So, we think we're divided, and we think we're so partisan right now. But in the end, the people that are pragmatic that want this country to move forward, nine out of 10 say, get together, compromise, and we haven't seen any of that right now. And that kind of rhetoric that we hear from Congresspeople and people that have microphones that are on national television have no place in public discourse, quite frankly, if this is the goal to further divide, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Joe Concha, Raymond Arroyo, Byron York, great conversation, gentlemen. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Thanks, Trace.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Now, a story exclusive with former Republican New York Governor George Pataki, after a shock report detailing the gross undercounting of COVID deaths in nursing homes under current Governor Andrew Cuomo. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, New York's official count of more than 8000 nursing home deaths could be up to 50% higher bringing the potential tally closer to 13,000, maybe more. That's according to a bombshell report from the New York Attorney General's Office revealing the state is severely under counted. It also addresses Governor Cuomo's controversial mandate for nursing homes to take in COVID-19 patients finding a "may have put residents at increased risk of harm in some facilities."\par \par \uc2 Here now exclusively former New York Governor George Pataki. Governor, good to have you come on, sir. We appreciate it. I find the numbers here striking as I'm sure you do, 50% of COVID deaths under counted. The state attorney general said this, the following, as the pandemic and our investigation continue, it is imperative that we understand why the residents of nursing homes in New York unnecessarily being the key word suffered at such an alarming rate. And yet, there have been little if any consequences. I mean, you have the governor getting awards and praise, and book deals. Your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE PATAKI (R), FORMER NEW YORK GOVERNOR: Trace, this is a new low. For months, legislators, everyone has been trying to get those numbers from the governor's office and they've just stonewall. And now we know why. And the reason is because they were misrepresenting it from the beginning.\par \par \uc2 You know, I know a little bit about leaving in a crisis and you want to do two things. You want to make the right decisions or if you make a decision that you question, you change it quickly. And the second thing is, you want to communicate honestly with the people.\par \par \uc2 Both of those fronts, the Cuomo administration has failed miserably. They made the wrong decision on nursing homes. They refuse to correct it when it was pointed out, and now it appears they've lied to the people of New York about what those consequences actually were. This is inexcusable.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. We should know that Governor Cuomo, his office have not yet responded to this report, but the governor did say this yesterday on a different network. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW CUOMO (D), NEW YORK GOVERNOR: Incompetent government kills people. Incompetent government kills people. More people died than needed to die in COVID, that's the truth. Incompetent government can kill people. This is not a joke, and more people died here than needed to.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: He's not talking about himself but he is correct, incompetent government can kill people.\par \par \uc2 PATAKI: Trace, that's one of the few things Governor Cuomo got right. And he should have been looking at the mirror when he said it. Because he's the one who dictated that nursing homes take COVID positive patients, when they were desperately saying they were not qualified and not capable of dealing with it.\par \par \uc2 I remember I was one of the first to say this is idiotic, and it's going to kill people. I went on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  for 47 straight days, I was on there saying stop the madness, stop killing our most vulnerable and he wouldn't change the policy. And then finally at the end, it just quietly was reversed without any comment.\par \par \uc2 But it's not just that you look at today. Today, less than half of nursing home staff in New York State have been vaccinated. You know, they had a priority on nursing home staff in the beginning you get because of the incompetence. It just came out today that less than half of the vaccinated is nursing homes, the staffs.\par \par \uc2 This has just been a disaster from the beginning. It's kind of ironic that the press looks at him and says, what a great job when all you have to do is look at the fact that we have almost twice as many deaths as in Florida, and see how incompetently the quality administration has handled this crisis.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: New York Congresswoman Elise Stefanik, said the following, quoting here, "This is a massive corruption scandal at the highest level of New York State government. There needs to be subpoenas issued immediately of Cuomo and his entire senior team on all documentation and internal communications on how they orchestrated to hide these numbers from the people of New York." The question is, will there be consequences in your estimation for this kind of governing? Last thoughts.\par \par \uc2 PATAKI: Congresswoman Stefanik is absolutely right. There has to be an investigation. There has to be subpoenas. It has to be the state legislature. It has to be the attorney general. And I believe federal officials should look at this as well.\par \par \uc2 You can't trust the former administration to investigate itself. They did that at the beginning. They conducted their own internal investigation and concluded their policy of putting COVID positives in nursing home patients wasn't a problem. It was the staff that was the problem. It was just a cover up. Now, we know that the numbers have been covered up. This demands a subpoena investigation to hold them accountable. And I hope it comes quickly and I hope it's aggressive.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Former Governor George Pataki, thank you for coming on, governor. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 PATAKI: Thank you, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And coming up next, the devastating impact of remote learning on students mental health as parents across the country grow impatient.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You're a bunch of cowards, hiding behind our children as an excuse for keeping schools closed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Former Texas Congressman and physician Dr. Ron Paul here exclusively on "The Story," next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The garbage workers who pick up my freaking trash risked their lives every day, more than anyone in the school system, figure it out or get off the podium. Because you know what, there are people like me and a lot of other people out there who will gladly take your seat and figure it out. It's not a high bar, raise a freaking bar.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: A father in Virginia capturing the pain of parents all across America following a temporary return to fully remote learning. The CDC says it can be safe to let kids back in school with safety measures as we learn more about the devastating impact the pandemic is having on children's mental health.\par \par \uc2 In moments, reaction from former Texas Congressman and Physician, Ron Paul, but first to Jeff Paul. He's live for us in Los Angeles. Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Trace. Virtual learning has certainly taken its toll on not only parents, but you just saw there a little bit ago, but also students in the nation's fifth largest school district which includes the Las Vegas area. It's reported at least 18 students have died from suicide in a nine-month span. That district is now working to speed reopening up of schools.\par \par \uc2 It's a heartbreaking reality that's happening all across America, Trevor Taylor, who you see here, was an 18-year-old student, an athlete, a musician in Illinois. He committed suicide last October. And his mother says she went 100% believes he died because of the isolation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LISA MOORE, SON COMMITTED SUICIDE: I tell parents, helicopter the heck out of your kids. Watch them. Watch for any change, any slight change in their behavior, make opportunities for them to see their friends, do whatever you have to. I don't want any other mother or father, or parent, or sibling to have to go through this. It's horrific.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J. PAUL: The CDC is now talking about a return to the classroom saying there is evidence showing it can be safe. They noted mask would have to be mandatory and there needs to be strict social distancing. CDC researchers also note the kinds of spreads they've seen in crowded offices or nursing homes haven't been reported in schools. Dr. Anthony Fauci agrees.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI, BIDEN CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISE: What the science tells us is the following, that children in school right now, it appears that if you follow the safety guidelines of the CDC, that they are less likely to be getting infected than the community rate of infection.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J. PAUL: The Biden ministration today clarifying that schools in different areas with higher populations might face different challenges with reopenings. They have set a goal of getting most K through eight students back into the classroom in this first 100 days. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Jeff Paul live for us in Los Angeles. Jeff, thank you. Let's bring in former Texas Congressman and Physician Ron Paul. Congressman, Dr. Paul, thank you for joining us, sir. The gentleman we showed at the top, the parent in Loudoun County, Virginia who's so upset. The reason partly he's upset is because they've got 6,000 teachers in Loudon County who have already had their first round of vaccinations, and they still won't go back to the classroom even though the CDC says the five studies that they have cited, show there is very low risk of transmission. Parents are frustrated, and a lot of people think they have a right to be.\par \par \uc2 RON PAUL (R), FORMER TEXAS CONGRESSMAN: It's such a tragedy because the suicide and the death (inaudible) school is just tragic. It's preventable, and they don't change. They're even talking about maybe we will let them back in but they'll have to have the mask and the social distancing. It's not going to do it.\par \par \uc2 They should look to the statistics of the states that never locked him out of their schools, and they've done quite well. It doesn't stop the disease but it stops the complication. Well, we've suffered from it the most of past year. I think 90% of it has been the lockdown. The economic crisis has come, the economic consequences. And right now, they're talking about what we have to.\par \par \uc2 You know, look at what happened to the elderly in the nursing homes in New York. Everybody knows that story, but there's stories out now that the elderly are going to be the most vulnerable to get the vaccine because their immune systems are down, so government management of a delicate situation of a medical problem where it shouldn't be patient, the doctor making decisions. It's a tragedy. It was preventable. It's really sad.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I know you're a doctor, I know you're concerned about these kids health, their mental health specifically. We talked about the suicide rate in Las Vegas, 18 suicides in nine months. I want to put these numbers on the screen for you, Dr. Paul, because I think it's important to know that mental health related visits for children increased during the pandemic. Ages 5 to 11 up 24%, ages 12 to 17, up 31%. There is clearly an impact on our children across the country by remote learning.\par \par \uc2 R. PAUL: Oh, there's no doubt about it. And these parents are the only thing left to try to wake people up and that, you know, the dad was pretty upset than he should be. And that's what's going to have to happen, have to have a lot more people, you know, get excited about this to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 But right now, it looks like it's going to continue, but there's some special interest behind so many of the problems that have come out of lockdown. And when you think about it, who's the strongest defenders of gifted kids and keep the kids out of school forever. And we know where that comes from.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 R. PAUL: It happens to be the teachers themselves. That's a shame, why do you want people like that being the teachers.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: The Teachers Unions have a great deal of influence in a lot of these states around the country. I want to play the sound bite because you're a doctor, I want to get this I want to play Dr. Fauci and the contradictory information we're getting watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: If you have a physical covering with one layer, you put another layer on it just makes common sense that it likely would be more effective. And that's the reason why you see people either double masking or doing a version of an N95.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROCHELLE WALENSKY, DIRECTOR, CDC: I do think that if everybody is wearing a mask, if you're wearing it, if you're six feet apart, and you're wearing -- anybody else who you might be with is also wearing a mask, that you have enough protective efficacy in the barriers of those two masks and the space between you, then you probably don't need it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Fauci says double mask, the CDC director, the new one, says you need a single mask. Is this contradictory information we have been getting from the start? Your final thoughts about 15 seconds, Dr. Paul.\par \par \uc2 R. PAUL: Well, I would say for a gentleman who's making the most money of anybody in the government in the bureaucracy, he's giving out bad information is not very scientific to talk like that. Today, there was somebody came out and says if who's good, I recommend and this was a sign, you know, because I don't call on much of assignment.\par \par \uc2 He says, make it for if it's not (inaudible) a group that mask it didn't ask the mask have not prevented any significant disease.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Congressman, former congressman, Dr. Paul, thank you, sir, for coming on.\par \par \uc2 R. PAUL: Sure thing, thanks.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, Lara Logan on the reversal to Trump era immigration policies in the pipeline. That's coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: You come to the United States and you make your case that you've seek asylum based on the following premise, why I deserve it under American law. They're sitting in squalor on the other side of the river.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, several immigration orders are on the back burner until next week, including President Biden's plans for a task force to reunite families separated at the border, and an increase in the number of refugees allowed into the US. National Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has the details from Capitol Hill. Jacqui?\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon to you, Trace. Well, President Biden doesn't want to repeat President Obama's mistake of waiting too long to pursue comprehensive immigration reform with the midterms on the horizon. So now, Democrats are breaking up a bill that Biden revealed his first day in office into smaller pieces that could get bipartisan support to strike while the iron is hot. And one of the first pieces we might see is a bill dealing with TPS DREAMers and DACA.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Senator \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2  is working with Republican Senator Lindsey Graham on a package modeled off previous legislation they introduced in 2019 that would provide permanent residency and a path to citizenship for immigrants brought to the US as children who meet education and work requirements. Now, the proposal may include some of the items the Biden package proposed, like automatic green cards for those groups, but the talks are still early.\par \par \uc2 Durbin told me the hope is that this base bill could be expedited and serve as a kicking off point for broader negotiations. And Senator Graham also told Fox he wants to work in border security provisions, but said comprehensive reform will be an uphill battle because Democrats stopped building the wall.\par \par \uc2 Now, one Democratic senator told me they've got support from a few quote "hardline" Republicans who you wouldn't expect, and because the country just spent billions of dollars on border security, there's more support than previous years to do something about DACA and DREAMers.\par \par \uc2 But still, there's plenty of pushback from Republicans, Senator Rick Scott wrote to the acting DHS Secretary yesterday, "Securing our border is the most important thing we can do to protect our citizens and the thousands of individuals working their way through the legal immigration process. We can make much needed reforms to our broken system, but it has to start with securing the border."\par \par \uc2 And the White House tells me that Biden has not all out endorsed this but understands that immigration cannot be an all or nothing approach. And in the meantime, those executive orders Biden was expected to sign tomorrow have been delayed until next week. Future reforms include establishing that task force for families separated at the border and also increasing refugee admissions. Trace\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Jacqui Heinrich live for us on Capitol Hill, Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox Nation Host Lara Logan. Her new series, "Laura Logan Has No Agenda Return to the Border" starts Monday, February 1st.\par \par \uc2 Lara, it's always great to see you. As always your reporting on this is exceptional, very compelling. I want to play a clip of it, and then get your response on the other side. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LARA LOGAN, HOST: In Biden administration, you're not going have a job.\par \par \uc2 MARK MORGAN, BORDER PATROL COMMISSIONER: Correct.\par \par \uc2 LOGAN: There's also no deportations for 100 days, what else?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: Oh, it keeps going. If you look at the entire immigration strategy that has been talked about, it will systematically undo everything that's been done for the past four years, that has absolutely been successful.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. I mean, talking with Mark Morgan there, he's right. I mean, illegal immigration, they're still coming through, I mean, there's still some of the drugs coming through, but not at the levels that we have seen. Lara, what are your thoughts on this?\par \par \uc2 LOGAN: Well, this is their reality, the warning orders that border agencies have been given under the new guidelines from the new administration will shut down all of the internal checkpoints. So, the last line of defense that's about, you know, 80 miles from the border, for example, will go first. It will move all of those resources, they're preparing for a surge of people.\par \par \uc2 So, the new administration's plan is built around expecting, you know, thousands and thousands of people to come into the United States. The special units like hospitals, and boat units, and ATVs, those will be taken off their normal duties, their security operations. And they'll be put with, along with most of the other resources on migrant care, right?\par \par \uc2 So, what you're doing is defining the border in terms of immigration, as opposed to border security, or as opposed to an approach that, you know, sort of that balances the both of them.\par \par \uc2 And this is where the real issue comes in, is that for border security operations, the reality is that the cartels in Mexico, they know perfectly well how to exploit the flow and the movement of people. And they use that as a cover. So that's when they move the kind of people high value, you know, sort of people who are paying a lot more money to come in to the US undetected, whether they're spies or terrorists, or hackers or whoever it happens to be.\par \par \uc2 That's when they're moving more the drugs. That's when they're moving their cash. That's when they're moving the weapons. That's when they're moving everything. They exploit that issue. And as long as it's only defined as a conversation about immigration, then you don't address the security issues.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And I think we should point out that there's a reason they're coming. I mean, the whole idea is the senior national security fellow at the Center for Immigration Studies said the following here, "I've just returned from interviewing migrants in Juarez, without exception, all of them had been made expected and excited that they will be able to come into the US with no fear or possibility of rejection or later deportation."\par \par \uc2 He's right. I mean, these migrants are on social media, they know what's going on in the United States. They know the climate is changing rapidly, Lara.\par \par \uc2 LOGAN: Well, look at what some of the leaders are saying. I mean, that leaders -- the statement from Honduras, when the migrant caravan that's heading for the US right now, came up against Guatemalan forces, when they forced their way into Guatemala, and two people were killed. The Honduran statement was that migration is a right, is a human rights.\par \par \uc2 So they're coming up with, you know, with government, given human rights versus what the US Constitution protects, which is God given rights, right? There's a distinction there, and those lines are being blurred in border policy. And yes, there are lots and lots, and lots of people being interviewed and picked up were saying, we know that Biden is coming into power. We know that now we can stay.\par \par \uc2 And this is why, Trace, because this is what will change. Before border patrol agents would apprehend you, you'd be processed. It's a long process, right? That's when you'd see people in so called cages, right?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Right.\par \par \uc2 LOGAN: That's in detention processing centers, facilities, right? Because you're trying to figure out who they are. If you have someone coming from China, and I've seen many Chinese people trying to -- crossing the border illegally when I've been down there. And they say, this is my name, this is where I'm from, how do you prove that? How do you know who they are? You think the Chinese Communist Party is telling you, you know, everything you want to know this person has a criminal record or whatever? No. I mean, the reality is they have no idea who's coming.\par \par \uc2 So there was a detailed process, now, you'll be given a notice to appear with five years, Trace, have your fingerprints and biometrics taken, and everyone except convicted felons will be led into the country.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Lara, I've got about literally 15 seconds for you.\par \par \uc2 LOGAN: And to be legal. When they let you go, you'll be legal.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: 15 seconds, what's the big takeaway from this special? 15 seconds left.\par \par \uc2 LOGAN: The big takeaway is that we are really in the midst of the most unprecedented narcotics crisis. Fentanyl is a synthetic. It's the deadliest drug we've ever seen. And the quantities flooding US streets are phenomenal. They're mixed with cocaine, they're mixed with meth, they're mixed with marijuana, and they're making fake pills. Your kid is not taking Adderall if they got it on the street.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, got it. Look forward to it very much. Thanks for coming on. That's "The Story" for Thursday, January 26th. As always, "The Story" continues. I'll see you back here tomorrow. "Your World with Neil Cavuto" right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020210129eh1s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Joe Biden Ignores Left-Wing Extremism In DHS Bulletin; Democrats Work To Criminalize Political Differences; Joe Biden's DHS Targets Trump Supporters; Fauci Denies Goalposts Keep Moving On Masks; Recommendations To Wear Two Or Three Masks To Reduce Spread Of Coronavirus Examined; President Biden Announces Initiative To Combat Climate Change And Create New Green Jobs; Media Coverage Of Bi