en't super-spreader places given Europe's experience.\par \par \uc2 We went into Italy and France and Germany and Sweden. We went over the data. There were a few little upticks here and there, but they were all handled. They didn't care. This is what I'm saying. You're being way too polite, and that's why we love you, but they didn't care. Now it's a double masks, triple masks, now they are saying triple masks. Let's play it. Let's watch about the triple masking.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CONTESSA BREWER, CNBC CORRESPONDENT: Experts say you can double up with a tight weave fabric mask for added protection. Now, \uc2 Virginia Tech\uc2  researchers found doubling up these cloth masks increases the efficacy from 50 to 75 percent. A three-layer mask could block up to 90 percent of the particles.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Are they not wearing masks in California where you had all of the cases? They're not wearing masks in New York? Were they not wearing masks in Michigan? It seems like everyone I know is wearing masks, and they seem to be pretty good masks. I don't know. I guess we could all walk around in scuba suits all day.\par \par \uc2 ATLAS: When you look at the data from all the surveys, whether it's YouGov, Gallup, this Consortium, Americans have been wearing masks for months. And so that's not the issue. I'm fine, people can wear as many masks as they want as long as we open up -- there should be choices and things. This is a free country. I think what's destructive really is not so much the masks, but what we know is killing people is keeping businesses closed, schools closed, keeping people isolated, suicides, child abuse, domestic --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dr. Atlas, I've got to get this in. I don't want to interrupt, but I've got to get this in. Vaccinations as a percentage of population, OK, we've heard Birx or Fauci complain about this, right. Here's our percentage of vaccinations per population -- 11.3 percent, OK, 11.3 percent U.K., U.S. is 7.1 percent, Germany 2.4, Canada 2.4, France is 1.8. Only a few other countries have better percentages than we have. I think Bahrain and UAE is really good. But they were handed a really good situation with the vaccines. And now they are trying to say, we were handed a disaster. That's untrue. And they have to be held to account for that smear of the Trump administration. Close it out.\par \par \uc2 ATLAS: It's inarguable, really, that Operation Warp Speed was really a tremendous success. We're thrilled -- as people, a regular person should be thrilled that they developed drugs and vaccines in that kind of record time without cutting safety corners. And it's not a political issue. I don't even know why everything has to be political. I went to Washington very naive. I had nothing to do with politics. These people are political animals, and their interested in their own image rather than the facts. You're supposed to be doing the best for the American people. And enough trashing -- it's challenging to get the vaccines, but let's get them to the people who are at risk for dying. That might be a good strategy.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Bingo. As far as I can tell and looking at what the Trump people did, if Biden just went away and just followed what the Trump plan was, we would be actually by three or four months we would actually be in really good shape. But they can't leave it at that. Dr. Atlas, thank you for coming on. Fantastic appearance last night as well.\par \par \uc2 Biden unveils his green jobs plan. We understand the green, but where are the jobs? And why are media ditching their fact-checkers in the Biden era? Raymond Arroyo has it all, "Seen and Unseen" next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for our "Seen and Unseen" segment where we reveal the stories behind the headlines. And for that, we go to FOX News contributor Raymond Arroyo. Raymond, as we've been reporting, Biden unveiled his big climate initiatives today.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, but it wasn't only about climate regulations, Laura. It was about jobs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Today is climate day at the White House, which means that today is a jobs day at the White House. We're not going to lose jobs in these areas. We are going to create jobs. They're going to get prevailing ways to cap those over a million wells. These aren't pie-in-the-sky dreams. These are concrete, actionable solutions. And we know how to do this. The Obama-Biden administration reduced the auto industry -- rescued the auto industry and helped them retool.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, he was right the first time. The Obama Biden administration did reduce the auto industry. They spent $23 billion on a \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  bailout, if you'll remember. This was a sweetheart taxpayer funded deal to shield their union friends from taking pay cuts during the auto bankruptcies, period.\par \par \uc2 But the biggest joke of today was that banning of new gas and oil leases is going to create more jobs.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What a joke.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: He says, well, they can close those abandoned wells. Under Biden's plan, there will be many, many more abandoned wells. This is like me saying my policies are going to create tons of going out of business sign makers, Laura. That's not good. This is all bad.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But wait a second, Raymond, I remember back in the radio days tracking Biden's promises about green jobs, I think it, what, was 2009?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: More green jobs means more money in your pocket at the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes, but the money landed in somebody else's pocket, Laura. It wasn't the taxpayers. Obama and Biden promised 5 million green jobs as part of that $787 billion stimulus package. The only thing green was the piles of cash spent on this boondoggle. They never hit their goal. And remember, they counted janitors, cleaners, and bus drivers as green jobs. This was a boondoggle from a start to finish, including \uc2 Solyndra\uc2 , which you mentioned.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: When they go back and they keep saying, well, it will all be solar, solar has so many great jobs. I'm sure there will be some jobs, but what's going to stop the solar panel manufacturers without Trump's tariffs and other trade protections in place from moving over to Asia, whether it's China or Vietnam or India. They're all going to move overseas. So we're going to be left with no oil and gas jobs, and no solar panel jobs. So I guess we'll -- I don't know what we'll do.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You're 100 percent right. Laura, it China that put \uc2 Solyndra\uc2  out of business, remember that. It really is the job of the media, though, to hold these politicians accountable and call out errors, which is why it is astounding that after years of ruthlessly fact-checking Trump, "The Washington Post" has announced they are backing off of Biden and discontinuing their presidential lie tracker, Laura. Glenn Kessler tweets "We have no plans to start a Biden false or misleading claims tracker, just as we had no plans at this point to start a Trump tracker. The constant tweeting of falsehoods forced our hand. But we will have an open mind and if the need arises we will consider one.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But wait a second, Raymond, Jim Acosta over at CNN, he believes the fact-checkers should not be packed away just yet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIM ACOSTA, CNN CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: We need to devote in our news organizations some resources to fact-checking these fringe ideas that are going to continue to exist on the far right in this country.\par \par \uc2 This is not a time to put away our fact-checkers in some sort of box on the shelf. They are going to be needed to fact-check this movement that is not going away. Trump may be going away, but Trumpism is not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: So it's the movement, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He reveals himself.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The bad man at Mar-a-Lago, he needs to be fact-checked, not the guy spending billions or executive -- issuing executive orders like they are candy at Halloween. I've never seen anything like it.\par \par \uc2 But fear not, Laura. The "Los Angeles Times" is stepping into the gap. They have launched a new project called, "Covering Kamala Harris," which they say is a beat dedicated to her historic rise to the White House, or at least the naval observatory. I guess they don't realize the vice president doesn't live at the White House. But maybe they know something we don't know, Laura. Who knows?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, let's look at some of the penetrating coverage, and it is pretty tough from the "L.A. Times." This is from their \uc2 Instagram\uc2  account. OK, it's just a reposting of Harris' own social media propaganda with absolutely no comment.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Right. Since when is hugging a spouse news? Though there are some important reports here, Laura. Here's one, "How Kamala Harris' decision to join a sorter helped her become vice president." And my favorite, "My grandmother is named Kamala. So is my vice president, why that matters." This is like the In Style magazine or Teen Beat for the political class, Laura. I've never anything like this.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is the tiger beat of politics. And poor old Jim Acosta, Jim Acosta is like Trump's gone, Trump's gone, but Trumpism isn't. Does he not understand that he's just proving to everyone that he's an ideologue on the left who hates populist conservatives. OK, then be a pundit. Don't stand there with your tie on and be some objective journalist. You just look like a fool, OK?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Right or left, these politicians need to be held accountable by the media. That's our job.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And how did Biden's unity message devolve into such a dystopian nightmare? Plus, a shocking poll with some interesting findings about Trump's standings right now. Senator Lindsey Graham here on deck.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Here now, Senator Lindsey Graham, who is the Senate Judiciary Committee chairman, and we're delighted he's with us. Senator Graham, I am just -- I'm not surprised, but with all the negative news that's persisted, of course the second impeachment push, just out, a new Morning Console poll is showing that 50 percent of Republican voters want Trump to play a major role in the party. That's up nine percent since the Capital riots. And it's being driven by a 15-point surge in support from GOP women. And then it goes on to say that, I think it's 81 percent have a positive view of President Trump. That's up from 76 percent before the impeachment vote. So he's off \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , he's not commenting on every news item. So has the absence made the heart grow fonder, Senator Graham?\par \par \uc2 SEN. LINDSEY GRAHAM, (R-SC): Well, I've talked to him twice. So absolutely. So what have we learned? That Trump policies are universally thought to be good by every corner of the Republican Party. If you are a Reagan national security guy, Trump delivered. If you are a pro-life person, he's the best pro-life person in my lifetime, really. If you wanted order out of chaos at the border, he delivered. If you wanted your taxes cut, he did.\par \par \uc2 So there's a fondness for President Trump's policies. There's a respect for what he did. And there's a backlash brewing. This effort to destroy him morning, noon, and night for four years plus, to impeach him when he's in Florida, has created a backlash not just among Republicans. But I'll make a prediction. If he'll chill out, play golf, get involved constructively with the Republican Party, by June of 2021, his numbers are going to be up all over the place.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He will be more popular out of office, perhaps, then he was when he was in office.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But isn't that also partly because people are seeing, Senator Graham, that in many ways he was just a proxy for the American people. Jim Acosta today said that Trump is gone, but Trumpism is here, so we have to fact-check the policies, and targeting conservatives as domestic terrorists. People are seeing this now. It's becoming clearer without Trump necessarily commenting on it every day.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes, I think the reasons that people turned to Trump are even more noticeable after he left. Look at what's going on at the border. They're unraveling the border security that President Trump put in place. The caravans are forming. They are declaring war on the fossil fuel industry in America at the expense of thousands of jobs, making us less energy independent. National Security is best achieved when you don't have to buy oil and gas from people who hate your guts and are crazy. So that's all been rolled back.\par \par \uc2 So I think we'll come back strong in 2022. To my Republican colleagues, there is no way in hell we're going to retake the House and the Senate without President Trump's help. If you don't understand that, you are just missing a lot.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And by the way, Liz Cheney's vote to impeach Trump -- a new poll out on that. This is a Save America PAC, so people say it's not accurate. It sounds pretty accurate to me -- disapprove 69 percent, approve, 29 percent. They are not happy with this vote to impeach the president. It's not that they don't like Liz Cheney, they don't think she's a nice person. They like the fact that Donald Trump fought for the middle class. Energy prices -- he fought for the middle class. Pat Cipollone, you worked on this. McConnell got all these great judges in. People like the policies. They might not have liked some of the other things. They love the policies, and they know Trump in the end was a great president for the middle class. They know that.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes, he was. And Liz is very strong on national security. I agree with them most of the time on national security. Name a president who has been better for the military than President Trump. The problem with House impeachment, it ocurred without a witness being called within 50 hours, and the president didn't have a lawyer.\par \par \uc2 So to my Republican colleagues, if you're looking for a reason to stop this impeachment and to dismiss it as soon as possible, don't legitimize basically and impeachment of the president that you get more rights with a parking ticket than you do in this impeachment.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Senator, thank you so much, great to see you tonight.\par \par \uc2 And up next, the Last Bite. From Russia with love?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: For most Americans, the Russia hoax was debunked long ago, but not for MSNBC's Rachel Maddow. For her, some love affairs are tough to quit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: A weird thing about the previous president and Russia. Every time President Trump spoke with Russian President Vladimir Putin, every time that we know of, we would learn about them having a conversation from the Kremlin. That's what it's been like for the past four years. But hello, new President.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Hello. Is Russia her white whale or Jimmy Hoffa's Waltz? Hmm. Shannon Bream, the "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT" team take it all from here, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020210128eh1r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 President Biden Revokes Keystone XL Permit; Pelosi Send Impeachment To Senate; National Guard in DC Told To Sleep In Parking Garage\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Ari Fleischer, Steve Hilton \par \uc2 8,177 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you very much, guys. Thank you, John, and Sandra. So, any moment now President Biden is going to roll out the reversals of the Trump economic measures, several of them focusing today on expanding food stamps, which, if you remember, was a program that was greatly expanded during the Obama administration. And it was shrunk during the economic expansion under the Trump years at the White House.\par \par \uc2 So we're going to hear about a hike to the minimum wage for federal workers to $15 an hour, which is a doubling of that minimum wage. And we expect that we're going to hear a lot more student debt and other issues that we will address very soon.\par \par \uc2 So it's all part of a massive wave of executive orders and actions, that in sheer numbers surpasses anything that we have seen in the early days of prior presidents. Axios describing these first three days of the first 100 as regulatory a quote, Biden's centrist words, and liberal actions, writing Biden talks like a soothing centrist, he promises to govern like a soothing centrist, but early moves show that he is keeping his promise to advance a liberal agenda.\par \par \uc2 Now, President Biden has said that he could go down as quote one of the most progressive presidents in American history. And Bernie Sanders says that Democrats are ready to make sure that that happens.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): I think if we can give them the support that he needs in Congress, he can in fact, be an extremely progressive president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So for example, while Treasury went to former Fed Chair Janet Yellen who cruised through the Senate Finance Committee with a 26 to zero vote. This is a seat to watch though. Rohit Chopra will be running the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2 . He helped to establish that Bureau in the post 2008 period after the financial crisis with Senator Elizabeth Warren, who was seen as too controversial to take over Treasury, but whose influence is likely to be felt in this powerful position that will have sway over a lot of rules and regulations that affect American businesses.\par \par \uc2 And the banks are also likely to get a new regulator from the Obama Treasury team in Michael Barr signaling a tightening likely of government oversight there as well. President Trump's tax cuts and loosening of government control over businesses in America was cited as one of the strongest igniters of economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Now, this economy, devastated by COVID lockdowns may run into ever tightening government control. So let's get some analysis on all of this from Ari Fleischer, former White House press secretary and a Fox News contributor. And Steve Hilton, host of The Next Revolution. Gentlemen, great to have both of you with us today.\par \par \uc2 We are waiting. We know that the pool was called. So we expect that this economy discussion is going to get underway shortly. Ari, what you would expect to hear from President Biden when he enters this room to start talking about his next part of his agenda on day three.\par \par \uc2 ARI FLEISCHER, FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Well, he won't put it this way. But what's happening is the heavy hand of the regulatory state is back. And that is a hand that we all know makes it very hard for people in the private sector, particularly small businesses to create jobs to grow and to pay their employees more money.\par \par \uc2 Look, if there's one thing we do know about the previous economy, right prior to the pandemic, it was booming. We had the lowest poverty rate since 1959. For people without a high school degree, it was the lowest unemployment rate ever in recorded history.\par \par \uc2 So why wouldn't we want to return to the economic environment that created that type of opportunity for lower income, middle income people? The heavy hand of the regulatory state is already causing people to lose jobs such as the Keystone pipeline. That's what I fear.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, this is a huge issue, Steve, and it's something that I've heard business owners talk about all across the country. And President Trump talked about it a lot. He used to say, Oh, you know, the tax cuts. Everybody says that was so great. But I think the regulations cutting was even better for unleashing economic growth in the country.\par \par \uc2 So now all those businesses across the country are going to look at all this and they're going to see the way this is going and it's going to change I would imagine their approach to the future and how many people they hire and all of that kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HILTON, FOX NEWS HOST: That's exactly right, Martha and I take it one step even further back, because if you actually look at the economic data, what you saw was a rebound in investment, investment in new plant and machinery and job opportunities that caused jobs to rise, employment to rise, wages rise, all of that happened even before suddenly before the tax cuts, even before some of the regulatory cuts.\par \par \uc2 Why? Because businesses all over the country could sense that there was a pro enterprise pro growth administration, it wasn't going to make their lives harder as Ari's quite rightly so it's going to make their life easier. That increased confidence, so much of businesses confidence,\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Gentlemen, stick around. Let's watch this. And we'll talk on the other side. This is President Biden and Vice President Kamala Harris\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: -- the economic crisis in modern history. And the crisis is only deepening, it's not getting better, it's deepening. Yesterday, we learned that 900,000 more Americans file for unemployment. 900,000.\par \par \uc2 They join millions of Americans who through no fault of their own, have lost the dignity and respect that comes with a job and a paycheck. So many of them never thought they'd ever be out of work in the first place.\par \par \uc2 And just like my dad did when he was used to lie awake at night, when I was a kid staring at the ceiling, unable to sleep, because he worried about whether or not he's about to lose his health care, or whether we were going to have the money to pay the mortgage because of the economic circumstances.\par \par \uc2 And now a lot of these folks are facing eviction, or waiting hours in their cars, literally hours in their cars, waiting to be able to feed their children as I drive up to a food bank. It's United States of America, and they're waiting to feed their kids.\par \par \uc2 Folks who are able to still keep their job many have seen their paychecks reduced and they've -- they're barely hanging on and wondering what's next. Sometimes the anxiety about what's going to happen next is more consequential than what actually happened.\par \par \uc2 But this is happening today in America. And this cannot be who we are as a country. These are not the values of our nation. We cannot will, not let people go hungry. We cannot let people be evicted because of nothing they did themselves. They cannot watch people lose their jobs. And we have to act, we have to act now.\par \par \uc2 It's not just to meet the moral obligation to treat our fellow Americans with the dignity respect they deserve. This is an economic imperative, a growing economic consensus that we must act decisively and boldly to grow the economy for all Americans, not just for tomorrow, but in the future.\par \par \uc2 There's a growing chorus of top economic -- top economists that agree that at this moment of crisis, with this, with interest rates, as low as they are historic lows, it is smart fiscal investment, including deficit spending. And they're more urgent than ever.\par \par \uc2 You know, and that return on these investments in jobs and racial equity is going to prevent long-term economic damage and benefits that are going to far surpass their cost. If we don't act, the rest of the world is not standing still in terms of their competitive advantage of the competitive possibilities relative to us, that our debt situation will be more stable and not less stable according to these economists and that such investments in our people is going to strengthen our economic competitiveness as a nation and help us out compete our competitors in the global economy. Because we're going to grow the economy with these investments.\par \par \uc2 The COVID-19 package that passed in December was the first step. As I said at the time, it's just a down payment. We need more action, right. We need to move fast.\par \par \uc2 Last week, I laid out a two-step plan of rescue and recovery, to get through the crisis, and to a better and stronger and more secure America. The first step of our American Rescue Plan is a plan to tackle the pandemic and get direct financial relief to Americans who needed the most.\par \par \uc2 You know, in just a few days, it's just been a few days since outlined this plan and received bipartisan support from majority of American mayors and governors, businesses and labor organizations have together welcomed it as urgent action is needed. Even Wall Street firms have underscored its importance.\par \par \uc2 In fact, an analysis by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  estimates that if we pass our American rescue plan, the economy would create 7.5 million jobs just in this year alone. That would be on the way to the more than 18 million, I think is 18,600,000 jobs that they believe would be created over the four-year period with our build back better recovery plan.\par \par \uc2 And with our American rescue plan, our economy would return to full employment a full year faster than without the plan. Even President Trump's -- President Trump's now, not some liberal organization, President Trump's top former economic adviser, \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2  said, quote, he absolutely is in favor of this rescue plan. This almost doesn't have a partisan piece to it.\par \par \uc2 We're seeing the support because this plan takes a step that we so urgently need more than just a step number steps. It funds big parts of the COVID-19 national strategy that I released yesterday -- we released yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Our national strategy puts on us on a war footing to aggressively speed up our COVID-19 response, especially on vaccines and testing and reopening in our schools. I found a fascinating yesterday the press asked the question is, you know 100 million enough week before they said, Biden you crazy you can't do 100 million in 100 days. Well, we're gong to -- God Willie, not only do 100 million, we're going to do more than that. But this is we have to do this, we have to move.\par \par \uc2 The American Rescue Plan also includes economic relief for most Americans who are in need. We're going to finish the job of getting a total of $2,000 in direct payments to folks, $600 which has already passed is simply not enough. If you still have to choose between paying your rent putting food on the table.\par \par \uc2 We'll will extend unemployment insurance benefits for millions of workers beyond the deadline is now set. It means that 16 million Americans are currently relying on unemployment benefits while they look for work can count on these checks continuing to be there in the middle of this crisis.\par \par \uc2 The American Rescue Plan also addresses the growing housing crisis in America, approximately 14 million Americans, 14 million have fallen behind on rent, and many risks of eviction. If we fail to act, there will be a wave of evictions and foreclosures in the coming months as this pandemic rages on, because there's nothing we can do to change the trajectory of the pandemic in the next several months.\par \par \uc2 So look, you should overwhelm emergency shelters increased COVID-19 infections as people have to -- have nowhere to go and are socially -- can't socially distance. The American Rescue Plan ask Congress to provide rental assistance for millions of hard hit families and tenants. This will also be abridged economic recover for countless mom and pop landlords who can't afford not to have the rent. But they can't wait.\par \par \uc2 So on Inauguration Day, I directed my administration to extend nationwide restrictions on evictions and foreclosures. These crises are straining the budgets of states and cities and tribal communities that are forced to consider layoffs and service reductions among essential workers, police officers, firefighters, first responders, nurses are all at the risk of losing their jobs. Over the last year, more than 600,000 educators have lost their jobs in cities and towns.\par \par \uc2 The American Rescue Plan will provide emergency funding to keep these essential workers on the job and maintain essential services. Look, it will also help small businesses that are the engines of economic growth.\par \par \uc2 When you say small business, most people think the major corporate entities are the ones who hire everybody. These small businesses glues that hold and they're important but these small businesses that hold the community that the glue that holds these communities together. They are hurting badly and they account for nearly half of the entire U.S. workforce.\par \par \uc2 Now we have our Rescue Plan, which will provide flexible grants to help the hardest hit small businesses survive the pandemic. And low cost capital to help entrepreneurs of all backgrounds create and maintain jobs, plus provide essential goods and services that communities so desperately depend on.\par \par \uc2 Look, our Recovery Plan also calls for an increase in the minimum wage at 15, at least $15 an hour. No one in America should work 40 hours a week, making below the poverty line. $15 gets people above the poverty line.\par \par \uc2 We have so many millions of people working 40 hours a week working in somewhat two jobs. And they're still below the poverty line. Our plan includes access to affordable childcare, that's going to enable parents particularly women to get back to work. Millions who are not working now because they don't have that care.\par \par \uc2 All told the American Rescue Plan would lift 12 million Americans out of poverty and cut child poverty in half. That's 5 million children lifted out of poverty. Our plan to reduce poverty in the black community by one-third and reduce poverty in Hispanic community by almost 40 percent.\par \par \uc2 I look forward to working with members of Congress of both parties to move quickly to get this American Rescue Plan to the American people. And then we can move with equal urgency and bipartisanship to the second step of our economic plan, to build back better the Recovery Plan.\par \par \uc2 It's a plan that's going to make historic investments in infrastructure, manufacturing, innovation, research and development, and clean energy and so much more. That's going to create millions more jobs, good paying jobs, not minimum wage jobs.\par \par \uc2 While we work with members of both parties in the Congress, there are steps that we can and must take right now. For example, on Inauguration Day, I directed my administration to pause student loan repayments for interest -- for the interest payments for Americans with federal student loans until at least September. So they're not going to have to pay till September, they still pay the bill as it stands now. But they will not accrue interest, and they don't have to pay begin to pay until September. And we may have to look beyond that I might add.\par \par \uc2 Today, I'm signing an executive order that directs the whole government. A whole government effort to help millions of Americans who are badly hurting requires all federal agencies to do what they can do to provide relief to families, small businesses and communities. And in the days ahead, I expect agencies to act.\par \par \uc2 Let me touch on two ways these action can help change Americans lives. We need to tackle the growing hunger crisis in America, one in seven households in America, one in seven. More than one in five black and Latino households in America report, they do not have enough food to eat. That includes nearly 30 million adults, and as many as 12 million children.\par \par \uc2 And again, in this situation through no fault of their own. It's unconscionable. The American Rescue Plan provides additional emergency food and nutrition assistance for tens of millions of children and families to address this crisis. But families literally can't wait another day.\par \par \uc2 As a result of the executive order, I'm going to shortly sign it, the Department of Agriculture will consider taking immediate steps to make it easier for the hardest hit families to enroll and claim more generous benefits in the critical Food and Nutrition Assistance area. This can help tens of millions of families, especially those who can't provide meals for their kids, who are learning remotely at home or not receiving the regular meal plans that they have at school for breakfast or lunch. It's going to also -- we also need to protect the health and safety the American worker.\par \par \uc2 Right now approximately 40 percent of households in America have at least one member with a preexisting condition. Just imagine, you're out of work through no fault of your own. You file for unemployment while you're looking for a job. You find one and you get an offer. But then you find out there's a high risk of you're getting infected with COVID-19. Because of your condition, you and your loved one, and you and your loved ones have ever greater risk of death and serious illness because of the preexisting conditions. So you turn it down.\par \par \uc2 Right now if you did that, you could be denied unemployment insurance because you're offered a job he didn't take it. It's wrong. No one should have to choose between their livelihoods and their own health or the health of the loved ones in the middle of a deadly pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Because the executive order I'm about to sign, I expect the Department of Labor to guarantee the right to refuse unemployment, employment, that will jeopardize your health. And if you do so, you'll, still be able to qualify for the insurance. That's a judgment the Labor Department will make.\par \par \uc2 Look, there are just two consequential ways that the action I'm taking today will help people in need. Another way to help approximately 2 million veterans maintain their financial footing by pausing federal collections on overpayments and debts.\par \par \uc2 Another make sure that federal contractors are saving taxpayers dollars, provide their workers with the pay and benefits they deserve. These are places where federal tax dollars are administered are being made available to build things from ships to staircases.\par \par \uc2 And we let out the federal government let's the contract and we're going to make sure that they buy American and are made in America. There's another right now there are up to 8 million people that are eligible for direct payments from the CARES Act and a relief bill passed in December. They're entitled those payments but there's not an easy way for those folks to assess -- access them. So we're making it a priority today to fix that problem and get the relief, they're entitled to.\par \par \uc2 Look, I'm going to close and summarize this way, a lot of America is hurting. The virus is surging, were 400,000 dead expected to reach well over 600,000. Families are going hungry, people are at risk of being evicted. Job losses are mounting again, we need to act.\par \par \uc2 No matter how you look at it, we need to act. If, if we act now our economy will be stronger in both the short and long run. That's what economists left, right and center telling us, both liberal and conservative will be better and stronger across the board. If we act now, we'll be better able to compete with the world. If we act now, we better able to meet our moral obligations to one another, as Americans.\par \par \uc2 I don't believe that people in this country just want to stand by and watch their friends, their neighbors, co-workers, fellow Americans go hungry, lose their homes and lose their sense of dignity and hope and respect. I don't believe that, especially in the middle of a pandemic that's so weakened and wrecked so much havoc and caused so much pain on America. That's not who we are.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is this. We're in a national emergency. We need to act like we're in a national emergency. So I've got to move with everything we've got. And we got to do it together. I don't believe Democrats or Republicans are going hungry and losing jobs. I believe Americans are going hungry and losing their jobs. We have the tools to fix it. We have the tools to get through this. We have the tools to get this virus under control, and our economy back on track. And we have the tools to help people. So let's use the tools. All of them use them now.\par \par \uc2 So I'm going to sign this executive order, let me conclude again by saying, folks, this is one of the cases where business, labor, Wall Street, mainstream, liberal, conservative, economists know we have to act now, not only to help people are in need now. But to allow us to be in a competitive position worldwide and be the leader of the world economy in the next year or two and three and going forward.\par \par \uc2 So thank you, I'm going to sign this executive order.\par \par \uc2 First one is the Economic Relief related to COVID-19 pandemic reference.\par \par \uc2 The second one is Protection, Protecting the Federal Workforce.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, do you support (INAUDIBLE) timeline for February?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I haven't heard the detail of it. But I do think that having some time to get our administration up and running we may. I want to thank the Center for passing out our Secretary of Defense, looks like our Secretary of Treasury, looks like our Secretary of State's in place. So the more time we have to get up and running and meet these crises, the better. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, well, there you have it. President Biden on his third round of executive order signing in the 48 hours or so that he has been President of the United States. Let's bring in Ari Fleischer and Steve Hilton once again, with reaction to this.\par \par \uc2 You know, it strikes me this has been a two part program he talked about rescue is dealing with COVID-19 and recoveries getting the economy back up and running. From everything that I heard Ari, recovery means tools, which equals money out the door to a number of different entities. And we heard it going to the military, how to go into people food stamp programs, all of that kind of thing. Not a lot of discussion about reopening America businesses here.\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: Exactly, exactly. Well, first and foremost, it does seem to me the president allergic to taking questions. Normally, presidents take a bunch of questions after an appearance like that.\par \par \uc2 In terms of what the President did by executive order today, I think is absolutely accurate to say that the return of big government is back. He said, we're going to grow the economy with these investments. Big spending has never grown the economy, it's grown the government, it hasn't solved people's problems. If you want to solve people's problems now, do it by going after COVID, to reopen the economy as fast as possible.\par \par \uc2 I think the ideas of doing more doing what we can on COVID even though much of it is already in the pipeline and being done. The President is empathetic, and that's good. But empathy is not what people need. They need their jobs back.\par \par \uc2 Now, one other just point, Martha --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Yes, I think -- please.\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: The President talks about raising the minimum wage and making sure no one's in poverty again, doesn't that sound good. The poverty level for one individual in America is $12,760. The President is talking about paying Americans on an annualized basis, at least $30,000 a year. That's what a $15 minimum wage translates to.\par \par \uc2 So if there's two people in a family, that's 60,000, at least, and the poverty rate for a family of four is 24 -- $26,200. These are job killers, and it's big government big spending, old time liberalism.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Steve, he said he wanted to treat people with the dignity and the respect that they deserve. And the first person who popped into my mind is the gym owner in New Jersey, you know, who wants to be treated with dignity and respect. He wants to open his business. He wants to feed his family and he wants to feed the workers who work with him in his business. We didn't hear one thing in any of this, about getting America back open again, so people can help themselves.\par \par \uc2 HILTON: Exactly right, Martha, that was exactly my reaction. I was thinking of all the restaurant owners and bar owners and others who you've had on your show I've had on my show, the making that exact point.\par \par \uc2 I listened to the president there with just a rising level of frustration, even anger. He's absolutely right about how many millions of people in this country are hurting and suffering small businesses, workers families, yes. But why are they hurting and suffering because of the lockdowns.\par \par \uc2 These lockdowns are not just cruel. They're actually counterproductive, because they even make the virus worse. Here in California where outdoor dining is shut down sending people back indoors is where they catch the virus.\par \par \uc2 There's a study just a couple of weeks ago, \uc2 Stanford University\uc2  they looked at the data from 10 countries around the world. They showed with the data that lockdowns have no impact on actually reducing the spread. And so we didn't hit anything about reopening the economy. And worse than that what we actually are getting from this administration are incentives to keep the lockdown going.\par \par \uc2 It's kind of like a lockdown bailout, especially to the blue states run by Democrats like California, like New York, where they love all this. They love the big government that Ari's talking about. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is just giving them incentives to keep it all going instead of to reopen the country and help those people for real.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. You know, Ari last thought here, when he talked about deficit spending equaling economic growth. And I know you brought this up a moment ago. But this is the sort of the mentality that we heard a lot in the past in the Obama-Biden administration, that the more that you fuel, the government, the more that you give people programs, food stamps, all of those things. And we all understand that there's a compassionate need for many people in this country. We all want that need met. But the idea that that stimulates growth in the economy, which he said several times, he said lots of top economists, you know, sort of phrases like that, believe this, is that true?\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: Well, he's actually contradicted by his own incoming Treasury Secretary Janet Yellen, who when she was the head of the Federal Reserve warned about the job killing impact of increasing the minimum wage to eye level.\par \par \uc2 And so, across the board from a spending to regulations, to the heavy handed government, the answer is no. We had the highest rate of economic growth early in the Trump administration after the tax cuts were passed higher than anything in the Obama years.\par \par \uc2 But we know from doing what President Obama did that now President Biden is going to continue is it led to low growth 10 years of no salary increases, particularly for middle income and low income people and economic stagnation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: There's a history here. Free markets work the heavy handed government not so much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, and I would just cite the individuals that I mentioned at the top of the show. I know a lot of people don't pay attention to sort of the head of the Consumer Finance Protection Bureau or the head of the Comptroller of the Currency. These sounds like the, you know, sort of obscure jobs to a lot of people, but they're going to have a heavy hand in rules and regulations on a lot of businesses across this country.\par \par \uc2 So I would urge everybody to pay attention to all of these appointments and the power that they could hold in the future in this administration, the current administration, Great to have you both with us to watch all of that, very pleasure. Thank you, Steve Hilton. Thank you as always. Thanks, gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 HILTON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So coming up, a Canadian premier of Alberta, the oil rich area of Canada goes after President Biden's "insulting," he says, cancelation of the Keystone XL Pipeline Project, and threatens legal means even sanctions against the United States. So how is this improving international relations, that's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: With regard to the construction of the Keystone pipeline, something that's been in dispute and it's subject to a renegotiation of terms by us. We're going to renegotiate some of the terms. And if they'd like, we'll see if we can get their pipelines built. A lot of jobs, 28,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's been nearly four years to the day since President Trump green lighted construction on the Keystone XL pipeline. You might remember at this time, his conversations with foreign leaders were being leaked on a regular basis. He was accused of ruining relationships with our closest allies. So will the same now be said potentially of President Bided.\par \par \uc2 Later today, he's going to speak with the Canadian Prime Minister Justin Trudeau, after bringing us back to the Obama day is blocking the Excel project, which would have secured the transport of 830,000 barrels of oil each day, which firms up our energy independence, of course, Trudeau called this disappointment by the new President Biden "disappointing." Other officials there are going further talking about sanctions or retaliation against the United States.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now exclusively Alberta Premier Jason Kenney. Premier Kenny, thank you very much. Good to have you here today.\par \par \uc2 JASON KENNEY, ALBERTA PREMIER: Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you have some pretty strong language on this. We understand that Prime Minister Trudeau will be speaking, in the first phone call as president that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will have with a foreign leader this afternoon. How do you -- what's the impact on the relationship between our two countries over this?\par \par \uc2 KENNEY: Well, first, we congratulate President Biden on his inauguration and election, and hope to have a really close and strong relationship. We have the biggest bilateral trade relationship in world history between Canada and the United States. But the biggest part of that trade is Canadian energy exports, largely from our province here of Alberta. We have the third largest oil reserves in the world. We ship about nearly $100 billion worth of energy to the US every year.\par \par \uc2 Keystone XL would have been a significant, safe, modern increase in that shipment. And it is very frustrating that one of the first acts of the new president was, I think, to disrespect America's closest friend and ally Canada, and to kill a good paying union jobs on both sides of the border. And ultimately, to make the United States more dependent on foreign oil imports from OPEC dictatorships.\par \par \uc2 We don't understand it. Then at the very least, we believe that those who have invested in this project, trusting in the regulatory process in the US should be compensated by the US administration.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, you make a lot of great points there. I want to unpack some of those with you, if I may. One of them is that one of the greatest achievements we've seen in the past several years for the United States and Canada, for all of North America, is energy independence. So if you're a Saudi Arabia and you're looking at the cancellation of this deal today, what's going through your mind?\par \par \uc2 KENNEY: Exactly. This is a good day, a good decision for the OPEC producers in reducing Canadian shipments of energy to the world's largest energy consumer. In particular, Keystone XL would like the current Keystone pipeline, largely ship Alberta heavy crude. And many of the US refineries down along the Gulf Coast are keyed up to process heavy. If they don't get it from Canada, they've got to get it from places ultimately like Venezuela. And I don't see how that's in the interests of the United States.\par \par \uc2 So, our plead to the Biden ministration is, please take a half step back here, get into a conversation with your closest friend and ally Canada, about how we can ensure a future for safe energy exports, continental energy independence and security between Canada and the US, while also taking very seriously, climate policy and environmental policy.\par \par \uc2 You know, President Obama State Department concluded after rigorous study that Keystone XL would actually reduce carbon emissions, because the alternative will be shipping it by rail, which is higher emitting and less safe.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, it's a great point. I don't know how they get around that in the Biden ministration because, as you point out, five times the \uc2 United States State Department\uc2  evaluated the environmental aspects of the Keystone pipeline deal. And all five times the State Department said that they could not find any increase in environmental issues that would arise from this.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, one of my questions for you is when you look at what happens now, right? So your country has put a billion dollars in taxpayer money into building this pipeline. It's not finished yet. So what happens now and how does this oil get from Canada to the Gulf? How will it get there?\par \par \uc2 KENNEY: Well, first we hope that Prime Minister Trudeau will raise it with the President, asked him to sit down and discuss this in the broader context of energy and the environment, we are so closely integrated in our economies, that we need that kind of cooperation.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, we are sending a message that there should be, the Canadian national government should stand up for our interests, just like we did against President Trump's unfair steel tariffs against our country, just like we stood up to his effort to rip up NAFTA. And so, we hope a similar position is taken here.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, we would at the very least, it demanded the \uc2 US government\uc2  provide a compensation to Canadian investors, including Alberta's government for having taken the US regulatory process at face value. There was a permit, it was legally approved, investments were made on that basis. But ultimately, Canadian energy continues to flow to the United States.\par \par \uc2 One thing we're concerned about, our political forces trying to decommission a number of other safe pipelines that have been operating for decades. So I would call on our friends in the United States to realize Canada is your closest friend and ally, we're a much safer source of energy to fuel your economy than OPEC dictatorships.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Before I let you go, you called for potential sanctions against the United States over this decision. Do you think that Prime Minister Trudeau will go that far when he speaks with President Biden on that phone call?\par \par \uc2 I don't know, but all I can do is represent the interests of Albertans. We have the third largest oil reserves on Earth. We ship nearly 100 billion of that to the United States. We need that partnership. And we need to defend that as well as other efforts to decommission long standing pipelines. Doing so would be devastating to the American economy, we need to find a way to work together on these issues.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I got to let you go. I know you're short on time, but just, you know, 30 more seconds here. Were you shocked by this decision? Did you look at President Biden as somebody who might say, well, let's look at this deal. Let's see how it's working, and maybe we want to pull out and maybe we don't?\par \par \uc2 KENNEY: Well, I wasn't shocked because his campaign did say that they would veto the presidential permit. Having said that, I was really disappointed that the new president didn't give enough respect to America's closest friend and ally, to at least sit down with us and hear our case about how we've reduced carbon emissions from Canada's oil sands about how this pipeline would operate on a net zero carbon emitting basis about First Nations, American native communities that are taking an ownership stake in this, but the huge support of unions, the economic benefits, we have a strong case to make. And we just wish the US administration would let us make that case.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, very interesting. We're going to be following it closely. Premier Kenney of Alberta, thank you so much for being here today. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 KENNEY: Thanks for your interest.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: National Guard troops were sent to the Capitol in enormous numbers to protect lawmakers than they ended up on the garage floor. They have slept in worse places, of course, but the lack of respect comes on top of vetting to make sure that they weren't strong Trump supporters. So now, the governors of Texas and Florida say enough is enough. They're bringing their guards home immediately. Senator Tom Cotton an Iraq Afghanistan veteran joins us, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So even those who think that the President's incited, President Trump incited the Capitol Hill rioters have questioned the wisdom of impeaching a now private citizen living in Florida. But former President Trump's impeachment trial will now start on Monday.\par \par \uc2 House Speaker Nancy Pelosi got that ball rolling. She signaled that she will deliver the article of impeachment to the Senate, for second time. In recent memory, we all remember this procession as we watched it the first time around. This article of impeachment incitement of insurrection at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 The President will be represented by Attorney Butch Bowers of South Carolina. He is a newly appointed attorney on that case. So that moves forward and we will be following it closely. The now, Minority Leader Mitch McConnell was also pushing to delay that trial until February. He did not get his wish on that.\par \par \uc2 Also, Senator Josh Hawley says that Democrats are trying to silence dissent. That's after seven Democrats asked for an ethics committee investigation into Senators Hawley and Cruz for leading the challenge to the election results in the Senate, and any rule that may have played in the Capitol Hill riot.\par \par \uc2 Senator Hawley calls that an abusive process. Senator Cruz says Democrats are playing political games and ignoring President Biden's call for unity.\par \par \uc2 Inside the Capitol after they spent part of the night in a parking garage, it's got a lot of attention today. The guard says that it did have heat down there so that was good. But Politico reports, there was just one bathroom with two stalls for 5,000 troops. So that was a long several hours no doubt. Lawmakers scrambled to bring pizza and take pictures with them, and offer their couches. What a mess this whole thing was.\par \par \uc2 Still, some veterans point out, no, they've slept in worse places than that. So, Republican Senator Tom Cotton of Arkansas joins me now, a veteran of the Iraq and Afghanistan wars. Senator Cotton, good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 You know, these guys are tough. They've slept in a lot of tough places and probably worse than the garage, but it's an insult to their service no doubt.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): Well, yes. Martha, it's safe to say that most of those troopers have slept in much tougher places in a parking garage, but it shouldn't have happened.\par \par \uc2 Well, yeah, Martha, it's safe to say that most of those troopers have slept in much tough, tougher places in a parking garage, but it shouldn't have happened. I'm not sure why it happened. I'm glad it was corrected quickly and they were readmitted into the Capitol Complex. And we should get to the bottom of why it happened, but I think it underscores a broader reality, Martha. It's time to send home the troops.\par \par \uc2 What happened on January 6th was a failure of the senior leadership of the Capitol security services, to anticipate what was widely speculated about on social media to prepare their police officers with right gear and proper rules of engagement, and to ask for backup from the National Guard. That was a failure of the senior leadership, the Capitol security services and the Washington DC mayor.\par \par \uc2 Now, the National Guard rushed to the scene on January 6th, and we saw over 25,000 National Guard there for the last two weeks to help protect the Capitol from the inauguration, during the inauguration. The inauguration is over. We don't want to turn our capital into an armed fortress. It's time for these National Guard troopers to go home to their families and do their jobs in their communities.\par \par \uc2 If there appears to be credible threats in the future, then the Capitol Police can be prepared for it. They can rely on federal law enforcement agencies like the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Park Police to back them up. And if necessary, the Department of the Army can have the National Guard from Washington and Maryland, and Virginia, on rapid standby. We don't have to see a repeat of what we saw on January 6th, but we also don't have to turn our Capitol, the people's house, into an armed fortress.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. There's a practical response to the threat and a proportionate response to the potential threat at the Capitol that can meet -- be met by the forces that you discussed. And we know that already Florida and Texas have recalled their National Guard. I believe New Hampshire also recall this National Guard.\par \par \uc2 25,000 is an overwhelming force that does not appear to be necessary, and they can get back to business and be ready for whatever is next to come on their agenda.\par \par \uc2 I want to skip about a couple of other quick things, if I may, Senator Cotton, and that is the renewal of the START Treaty, which is going to be part of the Biden White House agenda. Here's a statement by John Kirby, Chief Pentagon spokesman on this.\par \par \uc2 He says, we cannot afford to lose new starts intrusive inspection and notification tools. Failing to swiftly extend New START would weaken America's understanding of Russia's long range nuclear forces.\par \par \uc2 So they're ready to resign that deal for five years. I know, President Trump's administration wanted to renegotiate it, felt that it was weak, and that it needed to be strengthened. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: This is a very bad decision, Martha. It puts America's national security at risk. The New START treaty is a one-sided treaty that favors Russia. The way you know that is Russia has been pleading for a five-year extension with no conditions and no other concessions. The Trump administration was right to refuse that. Unfortunately, the administration, on their very first full day in office, granted that major concession to Russia and to Vladimir Putin.\par \par \uc2 So, I guess after four years of the Democrats beating their chest about Russia, and continuing on and on about the Russia collusion hoax, have now gone back to their old weak, dovish ways of appeasing Russia. We are at the risk, Martha, of facing nuclear overmatch by the combined nuclear forces of Russia and China. And it was a terrible decision for the Biden ministration to simply fold their cards and give Vladimir Putin exactly what he wanted on their very first full day in office.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. You have said that there are other technologies, other warfare that Russia is working on, and that they fall outside of this treaty, that we need to expand this treaty in order to encapsulate those other threats that are out there. What fall short if we resign this?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Martha, the New START treaty covers what's called the traditional nuclear triad air-launched cruise missiles from bombers, sea launched ballistic missiles from submarines and our missiles that are based in ground silos. But it doesn't cover tactical nuclear weapons, the smaller nuclear warheads in which Russia has many, many times what the United States has, or some of these very new, very sophisticated weaponries and also have nuclear devices on them as well that Vladimir Putin has boasted about his annual press conferences.\par \par \uc2 Those are just some more of the reasons why the New START treaty is one- sided, benefits Russia. It ties one hand behind America's back, but it leaves Russia free to continue to develop all these other nuclear technologies, while not requiring China to do anything while China build nuclear forces at a breakneck pace.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. It seems curious to not want to at least open up that negotiation and get some more things put into it before you sign a five- year deal. I mentioned the reasons why the Pentagon believes that it's a good idea.\par \par \uc2 Last minute here, I just want to read you this with regard to a totally different topic, the 40th anniversary of this of the Roe v Wade. Biden reaffirms his -- President Biden marked the 48th anniversary of the Supreme Court's landmark ruling establishing the right to an abortion by reaffirming his support for enshrining abortion rights in federal law. Your reaction to that, senator, before we go today.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, Joe -- yes. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  also re-announced or announced that he would repeal the Mexico City policy which will allow taxpayer dollars, once again, go to promote and provide abortion around the country. I think it's a sad fact that the Biden ministration his first week in office wants to do more to encourage abortion to make abortion more common, as opposed to trying to help women in need so we can save every single unborn child so they can live a full and happy life.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Senator Tom Cotton, thank you very much, sir. Good to see you today.\par \par \uc2 We are going to have more of "The Story" right after this. Stay with us, after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So that is the story of Friday, January 22, 2021. And that wraps up our first week here at 3:00 pm. If your friends or family are looking for us, tell them they can find The Story right here at 3:00 pm. We so appreciate you being with us each day and the story always continues. So we will see you back here on Monday at 3:00, when the impeachment hearing trial will get underway, or at least the articles will be delivered. "Your World with Neil Cavuto," though, is coming up right now. So stick around.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020210123eh1m00001\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pa